window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("system-design", {"topics":[{"key":"system-design/ai-solution-architecture","sources":["Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","Zaharia et al., 2024 — The Shift from Models to Compound AI Systems (BAIR blog)"],"why":"- **The prototype is one function, and its failures are not in the model.** Build a prompt, call the\n  API, show the answer: it demos and ships. Then answers vary between runs (regression or noise?), cost\n  scales with every user and retry, the prompt swells to 3,000 tokens of rules for lack of retrieval and\n  tools, a pasted contract gets an invented clause, security asks who sees whose documents, and a\n  provider deprecation breaks the tuned prompt.\n- **A model breaks three classical assumptions.** Classical components are deterministic, unit-testable\n  and cheap per call. A model is specified by data (prompts, examples, retrieved context) rather than\n  code, its quality is measurable only statistically over a dataset, and latency and per-call cost are\n  first-order design constraints.\n- **The model is the small box.** Sculley et al. called the classical version hidden technical debt:\n  the model is a small box and plumbing is the rest of the diagram. With rented, general-purpose LLMs\n  the ratio is more extreme, because your competitor rents the same model. Differentiation lives in\n  context assembly, output constraints and the evaluation loop.\n- **\"Better prompts will fix it\" fails.** A prompt is a configuration surface, not an architecture. It\n  cannot give you access control, retrieval freshness, caching, retries, fallbacks, schema guarantees,\n  evaluation or cost accounting.","how":"### The layers and why each is separate\n1. **Experience and integration layer.** UI, public API, chat channels, IDE plugins; owns identity,\n   sessions and streaming to the client. It never holds a prompt or a model key, *because* otherwise\n   every surface couples to one model's behaviour and cannot be changed centrally.\n2. **Orchestration layer.** Turns a request into one or more model calls: context assembly (retrieval,\n   memory, tool results), prompt rendering, routing, the tool-execution loop, output parsing. Most\n   application code lives here *because* this is the deterministic part you can test.\n3. **Model layer.** A portfolio of models behind a gateway: pinned versions, fallbacks, rate limits,\n   cost metering, provider credentials. Separate *because* models change on the provider's schedule,\n   not yours, and the gateway is the one place to enforce budgets and policy.\n4. **Knowledge and data layer.** Ingestion, chunking, indexes, memory stores, attribute lookups, each\n   carrying access-control metadata. Separate *because* freshness, permissions and provenance are data\n   engineering problems; a prompt cannot know a document was revoked an hour ago.\n5. **Control layer.** Input and output guardrails, schema validation, policy checks, approval gates\n   before consequential actions. Exists *because* model output is untrusted input: a retrieved document\n   can carry an injected instruction, and the model will sometimes follow it.\n6. **Evaluation and observability.** Traces of every call (prompt, context, output, tokens, latency,\n   cost), golden datasets, offline eval gates in CI, online metrics, feedback capture. Exists *because*\n   quality is statistical: a test case tells you nothing, a dataset tells you a distribution.\n\n### Design moves specific to AI systems\n- **Push determinism out of the model.** Lookups, arithmetic, routing on known fields, formatting and\n  validation belong in code: cheaper, faster, testable, no hallucination. Reserve the model for language\n  understanding and generation.\n- **Treat context assembly as the primary data flow.** Quality is bounded by what is in the context\n  window. The architecture decides what is retrieved, in what order, under what token budget and with\n  which permissions; most quality bugs originate here.\n- **Make budgets non-functional requirements.** A latency budget (say p95 ≤ 3 s, split across\n  retrieval, prefill and decode), a token budget per request, a cost per successful task. These drive\n  model choice, streaming and caching.\n- **Use typed output contracts.** Schema-validated structured outputs give downstream code a real\n  boundary; a parse failure is an error you can count, not silent corruption.\n- **Place humans deliberately.** Where a wrong output is expensive, add review or approval. The\n  architecture decides where that risk boundary sits, not the prompt.\n- **Keep the feedback loop cheap.** Traces → labelled failures → golden set → eval → change in prompt,\n  model or retrieval → release gate. If the loop takes a week, the system will not improve.\n\n### The two budgets as formulas\nLatency and cost are the two NFRs the model call dominates; both decompose into terms the architecture\ncontrols:\n\n```\np95_latency  ≈ t_auth + t_retrieval + t_prefill(input_tokens) + output_tokens / decode_rate\n               + t_validation + P(retry) × t_retry\ncost_per_successful_task = Σ over calls (in_tokens × price_in + out_tokens × price_out)\n                           ÷ fraction of tasks that succeed\n```\n\n- **Output length is usually the biggest latency lever, not model choice.** Decode time is linear in\n  tokens generated, so an answer-length cap, or streaming (which moves perceived cost to\n  time-to-first-token), beats shaving milliseconds off retrieval.\n- **Trap 1: input tokens multiply.** They are paid again on every call in an agent loop or retry.\n- **Trap 2: the denominator.** A cheaper model with a lower success rate can cost *more* per useful\n  outcome once retries, escalations and human rework are counted.\n\n### Solution architecture versus AI architecture\n| Half | Covers |\n|---|---|\n| *Solution* (enterprise integration) | single sign-on, systems of record the context comes from, deployment constraints such as VPC or data residency, compliance evidence, operations and support boundaries |\n| *AI* (model-facing decisions) | retrieval versus fine-tuning, model tiers, agent versus workflow, guardrails |\n\nA working architecture needs both; the FDE usually lives exactly at their seam.\n\n### Artefacts a reviewer expects\n- **C4 diagrams** for structure.\n- **ADRs** for hard-to-reverse decisions: model family, RAG versus fine-tune, agentic versus workflow.\n- **An NFR table** with the budgets.\n- **A data-flow diagram** showing every path by which data reaches a model and where it is stored.\n- **A threat model** covering prompt injection and cross-tenant leakage.\n- **A cost model** tied to expected traffic.","example":"### Worked scenario: claims-handling assistant for an insurer\nAll figures illustrative. Requirements: answer adjusters' questions over policy documents and the claim\nfile, draft customer letters, never expose another customer's data, p95 under 4 s. Load: 2,000\nadjusters at about 20 requests each per day, roughly 40k requests per day.\n\n**Request path:**\n1. SSO token → orchestration service.\n2. ACL-aware retrieval scoped to the claim and the adjuster's entitlements: top 8 chunks ≈ 2,500 tokens.\n3. Prompt = 600-token system prompt + context + 100-token question ≈ 3,200 input tokens.\n4. Gateway routes Q&A to a mid-tier model and letter drafting to a frontier model.\n5. Output parsed into `{answer, citations[]}`; a citation checker verifies every citation points at a\n   retrieved chunk.\n6. Output guardrail scans for identifiers belonging to other claims.\n7. Streamed to the UI; trace written with tokens, latency and cost.\n\n**Latency budget:**\n\n| Term | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Retrieval | 300 ms |\n| Prefill | ≈ 500 ms |\n| Decode, 300-token answer at ≈ 50 tokens/s | 6 s, which blows the 4 s target |\n| Fix 1: stream | user perceives time to first token (≈ 1 s) |\n| Fix 2: cap Q&A answers at 200 tokens | with a \"show more\" follow-up; decode falls to 4 s, so the full answer (≈ 4.8 s) still leans on streaming |\n\n**Cost and where to optimise:**\n- **Volume.** 40k × 3.2k ≈ 128M input tokens per day, plus 40k × 200 ≈ 8M output tokens.\n- **Tiering.** That volume justifies the mid-tier model for Q&A: at an illustrative 10× price ratio\n  between tiers, sending Q&A to the frontier model multiplies the daily bill by ten for answers the eval\n  shows are no better.\n- **Input breakdown.** Caching the fixed 600-token prefix touches only about 19% of input. Retrieved\n  context is 2,500 tokens, about 78%, so cutting top-k from 8 to 5 chunks (≈ 940 fewer tokens, about 29%\n  of input) is the larger saving, *if* the eval shows citation recall does not drop.\n- **Evaluation.** 150 real adjuster questions with reviewed answers, faithfulness measured by citation\n  match, run on every prompt or model change and weekly in production.\n\n### Budget calculator for the two formulas\nRunnable Python; prices are illustrative placeholders, not any provider's list price.\n\n```python\ndef p95_latency_s(t_auth, t_retrieval, t_prefill, output_tokens, decode_rate,\n                  t_validation=0.0, p_retry=0.0, t_retry=0.0):\n    return (t_auth + t_retrieval + t_prefill + output_tokens / decode_rate\n            + t_validation + p_retry * t_retry)\n\ndef cost_per_successful_task(calls, price_in, price_out, success_rate):\n    \"\"\"calls: list of (in_tokens, out_tokens); prices in $ per token.\"\"\"\n    spend = sum(i * price_in + o * price_out for i, o in calls)\n    return spend / success_rate\n\n# Claims assistant, illustrative figures\nprint(p95_latency_s(0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 300, 50))   # 6.8 s: blows the 4 s target\nprint(p95_latency_s(0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 200, 50))   # 4.8 s total, but streamed TTFT is ~1 s\n\nM = 1e-6                                       # $ per million tokens -> $ per token\nmid = cost_per_successful_task([(3200, 200)], 0.5 * M, 2 * M, 0.9)\ncheap = cost_per_successful_task([(3200, 200)], 0.15 * M, 0.6 * M, 0.25)\nprint(f\"mid ${mid:.5f}  cheap ${cheap:.5f} per successful task\")\n# mid $0.00222  cheap $0.00240: the model with a 70% lower price costs more per useful outcome\n```","patterns":["**Gateway with pinned versions and fallback.** When more than one surface calls a model. Route a logical model name through a gateway (for example LiteLLM proxy or Portkey) to a dated, pinned model version with a configured fallback list. Monitor fallback rate, error rate and p95 per provider.","**Eval gate in CI.** On every prompt, model or retrieval change. Run the golden set in the pipeline and fail the build when faithfulness or task success drops below the agreed bar. Monitor the eval score trend and the weekly production re-run.","**Trace every call with cost attribution.** From day one in production. Emit one span per model call with prompt and context references, input and output tokens, latency, cost and tenant, using OpenTelemetry's GenAI attributes where your stack supports them. Monitor cost per successful task and token growth per route.","**Typed outputs with a counted failure path.** Whenever code consumes model output. Validate against a schema (for example a Pydantic model), retry once on parse failure, then fail closed. Monitor parse failure rate per model version; a jump usually signals a silent model change.","**Permissions enforced at retrieval, not in the prompt.** For any multi-user corpus. Pass the caller's entitlements as a metadata filter in the index query so unauthorised chunks never reach the context. Monitor with cross-tenant canary queries that must return nothing.","**Stream and cap output per route.** For interactive answers. Set a maximum output-token limit per route and stream tokens to the UI. Monitor time to first token and total p95 separately."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User via UI or API\"] --> E[\"Experience layer\"]\n    E -->|\"identity and session\"| O[\"Orchestration layer\"]\n    O -->|\"query with caller entitlements\"| K[(\"ACL-aware knowledge layer\")]\n    K -->|\"permitted chunks only\"| O\n    O -->|\"rendered prompt\"| G[\"Model gateway\"]\n    G -->|\"pinned version or fallback\"| M[\"Model portfolio\"]\n    M -->|\"raw output\"| C[\"Control layer\"]\n    C -->|\"valid output, streamed\"| E\n    C -.->|\"parse failure: retry once, then fail closed\"| O\n    O -.->|\"trace: tokens, latency, cost\"| T[(\"Traces and golden set\")]\n    T -.->|\"eval gate on every change\"| O","caption":"Follow one request through the six layers: only the gateway talks to models, retrieval enforces\npermissions before context is built, and every call feeds the trace and eval loop."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes architecting around an LLM different from architecting around a normal service dependency?","a":"- **It breaks three classical assumptions.** A model is specified by data rather than code, its quality\n  is measurable only statistically over a dataset, and latency and per-call cost are first-order\n  constraints.\n- **So the design surrounds it with deterministic code.** Lookups, arithmetic, routing and validation\n  move into code, and an evaluation set replaces unit tests as the measure of quality.\n- **And it is rented.** Versions change on the provider's schedule, so pinning and fallback live in a\n  gateway rather than in every caller."},{"q":"Design a document Q&A assistant for 2,000 users with a p95 budget of 4 seconds. Where does the time go?","a":"- **Decompose the budget:** auth, retrieval (about 300 ms), prefill (about 500 ms), then decode, which\n  is output tokens divided by decode rate.\n- **Decode dominates.** At about 50 tokens/s, a 300-token answer alone takes 6 s, so the levers are an\n  output cap per route and streaming, which moves perceived latency to time to first token.\n- **Then add the rest of the shape:** ACL-aware retrieval, a gateway with a pinned model, typed output\n  with citations, and traces feeding a golden set."},{"q":"A cheaper model cut the per-call price by 70%, but the monthly bill went up. How is that possible?","a":"- **The denominator moved.** Cost per successful task divides spend by the success rate, so a model\n  that succeeds far less often costs more per useful outcome.\n- **Retries and escalations add calls.** Every failure is paid again as a retry, a fallback to a larger\n  model or human rework.\n- **The fix is to measure cost per successful task** on the eval set before switching, not price per\n  token."},{"q":"When would you not split the system into separate layer services?","a":"- **For a single internal tool with one model.** Each hop adds latency and another service to operate,\n  which is not worth it at that size.\n- **Keep the boundaries as modules instead.** A modular monolith keeps orchestration, gateway, retrieval\n  and control code separate, so they can be split into services later without a rewrite.\n- **The flip is multiple surfaces or teams** sharing models, where a central gateway and shared\n  retrieval start to pay for themselves."},{"q":"Why do permissions belong in retrieval rather than in the system prompt?","a":"- **A prompt cannot enforce anything.** The model applies instructions probabilistically, and a\n  retrieved document can carry an injected instruction it may follow.\n- **Filtering at query time means unauthorised chunks never reach the context,** so the model cannot\n  leak what it never saw.\n- **Monitor it with canary queries** across tenants that must always return nothing, plus an output\n  guardrail scanning for identifiers that belong to other customers."}],"tradeoffs":["**Layering vs latency and operations.** Layers give testability and swappable models, but each hop adds latency and another service to run. A single internal tool with one model is better served by a modular monolith that keeps the same boundaries as modules rather than services.","**Logic in code vs iteration speed.** Code gives determinism and tests but iterates more slowly. While the problem is still being discovered a prompt-heavy prototype is right; the architecture hardens as behaviour stabilises.","**Guardrails vs friction.** Guardrails and approval gates reduce risk but add latency and user friction. The flip point is the cost of a wrong action: low-stakes drafting needs far fewer gates than money movement.","**Full tracing vs data exposure.** Tracing prompts and outputs makes evaluation possible but creates a store of sensitive data; retention, redaction and access policies are designed with it, not after.","**Where tracing flips.** The flip is data class: for regulated data, keep redacted traces by default and a small sampled full-fidelity set under tighter access, accepting slower debugging for a smaller breach surface."],"pitfalls":["**Prompt-as-architecture.** A 4,000-token system prompt holding business rules, data and formatting. Symptom: every edit breaks something else and nobody can say what the system does. Fix: move rules to code and data to retrieval.","**No evaluation set before launch.** Symptom: whether a new model or prompt is better is argued from anecdotes, and users discover regressions. Fix: a golden set and a release gate.","**Trusting model output.** Feeding it to SQL, a shell, or an email-send without validation. Symptom: a retrieved document containing an instruction causes an unintended action. Fix: validate and gate.","**Designing for average latency.** Symptom: the demo is snappy; production p95 is 15 s because of long contexts, retries and queueing on the provider. Fix: budget p95 per layer.","**Forgetting the model is rented.** No version pinning, no fallback. Symptom: a provider deprecation or regional outage becomes a product incident with no runbook. Fix: pin dated versions in the gateway, configure a fallback, and write the runbook before launch.","**No cost model.** Symptom: usage grows, the bill grows faster, and the feature is throttled or quietly removed. Fix: a cost model tied to expected traffic, tracked as cost per successful task."],"when":"This is less a pattern you choose than the way to think about any system with a model in it, applied\nat the right weight.\n- **First question:** is a model needed at all? If a rule or a lookup solves it, that is the better\n  architecture.\n- **Prototype:** sketch the layers and implement them as modules in one process.\n- **Moving to production, add in this order:** tracing; an evaluation set and gate; a gateway with\n  pinned versions and fallback; retrieval with access control; output contracts; then guardrails sized\n  to the risk.\n- **Measure:** p95 latency per layer, cost per successful task, eval score, and failure rate by category.\n- **Interviews:** \"design a document assistant for X\" expects the layers above, explicit budgets, the\n  eval loop and the trust boundary around model output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the orchestration layer: context assembly, schema-validated outputs, tracing, and a single model interface so the model can be swapped without touching business logic.","architect":"Owns the layer boundaries, the NFR budgets for latency, cost and quality, the RAG-versus-fine-tune and agent-versus-workflow decisions as ADRs, and the evaluation gate.","fde":"Maps the reference shape onto the customer's identity provider, data sources and deployment constraints, and explains to stakeholders why the model call is the smallest box on the diagram."},"check":{"q":"A team has a working prototype: a web form sends the user's question plus a pasted document to\na frontier model and shows the answer. They want to ship it to 5,000 enterprise users next quarter.\nWhat do you change in the architecture, and in what order, and why that order?","a":"First move the model call behind an orchestration service and a gateway so the UI holds no\nprompts or keys, pin the model version and add a fallback; that is the cheapest change and removes the\nsingle biggest operational risk. Second, build the evaluation loop before touching quality: capture\ntraces, collect one to two hundred real questions with reviewed answers, define the bar. Without it\nevery later change is a guess. Third, replace pasted documents with ingestion and ACL-aware retrieval,\nwhich bounds context size, enforces permissions and enables caching, fixing cost and the data-leakage\nexposure at once. Fourth, add typed outputs with citations and input and output guardrails, with human\nreview wherever an answer drives a decision. Then set budgets, p95 with streaming and cost per task,\nand choose the cheapest model that clears the eval bar. Record each decision as an ADR."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an AI solution architecture keep the orchestration layer separate from the model layer?","options":["Because models cannot execute code, so tools must run in a different process","Because models change on the provider's schedule and the deterministic logic must stay testable and model-independent","Because provider APIs do not allow direct calls from application servers","Because orchestration frameworks require their own deployment unit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The boundary exists so that pinning, fallback, routing and cost policy live in one place while the application logic can be unit-tested against a stable interface. Tool execution location and framework packaging are implementation details, not the reason for the boundary."},{"q":"A team keeps adding business rules to the system prompt because it is faster than writing code. What breaks first?","options":["Token cost rises, and that is the only consequence","The model stops reading instructions past a fixed length","Behaviour becomes untestable configuration: rule changes interact unpredictably and regressions cannot be caught","Provider rate limits are exceeded by the longer prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rules in a prompt are applied probabilistically and cannot be unit-tested, so each edit can silently change unrelated behaviour. Cost does rise, but the architectural failure is losing the ability to reason about and verify what the system does."},{"q":"Which component most determines whether an AI system improves after launch?","options":["A larger model behind the same prompt","A vector database with more documents","Tracing plus a golden evaluation set wired into the release process","A streaming interface that lowers perceived latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Improvement requires seeing failures, turning them into labelled examples, and measuring each change against them before release. A bigger model or more data may raise quality once, but without the loop nobody can tell whether the next change helped or hurt."}]},{"key":"system-design/reference-architectures","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents"],"why":"- **Every first team leaves out the same boxes.** The first document assistant omits evaluation,\n  tracing, access control on retrieval, a gateway with version pinning and output validation, and finds\n  out in production, one incident at a time. The second team repeats it with different names, so the\n  two systems cannot share a review checklist, a monitoring dashboard or an engineer.\n- **It solves three problems at once.** It is a **memory** of which components a shape needs and why,\n  including ones invisible in a demo; a **vocabulary** (\"where is your control layer\" points at the\n  diagram); and a **baseline for deviation**: a team that omits the reranker or approval gate must say\n  why, which is exactly the conversation a review should be.\n- **First principles per team fails.** It is slower and, worse, inconsistent where risk lives, because\n  the omitted parts are precisely the ones that only hurt later (evals, guardrails, cost metering).\n- **One mandated architecture fails too.** A batch extraction job and an interactive agent have\n  different latency, state and risk profiles; forcing one shape onto both produces a bad version of\n  each. A small catalogue, each entry matched to a problem shape, sits between the two.","how":"### What a reference architecture contains\nMore than a diagram. A useful one states:\n- **Problem shape** it fits: interaction pattern, latency class, risk class.\n- **Components** with one-line responsibilities.\n- **Data flows**, including what reaches the model and what is stored.\n- **Default NFRs**: typical latency budget, cost driver, availability target.\n- **Variation points** where legitimate choices exist.\n- **Anti-patterns** seen in the wild.\n\nIt should fit on a page and map directly onto a C4 container diagram; if it needs twenty pages it is a\ndesign document, not a reference.\n\n### The recurring shapes in AI systems\n1. **Retrieval-augmented assistant.** Ingestion pipeline → chunk and embed → index with metadata and\n   ACLs → query path: rewrite, retrieve, rerank, assemble context → model → cite and validate → UI.\n   Interactive; risk is wrong or leaked information. Variation points: hybrid versus dense retrieval,\n   reranker or not, long context versus retrieval. Deep treatment in `rag-architecture`; every box\n   exists for a reason that surfaces only when it is missing.\n2. **Extraction and classification pipeline.** Queue or batch scheduler → document parsing → model with\n   a strict output schema → validation and confidence scoring → human review queue for low-confidence\n   items → system of record. Batch; risk is silent data corruption. Variation points: small fine-tuned\n   versus general model, review sampling rate. Small models and distillation pay off most here.\n3. **Tool-using agent or copilot.** Orchestrator loop: plan, call tools through typed contracts,\n   observe, repeat, with step budgets, approval gates before consequential actions and an execution\n   sandbox. Seconds to minutes; risk is an unintended action. Variation points: single versus\n   multi-agent, autonomy per action class. Deep treatment in `agentic-architecture`.\n4. **Multi-step workflow.** A fixed DAG where some nodes are model calls and others code; each node has\n   a contract and a test. Latency depends on the longest path; risk is low because control flow is\n   deterministic. The default when steps are known in advance; `workflow-orchestration` covers engines.\n5. **Conversational assistant with memory.** The RAG assistant plus session state, memory retrieval and\n   update policies, context compaction for long sessions, and streaming. New risk: memory poisoning and\n   stale personalisation; see `memory-architecture`.\n6. **Platform layer.** A gateway in front of every model: authentication, routing, version pinning,\n   rate limits, caching, cost attribution, tracing, central guardrails. It makes the other five\n   governable at company scale; see `ai-gateway`.\n\n| Shape | Latency class | Main risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 RAG assistant | interactive | wrong or leaked information |\n| 2 Extraction pipeline | batch | silent data corruption |\n| 3 Agent / copilot | seconds to minutes | unintended action |\n| 4 Workflow | longest path | low (deterministic control flow) |\n| 5 Assistant with memory | interactive, streaming | memory poisoning, stale personalisation |\n| 6 Platform layer | adds to every call | shared dependency |\n\n- **Agents need a justification.** The agent shape is justified only when the steps cannot be\n  enumerated up front; the Anthropic guidance on building effective agents is the standard argument for\n  starting with the simplest shape that works.\n\n### Cross-cutting components every shape shares\n- **Evaluation harness and golden set,** because every shape's output is probabilistic and quality\n  is only visible over a dataset.\n- **Tracing with cost per call,** so failures and spend can be attributed to a route and a version.\n- **A prompt and model registry,** so what is running in production is versioned, not folklore.\n- **A control layer** for validation and policy, because every shape's output reaches something\n  downstream.\n\nThey are drawn once in each reference so that no team can claim they were optional.\n\n### How to apply one\nAsk these in order, *because* each one removes shapes the later ones would otherwise have to rule out:\n1. **Does the system act, or only inform?** Actions (writes to a system of record, money, messages\n   sent) put an approval gate and a validation contract on the path regardless of shape.\n2. **Can the steps be enumerated in advance?** Yes → workflow (shape 4), possibly with extraction or\n   retrieval nodes. No → agent (shape 3), with step budgets.\n3. **Is the input a stream of items or a person waiting?** Items → batch extraction (shape 2) with a\n   review queue. A person → interactive, so a latency budget and streaming.\n4. **Must answers be grounded in a corpus the model has not seen?** Yes → retrieval (shape 1).\n5. **Does state persist across sessions?** Yes → add memory (shape 5).\n6. **Is more than one team or product calling models?** Yes → the platform layer (shape 6) belongs\n   underneath all of them.\n\n### Compose, deviate, record, revisit\n- **Real systems are compositions.** Most production assistants are a workflow whose nodes include a\n  retrieval step and an extraction step, behind the platform layer.\n- **Vendor references are inputs, not answers.** Cloud providers and vendors publish reference\n  architectures, but they tend to put the vendor's product in every box; extract the shape and keep\n  component names abstract.\n- **Record each deviation as an ADR with the reason.** \"No reranker because the corpus is 400\n  documents and recall is already above the bar\" is a fine decision; silence is not.\n- **Use it in review.** The component list is the review checklist; the variation points are the\n  agenda for the design discussion.\n- **Keep it alive.** When a system fails in a way the blueprint did not anticipate, fold the lesson\n  back in; a reference architecture that is never updated becomes folklore.","example":"### Worked scenario: \"AI to handle inbound supplier emails\"\nA logistics company asks for this. Discovery shows two distinct jobs:\n- **(a)** classify each email (invoice, delay notice, dispute, other) and extract fields such as PO\n  number and new delivery date into the ERP;\n- **(b)** let a coordinator ask questions like \"which suppliers have slipped twice this quarter\" over the\n  resulting data plus contracts.\n\n**Job (a) is shape 2, the extraction pipeline.**\n1. Mail connector → queue → parser for PDF attachments.\n2. Model call with a JSON schema of about ten fields.\n3. Validation: the PO number must exist in the ERP, dates must be in the future.\n4. Confidence from a second, independent check: the model's PO number must also match a regex pass over\n   the raw text, and the supplier on the PO must match the sender's domain.\n5. Anything that fails a check or disagrees goes to a review queue; everything else writes to the ERP\n   with provenance.\n\n| Aspect | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| Default NFRs | processing within 15 minutes, zero silent writes of invalid data, cost dominated by attachment pages |\n| Variation point taken | start with a general model, route *every* item to review for the first weeks |\n| Lowering review | only as measured precision on reviewed items stays above the bar |\n| Review queue role | simultaneously the safety net and the labelling pipeline |\n| Next step | evaluate a small fine-tuned model once a few thousand reviewed examples exist |\n\n**Job (b) is shape 1 plus a structured-data tool, not an agent.**\n- The questions are answerable by retrieval over contracts plus a parameterised SQL query over the\n  extracted table, so a two-node workflow (route, then answer with citations) is enough.\n- ADR deviation: \"no free-form SQL generation; queries are templates with parameters\", because the risk\n  class is read-only information but the coordinators are not able to check generated SQL.\n\n**Shared:** both jobs sit behind the same gateway and tracing stack; each has its own golden set: 300\nlabelled emails for (a), 80 question-answer pairs for (b).\n\n### Job (a)'s confidence check as code\nEvery independent check must agree before a write; anything else goes to review with its reasons. The\nPO format is illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport re\nfrom datetime import date\n\nPO_RE = re.compile(r\"\\bPO-\\d{6}\\b\")          # illustrative PO format\n\ndef route(extracted, raw_text, sender_domain, erp, today=None):\n    \"\"\"Return ('erp', record) only if every independent check agrees, else ('review', reasons).\"\"\"\n    today = today or date.today()\n    po = extracted.get(\"po_number\")\n    failed = []\n    if po not in erp:\n        failed.append(\"po_not_in_erp\")\n    if po not in PO_RE.findall(raw_text):\n        failed.append(\"po_not_in_raw_text\")      # second, independent pass over the source\n    if erp.get(po, {}).get(\"supplier_domain\") != sender_domain:\n        failed.append(\"supplier_mismatch\")\n    if not extracted.get(\"new_delivery_date\") or extracted[\"new_delivery_date\"] <= today:\n        failed.append(\"date_not_in_future\")\n    if failed:\n        return \"review\", failed\n    return \"erp\", {**extracted, \"provenance\": {\"source\": \"email\", \"checks\": \"all_passed\"}}\n\nerp = {\"PO-123456\": {\"supplier_domain\": \"acme.example\"}}\nemail = \"Hi, PO-123456 will now ship on 2026-11-03.\"\nprint(route({\"po_number\": \"PO-123465\", \"new_delivery_date\": date(2026, 11, 3)},\n            email, \"acme.example\", erp, today=date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ('review', ['po_not_in_erp', 'po_not_in_raw_text', 'supplier_mismatch']): a transposed digit is caught\n```\n\n### The six questions as a shape matcher\nA runnable sketch of \"How to apply one\", fed the support-ticket refund case from Check yourself.\n\n```python\ndef match_shapes(acts, steps_known, person_waiting, needs_corpus, persistent_state, many_teams):\n    \"\"\"The six questions, in order; returns the composition to start from.\"\"\"\n    plan = []\n    if acts:\n        plan.append(\"approval gate + validation contract on the action path\")\n    plan.append(\"workflow (4)\" if steps_known else \"agent (3) with step budgets\")\n    plan.append(\"interactive: latency budget + streaming\" if person_waiting\n                else \"batch extraction (2) + review queue\")\n    if needs_corpus:\n        plan.append(\"retrieval (1)\")\n    if persistent_state:\n        plan.append(\"memory (5)\")\n    if many_teams:\n        plan.append(\"platform layer (6) underneath\")\n    return plan\n\nfor line in match_shapes(acts=True, steps_known=True, person_waiting=False,\n                         needs_corpus=True, persistent_state=False, many_teams=True):\n    print(\"-\", line)\n```","patterns":["**One-page catalogue entry.** When a shape has been built twice. Keep a template in the repo next to the ADRs with fixed headings: problem shape, components, data flows, default NFRs, variation points, anti-patterns. Monitor how often reviews find missing cross-cutting components; it should trend to zero.","**Deviation requires an ADR link.** At every architecture review. The checklist lists each blueprint component; an omitted one needs a linked ADR with the reason or the review fails. Monitor deviations without ADRs, and the same unblessed deviation recurring across teams (the signal for a new entry).","**Review everything, then sample.** For extraction pipelines that write to a system of record. Start with 100% of items routed to human review, lower the rate step by step only while measured precision on reviewed items stays above the bar. Monitor sampled precision, review queue depth and age.","**Exploration variant versus production gate.** For prototypes. Allow a lighter variant (stubbed guardrails, no reranker) during exploration, and enforce the full shape, including eval set, tracing and gateway, as the production gate. Monitor how many prototypes reach users without passing the gate.","**Platform layer as a shared dependency.** Once several products call models. Put one gateway underneath all of them and treat it as tier-0 infrastructure with its own SLO. Monitor added latency per call, gateway availability and per-product cost attribution."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q1{\"Acts, or only informs?\"} -->|\"acts: add approval gate\"| Q2{\"Steps known in advance?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"informs\"| Q2\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| WF[\"Workflow, shape 4\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| AGT[\"Agent, shape 3, step budgets\"]\n    WF --> Q3{\"Items or a person waiting?\"}\n    AGT --> Q3\n    Q3 -->|\"items\"| EX[\"Batch extraction, shape 2\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"person\"| IA[\"Interactive, latency budget\"]\n    EX --> Q4{\"Needs an unseen corpus?\"}\n    IA --> Q4\n    Q4 -->|\"yes: add retrieval, shape 1\"| Q5{\"State across sessions?\"}\n    Q4 -->|\"no\"| Q5\n    Q5 -->|\"yes: add memory, shape 5\"| Q6{\"Several teams call models?\"}\n    Q5 -->|\"no\"| Q6\n    Q6 -->|\"yes: platform layer, shape 6\"| D[\"Record deviations as ADRs\"]\n    Q6 -->|\"no\"| D","caption":"The six questions as a decision path: each answer adds a shape or a component, and the result is a\ncomposition, not a single box."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a reference architecture, and why not just design each AI system from first principles?","a":"- **A validated blueprint for a recurring shape:** problem shape, components, data flows, default NFRs,\n  variation points and anti-patterns, on about one page.\n- **First principles per team is slower and inconsistent where risk lives,** because the parts teams\n  omit (evals, guardrails, cost metering) are exactly the ones that only hurt later.\n- **It also gives a shared vocabulary and a baseline,** so a review discusses deliberate deviations\n  instead of rediscovering missing boxes."},{"q":"A customer asks for \"AI to process inbound supplier emails\". How do you pick the shape?","a":"- **Split the request into jobs first.** Classifying emails and writing fields to the ERP is the\n  extraction pipeline; answering coordinators' questions over that data is retrieval plus a\n  structured-data tool.\n- **Walk the six questions for each job:** acts or informs, enumerable steps, items or a person\n  waiting, corpus, state, number of teams.\n- **Put the human at the action boundary:** a review queue for low-confidence extractions, which also\n  becomes the labelling pipeline."},{"q":"When would you push back on a team that wants the agent shape?","a":"- **When the steps can be enumerated up front.** A fixed workflow gives the same result with\n  deterministic control flow, predictable cost and nodes you can test.\n- **The symptom of the wrong choice** is unpredictable latency and cost, and debugging that ends in\n  \"the model decided to\".\n- **The agent is justified only when the path genuinely depends on what the model observes,** and then\n  it comes with step budgets, approval gates and a sandbox."},{"q":"Which components must exist in every shape, and how do you enforce that in practice?","a":"- **Cross-cutting boxes:** an evaluation harness with a golden set, tracing with cost per call, a prompt\n  and model registry, and a control layer for validation and policy.\n- **Enforce them through review:** the blueprint's component list is the checklist, and an omitted\n  component needs a linked ADR or the review fails.\n- **Measure the process:** missing cross-cutting components found in review should trend to zero."},{"q":"How do you keep a catalogue of reference architectures from going stale or becoming vendor brochures?","a":"- **Fold incidents back in.** When a system fails in a way the blueprint did not anticipate, update the\n  entry, or it becomes folklore.\n- **Watch for recurring unblessed deviations** across teams; that is the signal a new entry is needed.\n- **Keep component names abstract.** Vendor references put their product in every box; extract the\n  shape so switching a component does not mean rewriting the architecture document."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shared blueprint vs novelty.** It speeds design and review but biases teams toward the known shape; a genuinely new problem may need a new reference, and forcing it into an old one hides the novelty. The signal for a new entry is the same unblessed deviation recurring across teams.","**Rich mandatory components vs prototype cost.** Many mandatory components raise the quality floor but also the cost of a prototype. Keep a lighter \"exploration\" variant and insist on the full shape only at the production gate.","**Central gateway vs shared blast radius.** Standardising on the platform gateway centralises governance and cost control but creates a shared dependency whose outage or latency affects every product. It flips to per-team gateways only when teams have truly independent risk and release cadences.","**Central vs incident-driven ownership.** Centrally owned catalogues stay consistent but go stale; ones owned by whoever last had an incident stay current but drift apart. A small owning group with a lightweight change process is the usual compromise."],"pitfalls":["**Agent shape for known steps.** Symptom: unpredictable latency and cost, and debugging sessions that end in \"the model decided to\". Fix: a workflow with contracted nodes.","**Dropping the cross-cutting boxes** because \"we will add evals later\". Symptom: the first model upgrade is a coin flip and nobody can say whether quality moved. Fix: the eval set, tracing and gateway are part of the production gate, not a follow-up.","**Treating the reference as mandatory in every detail.** Symptom: a 400-document corpus gets a reranker, a query rewriter and a multi-hop planner, and the simplest question takes eight seconds. Fix: treat variation points as choices and record each omission as an ADR.","**Reference describes a vendor's product, not a shape.** Symptom: switching a component requires rewriting the architecture document, and the exit cost is discovered at renewal. Fix: keep component names abstract and map vendors onto them per system.","**Never recording deviations.** Symptom: six months later nobody knows whether the missing approval gate was a decision or an oversight. Fix: a review that fails any omitted component without an ADR."],"when":"- **Use when:** starting any design, during an architecture review, and when onboarding an engineer to\n  an existing system. The first question is always \"which shape is this, and where does it deviate\".\n- **Avoid when:** you would use it as a substitute for discovery. The shape follows from the\n  interaction pattern and risk class, which only requirements work can establish.\n- **Measure:** how often reviews find missing cross-cutting components (should trend to zero) and how\n  long design takes for a standard shape.\n- **Interviews:** naming the shape, its components and its default risks in the first two minutes of a\n  system-design question separates practised candidates from those who start with a box labelled \"LLM\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses the blueprint as a build checklist and knows which components can be stubbed in a prototype and which must exist before anything is user-facing.","architect":"Owns the catalogue, decides which shape a new problem belongs to, and insists that every deviation from the blueprint is written down as an ADR with its reasoning.","fde":"Carries the blueprints into customer engagements to scope quickly, and adapts them to the customer's identity, data and deployment constraints without dropping the governance boxes."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a system that reads incoming support tickets, looks up the customer's account,\ndecides whether to issue a refund under policy, and replies. Which reference shape or shapes apply, and\nwhere would you place the human?","a":"This is a workflow, not an agent: the steps are known in advance, so a fixed DAG with contracts\nper node is the right shape. Classify the ticket and extract the claim (extraction shape with a strict\nschema and validation), look up the account through a typed tool, evaluate the policy in code rather\nthan in the model because refund rules are deterministic and auditable, draft the reply with a model\ngrounded on the policy text and the account facts, and send. The human belongs at the action boundary:\nthe refund itself is a consequential, irreversible action, so it goes through an approval gate at\nleast until the system's precision on a golden set justifies auto-approval below a monetary threshold.\nReplies that do not move money can be sent after output validation. Both halves share the gateway,\ntracing and an evaluation set built from historical tickets with their resolutions."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes a reference architecture from a design document for one system?","options":["A reference architecture names specific vendors for each component","A reference architecture describes a reusable shape with variation points and known failure modes, independent of any one system","A reference architecture is produced after the system ships, as documentation","A reference architecture only covers the model and prompt, not the infrastructure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A reference is a blueprint for a class of problems: components, responsibilities, defaults and anti-patterns, with explicit places where choices are allowed. A design document instantiates one for a particular system and records its deviations."},{"q":"A team proposes an autonomous agent for a process whose five steps are the same for every input. What is the architectural objection?","options":["Agents cannot call more than one tool per step","Agents are only appropriate for batch workloads","A fixed workflow gives the same result with deterministic control flow, predictable cost and testable nodes, so the agent's flexibility buys nothing and costs reliability","Agents require fine-tuned models, which the team does not have"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The agent shape is justified when the steps cannot be enumerated up front. When they can, a DAG of contracted nodes is cheaper to run, easier to test and easier to debug; choosing the agent adds variance without adding capability."},{"q":"Which components appear in every AI reference architecture regardless of shape?","options":["A vector database and a reranker","A planning loop and a sandbox","An evaluation harness with a golden set, tracing with cost attribution, and a control layer for validation and policy","A fine-tuning pipeline and a distillation step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retrieval, planning and fine-tuning are shape-specific. Evaluation, tracing and output control are cross-cutting because every shape has probabilistic output whose quality must be measured and whose downstream effects must be bounded."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-selection","sources":["Chiang et al., 2024 — Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference","Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"- **The choice is multiplied by every request.** At any moment dozens of plausible models span one to\n  two orders of magnitude in price per token and several-fold in latency, and the pick is paid for the\n  life of the feature. It deserves a method, and the obvious method is wrong.\n- **\"Top of the leaderboard\" fails for four reasons.** (1) Benchmarks measure broad ability on public\n  data, not your narrow distribution, vocabulary and failure costs, and may have leaked into training.\n  (2) The quality gap among the top two or three is often smaller than their cost gap, so the ranking is\n  unstable exactly where the money is. (3) Non-quality dimensions decide feasibility. (4) Your prompt\n  was tuned on the starting model, so a naive swap underrates every other candidate.\n- **The feasibility dimensions.** Context length at your p95 prompt size, reliable structured output,\n  tool-calling behaviour, language coverage, rate limits at your peak, data residency and the\n  provider's deprecation cadence.\n- **\"Cheapest and hope\" fails on the tail.** A weaker model's errors concentrate in the hard,\n  high-stakes cases, precisely the ones that generate support tickets or compliance findings.\n- **Threshold versus linear.** Below the bar the feature does not work; above it, extra points rarely\n  change the outcome, while cost and latency grow with volume. So the optimum is the cheapest model that\n  clears the bar with a margin, and only measuring your task tells you which.","how":"### Step 1: apply hard constraints first\nFilter before evaluating quality, *because* no quality number rescues a model that cannot run where the\ndata is. The usual gates:\n- **Modality:** text, vision, audio.\n- **Context window** versus your *measured* p95 prompt size, not the mean.\n- **Output and tools:** native structured output or JSON-schema support; tool calling; languages.\n- **Deployment:** a region, a VPC, on-prem or air-gapped, which narrows the field to open-weight models\n  or private endpoints.\n- **Commercial terms:** licence terms; quota and rate limits at launch peak; version pinning and\n  deprecation policy; data-use and retention terms.\n\n### Step 2: build the task evaluation before touching candidates\n- **Size and source.** Fifty to a few hundred examples from real or realistic traffic, with expected\n  outputs or a scoring rubric.\n- **The task's own metric.** Exact or normalised match for extraction and classification; citation\n  faithfulness for retrieval-augmented answers; a calibrated LLM-as-judge rubric for free-form\n  generation; pass@k for code.\n- **First, because of bias.** Otherwise the prompt gets tuned to the first model tried and every later\n  comparison favours the incumbent.\n- **Over-sample the hard cases.** Hard and high-cost cases are where candidates differ.\n\n### Step 3: run candidates fairly\n1. **Same examples, same prompt structure,** then a bounded, equal amount of prompt adaptation per\n   model, *because* format sensitivity differs between families and zero adaptation punishes the newcomer.\n2. **Fix sampling settings, run several seeds,** and report a mean with a confidence interval.\n3. **Know the noise floor.** On 100 examples a proportion near 0.9 has a 95% half-width of roughly ±6\n   points, so a 2-point gap is noise:\n\n| Fact from the formula | Consequence |\n|---|---|\n| 1.96 × √(0.9 × 0.1 / 100) ≈ 0.059 | a 2-point gap on 100 examples is noise |\n| Repeated runs average only sampling randomness | they do not shrink the error from drawing *these* 100 examples; only more examples do |\n| Halving the interval needs four times as many examples | about 860 examples for ±2 points at 0.9 |\n\n4. **Compare paired.** Every candidate runs on the same examples, so count the examples only model A\n   gets right and only model B gets right (McNemar's test uses exactly these discordant counts).\n   Pairing cancels each example's difficulty, so it detects real differences two overlapping intervals\n   hide.\n5. **Read the discordance pattern.** Few discordant examples means the models behave alike. Many in both\n   directions means they fail on different inputs, which argues for a cascade rather than a straight swap.\n6. **Keep the set private.** A model that has seen your examples is not being evaluated.\n\n### Step 4: measure cost and latency on real request shapes\n- **Cost per request** = input tokens × input price + output tokens × output price, adjusted for cached\n  prefixes.\n- **Measure tokens from the eval runs, don't estimate,** *because* tokenizers differ across families\n  (the same document can be 20–30% more tokens in one than another, and more for non-English text) and\n  output verbosity differs.\n- **Reasoning models.** Hidden reasoning tokens are billed as output on most providers and can dominate,\n  so price per token alone misleads.\n- **Latency.** Time to first token and total latency at p50 and p95, under realistic concurrency, at\n  the hours you will run.\n\nThe number to compare is not cost per call but:\n\n```\ncost per successful task = (cost of all calls, including retries after format failures and escalations)\n                           ÷ (tasks that pass the eval)\n```\n\n*Because* a model 40% cheaper per call that needs a retry on one call in five, or escalates often, can\nlose its advantage once the denominator and the extra calls are counted.\n\n### Step 5: find the frontier and apply the rule\n1. **Plot** candidates on quality versus cost versus latency.\n2. **Discard** any model another beats on all three.\n3. **Choose** the cheapest whose *lower* confidence bound clears the bar and whose p95 fits the\n   latency budget.\n4. **Consider a cascade** if the cheap model fails only on an identifiable tail: cheap by default,\n   escalating to a stronger one on low confidence or a trigger condition, often cheaper than running\n   the strong model everywhere.\n\n### Step 6: operational factors and re-evaluation\n- **Pin an exact model version,** never a floating alias, so quality cannot change under you.\n- **Have a second source.** A fallback model that also clears the bar, even at higher cost, turns an\n  outage or deprecation notice into a configuration change.\n- **Schedule re-selection:** when a deprecation is announced, when a new model could move the frontier,\n  or on a fixed cadence. The eval set makes each re-run cheap; this is the compounding payoff of\n  building it first.","example":"### Selection harness for the quantitative steps\nEach candidate exposes a `call` that returns the text plus the measured token counts; prices are\nplaceholders you fill from the provider's current sheet. The rule requires the lower confidence bound,\nnot the mean, to clear the bar.\n\n```python\nimport statistics, time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Callable\n\n@dataclass\nclass Candidate:\n    name: str\n    price_in: float          # currency per 1M input tokens (fill from current price list)\n    price_out: float         # currency per 1M output tokens\n    call: Callable           # prompt -> (text, input_tokens, output_tokens)\n\ndef evaluate(c: Candidate, golden, score, runs=3):\n    per_example, lat, cost = [], [], []\n    for ex in golden:\n        s = []\n        for _ in range(runs):\n            t0 = time.perf_counter()\n            text, n_in, n_out = c.call(ex[\"prompt\"])\n            lat.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n            s.append(score(text, ex[\"expected\"]))\n            cost.append((n_in * c.price_in + n_out * c.price_out) / 1e6)\n        per_example.append(statistics.mean(s))   # repeats of one example are not independent samples\n    n = len(per_example)                          # the CI is over examples, not over example x run\n    mean = statistics.mean(per_example)\n    half_width = 1.96 * statistics.pstdev(per_example) / n ** 0.5   # rough 95% CI\n    p95 = sorted(lat)[int(0.95 * (len(lat) - 1))]\n    return {\"model\": c.name, \"quality\": mean, \"lower\": mean - half_width,\n            \"p95_s\": p95, \"cost_per_1k\": 1000 * statistics.mean(cost)}\n\ndef select(results, bar, max_p95):\n    eligible = [r for r in results if r[\"lower\"] >= bar and r[\"p95_s\"] <= max_p95]\n    return min(eligible, key=lambda r: r[\"cost_per_1k\"]) if eligible else None\n\n# results = [evaluate(c, golden_set, exact_match) for c in candidates]\n# print(select(results, bar=0.92, max_p95=2.5))\n```\n\nIf `select` returns `None`, no candidate clears the bar within budget. The next moves, in roughly that\norder of cost: prompt work, retrieval improvements, a cascade, or fine-tuning a smaller model.\n\n### Is the gap real? Interval width and the paired test\nRunnable, standard library only. It reproduces the noise-floor numbers above and the paired reading\nused in Check yourself.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb, sqrt\n\ndef half_width(p, n, z=1.96):\n    \"\"\"95% half-width of a proportion; only more examples shrink it.\"\"\"\n    return z * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\n\ndef mcnemar_exact(only_a, only_b):\n    \"\"\"Two-sided exact McNemar p-value from the discordant counts.\"\"\"\n    n, k = only_a + only_b, min(only_a, only_b)\n    return min(1.0, 2 * sum(comb(n, i) for i in range(k + 1)) / 2 ** n)\n\nfor n in (60, 100, 400, 860):\n    print(f\"n={n:4d}  p=0.9  ±{half_width(0.9, n):.3f}\")\n# n=60 ±0.076, n=100 ±0.059, n=400 ±0.029, n=860 ±0.020\nprint(\"3 vs 1  p =\", round(mcnemar_exact(3, 1), 3))     # 0.625: few disagreements, models alike\nprint(\"14 vs 12 p =\", round(mcnemar_exact(14, 12), 3))  # 0.845: many both ways, different failures\n```\n\nBoth 2-point gaps are statistically invisible, but they mean different things: the first says \"read\nthose four examples\", the second says \"the models fail on different inputs; consider a cascade\".\n\n### Worked scenario: the 40%-cheaper model that costs more\nIllustrative numbers, normalised so the strong model costs 1.0 per call.\n\n| | Strong model | Cheap model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost per call | 1.0 | 0.6 (40% cheaper) |\n| Cost with retries (one call in five retried) | 1.0 | 0.6 × 1.2 calls = 0.72 |\n| Escalations to the strong model | none | 25% of tasks → +0.25 |\n| Spend per task | 1.0 | 0.97 |\n| Tasks that pass the eval | 95% | 90% |\n| **Cost per successful task** | **1.05** | **1.08** |\n\nThe per-call price says \"save 40%\"; the per-successful-task number says the swap loses money.","patterns":["**Selection ADR with an evidence table.** For every model choice. Record each candidate's eval score with its interval, cost per successful task, p95 latency and failure categories, plus the re-selection triggers. Monitor whether production metrics stay inside the ADR's assumptions.","**Pinned version in config, never an alias.** For every production route. Store the dated model identifier in configuration behind one model interface, so a change is a reviewed config diff. Monitor provider deprecation notices and have a re-selection ticket opened automatically.","**Exercised second source.** For any user-facing route. Configure a fallback model that also clears the bar and send a small scheduled share of traffic or a synthetic test through it, so it is not first used during an outage. Monitor 429 and 5xx rates per provider and fallback success rate.","**Shadow comparison before a swap.** When a cheaper or newer candidate looks viable offline. Send a sample of production requests to the candidate in parallel without showing its answers, then score both offline. Monitor the paired disagreement rate and which categories the disagreements fall into.","**Log provider-reported usage per request.** Always. Store input, output and cached token counts from each response next to the trace, so cost per successful task is computed from data. Monitor forecast-versus-bill error and output-token growth per route.","**Scheduled re-run of the eval.** On a fixed cadence and on deprecation notices. Run the same harness against current candidates and compare lower bounds with the bar. Monitor the frontier, not release announcements."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"All plausible models\"] -->|\"Step 1\"| F[\"Hard-constraint filter\"]\n    F -->|\"eligible only, Step 2\"| E[\"Private task eval set\"]\n    E -->|\"Step 3\"| R[\"Fair, paired runs\"]\n    R -->|\"Step 4\"| C[\"Measured cost and p95\"]\n    C -->|\"Step 5\"| P[\"Drop dominated models\"]\n    P --> Q{\"Lower bound clears bar, p95 fits?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes, cheapest such model\"| S[\"Pin version, second source, ADR\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| T{\"Cheap model fails only on a tail?\"}\n    T -->|\"yes\"| K[\"Cascade with escalation trigger\"]\n    T -->|\"no\"| N[\"Prompt, retrieval or fine-tune work\"]\n    S -.->|\"deprecation, release or cadence\"| E","caption":"The selection funnel: hard constraints (modality, context at p95, deployment, terms) remove candidates\nbefore any quality work, and the rule at the bottom compares lower confidence bounds with the bar, not\nleaderboard ranks."},"interview":[{"q":"How would you choose a model for a new production task?","a":"- **Filter on hard constraints first:** modality, context window against measured p95 prompt size,\n  structured output and tools, deployment region or environment, licence and rate limits.\n- **Build a private task eval set before trying candidates,** because otherwise the prompt is tuned to\n  the first model and every comparison favours it.\n- **Pick the cheapest model whose lower confidence bound clears the bar** and whose p95 fits the latency\n  budget, then pin the version and keep a second source."},{"q":"Why is \"quality is a threshold, cost is linear\" the core of the decision?","a":"- **Below the bar the feature does not work;** above it, extra points rarely change the outcome.\n- **Cost and latency are paid on every call** and grow with volume, for the life of the feature.\n- **So the optimum is the cheapest model that clears the bar with a margin,** and only measuring your\n  own task tells you which one that is."},{"q":"Your forecast said the new model would cost 30% less, but the first bill matched the old one. What went wrong?","a":"- **Tokens were estimated, not measured.** Tokenizers differ across families, so the same document can\n  be 20-30% more tokens in one, and more for non-English text.\n- **Output verbosity and hidden reasoning tokens,** which most providers bill as output, can dominate.\n- **Retries and escalations were left out of the denominator.** Compare cost per successful task\n  computed from provider-reported usage logged per request."},{"q":"How do you make a comparison between the incumbent model and a newcomer fair?","a":"- **Same examples, same prompt structure,** then a bounded, equal amount of prompt adaptation per model,\n  because format sensitivity differs between families and zero adaptation punishes the newcomer.\n- **Fix sampling settings and run several seeds,** reporting a mean with a confidence interval over\n  examples, not over example-times-run.\n- **Keep the set private and over-sample hard cases,** because a model that has seen your examples is\n  not being evaluated and the hard cases are where candidates differ."},{"q":"When would you deliberately not pick the cheapest model that clears the bar?","a":"- **When the task drifts often.** The cheapest-that-clears choice leaves little headroom, so buy margin\n  with a stronger model or a cascade.\n- **When it has no second source that also clears the bar,** because an outage or deprecation then\n  becomes an emergency migration whose cost can exceed the saving.\n- **When it cannot hold the rate limits or p95 at peak,** because feasibility beats a quality score."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheapest-that-clears vs headroom.** It minimises spend but leaves little margin when traffic drifts harder; if your task changes often, buy margin with a stronger model or a cascade.","**Private eval set vs effort.** It gives a trustworthy comparison but costs expert time and goes stale; a small set has wide intervals, so differences of a few points cannot be acted on.","**Per-candidate prompt adaptation vs effort.** It makes the comparison fair but multiplies the work; skipping it is cheaper and systematically favours the incumbent model.","**One provider vs second source.** Standardising on one provider's best model simplifies operations but concentrates deprecation and outage risk; a second source doubles evaluation work and halves the blast radius.","**Reasoning-heavy models vs cost and variance.** They raise quality on genuinely hard tasks but bring high output-token cost and latency variance; the choice flips as soon as the task is not reasoning-bound."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing on public leaderboard rank.** Symptom: the \"better\" model scores lower on your eval, or wins it and then fails on JSON formatting or tool calls in production. Fix: a private task eval that also scores format and tool-call compliance.","**Estimating cost from list price without measuring tokens.** Symptom: the bill differs from the forecast by a large fraction because of tokenizer differences or verbose outputs. Fix: compute cost from token counts measured in the eval runs.","**Comparing single runs and treating a 2-point gap as real.** Symptom: the ranking flips when someone re-runs the comparison the following week. Fix: report intervals, compare paired, and add examples.","**Deploying a floating \"latest\" alias.** Symptom: quality or format changes overnight with no deploy and no entry in the change log. Fix: pin the dated version in config.","**Ignoring quotas and rate limits at peak.** Symptom: 429 errors at launch, and the fallback path has never been exercised. Fix: confirm quota for launch peak and exercise the second source beforehand.","**Evaluating in English only for a multilingual product.** Symptom: context overflows and higher cost for languages whose tokenization is two to three times less efficient. Fix: include each supported language in the eval set and the token measurements."],"when":"- **Run the funnel when:** a new task is introduced, a deprecation notice arrives, a model release\n  plausibly moves the quality-cost frontier, or the latency or cost target changes.\n- **Avoid:** re-selecting on every release announcement. The eval harness makes a scheduled re-run\n  cheap, so put it on a cadence instead.\n- **Measure:** eval score with its interval, cost per successful task, p95 latency, and failure rates by\n  category (format, refusal, timeout).\n- **Day to day:** this shows up as \"can we move this to the cheaper model\", and the honest answer is\n  always \"the eval says\".\n- **Interviews:** articulate the threshold-versus-linear reasoning, the hard-constraint filter, and \"I\n  would build the eval set first\"; naming a favourite model is not the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the eval harness, measures real token counts and latency percentiles, and keeps the model behind one interface so the next re-selection is a configuration change.","architect":"Sets the quality bar and the latency and cost budgets per task, decides on second sourcing and cascades, and records the selection and its evidence as an ADR.","fde":"Runs the funnel on the customer's own data under their deployment constraints, and shows stakeholders the quality-cost table rather than a vendor's benchmark slide."},"check":{"q":"You are asked to replace an expensive frontier model with one at a third of the price for a\nretrieval-augmented assistant. On your 100-example eval the cheaper model scores 2 points lower. How do\nyou decide, and what would make you say no?","a":"First, 2 points on 100 examples is inside the noise: the 95% interval around a score near 0.9 is\nroughly ±6 points, so the gap is not established, and re-running seeds will not fix that, only more\nexamples will. Expand the set toward several hundred and compare each model's lower bound against the\nbar, not against each other. Because both ran on the same examples, look at the paired view: if the\n2-point gap is 3 losses against 1 win, the models behave almost identically and I read those few\nexamples; if it is 14 losses against 12 wins, they fail on different inputs, which means the score is\nunstable and a cascade may beat either model alone. Second, read the failures by category: if the cheaper model's extra misses cluster in a high-cost class such as\nwrong citations on compliance questions, that is disqualifying even if the total is small; if they\nare stylistic, it is not. Third, check the dimensions the score ignores: format compliance, p95\nlatency, rate limits at peak, and the real cost ratio measured from tokens, since tokenizers differ.\nFourth, consider a cascade, cheap by default with escalation on low confidence, which may capture most\nof the saving while covering the tail. I would say no if the lower bound misses the bar or the misses\nland in the costly category."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"pick the model at the top of a public leaderboard\" a poor selection rule?","options":["Leaderboards are updated too infrequently to be useful","Benchmarks measure a different distribution from your traffic and may be contaminated, while your cost and latency are exact, so the rank says little about which model clears your bar most cheaply","Top-ranked models are always rate-limited more aggressively","Leaderboard models do not support structured output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leaderboards estimate broad ability on public data. Your task is narrow, has its own failure costs, and must fit a budget; the question is which eligible model clears your measured bar at the lowest cost, which only a task-specific evaluation answers."},{"q":"Two candidate models differ by 3 points on a 60-example evaluation set. What is the right conclusion?","options":["The higher-scoring model is better and should be chosen","The cheaper model should be chosen because 3 points never matters","The difference is probably within noise; enlarge the set, inspect the paired per-example disagreements, and compare lower confidence bounds against the quality bar before deciding","Both models should be rejected for a larger model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With 60 examples the 95% interval is roughly ±7 to ±8 points near 0.9, so a 3-point gap (about two examples) cannot be distinguished from sampling noise. More examples tighten it; repeated runs do not. The decision should be made against the bar with margin, not by comparing two noisy means."},{"q":"Which of these must be applied as a hard filter before any quality comparison?","options":["Price per million output tokens","Position on a human-preference leaderboard","Whether the model can be run in the region or environment where the data is allowed to be","How recently the model was released"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deployment and residency constraints are binary: a model that cannot legally or technically process the data is not a candidate regardless of quality or price. Price and recency are inputs to the later optimisation step, not gates."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-portfolio","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"- **Failure mode 1: one model for everything.** The frontier model classifies support intents at\n  frontier prices and latency, cannot be deployed in the region a customer requires, and one provider\n  outage or deprecation hits every product at once.\n- **Failure mode 2: every team picks its own.** Eighteen months later: a dozen models across half a\n  dozen providers, a dozen sets of prompt lore, no shared evaluation, a security review and\n  data-processing agreement per provider, incompatible data-use terms, no record of which model may see\n  personal data, and nobody knows which systems depend on a retired version.\n- **Every model has a fixed cost regardless of traffic.** An eval baseline per task family, a security\n  and privacy review, prompt expertise, monitoring, quota management and a deprecation watch.\n- **Marginal benefit falls fast.** Once the main tiers are covered, remaining tasks are already served\n  well enough by something on the list. So the optimum is small but greater than one: enough variety to\n  match difficulty to price and meet deployment constraints, few enough that the task-by-model eval\n  matrix is actually run.\n- **It is familiar discipline.** Engineering organisations already keep approved languages and\n  databases; the portfolio applies that to the most expensive dependency in the system.","how":"### Roles, and why each exists\n| Role | Used for | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Frontier or reasoning tier** | long-horizon agentic flows, complex code, low-volume high-value analysis | the hardest work; slow and expensive, so used by exception, behind a router or explicit task assignment |\n| **Workhorse tier** | most retrieval-augmented answers, drafting, summarisation | chosen by the model-selection funnel as the cheapest model that clears the bar for typical task families |\n| **Small and fast tier** | classification, routing, schema-bound extraction, PII detection, guardrail checks | most traffic is easy and the price gap between tiers is roughly an order of magnitude per step (illustrative, provider-dependent) |\n| **Embeddings and rerankers** | the retrieval stack | versioned with extra care |\n| **Specialised models** | vision and document understanding, speech-to-text, text-to-speech, code | modalities the text tiers do not cover |\n| **Fine-tuned variants** | a specific high-volume task, derived from a base | owned by the training team but registered centrally so their lifecycle is visible |\n\n- **Small tier specifics.** Often fine-tuned, sometimes self-hosted, expected to answer in well under a\n  second.\n- **Embedding care, because vectors are model-specific.** Changing an embedding model invalidates every\n  stored vector; vectors from two models are not comparable.\n\n### The registry entry\nEach model has a record containing:\n- **Identity:** exact pinned version, provider and deployment location.\n- **Data terms:** data-use and retention terms; approved data classifications (may it see customer PII,\n  source code, regulated data?).\n- **Quality:** eval baselines per task family that uses it.\n- **Cost card:** list price plus measured average tokens per request type; quota and rate limits.\n- **Lifecycle:** owner, announced deprecation date, designated fallback.\n\nThe record turns \"we use several models\" into something an auditor, a finance team and an on-call\nengineer can each read.\n\n### Sizing the set: when a tier earns its place\nThe fixed-versus-marginal argument as a rule you can compute. Moving a task family from model A to a\ncheaper model B is worth a new portfolio entry when\n\n```\nrequests per month × (cost per request on A − cost per request on B)\n  − added errors per month × cost per error   >   fixed monthly cost of running B as an entry\n```\n\n- **Fixed cost** = eval runs, amortised security and contract review, prompt maintenance, monitoring\n  and on-call attention.\n- **It explains both failure modes.** A small, high-volume task family clears it easily (the\n  classification move below saves about 2,300 units a day); a ninth model for one team's low-volume\n  task does not, even if slightly better.\n- **Re-run it** when volume grows, prices shift, or an incumbent gets cheaper.\n\n### Assignment versus routing\n- **Assignment (default).** Configuration says this task family uses `workhorse`; it changes only after\n  an eval. Start here *because* it is cheap and auditable.\n- **Routing.** Chooses a tier per request at run time (by difficulty, confidence or input length) and\n  needs its own evaluation of the router. Add it only for families where tracing shows a clear mix of\n  easy and hard requests, exactly what the agentic slice in the example turns out to be.\n\n### Entry and exit rules\n- **Entry.** A model enters only if it beats an incumbent on a shared eval for some task family, or\n  unlocks a constraint the set cannot meet (a new modality, a cheaper tier, a residency requirement).\n  This rule is what keeps the set from regrowing.\n- **Exit, as a planned migration:**\n  1. Deprecation notice.\n  2. List of consuming tasks from gateway logs.\n  3. Regression evals on each.\n  4. Cutover behind the gateway.\n  5. Removal.\n- **Routine by necessity.** Provider deprecations are routine, so the process has to be routine too.\n\n### Enforcement through indirection\n- **Logical names.** Applications reference names such as `workhorse`, `classifier-small` or `embed-v3`;\n  the gateway maps them to the pinned vendor model. Only registered models are routable.\n- **Why it works.** Fallback, rotation and vendor exit become possible *because* the dependency lives\n  in one configuration table rather than in a dozen code bases.\n- **What it does not do.** It does not make prompts magically portable; a rotation still runs\n  regression evals on every consuming task.\n\n### Diversity and concentration\n- **Second source per critical tier,** on a different provider, for outage resilience and negotiating\n  leverage.\n- **Bought per tier, not blanket,** because every additional provider is another security review and\n  contract.\n- **Two concentration metrics:** share of spend per provider, and share of requests landing on the\n  frontier tier. If the frontier handles a large fraction of volume, routing or task assignment is wrong.","example":"### Worked scenario: tiering and routing a million requests a day\n**Illustrative price ratios** (units per million tokens; real prices vary by provider and date):\n\n| Tier | Input | Output |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frontier | 10 | 30 |\n| Workhorse | 1 | 3 |\n| Small | 0.1 | 0.3 |\n\nDaily traffic is 1,000,000 requests:\n- **70% intent classification:** 300 input, 10 output tokens.\n- **25% RAG answers:** 3,000 in, 250 out.\n- **5% agentic tasks:** averaging 6 model calls each (20,000 in, 2,000 out per call).\n\n| Slice | Everything on frontier | Tiered portfolio | Portfolio + routing in agentic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Classification | 700,000 × (300×10 + 10×30)/10⁶ = 2,310 | small: 700,000 × (300×0.1 + 10×0.3)/10⁶ ≈ 23 | ≈ 23 |\n| RAG | 250,000 × (3,000×10 + 250×30)/10⁶ = 9,375 | workhorse: 250,000 × (3,000×1 + 250×3)/10⁶ ≈ 938 | ≈ 938 |\n| Agentic | 300,000 calls × (20,000×10 + 2,000×30)/10⁶ = 78,000 | still frontier: 78,000 | frontier 100,000 calls × 0.26 = 26,000 + workhorse 200,000 × (20,000×1 + 2,000×3)/10⁶ = 5,200 → 31,200 |\n| **Total per day** | **≈ 89,700** | **≈ 78,960 (only 12% saving)** | **≈ 32,160 (64% reduction)** |\n\n- **Why tiering alone saved little.** The agentic 5% of requests is 87% of spend.\n- **Where routing came from.** Tracing showed 4 of the 6 calls per task are tool-result summarisation\n  and formatting that the workhorse handles at the same eval score.\n- **The lesson.** The portfolio's first job is to make spend legible: the biggest lever was not the\n  obvious 70% of traffic but step-level routing inside the smallest slice. Prefix caching of the shared\n  20k context would cut it further.\n\n### The same numbers as a runnable calculator\nSwap in your own traffic mix and price card; the sizing rule is the last function.\n\n```python\nPRICE = {\"frontier\": (10, 30), \"workhorse\": (1, 3), \"small\": (0.1, 0.3)}  # illustrative, per 1M tokens\n\ndef daily_cost(slices):\n    \"\"\"slices: list of (calls_per_day, in_tokens, out_tokens, tier).\"\"\"\n    total = 0.0\n    for calls, n_in, n_out, tier in slices:\n        p_in, p_out = PRICE[tier]\n        total += calls * (n_in * p_in + n_out * p_out) / 1e6\n    return total\n\nsingle = [(700_000, 300, 10, \"frontier\"), (250_000, 3_000, 250, \"frontier\"),\n          (300_000, 20_000, 2_000, \"frontier\")]\ntiered = [(700_000, 300, 10, \"small\"), (250_000, 3_000, 250, \"workhorse\"),\n          (300_000, 20_000, 2_000, \"frontier\")]\nrouted = tiered[:2] + [(100_000, 20_000, 2_000, \"frontier\"), (200_000, 20_000, 2_000, \"workhorse\")]\n\nbase = daily_cost(single)\nfor name, s in ((\"single\", single), (\"tiered\", tiered), (\"routed\", routed)):\n    c = daily_cost(s)\n    print(f\"{name:7s} {c:9,.0f} units/day  saving {1 - c / base:4.0%}\")\n# single 89,685 (0%), tiered 78,961 (12%), routed 32,161 (64%)\n\ndef earns_a_place(req_per_month, cost_a, cost_b, added_errors, cost_per_error, fixed_monthly):\n    return req_per_month * (cost_a - cost_b) - added_errors * cost_per_error > fixed_monthly\n```\n\nWith an illustrative fixed cost of 2,000 units a month per entry and no added errors: the\nclassification family (about 21M requests a month) saves ≈ 68,600 units a month and clears it easily; a\n30,000-request-a-month team task with the same per-request saving gains ≈ 98 units and does not.\n\n### A registry record\nField names are this lesson's schema, not a specific product's format; values are illustrative.\n\n```yaml\nlogical_name: classifier-small\npinned_version: \"<provider model id with date or version>\"\nprovider: provider-b\ndeployment: eu-west, private endpoint\ndata_classes_approved: [internal, customer_pii]\ndata_use_terms: no training on inputs; 30-day retention   # copy from the signed agreement\neval_baselines:\n  intent-classification: {metric: exact_match, score: 0.94, set: intents-v3}\ncost_card: {price_in: 0.1, price_out: 0.3, avg_in: 300, avg_out: 10}\nquota: {rpm: 6000}\nowner: platform-ml\ndeprecation_date: null\nfallback: classifier-small-secondary\n```","patterns":["**Logical names enforced at the gateway.** As soon as two applications call models. Applications send `workhorse` or `embed-v3`; the gateway's routing table maps each to a pinned vendor model and rejects unregistered names. Monitor requests that bypass the gateway (direct vendor keys found in billing).","**Registry-driven deprecation runbook.** When any provider announces a retirement. Query gateway logs for every consumer of the logical name, run each consumer's regression eval on the replacement, cut over in the routing table, then delete the old mapping. Monitor days from notice to completed cutover.","**Monthly concentration report.** Ongoing. Publish spend share per tier and per provider and the share of requests on the frontier tier. A rising frontier share is the alert that routing or assignment has drifted.","**Sandbox tier for unregistered models.** For experimentation. Allow any model on non-sensitive data in a sandbox project with its own keys and budget cap; registration is required before production traffic. Monitor sandbox spend and how many sandbox models request registration.","**Embedding rotation as a re-index project.** When changing `embed-*` entries. Build a new index with the new model alongside the old, measure retrieval quality on the golden set, then switch the logical name and the index together. Monitor recall on the golden queries before and after."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Application\"] -->|\"calls a logical name\"| G[\"Gateway routing table\"]\n    G -->|\"look up name\"| R[(\"Model registry\")]\n    G --> F[\"Frontier or reasoning tier\"]\n    G --> W[\"Workhorse tier\"]\n    G --> S[\"Small and fast tier\"]\n    G --> E[\"Embeddings and rerankers\"]\n    G -.->|\"outage or 429\"| B[\"Second source, other provider\"]\n    N[\"Candidate model\"] -->|\"passes entry rule\"| R\n    R -->|\"deprecation runbook\"| X[\"Removed\"]\n    G -.->|\"spend and frontier share\"| M[\"Monthly concentration report\"]","caption":"Applications only ever name a role (`workhorse`, `classifier-small`, `embed-v3`); the gateway resolves\nit through the registry (pinned version, data classes, baselines, cost card, owner, fallback) to a\npinned model. Models enter only by beating an incumbent on a shared eval or unlocking a constraint, and\nleave only through the deprecation runbook (consumers from logs, regression evals, cutover)."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a model portfolio, and why is its optimal size small but greater than one?","a":"- **A governed set of models,** each with a role, owner, eval baselines, cost card and retirement plan,\n  registered centrally and called by logical name.\n- **One model overpays and concentrates risk:** frontier prices on easy work, and one outage or\n  deprecation hits every product at once.\n- **Every extra model has a fixed cost** (evals, security review, monitoring, deprecation watch) while\n  marginal benefit falls once the tiers are covered."},{"q":"You tiered your traffic and saved only 12%. Where do you look next?","a":"- **Look at spend share, not request share.** In the worked scenario the agentic 5% of requests was 87%\n  of spend, so tiering the 70% classification slice barely moved the total.\n- **Trace the expensive slice step by step.** Four of six calls per agentic task were summarisation and\n  formatting the workhorse handled at the same eval score.\n- **Route at step level inside that slice;** it took the saving from 12% to 64%, and prefix caching of\n  the shared context would cut it further."},{"q":"A team wants to add a ninth model because it scores slightly better on their task. How do you decide?","a":"- **Apply the entry rule:** it must beat an incumbent on a shared eval for some task family, or unlock a\n  constraint such as a modality, a cheaper tier or residency.\n- **Apply the sizing rule:** monthly saving minus added error cost must exceed the entry's fixed monthly\n  cost. A low-volume task rarely clears it, even with a better score.\n- **Offer the sandbox tier** for experimentation on non-sensitive data instead of production\n  registration."},{"q":"How do you handle a provider deprecation notice across many applications?","a":"- **Find consumers from gateway logs,** by logical name, rather than searching repositories.\n- **Run each consumer's regression eval on the replacement,** because prompts and tool-calling\n  behaviour are not fully portable even behind a logical name.\n- **Cut over in the routing table, then remove the old mapping.** Track days from notice to completed\n  cutover as the health metric of the process."},{"q":"Why is rotating the embedding model different from rotating any other model?","a":"- **Vectors are model-specific.** Vectors from two embedding models are not comparable, so changing the\n  model invalidates every stored vector.\n- **Treat it as a re-index project:** build a new index alongside the old one, measure recall on the\n  golden queries, then switch the logical name and the index together.\n- **The symptom of skipping this** is retrieval quality collapsing overnight with no code change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tiering vs overhead.** Tiering cuts spend, but every tier adds evaluation, prompt and monitoring overhead. Once a tier's monthly saving falls below that fixed overhead (the sizing rule), a two-model portfolio, one strong and one small, is the right size.","**Central approved list vs experimentation.** It gives governance and purchasing leverage but slows experimentation. The usual fix is a sandbox tier for unregistered models on non-sensitive data, with registration required before anything reaches production.","**Multi-provider diversity vs review burden.** It buys resilience and negotiating power, but each provider is another review, contract and set of behavioural quirks. For low-stakes tasks a single provider with graceful degradation is enough.","**Logical names vs portability.** They decouple applications from vendors, but prompts and tool-calling behaviour are not fully portable, so a rotation still costs a regression run per consuming task.","**Fine-tuned variants vs lifecycle work.** They give the best cost-quality on narrow tasks but multiply lifecycle work, since each must be retrained or re-evaluated when its base model is retired."],"pitfalls":["**Portfolio on paper, vendor IDs in code.** Symptom: a single deprecation notice turns into code changes and releases across a dozen repositories. Fix: logical names enforced at the gateway.","**Frontier tier carries bulk traffic because \"it works\".** Symptom: frontier share of spend far exceeds the share of tasks that need it, and cost grows linearly with adoption. Fix: assign easy task families to cheaper tiers after an eval, and route inside mixed families.","**Rotating the embedding model like any other model.** Symptom: retrieval quality collapses overnight because the index was not rebuilt with the new model's vectors. Fix: rotate as a re-index project.","**No per-task eval baselines in the registry.** Symptom: a rotation approved on one team's evaluation silently degrades another team's task. Fix: store a baseline per consuming task family and rerun every one before a cutover.","**Too many registered models.** Symptom: the task-by-model evaluation matrix is never fully run, so the quality of most combinations is unknown. Fix: enforce the entry rule and the sizing rule.","**Skipping data-classification approval per model.** Symptom: an audit finds personal data flowing to a model whose terms never permitted it. Fix: approved data classes in each record, checked by the gateway before routing."],"when":"- **Use when:** more than one task family or more than one team uses models; even a single product\n  benefits from a small-plus-strong pair with explicit assignment.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a lone prototype; it does not need one.\n- **Measure:** spend share per tier and provider, fraction of requests on the frontier tier, number of\n  registered models (should stay roughly flat), time to complete a migration after a deprecation\n  notice, and eval coverage of the task-by-model matrix.\n- **Daily work:** \"which model should this new feature use\" is answered by the registry, not by habit.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you manage models across an organisation\" is looking for tiers, a registry,\n  entry and exit rules, logical names behind a gateway, and the concentration metrics.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Calls models by logical name through the gateway, adds eval baselines for any task that joins the portfolio, and runs the regression suite when a rotation is proposed.","architect":"Owns the tier structure, the entry and exit rules, second-sourcing decisions per tier, and the concentration metrics that show whether routing matches intent.","fde":"Translates the portfolio into the customer's environment, substituting models that meet their residency and data-use constraints while keeping the same logical roles and baselines."},"check":{"q":"After eighteen months your organisation runs eleven models across six providers. Leadership\nwants this reduced without breaking products. How do you rationalise the portfolio, and how do you\nstop it regrowing?","a":"Start with an inventory from gateway or billing logs: which applications call which model, at\nwhat volume and spend. Cluster the calls into task families and assemble or collect an eval set per\nfamily. For each family, run the selection funnel across the models already in use, which reveals the\nminimal set that clears every family's bar, typically a frontier model, a workhorse, a small model,\nan embedding model and one or two specialised ones. Plan migrations family by family with regression\nevals, moving applications onto logical names behind the gateway as you go so the next change is\nconfiguration only. Keep a second provider for the tiers that matter. To prevent regrowth, enforce the\nentry rule, a new model must beat an incumbent on a shared eval or unlock a constraint, give every\nentry an owner and a retirement date, and publish spend share and frontier-tier share monthly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason to keep a model portfolio small rather than registering every useful model?","options":["Providers charge more when an organisation uses many models","Each model carries fixed costs for evaluation, security review, monitoring and deprecation tracking, while the benefit of another model diminishes once the tiers are covered","Gateways can only route to a limited number of back ends","Small portfolios always produce higher quality outputs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cost of a model is not only its tokens; it is the task-by-model evaluations, reviews and lifecycle work it demands. Once a strong, a mid and a small tier exist, an extra model rarely clears a bar that nothing on the list already clears."},{"q":"Why should applications reference logical names such as `workhorse` instead of vendor model identifiers?","options":["Logical names are billed at a lower rate","Providers require logical names in their SDKs","So fallback, rotation and vendor exit become a configuration change in the gateway rather than code changes across every application","Logical names avoid provider rate limits"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The indirection puts the dependency in one table. A deprecation or outage is then handled centrally, with regression evals per consuming task, instead of being discovered repository by repository."},{"q":"Which portfolio rotation requires more than a prompt regression evaluation before cutover?","options":["Replacing the frontier model with a newer version from the same provider","Replacing the embedding model used by the retrieval index","Replacing the small classification model with a fine-tuned variant","Replacing the speech-to-text model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vectors produced by different embedding models live in different spaces, so queries embedded with the new model cannot be compared against documents embedded with the old one. The whole corpus must be re-indexed and retrieval quality re-measured before any switch."}]},{"key":"system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","sources":["Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Hinton et al., 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network"],"why":"- **What \"small\" means.** Roughly up to the low tens of billions of parameters, runnable on one GPU or a\n  laptop; a fuzzy, moving boundary.\n- **The gap is not ten percent.** Between a 7B-class and a frontier model it is an order of magnitude or\n  more in price per token, a similar factor in weight bytes read per generated token and therefore\n  latency, and the difference between \"fits in the customer's VPC on one GPU, or on the device\" and\n  \"requires an external API\". That leverage needs a reasoned basis, not a default.\n- **\"Always use the biggest model\" is wrong.** It overpays on the large fraction of easy traffic, blows\n  latency budgets for interactive and on-device use, and is unavailable wherever data cannot leave the\n  premises.\n- **\"Small models are good enough now\" is wrong too.** It fails on the hard five percent of inputs where\n  errors, incidents and compliance findings concentrate. Small models follow long, complex instructions\n  less reliably, hallucinate more on knowledge (fewer facts fit in fewer parameters) and degrade more\n  sharply in long contexts.\n- **The gap is task-dependent.** Pattern-following tasks (classify, extract, reformat, answer from\n  supplied text) saturate early, and fine-tuning closes the remaining gap; broad reasoning and knowledge\n  keep improving with scale. The question is \"does this task saturate\", and that must be measured.","how":"### What shrinks when a model shrinks\n- **Scaling laws.** Loss falls smoothly with parameters and training tokens. The compute-optimal\n  follow-up showed smaller models trained on far more tokens recover much of the gap, which is why\n  current small models are much stronger than their parameter count once suggested.\n- **Capabilities do not shrink uniformly.** First to go:\n  - **stored world knowledge**: facts live in parameters, so fewer parameters means fewer facts and more\n    confident invention unless retrieval supplies them;\n  - **multi-step reasoning and planning**;\n  - **robustness to long or intricate instruction sets**;\n  - **long-context use**.\n- **What holds up:** in-distribution pattern behaviour, *because* a narrow task occupies a small region\n  of input space that fine-tuning can cover directly.\n\n### The physical arithmetic\n| Quantity | 7B model | 70B model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weights, fp16 (parameters × bytes per parameter) | about 14 GB | about 140 GB |\n| Weights, 4-bit | roughly 3.5 to 4 GB: a consumer GPU or a laptop | needs several GPUs or aggressive quantization |\n| Single-stream decode speed, same card | roughly ten times faster | baseline |\n\n- **Decode is memory-bandwidth bound.** Every generated token reads the active weights once, so\n  single-stream tokens per second ≈ bandwidth ÷ weight bytes. A model ten times smaller generates\n  roughly ten times faster (approximate; batching, KV-cache traffic and mixture-of-experts designs\n  change the constant).\n- **The KV cache is smaller too,** since there are fewer layers and heads. So SLMs win on latency and\n  edge deployment, not only on price.\n- **Mixture-of-experts blurs the size label.** Decode speed tracks the *active* parameters read per\n  token, but memory must hold *all* experts: an MoE with a small active count decodes like a small model\n  but needs the memory of a large one.\n\n### Closing the gap: fine-tuning and distillation\n1. **Use the large model as a teacher.** Run it over your task's inputs to produce labels or target\n   outputs.\n2. **Verify a sample by hand.**\n3. **Fine-tune the small model** on the result, typically with a parameter-efficient method. Narrow\n   tasks commonly need hundreds to a few thousand examples (an order of magnitude, not a rule).\n4. **Test on human labels.** The student learns the **task behaviour**, not the teacher's general\n   capability, so it matches in-distribution and falls off out of distribution. It also inherits the\n   teacher's mistakes, which is why the test set must be human-labelled, not teacher-labelled.\n\n### The decision dimensions\n| Dimension | Favours SLM | Favours LLM (or cascade) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Task breadth** | closed schema, bounded inputs | open-ended generation |\n| **Tail cost** | errors cheap and reviewable | one error in the hard cases is expensive |\n| **Latency budget** | sub-second interactive or on-device targets usually force it | relaxed budgets |\n| **Volume** | self-hosted SLM cost is fixed per GPU-hour and amortises only at volume | low volume |\n| **Residency and connectivity** | on-prem, air-gapped or offline rules out external APIs | data may leave |\n| **Knowledge need** | pair the SLM with retrieval | answers depend on facts the SLM lacks |\n| **Change rate** | stable task | definition shifts monthly, so retraining is a treadmill |\n| **Operational capacity** | someone owns serving, monitoring and upgrades | no one to run it |\n\n### The hybrid: a cascade\n- **Shape.** Run the SLM first; escalate to the LLM on low confidence, failed validation or a trigger\n  condition. It captures most of the saving with LLM quality on the tail, at the price of a confidence\n  signal that needs calibration.\n- **Classification signal is cheap.** With a self-hosted SLM, read the probability assigned to the\n  chosen label from the logits.\n- **Set the threshold empirically,** because raw probabilities from fine-tuned models are often\n  overconfident. On a held-out human-labelled set, sort by confidence and pick the cut-off at which\n  accuracy on accepted items still meets the bar; everything below escalates.\n- **The threshold is the dial.** It directly sets the trade between escalation rate (cost) and\n  accepted-item accuracy (quality).\n- **Free-form output** uses validators instead: schema checks, citation checks, or agreement between\n  two samples.\n- **Rarely binary.** Routing and cascading have their own lesson; the point here is the choice is\n  rarely SLM-or-LLM.","example":"### Worked scenario: classifying 5M support messages a day\n**Illustrative figures.** A support platform classifies 5,000,000 inbound messages a day into intents\nand PII categories; each call is about 300 input and 10 output tokens.\n\n**Evaluation on 1,000 human-labelled messages:**\n\n| Candidate | Accuracy |\n|---|---|\n| Frontier API model | 97% |\n| 7B open-weight, zero-shot | 90% |\n| Same 7B fine-tuned on 3,000 teacher-labelled examples (500 of them human-verified) | 96%, 95% interval about ±1 point |\n\n**Cost and load, step by step:**\n1. **API cost** at an illustrative 1 unit per million input tokens and 3 per million output:\n   5M × (300×1 + 10×3)/10⁶ = 1,650 units a day.\n2. **Self-hosted SLM:** a GPU node at an illustrative fixed 100 units per day, capacity-planned\n   separately. Naive saving: 1,550 units, about 94%.\n3. **Break-even.** An API request costs 330 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.00033 units, so the GPU's fixed 100 units a day\n   pays for itself only above about 300,000 requests a day: at 5M the SLM wins, at 100k it would lose.\n4. **Load.** 5M a day is about 58 requests a second on average, roughly 17,000 input tokens a second\n   of prefill, with peaks well above. Load-test whether one node carries the peak; if it takes two, the\n   fixed term doubles and the break-even moves with it.\n5. **Price the quality gap.** One point of accuracy on 5M messages is 50,000 misrouted messages a day.\n   At 0.02 units of human rework each, the SLM-only path's extra point costs 1,000 units a day: the\n   real saving is 550 units, and the interval means it could be close to zero.\n6. **Cascade.** The fine-tuned SLM is confident (calibrated probability above threshold) on about 85%\n   of traffic, with accuracy close to the LLM's; the remaining 15% escalates. API cost falls to\n   0.15 × 1,650 ≈ 248 units, plus 100 for the GPU: ≈ 348 units a day, with overall accuracy\n   indistinguishable from 97% on the test set. Saving about 79% with no measurable quality loss.\n\n**Lesson:** the error-cost term, quality gap × volume × cost per error, must be in the comparison; once\nit is, the cascade usually dominates both pure options at this volume.\n\n### The comparison as a function\nReproduces the scenario, including the error-cost term and the break-even.\n\n```python\ndef daily(volume, in_tok=300, out_tok=10, p_in=1, p_out=3, gpu_fixed=100,\n          acc_gap=0.01, rework=0.02, escalate=0.0):\n    api_per_req = (in_tok * p_in + out_tok * p_out) / 1e6\n    api_only = volume * api_per_req\n    slm_only = gpu_fixed + volume * acc_gap * rework          # error-cost term included\n    cascade = gpu_fixed + escalate * api_only                  # tail goes to the API model\n    return {\"api\": api_only, \"slm\": slm_only, \"cascade\": cascade,\n            \"break_even_req\": gpu_fixed / api_per_req}\n\nfor v in (100_000, 5_000_000):\n    r = daily(v, escalate=0.15)\n    print(v, {k: round(x) for k, x in r.items()})\n# 100000  {'api': 33, 'slm': 120, 'cascade': 105, 'break_even_req': 303030}   API wins\n# 5000000 {'api': 1650, 'slm': 1100, 'cascade': 348, 'break_even_req': 303030} cascade wins\n```\n\n### Calibrating the cascade threshold\nRunnable, standard library only; the held-out set is synthetic. In production `scored` comes from the\nSLM's top-label probability on human-labelled items.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef pick_threshold(scored, bar):\n    \"\"\"scored: (confidence, correct) pairs from a held-out human-labelled set.\n    Returns (threshold, escalation_rate, accepted_accuracy) for the largest accepted share meeting the bar.\"\"\"\n    ranked = sorted(scored, key=lambda x: -x[0])\n    best, correct = None, 0\n    for i, (conf, ok) in enumerate(ranked, 1):\n        correct += ok\n        if correct / i >= bar:\n            best = (conf, 1 - i / len(ranked), correct / i)\n    return best\n\n# Synthetic held-out set (illustrative): mostly easy, confident items plus a hard tail\nrandom.seed(0)\nheld_out = [(random.uniform(0.90, 1.00), random.random() < 0.985) for _ in range(850)]\nheld_out += [(random.uniform(0.40, 0.95), random.random() < 0.70) for _ in range(150)]\nt, esc, acc = pick_threshold(held_out, bar=0.97)\nprint(f\"threshold {t:.3f}  escalate {esc:.0%}  accepted accuracy {acc:.1%}\")\n# threshold 0.789  escalate 10%  accepted accuracy 97.0%\n```\n\nRaising `bar` raises the threshold and the escalation rate; that is the cost-quality dial in one number.","patterns":["**Prototype large, distil when stable.** For any new task. Ship on the LLM first, log inputs and outputs, and distil into an SLM once the task definition has stopped changing. Monitor how often the task's schema or labels change; frequent changes mean stay on the LLM.","**Evaluate the exact deployed artefact.** Whenever quantizing or changing the serving engine. Run the human-labelled set against the specific 4-bit (or other) build you will serve, not the fp16 original. Monitor task accuracy per artefact version.","**Recalibrate the cascade threshold on a schedule.** Monthly or after any retrain. Re-run the threshold search on fresh human-labelled samples and update the config value. Monitor escalation rate and accepted-item accuracy on sampled reviews; a drifting escalation rate signals input drift.","**Load-test the peak before signing the cost case.** Before replacing an API with a self-hosted SLM. Replay peak-hour traffic at production prompt lengths against one node and record p95 latency and throughput. Monitor GPU utilisation and queue depth; under-utilised GPUs erase the saving.","**Retrieval for facts, SLM for form.** For knowledge-heavy tasks on small models. Supply facts in the context and keep the SLM's job to formatting or extracting from them. Monitor answers without a supporting citation."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart LR\n    T[\"LLM as teacher on task inputs\"] -->|\"labels, sample human-verified\"| FT[\"Fine-tune SLM, parameter-efficient\"]\n    FT --> EV{\"Exact deployed artefact meets bar on human-labelled set?\"}\n    EV -->|\"no\"| L[\"Stay on LLM or larger open-weight model\"]\n    EV -->|\"yes\"| S[\"SLM serves every request\"]\n    R[\"Request\"] --> S\n    S --> C{\"Confidence above calibrated threshold and output valid?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes, most traffic\"| OK[\"Accept SLM answer\"]\n    C -->|\"no, the tail\"| ESC[\"Escalate to LLM or human review queue\"]\n    OK -.->|\"sampled reviews\"| CAL[\"Recalibrate threshold on fresh labels\"]\n    ESC -.-> CAL\n    CAL -.-> C","caption":"The usual answer is a cascade: the distilled SLM takes every request first, and only low-confidence or\ninvalid outputs pay for the LLM. The threshold on the confidence check is the cost-quality dial."},"interview":[{"q":"When does a small language model match a large one, and when does it not?","a":"- **It matches on narrow, in-distribution pattern tasks** such as classify, extract, reformat or answer\n  from supplied text, because these saturate early and fine-tuning covers the small region of input\n  space directly.\n- **It falls behind on stored world knowledge, multi-step reasoning, long intricate instructions and\n  long-context use,** because those keep improving with scale.\n- **So the real question is whether this task saturates,** and that must be measured on your own data."},{"q":"Explain where the latency advantage of a 7B model over a 70B model comes from.","a":"- **Decode is memory-bandwidth bound.** Each generated token reads the active weights once, so\n  single-stream tokens per second is roughly bandwidth divided by weight bytes.\n- **Ten times fewer bytes is roughly ten times faster:** about 14 GB versus 140 GB at fp16, and the KV\n  cache is smaller too.\n- **Mixture-of-experts breaks the size label:** speed tracks active parameters, but memory must hold all\n  experts."},{"q":"The self-hosted SLM looked 94% cheaper in the business case. Why might the real saving be near zero?","a":"- **The error-cost term was missing.** One point of accuracy on 5M messages a day is 50,000 misrouted\n  messages; at 0.02 units of rework each that is 1,000 units a day, cutting the saving to 550 units.\n- **The interval matters:** with about ±1 point on the fine-tuned score, the saving could be close to\n  zero.\n- **The fixed GPU cost needs volume.** Break-even is about 300,000 requests a day in the scenario, and\n  a second node for the peak doubles it."},{"q":"How do you set the confidence threshold for an SLM-first cascade?","a":"- **Use a held-out human-labelled set,** because raw probabilities from fine-tuned models are often\n  overconfident.\n- **Sort by confidence and pick the cut-off** at which accuracy on accepted items still meets the bar;\n  everything below escalates.\n- **Recalibrate monthly or after any retrain,** and watch escalation rate: drift in it usually signals\n  drifting inputs."},{"q":"When would you keep a task on the LLM even though an SLM scores well today?","a":"- **When the task definition shifts monthly,** because retraining becomes a treadmill and the\n  fine-tuned artefact goes stale.\n- **When volume is low,** because a self-hosted SLM's fixed GPU-hour cost never amortises; a small model\n  behind a hosted API is the better middle.\n- **When no one owns serving, monitoring and upgrades,** since the operational capacity is part of the\n  cost."}],"tradeoffs":["**SLM cost and latency vs competence.** Roughly an order of magnitude lower cost and latency but a narrower competence; it flips as soon as the task needs world knowledge or multi-step reasoning that retrieval cannot supply.","**Fine-tuning vs maintenance.** It closes the in-distribution gap but creates an artefact to retrain when the input distribution shifts or the base model is retired; it flips when the task changes faster than you can label.","**Self-hosting vs operations.** It delivers residency, offline operation and fixed cost, but needs operations capacity and pays only at volume; at low volume a small model behind a hosted API is the better middle.","**LLM via API vs dependency.** Breadth and zero infrastructure, at the price of variable cost, external data flow and exposure to the provider's deprecation schedule.","**Cascade vs complexity.** It captures most of the saving with LLM-level quality, but adds a confidence estimator to calibrate and monitor, and a second code path to operate."],"pitfalls":["**Judging the SLM on zero-shot results and discarding it.** Symptom: the fine-tuned version would have matched the bar, but the comparison never got that far.","**SLM for knowledge-heavy QA without retrieval.** Symptom: fluent, confident, wrong answers about facts the model never held.","**Handing an SLM a 3,000-token instruction set written for a frontier model.** Symptom: rules late in the prompt are ignored and output format drifts.","**Comparing price per token only.** Symptom: the business case omits the cost of errors and escalations, and the GPU's fixed cost at low utilisation.","**Assuming quantization is free.** Symptom: the 4-bit artefact that looked fine on a general benchmark loses several points on your task; always evaluate the exact artefact you deploy.","**Distilling without a human-labelled test set.** Symptom: 99% agreement with the teacher, and the same systematic errors the teacher makes."],"when":"- **Use an SLM for:** narrow, high-volume, latency-sensitive work: classification, routing, schema-bound\n  extraction, guardrail checks, on-device or on-prem features, and anywhere data cannot leave.\n- **Use the LLM for:** open-ended generation, complex tool use, low-volume high-value analysis, and any\n  task still being discovered: prototype with the large model, distil once the definition is stable.\n- **Measure:** accuracy with a confidence interval on your own set, cost per successful task including\n  the cost of errors, p95 latency, and the escalation rate if you cascade.\n- **Interviews:** name which capabilities degrade with size, the bandwidth arithmetic behind the latency\n  gain, distillation with a human-labelled test set, and the cascade as the usual answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval that compares the candidates on the real task, runs the distillation pipeline, and tests the quantized artefact rather than the fp16 original.","architect":"Decides which task families are SLM-eligible, sets the quality bar and the error-cost model, and chooses between pure SLM, pure LLM and a cascade per family.","fde":"Makes an SLM work inside a customer's VPC or air-gapped site when the API model cannot be used, and sets expectations about which capabilities will not carry over."},"check":{"q":"A customer requires that document extraction run entirely inside their VPC on a single GPU.\nYour current pipeline uses a frontier API model. How do you assess whether a small model can replace\nit, and what does the path look like if it can?","a":"Characterise the task first: fixed-schema extraction over a known document population is narrow\nand in-distribution, so it is a strong SLM candidate. Build a human-labelled golden set from the\ncustomer's real documents, measure the frontier model on it as the ceiling, then measure candidate\nopen-weight models zero-shot. If there is a gap, generate training data by running the frontier model\nover the customer's documents where terms permit, human-verify a sample, fine-tune with a\nparameter-efficient method, and re-evaluate the exact quantized artefact on the human-labelled set.\nCheck the memory arithmetic: a 7B model at 4-bit is a few gigabytes of weights, but long documents\nmean a large KV cache, so chunk pages and bound context. Plan operations: serving engine, monitoring,\na retraining trigger when templates change. Because there is no LLM to escalate to inside the VPC, add\na human review queue for low-confidence outputs, or budget a larger open-weight model for the tail."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which capabilities degrade first as a language model gets smaller?","options":["The ability to produce valid JSON for a fixed schema","Stored world knowledge and multi-step reasoning","Tokenization accuracy on common words","The ability to follow a single short instruction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Facts are stored in parameters and reasoning depth grows with scale, so both fall off first. Format compliance and simple instruction following are narrow pattern behaviours that small models retain, especially after fine-tuning."},{"q":"Why does a dense 7B model generate tokens roughly ten times faster than a dense 70B model on the same hardware at batch size 1?","options":["It uses a smaller vocabulary, so each token is shorter","It produces fewer output tokens for the same prompt","Decode is memory-bandwidth bound and each token reads all the weights once, so ten times fewer weight bytes means roughly ten times more tokens per second","Smaller models skip the attention layers during decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Per-token decode time is dominated by streaming the weights from GPU memory, not by arithmetic. Fewer bytes per token means proportionally more tokens per second; vocabulary and output length do not change the per-token cost."},{"q":"A team reports 99% agreement between a distilled small model and its large teacher on a held-out set labelled by the teacher. What is missing before shipping?","options":["A larger teacher model to generate more labels","A human-labelled test set, because the student inherits the teacher's systematic errors and agreement with the teacher cannot reveal them","A longer fine-tuning run to reach 100% agreement","A quantized version of the teacher for comparison"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agreement measures how well the student copies the teacher, including its mistakes. Only labels from an independent source show whether either model is actually correct on the task."}]},{"key":"system-design/build-vs-buy","sources":[],"why":"- **The boundaries are not settled yet.** In mature domains nobody writes their own database. The AI\n  stack is young: is an AI gateway a product or three hundred lines? Is eval tooling a vendor or a script\n  and a spreadsheet? Is a retrieval pipeline a managed service or core IP? Unclear answers push teams\n  into one of two mistakes.\n- **Mistake 1: build everything, because AI is strategic.** Six months later there is a home-grown\n  vector store, gateway, tracing system and prompt manager, the product is behind schedule, and the\n  platform competes with open-source projects that improved faster. The error conflates strategic\n  importance with differentiation: the model is the most strategic component and almost nobody should\n  train one.\n- **Mistake 2: buy end-to-end, because speed matters.** Fast start, then limits: the vendor's workflow\n  is not yours, your most sensitive source cannot be sent to them, per-seat pricing scales with adoption\n  rather than value, and the quality loop is a black box you cannot evaluate. At renewal, prompts,\n  analytics and the test set all live inside their platform.\n- **What mistake 2 gets wrong.** It treats the application layer as commodity when it is precisely where\n  your domain knowledge and data live.\n- **Each default is wrong at specific layers,** not everywhere. That is why the decision must be\n  decomposed before it can be made.","how":"### Decompose the stack into layers\n| # | Layer | Default | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Foundation model** | rent through an API or run open weights | training from scratch is for a handful of organisations |\n| 2 | **Serving** | buy (the API) or build on open-source engines if self-hosting | rarely write your own |\n| 3 | **Gateway and routing** | buy, adopt open source, or a thin build | commodity, but you must own the configuration because it encodes your portfolio and budgets |\n| 4 | **Orchestration and agent logic** | usually built, in plain code or on an open-source framework | this is where your workflow lives and most testing happens |\n| 5 | **Retrieval and knowledge** | buy the vector store or search engine; build ingestion, chunking, access-control mapping and ranking tuned to your corpus | that is where quality is decided |\n| 6 | **Evaluation and observability** | buy or adopt the tracing and eval platform; always build golden sets and task metrics | nobody can sell you your own test set |\n| 7 | **Guardrails** | buy generic detectors (PII, toxicity, injection); build domain policies and the evals that prove they hold | generic is commodity, policy is yours |\n| 8 | **Application and experience** | build if it is your product; buy if internal productivity with no differentiation | differentiation lives here |\n\n- **The model-layer \"build\" open to most teams is *adaptation*:** fine-tuning or distilling a rented\n  or open base for a narrow task.\n- **The other real model-layer choices** are self-host versus API and open-weight versus proprietary,\n  each with its own lesson.\n\n### Four questions per layer\n1. **Does it differentiate?** Would a customer choose you or pay more because of your version of this\n   layer? Differentiation usually lives in data, domain workflows, evaluation sets and integrations,\n   rarely in plumbing. If no, buy.\n2. **How fast is the layer moving?** Buy or adopt a fast-moving commodity layer, *because* a build\n   will be obsolete before it is finished. Build a fast-moving core layer thin and keep it switchable.\n3. **What do data and compliance require?** A source that cannot leave your environment, retention\n   rules or audit requirements narrow buy options to vendors that deploy where you are, and sometimes\n   force a build.\n4. **What is the total cost including exit?** Compare over three years, not year one:\n   - **Build:** engineering time to a first version, plus ongoing maintenance (a material fraction of\n     build cost every year), plus the opportunity cost of what those engineers did not ship.\n   - **Buy:** licence, plus integration (routinely underestimated), plus the lock-in premium at renewal,\n     plus exit cost: data migration, prompt re-tuning, user retraining.\n\n```\nTCO_build = build effort + 3 × annual maintenance + infrastructure + usage + opportunity cost\nTCO_buy   = licence over 3 years (at projected seats or usage) + integration\n            + P(switch) × exit cost\n```\n\n- **Writing it out exposes dropped terms:** maintenance and opportunity cost on the build side;\n  integration and exit on the buy side.\n- **The scale asymmetry.** The licence term grows with adoption while most of the build side is fixed,\n  which is why the answer often flips with scale.\n\n### Patterns that come out of the questions\n- **Buy then build.** Adopt a vendor to learn real requirements, then replace the differentiating part\n  once usage shows what it is. Justified when requirement uncertainty is high, *because* you cannot know\n  what differentiates before users use it.\n- **Build on open source.** The middle path for gateways, serving, eval frameworks: you own deployment\n  and configuration but not development. It trades licence fees for operational expertise.\n- **Buy the platform, build the product.** The common enterprise shape: a managed model platform with\n  governance, in-house applications on top.\n- **Build the eval whatever else you buy.** The evaluation set is the one asset always yours, *because*\n  it is the only instrument that holds a vendor accountable and makes switching measurable.\n- **Own the seams.** Wrap every bought component in an interface you control. The test of a seam is\n  \"how long would it take to swap this\", and that number should be known, not guessed.\n\n### Procurement realities\n- **Calendar time is fixed.** Security review, data-processing agreement, residency, subprocessor lists\n  and certifications take calendar time regardless of technical merit.\n- **Pricing model matters as much as price.** Per-seat pricing punishes successful adoption; usage\n  pricing without caps punishes success differently.\n- **Leverage.** A credible build alternative is the strongest negotiating position.\n- **For FDEs the roles reverse.** The customer is making this decision about *your* product; your job is\n  to make \"buy\" credible by showing the integration path, the exit path and a transparent quality loop.","example":"### Worked scenario: an internal knowledge assistant\n2,000 employees over wikis, tickets and documents; one source is restricted and cannot be sent to third\nparties. Three options over three years, all figures **illustrative** and in abstract units.\n\n| Option | Cost build-up | Total | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Buy a vertical SaaS product** | 20 units per seat per month × 2,000 seats × 36 months = 1,440,000, plus about two engineer-months of connector and SSO integration | ≈ 1,440,000 + integration | no differentiation, restricted source excluded, evaluation loop is the vendor's |\n| **B. Build on managed components** (rented model API, managed vector database, open-source gateway and tracing) | build: two engineers for four months, 8 engineer-months at 15,000 each = 120,000; maintenance at roughly 25% of build per year ≈ 90,000 over three years; infrastructure ≈ 2,000 per month = 72,000; usage ≈ 50,400 | ≈ 332,000 | team owns the eval set, the access-control filter that lets the restricted source in, and the model seam |\n| **C. Fully self-host open weights** | B without API usage, plus a 24/7 GPU footprint at 8,000 per month = 288,000, plus extra operations effort of perhaps 180,000 over three years: 332,000 − 50,400 + 288,000 + 180,000 | ≈ 750,000 | GPUs cost almost six times the tokens they replace |\n\n- **B's usage line.** 2,000 users × 10 queries per day × 20 workdays × 36 months = 14.4M queries at\n  about 3,500 tokens each ≈ 50 billion tokens, at 1 unit per million ≈ 50,400.\n- **C is justified only if residency rules forbid the API,** which they do not here.\n- **Decision: B.**\n\n**The flip point is the useful insight:**\n1. Option A costs 720 units per seat over three years plus about 30,000 of integration (two\n   engineer-months).\n2. B is dominated by about 282,000 of fixed cost plus roughly 25 units per seat of usage.\n3. Setting 720s + 30,000 = 282,000 + 25s gives s ≈ 360 seats. Below that, buy; above it, build on\n   managed components.\n4. The order of magnitude matters more than the exact number; it moves if B's infrastructure grows\n   with seats or the vendor discounts at volume.\n5. The restricted data source would have forced B regardless: the compliance question overrides the\n   cost question.\n\n### Three-year TCO and flip point as code\nRunnable; defaults are the scenario's illustrative figures. Add `p_switch × exit_cost` and opportunity\ncost for your own case, because those are the terms people drop.\n\n```python\ndef tco_buy(seats, per_seat_month=20, months=36, integration=30_000, p_switch=0.0, exit_cost=0):\n    return seats * per_seat_month * months + integration + p_switch * exit_cost\n\ndef tco_build(seats, build=120_000, maint_rate=0.25, years=3, infra_month=2_000,\n              usage_per_seat=25.2, opportunity=0):\n    return build + years * maint_rate * build + infra_month * 12 * years + seats * usage_per_seat + opportunity\n\nfor s in (100, 363, 2_000):\n    print(f\"{s:5d} seats  buy {tco_buy(s):>11,.0f}  build {tco_build(s):>9,.0f}\")\n#   100 seats  buy     102,000  build   284,520\n#   363 seats  buy     291,360  build   291,148\n#  2000 seats  buy   1,470,000  build   332,400\n\nflip = next(s for s in range(1, 10_000) if tco_buy(s) > tco_build(s))\nprint(\"flip at\", flip, \"seats\")   # 363: the \"s ≈ 360\" of the worked scenario\n```\n\n### An owned seam with a contract test\nThe seam is an interface you own plus one test suite every implementation must pass; running it\nagainst a candidate is how \"time to swap\" becomes a measured number.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass\nclass Chunk:\n    doc_id: str\n    text: str\n\nclass Retriever(Protocol):\n    def search(self, query: str, user_id: str, k: int) -> list[Chunk]: ...\n\ndef contract_test(r: Retriever, golden: list[dict]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Same golden set for the vendor adapter and any replacement.\"\"\"\n    hits = 0\n    for case in golden:\n        got = r.search(case[\"query\"], case[\"user_id\"], k=5)\n        assert all(c.doc_id not in case[\"forbidden_docs\"] for c in got), \"ACL leak\"\n        hits += any(c.doc_id in case[\"relevant_docs\"] for c in got)\n    return hits / len(golden)          # recall@5 to compare implementations\n```","patterns":["**Layer map in the architecture record.** At the start of any AI initiative and at each renewal. Keep a table with one row per layer: build, buy or adopt, the vendor, the three-year cost including exit, and the measured time-to-swap. Monitor spend by layer and the integration-to-licence cost ratio.","**Vendor-neutral golden set from day one.** Whatever you buy. Store the evaluation set and task metrics in your own repository or bucket, and run every vendor and home-built option against it. Monitor score per vendor release, so last month's update is visibly better or worse.","**Adapter plus contract test per bought component.** For every vendor in the request path. Wrap it in an interface you own and keep a contract test suite that any replacement must pass. Monitor how long a trial swap takes in a staging environment; rehearse it once a year.","**Export clause and periodic export.** For any SaaS that stores prompts, analytics or labels. Make data export part of the contract and run the export on a schedule into storage you control. Monitor that the export job succeeds and is complete.","**Spend caps on usage-priced vendors.** For any usage-priced component. Set budget alerts and hard caps in the vendor console or your gateway, per environment. Monitor spend against forecast weekly."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    L[\"One stack layer\"] --> D{\"Does it differentiate?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| B[\"Buy or adopt open source\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| M{\"Is the layer moving fast?\"}\n    M -->|\"yes\"| TB[\"Build thin and switchable\"]\n    M -->|\"no\"| BU[\"Build\"]\n    B --> C{\"Can the data go to the vendor under compliance rules?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| BU\n    C -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Compare three-year TCO including exit\"]\n    TB --> T\n    BU --> T\n    T --> S[\"Own the seam: adapter plus contract test\"]\n    S -.->|\"renewal or layer commoditises\"| L","caption":"Run this path once per stack layer, not once per project: compliance can force the answer before cost\nis compared, and every outcome ends behind a seam you own."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is build versus buy in AI a per-layer decision rather than one decision for the project?","a":"- **Each default is wrong at specific layers.** Building everything conflates strategic importance with\n  differentiation; buying end-to-end treats the application layer as commodity when your domain\n  knowledge and data live there.\n- **Layers differ in differentiation, rate of change and data sensitivity,** so models, serving and\n  gateways usually lean buy while evaluation sets, workflows and context assembly lean build.\n- **Decomposing first** makes the four questions answerable for each layer."},{"q":"Walk me through a three-year TCO comparison. Which terms do people usually drop?","a":"- **Build:** effort plus about three years of maintenance, infrastructure, usage and opportunity cost.\n  Maintenance and opportunity cost are the ones left out.\n- **Buy:** licence at projected seats or usage, integration, and the probability of switching times\n  exit cost. Integration and exit are the ones left out.\n- **Look for the flip point,** because licence grows with adoption while most build cost is fixed; in\n  the scenario it was about 360 seats."},{"q":"A vendor's assistant scores well in the demo. What would make you reject it anyway?","a":"- **The data cannot go to the vendor.** A restricted source that cannot leave your environment overrides\n  the cost question.\n- **The quality loop is opaque:** you cannot run your own golden set against it or explain a wrong\n  answer.\n- **No exit path:** prompts, analytics and labels live only inside their platform, with no export clause\n  or scheduled export."},{"q":"When would you deliberately buy first and plan to build later?","a":"- **When requirement uncertainty is high,** because you cannot know what differentiates before users use\n  it.\n- **The cost is paying twice plus a migration,** so it is worth it only if the vendor's exit path is\n  clean.\n- **Keep your golden set and an adapter seam from day one,** so the later build is measured against the\n  vendor rather than started from zero."},{"q":"How do you make \"time to swap a vendor\" a measured number rather than a guess?","a":"- **Wrap the component in an interface you own,** such as a retriever protocol, and keep one contract\n  test suite every implementation must pass.\n- **Run the suite against a candidate replacement in staging,** checking both quality (for example\n  recall at 5 on the golden set) and safety properties such as no ACL leaks.\n- **Rehearse a trial swap about once a year** and record the time in the layer map."}],"tradeoffs":["**Buying: speed vs control.** Time-to-value and someone else's roadmap, but you pay lock-in and lose control of the quality loop; it flips the moment the capability is the product rather than a utility.","**Building: differentiation vs maintenance.** Control and differentiation, but you pay the build, permanent maintenance and the opportunity cost, while open-source alternatives keep improving; it flips when the layer commoditises.","**Open source: no licence vs ownership.** It avoids licence fees and vendor lock-in, but you own upgrades, security patches and the expertise to run it; with a small team that often exceeds a licence.","**Buy-then-build: de-risked vs paying twice.** It de-risks requirements, but you pay twice plus a migration; worth it when uncertainty about user needs is high and the vendor's exit path is clean.","**Seams: bounded switching vs abstraction.** Adapters bound switching cost but add abstraction and leak when a vendor-specific feature is the reason you bought; invest in proportion to switching probability × switching cost."],"pitfalls":["**Reading \"AI is strategic\" as \"build every layer\".** Symptom: a platform team six months in with an in-house vector database and no shipped product.","**Underestimating integration for a bought product.** Symptom: the licence was a third of the project cost; SSO, connectors and access-control mapping were the rest.","**Buying a product with an opaque quality loop.** Symptom: nobody can explain a wrong answer, run the internal eval set against it, or tell whether last month's update made it worse.","**Mismatched pricing model.** Symptom: per-seat pricing that doubled when adoption succeeded, or usage pricing with no cap that produced a surprise invoice.","**No exit plan.** Symptom: at renewal the price rises and the prompts, analytics and evaluation data all turn out to live inside the vendor's platform.","**Building the evaluation set only inside the vendor's tooling.** Symptom: switching vendors means starting the test set from zero."],"when":"- **Run the per-layer decision:** at the start of any AI initiative, again at each renewal, and whenever\n  a layer visibly commoditises (a capability that needed a vendor last year ships in an open-source\n  project this year).\n- **Buy by default:** models, serving, gateways, tracing, vector stores and generic guardrails.\n- **Build by default:** evaluation sets, domain workflows, context assembly, integrations with your\n  systems of record, and the experience if it is your product.\n- **Measure:** time-to-swap per bought component, spend by layer, the integration-to-licence cost ratio,\n  and whether a new engineer can point to which parts are yours.\n- **Interviews:** \"would you build or buy X\" is answered with the layer decomposition and the four\n  questions, never with one word.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the adapter seams, evaluates a vendor component and a home-built option against the same golden set, and measures how long a swap actually takes.","architect":"Owns the layer map and each layer's decision with a three-year cost that includes exit, and negotiates with a credible build alternative in hand.","fde":"Sits on the vendor side of the customer's decision, proving integration path, exit path and evaluation transparency, and recognising honestly when the customer should build instead."},"check":{"q":"A fintech wants a customer-facing assistant that answers questions about a user's own\ntransactions and explains fees. The CTO asks, \"build or buy?\" Walk through your answer.","a":"Decompose it. Model: rent, through an API or a private endpoint if the regulator requires it;\nnever train. Gateway, tracing and eval tooling: buy or adopt open source, they are commodity. Retrieval\nover fee documents: a managed vector store, but build the ingestion and, above all, the per-user\ntransaction tool, because that integration with the ledger and its access controls is both the most\nsensitive and the most differentiating piece. Guardrails: buy PII and toxicity detection, build the\n\"no financial advice\" policy and the evaluation that proves it holds. Evaluation set: always build,\nfrom real support conversations with compliance-reviewed answers. The experience: build, it is the\nproduct. A vertical banking-chatbot SaaS fails three tests at once: transaction data would flow to a\nvendor, fee explanations are the product, and compliance cannot review a black box. So buy the\nplatform layers, build the data, workflow and evaluation layers, keep seams at the model and the\nvector store, and revisit at renewal."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which layer should an organisation build regardless of what else it buys?","options":["The foundation model, to avoid vendor dependence","The vector database, because retrieval quality depends on it","The evaluation set and task metrics, because they are the only instrument that holds any vendor or model accountable","The AI gateway, because it handles credentials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Models, vector stores and gateways are commodity layers with mature options. The golden set encodes your task and your quality bar; without it you cannot compare vendors, detect regressions or switch with confidence."},{"q":"A vendor's end-to-end product costs less than building in year one. What most often reverses that over three years?","options":["Open-source alternatives become free, eliminating the build cost","Per-seat or usage pricing scales with adoption, integration costs more than the licence, and exit costs appear at renewal","Models become cheaper, so the vendor's margins shrink","Engineers become more productive, so builds get faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Year-one comparisons ignore how licence cost grows with success, how much integration work the purchase still requires, and the lock-in premium a vendor can charge once your prompts, data and analytics live inside their platform."},{"q":"What is the purpose of an adapter seam around a purchased component?","options":["To reduce the component's latency by caching its responses","To satisfy the vendor's API contract requirements","To bound the cost of switching so the build-versus-buy decision stays reversible","To hide the component's cost from the finance team"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A seam you own turns a vendor swap into a known amount of work rather than a rewrite. Its value is proportional to how likely and how expensive a switch would otherwise be."}]},{"key":"system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","sources":["Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"- **The real question is why anyone runs open weights.** The proprietary frontier usually leads on raw\n  capability and an API is the lowest-effort way to use it. But an API bundles conditions you may not be\n  able to accept.\n- **What the API bundles.** Data leaves your environment (mitigable by contract, not architecture). The\n  provider retires versions on its own schedule, taking the behaviour your prompts and evals were tuned\n  to. You cannot see or change weights, tokenizer, sampling internals or more than a sliver of the\n  logits. Fine-tuning is what the provider offers. Price is per token, forever, set by someone else. Your\n  core capability is a dependency you cannot fork.\n- **Open weights invert each point.** The weights are a file you keep: the same bytes in five years. Data\n  never has to leave. You can fully fine-tune, quantize, distil, prune, inspect and constrain decoding.\n  Cost is hardware and people, not tokens. In exchange you own serving, safety tuning, upgrades and\n  supply-chain checks, and usually accept a capability gap to the frontier.\n- **Both naive positions fail.** \"Open means free\" ignores GPUs, utilisation, serving engineering,\n  operations and the fact that the strongest open models trail the frontier. \"Proprietary is best, so use\n  it\" breaks under residency rules, deprecation churn, scale economics and deep customisation needs.\n- **\"Open\" is not open source.** Training data and code are usually withheld, and licences range from\n  permissive (Apache-2.0, MIT) to custom community licences with acceptable-use policies and clauses that\n  trigger at scale or restrict using outputs to train competitors. Read the licence; do not assume it.","how":"### The dimensions, and why each one matters\n1. **Control and lifecycle.** An open-weight model is an immutable artefact you pin by content hash; a\n   proprietary model is a versioned API with deprecations typically announced months ahead. This matters\n   *because* an LLM system's behaviour is tuned to one model; when it changes, the prompts, evals and\n   parsers were written against something that no longer exists.\n2. **Data governance.** Open weights on hosts you control mean data physically never leaves.\n   Proprietary access relies on data-processing agreements, zero-retention options and regional\n   endpoints. The deciding question: is the constraint legal (a contract and a network path can satisfy\n   it) or architectural (only hardware you control can)?\n3. **Customisation depth.** Open weights allow full fine-tuning, parameter-efficient adapters,\n   quantization, distillation, raw logit access, custom constrained decoding, speculative decoding with\n   your own draft model, and tokenizer work. Proprietary models offer prompting, structured output\n   features and sometimes a fine-tuning API. The gap matters most for narrow, high-volume tasks, where\n   deep customisation pays.\n4. **Capability.** The proprietary frontier generally leads; the best open models trail by a margin that\n   varies by task and shifts with every release. It is contested, so measure it on your own evaluation,\n   not a leaderboard.\n5. **Cost structure.** Proprietary is per-token operating cost that scales with usage. Self-hosted open\n   weights are fixed cost per GPU-hour, so economics hinge on utilisation (the self-host versus API lesson\n   works the break-even). Open weights served by a hosting provider turn back into per-token cost, often\n   below frontier proprietary pricing, with less control than self-hosting and no operations.\n6. **Licensing.** Permissive licences allow redistribution and commercial use with few conditions. Custom\n   licences may add acceptable-use policies, attribution or naming rules, restrictions on training other\n   models with outputs, and thresholds for very large deployments. The licence decides whether you can\n   ship on-device, redistribute fine-tunes, or embed the model in a product you sell.\n7. **Ecosystem and supply chain.** Mature serving engines, quantization formats and fine-tuning tooling\n   are a large part of open weights' practical value. The risk: hub weights can be tampered with,\n   pickle-format checkpoints can execute code on load, and a popular fine-tune may carry behaviour nobody\n   audited. Pin revisions, prefer safetensors, verify hashes, read the model card.\n8. **Safety and support.** A proprietary API includes the provider's safety tuning, moderation layers,\n   SLAs and someone to call. With open weights you own safety behaviour and guardrails, and outages are\n   your outages.\n\n### The governance middle ground\n- **What it is.** A proprietary model offered as a managed service by the cloud provider you already use,\n  reached over private networking in your region.\n- **What it gives.** Traffic never crosses the public internet, and the processor is one you have already\n  contracted.\n- **What it does not.** Inference still runs on the provider's infrastructure, not on hosts you operate.\n\n### Decoupling the axis from deployment\n| Combination | Gives | Costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open weights, self-hosted | maximum control and isolation | maximum operations |\n| Open weights through a hosting API | portability and lower price without hardware | the host's lifecycle and shared infrastructure |\n| Proprietary through the public API | frontier capability, least effort | least control |\n| Proprietary via your cloud provider's managed service, private endpoint | frontier-class capability with contractual and network isolation | often a premium; model availability lags the provider's own API |\n| Proprietary weights licensed for deployment inside the customer's environment (rarer) | physical isolation | no right to modify or redistribute |\n\nFour combinations exist and all are used, plus the rarer fifth. Choosing \"open\" does not force you to\nrun GPUs, and choosing \"proprietary\" does not force data onto the public internet.\n\n### How the decision runs\n1. **Constraints first:** isolation requirements, licence compatibility with how you ship, and the\n   customisation the task needs.\n2. **Capability** on your eval.\n3. **Cost** at your real utilisation.\n4. **Lifecycle risk and exit cost.**\n\nThe common outcome is a portfolio: proprietary frontier for the hardest and lowest-volume work, open\nweights (hosted or self-hosted, often fine-tuned) for high-volume narrow tasks, both behind a gateway so\nthe split can move as the models do.","example":"### Pinned bytes and the full next-token distribution\nThree things an open-weight artefact allows that an API-only model does not: pin the exact bytes, read\nthe full next-token distribution, and constrain the decision to an allowed set without generating text.\nThe restricted softmax yields a per-class score that a cascade can route on.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"                 # any small open-weight chat model\nrevision = \"<commit hash from the model page>\"      # pin exact weights, never a moving branch\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name, revision=revision)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    name, revision=revision, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,\n    use_safetensors=True,                           # refuse pickle checkpoints: they can run code on load\n).eval()\n\nlabels = [\"refund\", \"shipping\", \"other\"]\n# First token of each label, with no leading space: the chat template ends the assistant header with a\n# newline, so the reply's first token is not space-prefixed. Check the ids are distinct for your tokenizer.\nlabel_ids = [tok(l, add_special_tokens=False).input_ids[0] for l in labels]\nassert len(set(label_ids)) == len(labels)\n\nmsgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Classify this message: 'My parcel never arrived'. \"\n                                    \"Reply with exactly one lowercase word: refund, shipping or other.\"}]\nenc = tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\", return_dict=True)\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    next_logits = model(**enc).logits[0, -1]        # full-vocabulary distribution for the next token\nprobs = torch.softmax(next_logits[label_ids], dim=-1)   # renormalise over the allowed labels only\nmass = torch.softmax(next_logits.float(), dim=-1)[label_ids].sum().item()\nfor label, p in zip(labels, probs.tolist()):\n    print(f\"{label:9s} {p:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"mass on allowed labels: {mass:.3f}\")   # low mass: the model wanted to say something else\n# No decoding loop and no parsing of free text. The renormalised probabilities are a confidence signal\n# to calibrate on held-out data (not a calibrated probability as-is); low mass flags odd inputs.\n```\n\nThrough a proprietary API the same classification needs a generated answer parsed from text, and any\nconfidence must be inferred from limited top-k log probabilities, if the provider exposes them at all.\n\n### Supply-chain gate for open weights\nDownload only safetensors and config files at a pinned revision, then compare file hashes with a\nmanifest committed when the artefact was first reviewed. Run it in CI and at deploy time.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib\nfrom huggingface_hub import snapshot_download\n\nREPO, REV = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\", \"<commit hash from the model page>\"\npath = pathlib.Path(snapshot_download(\n    REPO, revision=REV,\n    allow_patterns=[\"*.safetensors\", \"*.json\", \"*.txt\", \"*.model\"],   # no pickle (.bin/.pt) files\n))\n\ndef sha256(p, chunk=1 << 20):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(chunk), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\nactual = {p.name: sha256(p) for p in sorted(path.glob(\"*.safetensors\"))}\npinned = json.loads(pathlib.Path(\"model-manifest.json\").read_text())   # reviewed, committed\nassert actual == pinned, \"weights differ from the reviewed artefact\"\n```\n\n### Worked decision: one company, three task families\nIllustrative; each row applies \"How the decision runs\" in order.\n\n| Task family | Binding constraint | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| Complex contract analysis, low volume | capability on the eval | proprietary frontier via public API, pinned version |\n| Patient-record extraction, high volume | legal residency: in-region, existing processor only | cloud provider's managed proprietary model over a private endpoint; a third-party open-weight host is excluded (new processor), self-hosted open weights only if volume keeps the GPUs busy |\n| On-device intent classifier in a shipped product | licence must allow embedding and redistribution; behaviour frozen for years | permissively licensed open weights, fine-tuned, pinned by hash |\n\nAll three sit behind one gateway and one eval harness, so a row can change when the models do.","patterns":["**Revision pin plus hash manifest.** For every open-weight artefact. Pin the model repository commit, download only safetensors, and fail CI and deployment if file hashes differ from the reviewed manifest. Monitor manifest changes; each one needs a review and an eval run.","**Licence register with legal sign-off.** Before any open-weight model reaches production. Record licence name, acceptable-use policy, attribution or naming rules, output-use restrictions and any scale thresholds, and how you ship (server, on-device, redistributed fine-tune). Monitor usage against scale thresholds and re-review on each new model version.","**Isolation requirement classified in writing.** At the start of any regulated engagement. Get the customer to state whether the constraint is legal (contract and network path suffice) or architectural (only hosts they operate). Monitor that the chosen deployment still matches it after any vendor change.","**Explicit guardrails when leaving an API.** When migrating a workload to open weights. Add input and output moderation and policy checks that the API model provided implicitly, and run a red-team and jailbreak eval before cutover. Monitor policy-violation and jailbreak success rates against the API baseline.","**Deprecation calendar for proprietary models.** For every pinned API version. Track announced retirement dates, and open the migration eval the day a notice arrives. Monitor days of runway left versus the time your last migration took."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Task family\"] --> I{\"Isolation requirement: legal or architectural?\"}\n    I -->|\"architectural: only hosts you operate\"| OW[\"Open weights, self-hosted, pinned by hash\"]\n    I -->|\"legal: contract and network path suffice\"| L{\"Licence fits how you ship? Customisation needed?\"}\n    L -->|\"deep customisation or on-device\"| OWH[\"Open weights, self-hosted or hosted API\"]\n    L -->|\"prompting suffices\"| CAP[\"Measure capability on your eval\"]\n    OWH --> CAP\n    CAP --> COST[\"Cost at real utilisation\"]\n    OW --> COST\n    COST --> LIFE[\"Lifecycle risk and exit cost\"]\n    LIFE --> P[\"Portfolio entry behind one gateway\"]\n    P -.->|\"model release or deprecation notice\"| CAP","caption":"The decision runs constraints first, then capability, cost and lifecycle; note that the isolation\nquestion picks a deployment, not just a weight type."},"interview":[{"q":"Is open-weight the same as open source? Why does the difference matter?","a":"- **No.** Open weights publish the parameters under a licence; training data and code are usually\n  withheld.\n- **Licences range widely,** from permissive (Apache-2.0, MIT) to custom community licences with\n  acceptable-use policies, scale thresholds and limits on using outputs to train other models.\n- **It matters because the licence decides how you can ship:** on-device, redistributed fine-tunes or\n  embedded in a product you sell. Skipping legal review surfaces later in due diligence."},{"q":"Why is open-weight versus proprietary a separate axis from self-host versus API?","a":"- **Open weights can be served by a hosting provider** as a per-token API, with no GPUs of your own.\n- **Proprietary models can be reached through your cloud provider's managed service** over a private\n  endpoint in-region, so data need not cross the public internet.\n- **So \"no data leaves\" is a deployment requirement first.** Classify it as legal or architectural\n  before picking a weight type."},{"q":"You migrated a workload from a proprietary API to an open-weight model and jailbreak success rose. What happened?","a":"- **The API's safety tuning, moderation layers and policy checks were implicit,** and they did not come\n  with the weights.\n- **Fix it explicitly:** add input and output moderation and policy checks, then run a red-team and\n  jailbreak eval against the API baseline before cutover.\n- **Keep monitoring** policy-violation and jailbreak success rates after cutover."},{"q":"What supply-chain checks would you require before an open-weight model reaches production?","a":"- **Pin the repository revision** to a commit hash, never a moving branch.\n- **Download only safetensors,** because pickle-format checkpoints can execute code on load.\n- **Verify file hashes against a reviewed, committed manifest** in CI and at deploy time, and read the\n  model card; a popular fine-tune may carry behaviour nobody audited."},{"q":"When does proprietary win even for a company that wants vendor independence?","a":"- **When frontier capability is needed now** and the open-weight gap on your own eval is material.\n- **When volume is low or spiky and the team is small,** because self-hosted GPUs at a small fraction of\n  capacity cost more per task than the API.\n- **Contain the dependency instead:** pin versions, keep a deprecation calendar, and put the model\n  behind a gateway with the same eval harness, so exit stays possible."}],"tradeoffs":["**Open weights: control vs operations.** Lifecycle control and physical data isolation, but you take on serving, safety tuning, upgrades and supply-chain checks; it flips when the team lacks operations capacity or the volume cannot keep a GPU busy.","**Proprietary: capability vs lifecycle.** Frontier capability with no infrastructure, but the behaviour you tuned to can be retired on the provider's schedule; it flips when behaviour must stay fixed for years, as in regulated workflows or embedded devices.","**Deep customisation vs re-certification.** It buys quality on narrow tasks but creates artefacts you must retrain and re-certify when the base changes; it flips when the task is broad and prompting suffices.","**Hosted open weights: middle path vs inherited lifecycle.** Portable and cheaper per token than frontier pricing with no operations, but you inherit the host's lifecycle and share its infrastructure; it fits when you want exit optionality without hardware.","**Capability vs licence permissiveness.** The most capable open model may carry the most restrictive licence; this flips the moment you redistribute, embed on-device, or sell the model as part of a product."],"pitfalls":["**Reading \"open weights\" as \"open source\" and skipping legal review.** Symptom: an acceptable-use or scale clause surfaces during a customer's due diligence or an acquisition.","**Loading weights from an unverified source or in pickle format.** Symptom: code execution on load, or a fine-tune that behaves differently from its model card.","**Assuming open weights are cheaper without a utilisation model.** Symptom: a GPU running at a small fraction of capacity costs more per task than the API would have.","**Pointing a proprietary integration at a floating \"latest\" alias.** Symptom: outputs change overnight, evals fail, and there is nothing in the change log.","**Expecting the provider's safety behaviour to follow you to open weights.** Symptom: jailbreak success and policy violations rise because the guardrails were implicit in the API model.","**Choosing by public benchmark instead of your own eval.** Symptom: a migration that was \"equivalent on benchmarks\" loses several points on the task that matters."],"when":"- **Choose open weights when:** data isolation must be architectural rather than contractual; behaviour\n  must be frozen for years; high-volume narrow tasks where fine-tuning pays; on-device or edge\n  deployment; you need logits or custom decoding; or vendor exit is a board-level requirement.\n- **Choose proprietary when:** you need frontier capability now, volume is low or spiky, the team is\n  small, and the provider's terms satisfy your governance.\n- **Most organisations run both** through a portfolio.\n- **Measure:** the eval delta between candidates, GPU utilisation if self-hosting, cost per successful\n  task, and time to migrate after a deprecation notice.\n- **Interviews:** separate this axis from deployment, name the licence question, and make the lifecycle\n  argument concretely.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pins weights by revision, verifies safetensors and hashes, runs the same eval against the open and proprietary candidates, and keeps both behind one interface.","architect":"Owns the decision per task family, the licence review with legal, the isolation requirement classification (legal versus architectural), and the exit plan.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"no data leaves\" demand into the cheapest option that truly meets it, and sets expectations about capability, safety and operations when open weights are the answer."},"check":{"q":"A healthcare customer insists on open-weight models \"so that no data leaves\". Their platform\nteam is three engineers and expected volume is low. What do you advise, and how do you reason about it?","a":"Separate the two axes first. The actual requirement is data isolation, and depending on what\n\"leaves\" means it can be met by self-hosted open weights, or by a proprietary model through their\nexisting cloud provider's managed service over a private endpoint in-region, with contractual zero\nretention. Which one depends on whether their constraint is legal, where a contract with an existing\nprocessor and a private network path satisfy the regulator, or physical, where only hosts they operate\nwill do. I would get that definition in writing before choosing. With\nthree engineers and low volume, a self-hosted GPU fleet is an operations burden with poor utilisation\neconomics, so if the constraint is legal, the private endpoint or a hosted open model in-region is\nlikely cheaper and safer. If the environment is genuinely air-gapped, open weights are the only\noption: pick a permissively licensed model, pin by hash, verify safetensors, serve with a standard\nengine, size the GPU from the memory arithmetic, and add explicit guardrails because the API's implicit\nsafety tuning is gone. Either way, build the eval on their data first and keep the model behind a\ngateway so the decision stays reversible."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does \"open-weight\" actually guarantee about a model?","options":["Its training data and training code are published","It may be used for any purpose without restriction","Its parameters are published for download and use under a licence; training data, code and unrestricted use are not implied","It is always cheaper to run than a proprietary model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Open-weight releases publish the parameters, usually with a licence that may carry acceptable-use, attribution or scale conditions. Data and code are typically withheld, and cost depends entirely on how the weights are served and how busy the hardware is."},{"q":"Which capability is available only with open weights, not through a proprietary API?","options":["Structured JSON output conforming to a schema","Pinning the exact model bytes by hash and keeping that behaviour indefinitely","A service-level agreement on availability","Lower latency on every request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A provider can retire any API version; a downloaded artefact cannot be retired. Structured outputs and SLAs are things proprietary APIs commonly offer, and latency depends on serving, not on whether the weights are open."},{"q":"A customer's rule is that data must stay in-region, never cross the public internet, and go to no processor other than their existing cloud provider. Which option is not automatically excluded?","options":["A proprietary model through its vendor's public API with a zero-retention agreement","An open-weight model served by a third-party hosting provider's multi-tenant API","A proprietary model offered as a managed service by their existing cloud provider, reached through a private endpoint in-region","None of these; only self-hosted open weights can satisfy the requirement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The public API and the third-party host both add a new processor and a public network path, so they fail the stated rule. The cloud provider's managed service over private networking satisfies it, which shows the isolation requirement is separate from the open-versus-proprietary choice. A rule demanding hosts the customer operates would exclude it too."}]},{"key":"system-design/self-host-vs-api","sources":["Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **Opposite cost structures.** An API is pure variable cost: pay per token, scale to zero, someone else\n  does capacity planning. A self-hosted cluster is mostly fixed cost: a GPU bills by the hour whether it\n  serves 2,000 tokens per second or idles at 3 a.m., and it comes with an engineer on call.\n- **\"The GPU is cheaper\" ignores utilisation.** Comparing X per million tokens with Y per GPU-hour fails,\n  because a GPU averaging 20% of its peak throughput delivers tokens at five times its nominal price.\n- **\"Sensitive data, so self-host\" is often wrong both ways.** Enterprise API terms with zero retention and\n  in-region endpoints often satisfy legal. Some regulated environments genuinely cannot send data to any\n  third party, which makes the API unavailable at any price.\n- **\"Open models are good enough now\" is a per-task claim** that only your eval set can settle.\n- **Both sides hide costs, and the answer changes over a product's life.** Self-host: engine and driver\n  upgrades, security patching, peak capacity, cold starts of minutes while a model loads, re-running evals\n  after every change. API: deprecation schedules you do not control, rate limits during incidents,\n  coupling to provider-specific features.","how":"### The cost model\n- **API cost** = sum over calls of tokens × price, with separate prices for input, output and (often)\n  cached input tokens.\n- **Self-host cost** = fixed spend divided by useful work:\n\n```\neffective_$ per 1M tokens = gpu_hourly_cost × 1e6 / (tokens_per_second × 3600 × utilisation)\n```\n\n| Term | What it really means | Why it bites |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tokens_per_second` | Aggregate throughput of one GPU at *your* batch size and prompt/output mix, not a vendor headline | Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so it rises with batching until the KV cache fills memory |\n| `utilisation` | Average load ÷ provisioned capacity | Interactive traffic peaks several times its average; you provision for the peak and idle the rest of the day, unless batch work fills the valleys |\n| People | A fraction of an engineer plus on-call | A fixed cost that does not shrink with traffic |\n\n### Break-even is a staircase, not a point\n1. **The API bill is linear:** `api = V × p_blended`. `p_blended` weights input, output and cached-input\n   prices by your real token mix. Prompt-heavy traffic blends close to the input price; the example below\n   works out to about $0.23 per million tokens.\n2. **The self-host bill is a staircase:**\n   `ceil(peak_load / per_gpu_throughput) × gpu_hourly × 730 + people`. It is flat until peak load needs\n   another GPU, then jumps.\n3. **The first step is whatever the model needs to run at all:** one GPU for a quantised 8B model, a\n   multi-GPU node for a 70B model at 16-bit precision.\n4. **Below the first step the API almost always wins,** because you pay for a whole node to serve a\n   fraction of its capacity.\n5. **Above it, anything that raises per-GPU throughput or lowers the peak pulls the staircase down:**\n   - a smaller or quantised model;\n   - larger batches;\n   - prefix caching for shared system prompts;\n   - batch jobs that fill night-time valleys, so the peak-to-average ratio falls.\n\nSo the comparison is really about model size and utilisation, which is why two teams quoting the same GPU\nprice reach opposite answers.\n\n### The constraints that decide before cost does\n| Constraint | Self-host offers | Check first |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Data** | Residency, retention, customer contracts, air gap | What the vendor's enterprise terms and in-cloud platforms (a model served inside your cloud account's boundary) actually provide |\n| **Control** | Pinning a version for a year, deterministic behaviour over time, custom fine-tunes or LoRA adapters, logit access, engine-level constrained decoding | APIs offer some of these; hosting offers all of them |\n| **Latency** | No WAN hop inside your VPC; tunable time-to-first-token | A small, poorly utilised cluster has worse tail latency than a provider's large fleet |\n| **Capability** | Fine-tuned small models often match on narrow tasks (classification, extraction) | Frontier proprietary models are usually ahead on open-ended tasks; only your eval knows |\n| **Elasticity** | Spikes absorbed up to your headroom; a new replica means minutes of model loading | APIs absorb spikes up to your quota |\n\n### The middle options\n- **Managed open-weight hosting:** a vendor serves open models per token.\n- **Provisioned-throughput plans** from API vendors: fixed cost, no ops.\n- **Cloud model platforms** inside your account boundary.\n- **Serverless GPUs** for spiky loads.\n- **The hybrid most teams end up with:** the API for low-volume frontier tasks, self-hosted small models for\n  one or two high-volume narrow tasks that dominate spend.\n\n### How the decision evolves\n1. **Prototype on the API,** because it is the fastest way to learn whether the product works.\n2. **Instrument** tokens per month per task, prompt/output ratio, peak-to-average ratio and quality on a\n   golden set.\n3. **Move one task** when a single narrow task dominates spend and an open model clears the eval bar; keep\n   the long tail on the API.\n4. **Revisit quarterly,** because API prices and open-model quality both move, and the break-even point\n   moves with them.","example":"### Break-even calculator\nEvery figure is an illustrative assumption to replace with your own measurements; the point is the shape\nof the result, not the numbers.\n\n```python\n# All numbers illustrative. Replace with your traces and a throughput benchmark on your prompt mix.\nmonthly_in, monthly_out = 40e9, 8e9           # tokens/month: 40B prompt, 8B completion\napi_in_price, api_out_price = 0.15, 0.60      # $ per 1M tokens, small-model tier (illustrative)\napi_cost = monthly_in / 1e6 * api_in_price + monthly_out / 1e6 * api_out_price\n\ngpu_hourly = 2.50                             # $ per GPU-hour on demand (illustrative)\nagg_out_tps = 1500                            # decode tokens/s per GPU at your batch size (measure it)\npeak_to_avg = 3.0                             # interactive traffic: provision for the daily peak\neng_cost = 12000                              # $/month: part of an engineer plus on-call\n\navg_tps = monthly_out / (30 * 24 * 3600)      # ~3,086 output tokens/s averaged over the month\ngpus = -(-avg_tps * peak_to_avg // agg_out_tps)          # ceil: GPUs needed at peak\ngpu_cost = gpus * gpu_hourly * 730\nutilisation = avg_tps / (gpus * agg_out_tps)\nprint(f\"API ${api_cost:,.0f}/mo vs self-host ${gpu_cost + eng_cost:,.0f}/mo \"\n      f\"({int(gpus)} GPUs at {utilisation:.0%} utilisation)\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what flips the answer\nChange two things: five times the traffic, plus reserved GPUs at $1.50 per hour and batch jobs that\nflatten the peak ratio to 1.5.\n\n| Scenario | API / month | Self-host / month | GPUs | Utilisation | Winner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Base inputs above | about $10,800 | $24,775 | 7 | 29% | API |\n| 5× traffic, $1.50/h reserved, peak ratio 1.5 | $54,000 | about $29,520 | 16 | 64% | Self-host |\n\n- **Caveat:** the model ignores prefill compute, which matters when prompts are five times longer than\n  outputs. Benchmark aggregate throughput on your real traffic before trusting it.\n\n### Sweep the staircase\nSame illustrative inputs, swept across volume. Higher utilisation, not a cheaper GPU alone, moves the\ncrossover.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef self_host(out_per_month, gpu_hourly, peak_to_avg, tps=1500, people=12000):\n    avg_tps = out_per_month / (30 * 24 * 3600)\n    gpus = max(1, math.ceil(avg_tps * peak_to_avg / tps))   # the staircase: whole GPUs only\n    return gpus, gpus * gpu_hourly * 730 + people\n\nP_OUT = 5 * 0.15 + 0.60      # API $ per 1M output tokens with 5 prompt tokens per output token\nfor b in (1, 8, 20, 40, 80):\n    out = b * 1e9\n    api = out / 1e6 * P_OUT\n    g1, on_demand = self_host(out, 2.50, 3.0)    # on-demand GPUs, spiky interactive traffic\n    g2, tuned = self_host(out, 1.50, 1.5)        # reserved GPUs, batch jobs fill the valleys\n    print(f\"{b:>2}B/mo  API ${api:>8,.0f}  on-demand ${on_demand:>8,.0f} ({g1:>2} GPUs)\"\n          f\"  tuned ${tuned:>8,.0f} ({g2:>2} GPUs)\")\n# 1B: API wins easily (one GPU plus people is the first step).\n# 8B: API $10,800 vs on-demand $24,775 vs tuned $16,380.\n# 20B: tuned ($20,760) beats the API ($27,000); on-demand ($41,200) never does in this range.\n```","patterns":["**Provider-agnostic client from day one.** When starting on an API, put every call behind one internal interface that takes a task name. vLLM serves an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API, so moving a task is a base URL and model-name change. Monitor tokens, cost and latency per task, not per provider.","**Hybrid split by task.** When one narrow task dominates spend, run it on a self-hosted small model and keep the long tail on the API. Gate the move on that task's golden-set score. Monitor quality per task and the share of spend still on the API.","**Shadow before you switch.** Before migrating, mirror a sample of production requests to the hosted model asynchronously and score both outputs offline. Monitor the quality delta and the hosted p95 time-to-first-token at the real daily peak, not at a quiet hour.","**Fill the valleys.** When interactive traffic has a high peak-to-average ratio, schedule offline jobs (evals, backfills, embedding refreshes) on the same GPUs overnight. Monitor average utilisation and the peak-to-average ratio, the two inputs that move the staircase.","**Benchmark on your own mix.** Before trusting `tokens_per_second`, replay your real prompt and output lengths at your target concurrency with the engine's serving benchmark (vLLM ships one). Monitor aggregate throughput and p95 latency together; one without the other misleads.","**Decision record with a revisit date.** Write the constraints, the cost model inputs and the break-even volume into an ADR. Re-run it quarterly or after a provider price change. Monitor actual monthly volume against the break-even volume."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"New task or quarterly review\"] --> C{\"Hard constraint? air gap, adapters, version pin\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Self-host this task\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| T{\"Do zero-retention terms or an in-cloud platform satisfy legal?\"}\n    T -->|\"no\"| H\n    T -->|\"yes\"| E{\"Open model passes the golden-set eval?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| A[\"Stay on the API\"]\n    E -->|\"yes\"| U{\"Cheaper at real peak, people included?\"}\n    U -->|\"no\"| A\n    U -->|\"yes\"| HY[\"Hybrid: move this one task, keep the long tail on the API\"]\n    A -.->|\"revisit as volume and prices move\"| S\n    HY -.->|\"revisit quarterly\"| S","caption":"Read it top to bottom: hard constraints (including a ban on any third-party processor) decide first,\nthen quality on your eval, then the cost model at real peak and utilisation with people included; most\npaths end in the API or a hybrid split, not a full migration."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is a cheaper GPU-hour not enough to say self-hosting is cheaper?","a":"- **Self-host cost is fixed spend divided by useful work.** The effective price per million tokens is\n  `gpu_hourly_cost × 1e6 / (tokens_per_second × 3600 × utilisation)`, so utilisation sits in the\n  denominator.\n- **Peaks force over-provisioning.** You size for the daily peak, so a GPU averaging 20% of peak throughput\n  delivers tokens at five times its nominal price, before adding people and on-call."},{"q":"Walk me through building a break-even model for moving one task off the API.","a":"- **Inputs from traces:** tokens per month for that task, the prompt/output ratio, and the peak-to-average\n  ratio; a blended API price from your real input, output and cached-input mix.\n- **Throughput from a benchmark:** aggregate tokens per second per GPU on your own prompt mix at target\n  concurrency, not a vendor headline.\n- **Model the staircase:** `ceil(peak_load / per_gpu_throughput) × gpu_hourly × 730 + people`, compared with\n  the linear API bill across a range of volumes to find where it crosses."},{"q":"When would you recommend staying on the API even though the volume is high?","a":"- **When the open model fails the eval.** Savings mean nothing if quality complaints and escalations rise\n  within weeks.\n- **When traffic is spiky and nothing fills the valleys.** A high peak-to-average ratio keeps utilisation\n  low, so the staircase stays above the API line.\n- **When the team cannot own operations.** Engine upgrades, patching, cold starts of minutes and on-call are\n  real costs; a provisioned-throughput plan or managed open-weight hosting may be the better middle option."},{"q":"You moved a task to self-hosted and the bill did not drop. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check utilisation first:** GPUs idling at 20% on dashboards means you are paying for peak headroom all\n  day; fix with batch jobs at night, reserved GPUs or fewer, larger batches.\n- **Check throughput assumptions:** measured aggregate tokens per second on real traffic may be far below\n  the benchmark, especially with long prompts, because the cost model ignored prefill compute.\n- **Check the people line:** on-call and upgrade work were often left out of the original model."},{"q":"How do you keep the option to switch providers or self-host later?","a":"- **One internal interface keyed by task name,** so application code never calls a vendor SDK directly.\n- **Avoid provider-specific features deep in code** (tool-call formats, cache controls, response schemas),\n  or wrap them, because hard-coding them is what produces \"we can't switch\" during a price negotiation.\n- **Use an OpenAI-compatible engine such as vLLM,** so moving a task is a base URL and model-name change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Marginal cost vs utilisation risk.** Self-hosting lowers marginal cost per token, but only above a utilisation threshold. Low or spiky traffic flips the advantage back to the API, and every provider price cut moves the break-even point.","**Control vs upgrade burden.** Full control over model version and behaviour, in exchange for owning every upgrade. Providers deprecate on their schedule; self-hosted, you must re-evaluate each engine and model change yourself.","**Data inside your boundary vs operations.** Zero-retention terms and in-cloud model platforms often meet the same requirement without operations. It flips only when no third-party processor is permitted at all.","**Frontier quality vs narrow-task fit.** Frontier APIs usually lead on open-ended quality. The gap narrows to nothing on narrow tasks where a fine-tuned small model beats a general model on your eval, which is when hosting that task pays.","**Quota vs headroom.** APIs absorb spikes up to the quota you negotiated; self-hosting needs idle headroom or a queue. It flips when provider rate limits themselves become the bottleneck during peaks."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing list prices without utilisation.** Symptom: projected savings never appear in the bill while dashboards show GPUs idling at 20%. Fix: use the utilisation formula with peak provisioning.","**Treating \"sensitive data\" as automatically meaning self-hosting.** Symptom: months of platform work for a requirement a contract addendum met. Or the reverse: learning at procurement review that data could never leave and the API prototype must be rebuilt. Fix: get the exact data rule in writing early.","**Migrating on cost alone without a golden-set eval.** Symptom: spend falls while quality complaints and escalations rise within weeks.","**Omitting people and on-call from the model.** Symptom: a single engineer is the cluster's real single point of failure and cannot take holiday.","**Hard-coding provider-specific features** (tool-call formats, cache controls, response schemas) deep in application code. Symptom: \"we can't switch\" during a price negotiation.","**Sizing for average load instead of peak.** Symptom: queueing and time-to-first-token collapse at the daily peak, exactly when the most users are present."],"when":"- **Default to the API** for new products and for any task whose volume is small or whose quality bar\n  needs a frontier model.\n- **Self-host when a hard constraint applies:** air gap, strict residency with no acceptable in-cloud\n  option, logit or adapter access, version pinning the vendor will not give you.\n- **Or self-host one task** when it has high sustained volume, an open model passes your eval, and your\n  utilisation model shows a clear margin after people cost.\n- **Measure first:** tokens per task per month, prompt/output ratio, peak-to-average ratio, p95 latency and\n  golden-set quality.\n- **Interviews:** give the utilisation formula plus the constraint checklist, ending in a hybrid portfolio\n  with a revisit cadence, not a one-word preference.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds behind a provider-agnostic interface from day one, instruments tokens and latency per task, and feeds real trace numbers into the break-even model rather than vendor estimates.","architect":"Owns the decision record: constraints first, cost second, a hybrid split of tasks across API and hosted models, and a scheduled review as prices and model quality move.","fde":"Maps a customer's residency, procurement and network rules to the deployment options, and often lands on an in-cloud managed model as the compromise between the API and a GPU cluster."},"check":{"q":"A customer insists on self-hosting a 70B-parameter model for a support assistant handling about\n2,000 chats a day, because \"APIs are too expensive and our data is sensitive\". How do you evaluate the\nrequest?","a":"Quantify first. 2,000 chats at roughly 4,000 tokens each is about 8 million tokens a day, around\n240 million a month, which is a modest API bill at any current price tier. A 70B model in 16-bit\nprecision has about 140 GB of weights, so it needs a multi-GPU node running around the clock; 240 million\ntokens a month averages under 100 tokens per second against a node that can serve many times that, so\nutilisation would be in the single-digit percent range and the per-token cost many times the API's. Then\ntest the data claim: what exactly may not leave, and would zero-retention enterprise terms or an\nin-cloud model platform in their region satisfy legal? If a genuine air-gap requirement survives that\nconversation, propose a smaller model on one GPU after an eval shows it meets the quality bar, budget the\non-call, and record the decision and its assumptions in an ADR so it can be revisited when volume grows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which parameter most often decides whether self-hosting beats the API on cost?","options":["The GPU's list price per hour","Average utilisation: served load divided by the capacity you had to provision for peak","The number of parameters in the model","The length of the system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-host cost is fixed spend divided by useful tokens, so idle capacity multiplies the effective price per token. GPU price and model size matter, but a cheap GPU at 15% utilisation still loses to the API, while an expensive one at 70% can win."},{"q":"A prototype runs on a frontier API and one high-volume classification task is 70% of spend. What is the best next step?","options":["Move all traffic to a self-hosted frontier-class model to eliminate API spend","Negotiate a discount and keep the architecture unchanged","Evaluate an open small model on that task's golden set and, if it passes, self-host only that task","Switch the classification task to a larger API model to raise accuracy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The hybrid split captures most of the saving with the least risk: one narrow task, measurable quality, predictable volume. Moving everything multiplies operational burden and puts the open-ended tasks on a weaker model."},{"q":"Which requirement cannot be met by a hosted API regardless of contract terms?","options":["Zero retention of prompts and outputs by the provider","Processing inside a specific cloud region","Inference inside an air-gapped network with no outbound connectivity","Sub-second time to first token for short prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retention and regional processing are routinely covered by enterprise agreements and in-cloud platforms, and APIs can deliver fast first tokens. An air gap removes the network path the API needs, so hosting is the only option."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-routing-cascading","sources":["Chen, Zaharia, Zou, 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance","Ong et al., 2024 — RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data"],"why":"- **Requests vary enormously in difficulty.** Everything on the strongest model overpays; everything on the\n  cheapest underdelivers.\n- **The price gap is huge.** Small vs frontier is commonly one to two orders of magnitude per token\n  (an illustrative range that changes with every price sheet), and the latency gap is several-fold. Yet a\n  large share of requests are easy: a lookup, a reformulation, a yes/no classification, a short summary.\n  Paying frontier prices for those is waste that scales linearly with success.\n- **The naive alternatives each fail.** One big model makes cost and latency the product's ceiling. One\n  small model fails on the hard tail, exactly the requests users remember and escalate. A model per\n  feature is coarse: difficulty within one feature still varies by an order of magnitude.\n- **So the decision must be per request, and cheap.** FrugalGPT showed a cascade with a learned \"is this\n  answer good enough\" scorer can match the strongest model's accuracy on several benchmarks at a fraction\n  of its cost. RouteLLM showed a router trained on human preference data can send most traffic to a\n  cheaper model with little quality loss.\n- **The engineering question** is where the quality signal comes from.","how":"### Routing: decide before the call\nThe router sees the request and its metadata (feature, tenant tier, language, length, conversation\nstate) and returns a model. In rough order of sophistication:\n\n1. **Static rules.** Feature or tenant maps to a model. Zero latency, no learning; the right first step.\n2. **Heuristics.** Token count, presence of code, number of constraints in the instruction, whether\n   tools are needed. Cheap and surprisingly effective for coarse splits.\n3. **Learned classifier.** An embedding plus a small classifier trained on past traffic labelled with\n   \"which model was needed\". Labels come from offline evals: run both models, judge the small model's\n   answer, and record whether it passed.\n4. **LLM-as-router.** Ask a small model to grade difficulty. Useful when you have no labels yet, but it\n   adds a model call to every request.\n\n**Two kinds of router error:**\n\n| Error | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Hard request routed down | Quality |\n| Easy request routed up | Money |\n\n- **Set the threshold** from the cost ratio and the quality penalty you accept, tune it on an eval set,\n  then monitor per route in production, because error rates drift as prompts and models change.\n- **Keep the router cheap** *because* it runs on every request: a 30 ms classifier is free, a 2 s LLM call\n  usually is not.\n\n### Deriving the routing threshold\n- The router estimates `p`, the probability that the small model's answer is adequate.\n- `L` is the cost of an inadequate answer in the same units as tokens (a retry, a human escalation, a lost\n  customer).\n- Send small when `c_small + (1 − p) × L < c_large`, so route small when `p > 1 − (c_large − c_small) / L`.\n\n| c_small | c_large | Cost of a bad answer | Route small when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 15 | 50 | p > 0.72 |\n| 1 | 15 | 500 (a billing dispute) | p > 0.972: only near-certain requests go small |\n\nThe threshold encodes the business cost of a miss, not the router's accuracy, which is why one router can\nneed a different threshold per feature (costs illustrative).\n\n### Cascading: decide after a cheap attempt\n1. Call the small model.\n2. Run a check.\n3. Return if it passes; escalate if it fails.\n\n**The check is the whole design.** Options, cheapest first:\n- **Deterministic gate:** JSON parses, required fields are present, the cited document id exists, the\n  generated SQL executes.\n- **Agreement across *n* sampled answers:** disagreement is a usable proxy for uncertainty.\n- **Verifier model or LLM judge.**\n- **Grounding check** against retrieved passages.\n- **Not self-reported confidence:** tempting and the least reliable, because models are poorly calibrated\n  about their own correctness.\n\n**Expected cost** per request is `c_small + c_check + p_escalate × c_large`. With illustrative units of 1\n(small), 0.5 (judge) and 15 (large) at a 25% escalation rate: 1 + 0.5 + 3.75 = 5.25 units against 15 for\nalways-large, a 65% saving.\n\n**The price is latency.** The 25% that escalate pay the small call, the check and the large call *in\nseries*, so p95 latency gets worse even as mean cost falls.\n\n### Routing versus cascading\n| | Routing | Cascading |\n|---|---|---|\n| Calls | One, so latency is bounded | Two or more on escalation, in series |\n| Main risk | Prediction error | Serial latency and check cost on escalations |\n| Needs | Labelled data to train | A reliable cheap check; no training data |\n| Quality floor | Router accuracy | Set by the check |\n\nIn practice they combine: route the obvious cases, cascade the ambiguous band in the middle.\n\n### Operating it\n- **Log the route taken and why.**\n- **Evaluate quality *per route*,** not only in aggregate, since a healthy average can hide a failing hard\n  tail.\n- **Keep a continuous baseline:** a small random slice (a few per cent) always served by the strong model,\n  so you see the gap move when a provider silently updates the small model.\n- **Budget and alert on the escalation rate,** because a degraded small model turns into 100% escalation\n  and a cost spike on the large one.\n- **Decide the consistency policy:** the same question may get different answers depending on the route,\n  acceptable for chat and not for an audit trail.","example":"### Two-rung cascade with LiteLLM\nA deterministic gate, then a small judge, using LiteLLM so the same code talks to several providers. The\nnumber to watch in production is `escalated`; its rate is what you tune.\n\n```python\nimport json, litellm\n\n# Model ids are illustrative and date quickly; use your providers' current ids. The judge should be a\n# different model from the generator it grades: models rate their own outputs favourably.\nSMALL, LARGE, JUDGE = \"openai/gpt-4o-mini\", \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\", \"anthropic/claude-3-5-haiku-20241022\"\n\ndef ask(model, messages, **kw):\n    r = litellm.completion(model=model, messages=messages, temperature=0, **kw)\n    return r.choices[0].message.content, r.usage\n\ndef passes_check(question, answer):\n    try:                                              # 1) deterministic gate: valid JSON, required fields\n        obj = json.loads(answer)\n        if not isinstance(obj, dict) or not {\"answer\", \"confidence\"} <= obj.keys():\n            return False\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        return False\n    verdict, _ = ask(JUDGE, [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":   # 2) cheap judge, only after the gate passes\n        f\"Question: {question}\\nAnswer: {obj['answer']}\\n\"\n        \"Is the answer complete and correct? Reply PASS or FAIL only.\"}], max_tokens=2)\n    return verdict.strip().upper().startswith(\"PASS\")\n\ndef cascade(question):\n    msgs = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": 'Reply as JSON: {\"answer\": str, \"confidence\": number 0-1}'},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    answer, usage = ask(SMALL, msgs)\n    if passes_check(question, answer):\n        return {\"model\": SMALL, \"answer\": answer, \"escalated\": False, \"usage\": usage}\n    answer, usage = ask(LARGE, msgs)                  # escalate: same prompt, stronger model\n    return {\"model\": LARGE, \"answer\": answer, \"escalated\": True, \"usage\": usage}\n\nprint(cascade(\"Our invoice says net-45 but the signed contract says net-30. Which applies, and why?\"))\n# Emit `escalated`, both usages and the judge verdict as metrics; alert when the escalation rate jumps.\n```\n\n- **Gate first** *because* it is free and catches the most common failure (unparseable output); the judge\n  only spends tokens on well-formed answers.\n- **Judge is a different model** from the small generator: LLMs show self-preference bias when grading\n  their own outputs, so a same-model judge under-escalates.\n- **Calibrate the judge** against human labels before its verdicts drive production routing.\n\n### Threshold and saving calculator\nPure Python; plug in your own unit costs.\n\n```python\ndef route_threshold(c_small, c_large, miss_cost):\n    \"\"\"Route small when P(small answer adequate) exceeds this.\"\"\"\n    return 1 - (c_large - c_small) / miss_cost\n\ndef cascade_cost(c_small, c_check, c_large, p_escalate):\n    return c_small + c_check + p_escalate * c_large\n\nfor miss in (50, 500):\n    print(f\"miss costs {miss:>3}: route small when p > {route_threshold(1, 15, miss):.3f}\")\n# miss costs  50: route small when p > 0.720\n# miss costs 500: route small when p > 0.972\n\nfor p in (0.10, 0.25, 0.40, 0.60):\n    cost = cascade_cost(1, 0.5, 15, p)\n    print(f\"escalate {p:.0%}: {cost:5.2f} units, saving {1 - cost / 15:.0%} vs always-large\")\n# 10%: 3.00 (80%)   25%: 5.25 (65%)   40%: 7.50 (50%)   60%: 10.50 (30%)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: mean cost falls, p95 rises\nIllustrative latencies: small 0.8 s, judge 0.4 s, large 3.0 s; 25% escalate.\n\n| Path | Share | Latency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Always-large | 100% | 3.0 s |\n| Cascade, passes | 75% | 0.8 + 0.4 = 1.2 s |\n| Cascade, escalates | 25% | 0.8 + 0.4 + 3.0 = 4.2 s |\n| Cascade mean | | 0.75 × 1.2 + 0.25 × 4.2 = 1.95 s |\n| Cascade p95 | | 4.2 s, because more than 5% of requests escalate |\n\nMean latency and cost both drop, but the tail is 40% slower than always-large. For a strict p95 product,\nroute instead.","patterns":["**Route the obvious, cascade the band.** When you have some labels, send high-confidence easy requests straight to small and high-confidence hard ones straight to large; cascade only the middle band of router scores. Monitor the share of traffic in each band and per-band judged quality.","**Deterministic baseline slice.** Serve a few per cent of requests on the strong model regardless of route, chosen by a stable hash so it is replayable: for example `int(hashlib.sha256(conv_id.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100 < 3`, not Python's per-process salted `hash()`. Hashing the conversation id keeps a whole conversation on one model. Monitor the quality gap between the slice and the routed traffic, per route.","**Escalation-rate budget.** Alert when the escalation rate over a rolling window leaves its normal band, and cap large-model spend per hour so a small-model regression cannot become an unbounded bill. Monitor escalation rate, large-model 429s and cost per successful task.","**Thresholds as versioned config.** Keep per-feature thresholds in config, not code, so a change is a reviewed config edit with a rollback. Monitor misroute rate after each change against the eval set.","**Log route, reason and scores on every request.** Emit model chosen, router score, check result and escalated flag as structured fields, so a complaint can be traced to the decision that produced it. Monitor quality and latency sliced by these fields.","**Retrain the router on fresh labels.** Rebuild the labelled set from recent traffic after any prompt-template or model change, because routers trained on old prompts drift. Monitor misroute rate on a held-out slice of recent traffic."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request plus metadata\"] --> RT{\"Router score, cheap and on every request\"}\n    RT -->|\"clearly easy\"| SM[\"Small model\"]\n    RT -->|\"clearly hard\"| LG[\"Large model\"]\n    RT -->|\"ambiguous band\"| CS[\"Small model attempt\"]\n    CS --> G{\"Deterministic gate: JSON parses, fields present\"}\n    G -->|\"fail\"| LG\n    G -->|\"pass\"| J{\"Cheap judge from a different model\"}\n    J -->|\"FAIL, escalate\"| LG\n    J -->|\"PASS\"| OUT[\"Answer\"]\n    SM --> OUT\n    LG --> OUT\n    OUT -.->|\"log route, score, check result, escalated flag\"| M[(\"Metrics and per-route evals\")]","caption":"The combined pattern: a cheap router sends obvious cases straight to one model, and only the ambiguous\nmiddle band pays for the small call, the check and, on failure, the large call in series."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not just put every request on the strongest model and skip routing?","a":"- **Cost scales linearly with success.** Small versus frontier is commonly one to two orders of magnitude per\n  token (illustrative), so paying frontier prices for lookups, reformulations and yes/no classifications is\n  pure waste.\n- **Latency becomes the product's ceiling** too, because the latency gap is several-fold. Routing exists\n  because difficulty varies per request, even within one feature."},{"q":"How would you derive the routing threshold for a feature where a wrong answer causes a billing dispute?","a":"- **Write the expected cost:** route small when `c_small + (1 − p) × L < c_large`, so the threshold is\n  `p > 1 − (c_large − c_small) / L`.\n- **Plug in the miss cost:** with units 1, 15 and a miss cost of 500, only requests with p above 0.972 go\n  small, versus 0.72 when a miss costs 50.\n- **So thresholds are per feature,** because they encode the business cost of a miss, not router accuracy."},{"q":"When would you choose routing over cascading?","a":"- **When p95 latency is strict** (voice, autocomplete), because escalated requests in a cascade pay the small\n  call, the check and the large call in series.\n- **When you already have labelled data** from offline evals to train a classifier.\n- **Choose cascading instead** when there is no training data but a reliable cheap check exists, such as\n  schema validation or SQL execution."},{"q":"Escalations jumped from 25% to 90% overnight with no deploy. What happened and what do you do?","a":"- **Likely causes:** the small provider silently updated or degraded the model, or a prompt-template change\n  broke its JSON output so the deterministic gate fails.\n- **Contain first:** the escalation-rate alert fires and the hourly cap on large-model spend stops an\n  unbounded bill; watch large-model 429s.\n- **Then diagnose** with logged route, gate result and judge verdict per request, and compare against the\n  continuous baseline slice served by the strong model."},{"q":"How do you keep a cascade's judge honest?","a":"- **Use a different model from the generator,** because LLMs show self-preference bias when grading their\n  own outputs, so a same-model judge under-escalates.\n- **Calibrate against human labels** before its verdicts drive routing, and re-check periodically.\n- **Watch for verbosity bias:** a judge that favours long answers lets bad small-model answers through, so\n  judge calibration belongs in the system's eval."}],"tradeoffs":["**Lower mean cost vs higher tail latency.** Escalated requests pay in series. This flips when the product has a strict p95 (voice, autocomplete), where routing or hedging beats cascading.","**Training data vs a cheap check.** Routing needs labelled data and a classifier you must retrain; cascading needs only a reliable cheap check. Which is cheaper depends on whether you already have an eval set with judgments.","**Per-request choice vs consistency.** The same question can get different answers; fine for chat, a problem for reproducible or audited workflows where one pinned model is required.","**Savings vs easy share.** Savings scale with the share of easy traffic; a uniformly hard workload yields little and adds a moving part, so measure the difficulty distribution before building.","**The check bounds quality.** A judge that favours verbose answers or misses subtle errors lets bad small-model answers through, so judge calibration is part of the system's eval."],"pitfalls":["**Self-reported confidence as the escalation signal.** Symptom: escalation rate near zero while judged quality on the small route is poor. Fix: use an external check.","**Measuring only aggregate quality.** Symptom: a healthy dashboard metric while complaints concentrate in the hard tail that was misrouted or wrongly passed. Fix: per-route evals.","**No continuous baseline.** Symptom: quality drift nobody notices for weeks after a provider silently updates the small model, because nothing compares it with the strong one.","**Escalation storms.** A small-model outage or regression sends everything to the large model. Symptom: 429s and a cost spike on the large model during an incident that was not about it.","**Unbounded cascades** (retry small with a new prompt, then medium, then large). Symptom: p99 latency several times the single-call latency and users abandoning before the answer arrives.","**A router trained on last quarter's prompts.** Symptom: a misroute rate that creeps up after a prompt-template change with no code change in the router."],"when":"- **Use when** traffic has a visible easy/hard split and cost or latency is a product KPI.\n- **Avoid when** features are low-volume or uniformly hard; a single pinned model is simpler and more\n  consistent.\n- **Before building,** sample traffic, run both models and judge the results. That single experiment gives\n  the easy share, router labels and a realistic saving.\n- **Measure:** escalation rate, quality per route, cost per successful task and latency per route.\n- **Interviews:** derive the expected-cost formula, name the latency penalty, and explain where the\n  quality signal comes from and why self-reported confidence is not it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the router or the check, logs the route and reason on every request, builds the per-route eval, and tunes thresholds against cost and judge scores.","architect":"Decides where routing lives (gateway versus application), the quality floor, budget guardrails on escalation, and the consistency policy for audited flows.","fde":"Tunes thresholds to a customer's actual traffic mix, explains why two similar questions used different models, and shows cost per resolved task before and after."},"check":{"q":"You are asked to cut LLM spend on a support assistant by half without hurting customer\nsatisfaction. Design a routing or cascading approach and explain how you would prove it did no harm.","a":"Start with data: sample a few thousand recent requests, answer each with both the small and the\nlarge model, and have a judge plus a human sample label whether the small answer was adequate. That gives\nthe easy share, which bounds the achievable saving, and labelled data for a router. Then do the\narithmetic: with unit costs of 1 for the small model, 0.5 for the check and 15 for the large one, halving\nspend means 1.5 + 15 × p_escalate ≤ 7.5, so at most 40% may escalate, which needs an easy share of at\nleast 60% even with a perfect check. If the measured share clears that with margin, a cascade with a\ndeterministic gate plus a cheap judge (or a trained router if p95 latency is tight) can reach the\ntarget; if it does not, no router will, and the conversation moves to prompt length or caching. To prove no harm: offline eval per route on the labelled set; an online A/B against the\nall-large baseline on resolution rate and satisfaction; a permanent few-per-cent shadow slice served by\nthe large model to track the gap continuously; and alerts on escalation rate and judge disagreement. Also\ncheck tail latency per route and that answer consistency is acceptable for the product."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the essential difference between routing and cascading?","options":["Routing uses a classifier, cascading uses rules","Routing chooses the model before any call; cascading calls a cheap model first and escalates on a failed quality check","Routing saves cost, cascading saves latency","Routing requires a gateway, cascading runs inside the application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both can live anywhere and use any decision mechanism. The structural difference is timing: a router predicts difficulty up front and pays prediction error; a cascade observes a real answer and pays serial latency and the cost of the check."},{"q":"Small model costs 1 unit, the check 0.5 and the large model 12 units; 30% of requests escalate. Expected cost per request?","options":["4.1 units","5.1 units","13.5 units","3.6 units"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expected cost is small + check + escalation share × large = 1 + 0.5 + 0.3 × 12 = 5.1. Forgetting the check gives 4.1; adding every component as if all ran every time gives 13.5; 3.6 is only the escalated portion."},{"q":"Which escalation signal is least reliable for a cascade?","options":["The answer fails JSON schema validation","Several sampled answers disagree with each other","The small model's own stated confidence is below a threshold","Generated SQL fails to execute against the schema"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Models are poorly calibrated about their own correctness, so stated confidence is often high on wrong answers. Schema checks, execution and sample agreement are external signals that do not depend on the model knowing when it is wrong."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-model-architecture","sources":[],"why":"- **Production requests pass through many jobs.** Safety classifier, PII redaction, query rewriting,\n  embedding, retrieval, reranking, generation, structured extraction, perhaps a judge, sometimes speech or\n  vision. Early LLM apps were one prompt and one model; production systems are not.\n- **No single model is best at every job,** and the best model for each job is rarely the cheapest. Pushing\n  everything through one frontier generator fails three ways:\n  - **Cost:** embedding a hundred thousand chunks through a generative model is nonsense when an embedding\n    model does it for a tiny fraction of the price.\n  - **Capability:** a chat model does not emit vectors, and a prompted generalist ranks query–document pairs\n    worse and far more expensively than a cross-encoder trained for exactly that.\n  - **Latency:** every extra job on the critical path is another multi-second call.\n- **The opposite naive design fails on operability.** \"Best model for every job from whichever vendor,\n  wired up ad hoc\": each vendor brings its own authentication, limits, failure modes and tokeniser; nobody\n  knows which versions are in production; and when quality drops nobody can tell which stage regressed.\n- **What the architecture buys:** specialisation while the system stays one thing you can reason about: a\n  governed portfolio of models, stage contracts, shared telemetry, and evaluation per stage *and* end to\n  end.","how":"### Decompose by job, not by vendor\nFor each job a request needs, record output type (vector, label, score, free text, structured object),\nquality sensitivity, volume, latency budget and data sensitivity. The mapping mostly writes itself:\n\n| Job shape | Model class |\n|---|---|\n| High-volume, narrow output | Small specialised model, often self-hosted |\n| Low-volume, open-ended generation | Frontier model |\n| Vectors | Embedding model |\n| Pairwise relevance | Cross-encoder reranker |\n| Safety screening on every request | Fast classifier; a larger model only for ambiguous cases |\n\nRouting within a job (see model routing / cascading) is orthogonal to decomposition across jobs.\n\n### Patterns of composition\n1. **Pipeline.** Guard → rewrite → embed → retrieve → rerank → generate → verify.\n   - Independent stages (guard and embed) run concurrently.\n   - A gate that almost always passes can run *speculatively*: start retrieval, or even generation, while\n     the guard decides, and discard the work if it blocks.\n   - You pay wasted calls on the few blocked requests to remove the guard's latency from every other one.\n   - Unsafe only if the speculative stage has side effects or streams to the user before the gate passes.\n2. **Generator plus verifier.** A second model checks grounding, schema or policy. Inline when it gates the\n   response; sampled and asynchronous when it feeds monitoring.\n3. **Planner plus executors.** A strong model decomposes a task and cheap models carry out subtasks; the\n   backbone of most agent designs.\n4. **Ensemble.** Several models answer and a selector or vote picks. Expensive; reserved for high-stakes\n   decisions where the extra calls are cheaper than a mistake.\n\n### Contracts between stages\n- **Typed, versioned schemas:** each stage's input and output is a Pydantic or Zod model, not free text.\n- **Model id and version in the trace** for every stage.\n- **Why it is not bureaucracy:** the next stage was tuned against *this* stage's output distribution.\n  - Swap the query rewriter and the embedder sees different text.\n  - Swap the embedding model and every stored vector is in a different space.\n  - Swap the generator and the citation format the verifier parses may change.\n- **Typed contracts make drift detectable** at the boundary instead of three stages downstream; versions\n  make it attributable.\n\n### Budgets compose, tails do not average\n- **Latency adds along the serial path,** so parallelise what you can and budget with tail numbers.\n- **Bounds:** the p99 of a sum of four stages is at least the largest single-stage p99 and can exceed the\n  sum of all four p99s, because tail events in different stages combine. The sum of the p99s is only\n  guaranteed to be at least the p96 of the total, by a union bound over the four stages.\n- **Independent slowness:** the sum of the p99s is a pessimistic budget, because tails rarely coincide.\n- **Correlated slowness** (two stages on one GPU pool or one provider, a retry policy at every stage, a\n  shared overloaded network path) drives the real p99 towards or past that sum.\n- **Rule:** budget with the sum, then measure the end-to-end distribution.\n- **Cost adds per request.** Find the dominant stage: usually generation, sometimes a reranker scoring 50\n  candidates with a large cross-encoder.\n- **Failure is per stage.** Each needs its own timeout, fallback and degraded mode (drop the reranker,\n  skip the judge).\n\n### Evaluate per stage and end to end\n| Level | Examples | Tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| Component | recall@k for retrieval, precision for the guard, schema pass rate for extraction | *Which* stage moved |\n| End task | Task success, judged answer quality | Whether it mattered |\n\n- **You need both** *because* a stage can improve on its own metric and hurt the task: a reranker that\n  prefers long passages raises nDCG and pushes the generator past its useful context.\n- **Any model change** ships behind a flag and a rerun of both evaluations.\n\n### Run it as a portfolio\n- **A registry** of approved models with versions, owners and deprecation dates.\n- **A gateway as the single egress,** so cost and usage are attributable per stage.\n- **A stage upgrade is a release,** with a canary, not a config edit.","example":"### Typed pipeline skeleton with a trace\nEach stage names its model and records timing and version; independent stages run concurrently; the judge\nruns off the hot path on a sample. `classify`, `embed`, `rerank`, `generate`, `judge` and `vector_search`\nare thin adapters around whatever providers or servers you use.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time, random\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nMODELS = {\"guard\": \"safety-classifier-v3\", \"embed\": \"text-embed-v2\",\n          \"rerank\": \"cross-encoder-v1\", \"generate\": \"frontier-chat-2026-06\", \"judge\": \"small-chat\"}\nBACKGROUND = set()                 # references to fire-and-forget tasks (asyncio only keeps weak ones)\n\n@dataclass\nclass Trace:                       # one record per request: which model served which stage, how long\n    stages: list = field(default_factory=list)\n    def add(self, stage, model, t0):\n        self.stages.append({\"stage\": stage, \"model\": model, \"ms\": round((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000)})\n\nasync def stage(name, fn, trace, **kw):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    out = await fn(MODELS[name], **kw)              # every adapter takes the model id as its first argument\n    trace.add(name, MODELS[name], t0)\n    return out\n\nasync def answer(question):\n    trace = Trace()\n    verdict, qvec = await asyncio.gather(          # guard and embedding are independent: run concurrently\n        stage(\"guard\", classify, trace, text=question),\n        stage(\"embed\", embed, trace, text=question))\n    if verdict != \"allow\":\n        return {\"answer\": \"I can't help with that request.\", \"trace\": trace.stages}\n    candidates = await vector_search(qvec, k=50)                                 # a database call, not a model\n    top = await stage(\"rerank\", rerank, trace, query=question, docs=candidates, k=5)\n    draft = await stage(\"generate\", generate, trace, question=question, context=top)\n    if random.random() < 0.05:                                                   # sampled judge, off the hot path\n        t = asyncio.create_task(stage(\"judge\", judge, trace, question=question, answer=draft, context=top))\n        BACKGROUND.add(t); t.add_done_callback(BACKGROUND.discard)              # keep a reference or it may be GC'd\n    return {\"answer\": draft, \"sources\": [d.id for d in top], \"trace\": trace.stages}\n```\n\n- **The trace makes it debuggable:** group judge scores by the `generate` model version, or latency by\n  stage, instead of guessing.\n- **Emit the trace late:** the sampled judge appends *after* the response returns, so emit the trace when\n  background work finishes, not at return.\n\n### Worked latency budget\nIllustrative p95s, read off the structure:\n\n| Stage | p95 | On the serial path? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Guard | 80 ms | Yes (parallel with embed, the longer of the two) |\n| Embed | 40 ms | No, hidden behind guard: concurrency saved these 40 ms |\n| Vector search | 30 ms | Yes |\n| Rerank | 150 ms | Yes |\n| Generation | 2.5 s | Yes, nine-tenths of the total |\n| **Serial budget** | 80 + 30 + 150 + 2,500 ≈ 2.8 s | |\n\n- **Stream the generator first.** It moves the user-visible number to time-to-first-token and does more for\n  perceived latency than any change to the other stages.\n- **Cost usually has the same shape,** until someone raises the rerank candidate count from 50 to 500 and\n  the reranker becomes the dominant line.\n\n### Why correlated slowness breaks the budget\nA runnable simulation: four stages at 200 ms that occasionally take 1,500 ms.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef p(xs, q):\n    xs = sorted(xs); return xs[int(q * (len(xs) - 1))]\n\ndef stage(slow):                              # 200 ms normally, 1,500 ms when slow\n    return random.uniform(180, 220) + (1300 if slow else 0)\n\nrandom.seed(0)\nN, P_SLOW, STAGES = 100_000, 0.02, 4\nindep, corr = [], []\nfor _ in range(N):\n    indep.append(sum(stage(random.random() < P_SLOW) for _ in range(STAGES)))  # independent causes\n    shared = random.random() < P_SLOW                                           # one shared GPU pool\n    corr.append(sum(stage(shared) for _ in range(STAGES)))\n\none = [stage(random.random() < P_SLOW) for _ in range(N)]\nprint(f\"single-stage p99 ~ {p(one, .99):,.0f} ms; sum of 4 p99s ~ {4 * p(one, .99):,.0f} ms\")\nprint(f\"end-to-end p99, independent slowness: {p(indep, .99):,.0f} ms\")\nprint(f\"end-to-end p99, correlated slowness:  {p(corr, .99):,.0f} ms\")\n# single-stage p99 ~ 1,499 ms; sum of 4 p99s ~ 5,997 ms\n# independent: ~2,130 ms (tails rarely coincide); correlated: ~6,000 ms (the full sum)\n```","patterns":["**Model registry checked in CI.** Keep one file listing every stage's approved model id, version, owner and deprecation date; fail the build if code references a model not in it. Monitor days until each model's deprecation and the number of stages on unregistered or retired versions.","**Single egress with stage tags.** Send every model call through the gateway with the stage name and pipeline version attached as request metadata, so spend and errors split by stage. Monitor cost per stage per request and which stage dominates.","**Re-index behind an alias on embedder change.** Build a new index with the new embedding model, compare recall@k on the golden set, then switch a read alias in one step; never mix query and document vectors from different models. Monitor recall@k and the share of documents re-embedded.","**Stage upgrades as canaries.** Put each stage's model id behind a flag, send a small share of traffic to the new version, and compare both the component metric and the end-task metric before widening. Monitor the two metrics side by side; divergence means hidden coupling.","**Per-stage timeout with a degraded mode.** Give optional stages a hard timeout and a defined fallback (rerank times out, so keep vector-search order; the judge is skipped). Monitor degraded-mode rate per stage; a rising rate is an incident before users notice.","**Sampled asynchronous judge.** Judge a fixed share (for example 5%) of production answers off the hot path and group scores by each stage's model version. Monitor judge score per version and judge agreement with periodic human labels."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"User question\"] --> G[\"Guard: fast safety classifier\"]\n    Q --> E[\"Embed: embedding model\"]\n    G -->|\"verdict allow or block\"| GATE{\"Allowed?\"}\n    E -->|\"query vector\"| GATE\n    GATE -->|\"block\"| REF[\"Refusal\"]\n    GATE -->|\"allow\"| VS[(\"Vector search, k=50\")]\n    VS -->|\"50 candidates\"| RR[\"Rerank: cross-encoder, top 5\"]\n    RR -->|\"typed passages\"| GEN[\"Generate: frontier model\"]\n    GEN --> A[\"Answer plus sources\"]\n    GEN -.->|\"5% sample, async\"| J[\"Judge: small model\"]\n    J -.->|\"scores by model version\"| T[(\"Trace and per-stage metrics\")]","caption":"A typical RAG pipeline as stages, matching the code below: guard and embed run concurrently and join at\nthe gate, each later stage depends on the previous one's typed output, and the sampled judge sits off the\nhot path."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not run every job in the pipeline through one frontier model?","a":"- **Cost:** embedding a hundred thousand chunks through a generative model costs far more than an\n  embedding model built for it.\n- **Capability:** a chat model does not emit vectors, and a prompted generalist ranks query–document pairs\n  worse and more expensively than a cross-encoder.\n- **Latency:** every extra job on the critical path is another multi-second call, so specialised small\n  models keep the serial path short."},{"q":"Design the model stages for a support assistant with retrieval and a safety check.","a":"- **Decompose by job:** guard (fast classifier), embed (embedding model), retrieve (vector search), rerank\n  (cross-encoder), generate (frontier model), sampled judge (small model, asynchronous).\n- **Compose:** run guard and embed concurrently, or start retrieval speculatively while the guard decides,\n  since retrieval has no side effects.\n- **Contracts:** typed, versioned schemas between stages, with model id and version in the trace for every\n  stage."},{"q":"How would you budget end-to-end latency across four serial stages?","a":"- **Budget with the sum of the stage tail numbers,** then measure the end-to-end distribution, because\n  tails do not average.\n- **Correlated slowness matters:** stages sharing one GPU pool or provider, or retries at every stage, push\n  the real p99 towards or past that sum.\n- **Find the dominant stage:** usually generation, so stream it to move the user-visible number to\n  time-to-first-token."},{"q":"When would you deliberately avoid adding another model stage?","a":"- **When there is no measured gain** on the end-task metric, because each stage is a dependency with its own\n  failure mode, timeout and version to govern.\n- **For low-volume products,** where one strong model's simplicity can win outright.\n- **In regulated settings,** where each new vendor needs a security review and a data-processing agreement,\n  so fewer vendors is the better trade."},{"q":"You must change the embedding model in production. What is your rollout plan?","a":"- **Re-index, never mix spaces:** query vectors and stored document vectors from different models make\n  recall collapse.\n- **Build the new index alongside,** compare recall@k on the golden set, then run the end-task eval, since a\n  better component metric can still hurt the task.\n- **Switch a read alias in one step** behind a flag and canary, and keep the old index until the end-task\n  metric holds."}],"tradeoffs":["**Right-sized models vs moving parts.** Each stage gets a right-sized model for quality and cost, but there are more vendors and versions to manage; for low-volume products one strong model's simplicity can win outright.","**Independent upgrades vs hidden coupling.** A swap is safe only with an end-to-end eval; independence evaporates when stages have been tuned to each other's quirks.","**Concurrency vs burst cost.** Running independent stages concurrently cuts latency, but raises peak concurrency and cost during bursts, and complicates per-stage rate-limit budgeting.","**Vendor diversity vs compliance load.** Best-of-breed quality and resilience, but every new processor needs a security review and a data-processing agreement; in regulated settings fewer vendors is the better trade.","**Inline verifier vs sampled judge.** Inline raises the quality floor on every request at a latency and cost premium; a sampled asynchronous judge is nearly free but only catches problems after users have seen them."],"pitfalls":["**Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing.** Symptom: retrieval recall collapses because query vectors and stored document vectors now live in different spaces.","**Budgeting latency with averages.** Symptom: an end-to-end p99 far above the sum of stage p50s while every stage looks healthy on its own dashboard.","**No per-stage telemetry.** Symptom: \"the answers got worse\" with no way to tell whether the rewriter, retriever, reranker or generator regressed.","**Free text between stages instead of typed objects.** Symptom: parse failures after a model update and prompt-injection text riding from one stage into the next.","**An LLM judge inline on all traffic.** Symptom: cost roughly doubling and latency rising with no gain the product can show, when a 5% sample would have caught the same regressions.","**Treating two vendors' \"same\" capability as interchangeable.** Symptom: dimension mismatches, silent truncation at a different token limit, or changed tool-call formats after a swap."],"when":"- **Always, explicitly:** any system beyond a prototype is already multi-model; the question is whether\n  the decomposition is explicit.\n- **Make it explicit when** you have more than one model class in the path, or more than one team touching\n  the pipeline.\n- **Measure:** cost and latency per stage (p50, p95, p99), each stage's component metric and the end-task\n  metric; alert on divergence between them.\n- **Avoid** adding a stage without a measured gain; each one is a dependency with its own failure mode.\n- **Interviews:** draw the pipeline, name the model class and budget per stage, and explain how you would\n  localise a regression after an upgrade. That coupling-and-eval story distinguishes architecture from a\n  diagram.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements typed stage adapters, records model versions in traces, writes component evals and makes sure independent stages actually run concurrently.","architect":"Owns the portfolio: which model classes are approved for which jobs, the version and deprecation policy, and how the latency and cost budget is split across stages.","fde":"Assembles the pipeline from models the customer has already approved, often under procurement limits, and explains why the reranker and the generator come from different vendors."},"check":{"q":"A RAG product swapped its generator for a newer model that benchmarks higher, and answer quality\ndropped. How does thinking in stages explain this, and how would you diagnose it?","a":"The generator is one stage whose prompt and context handling were tuned against the old model's\nbehaviour: citation format, response length, how it treated conflicting passages, how literally it\nfollowed the system prompt. First confirm the other stages did not move: retrieval recall@5 and reranker\nscores on the golden set should be unchanged, which isolates the regression to generation. Then compare\njudge scores old versus new on the same fixed set, check structured-output and citation parse failure\nrates, check for context truncation from a different tokeniser or limit, and read a sample of\ndisagreements. The fix is usually prompt re-tuning plus an end-to-end eval before a canary rollout. The\nlesson is procedural: a stage upgrade is a release that needs per-stage telemetry, end-to-end evaluation\nand a feature flag, regardless of what the public benchmark says."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do multi-model systems need typed, versioned contracts between stages?","options":["Because providers require JSON between calls","Because each stage's output is the next stage's input distribution, so drift must be detectable at the boundary and attributable to a version","Because typed contracts reduce token usage","Because it allows all stages to share one model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The coupling between stages is the central risk of the pattern. Schemas catch shape changes immediately and versions tell you which upgrade caused a downstream regression; neither has anything to do with provider requirements or token counts."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to rerank with a cross-encoder rather than asking the generator to rank candidates?","options":["Generators cannot read more than five documents","A cross-encoder scores query–document pairs jointly, cheaply and well, and leaves the generator's context for answering","Rerankers remove the need for embeddings entirely","Rerankers are always more accurate than generators at every task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specialisation is the point: the cross-encoder is trained for pairwise relevance and costs a fraction per pair, so the expensive model sees only the best few passages. Embeddings are still needed for the first-stage retrieval."},{"q":"Four serial model stages each have p50 = 200 ms and p99 = 1 s. What can you say about the end-to-end p99?","options":["It is 800 ms, the sum of the p50s","It is 1 s, since the slowest stage dominates","It is exactly 4 s, the sum of the p99s","It is at least 1 s, may exceed 4 s, and cannot be derived from the p50s; measure it end to end"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Latencies add pointwise, so the total is at least as slow as any stage. Tail events in different stages combine: the total can only exceed the sum of the p99s when at least one stage exceeds its own p99, which by a union bound happens on at most about 4% of requests, so the sum of the p99s is guaranteed only to be at least the p96 of the total, and the true p99 can be higher still."}]},{"key":"system-design/ai-gateway","sources":["Official docs: LiteLLM","Official docs: Portkey"],"why":"- **Without one, every team re-implements the same plumbing.** The second team copies the first team's\n  retry loop, pastes the same provider key into its own secret store, and spends against the same\n  organisation quota with no coordination.\n- **By the fifth team nobody can answer basic questions:** what feature X costs per month, which models are\n  in production, what data goes to which vendor, how to switch everything off if a key leaks or a provider\n  has an incident. Rate limits hit unpredictably because five services share one quota, and a provider\n  migration touches every codebase.\n- **A shared client library cannot *enforce* anything.** Teams pin old versions, polyglot stacks need one\n  library per language, keys still live in every service, and org-wide rate-limit and budget coordination\n  needs shared state a library does not have.\n- **It is the API gateway idea with model concerns added:** token accounting, model aliasing, provider\n  translation, streaming passthrough, prompt logging and guardrails.\n- **Policy moves to a network boundary,** where it applies to every caller whether or not they remembered.","how":"### The request path\n1. A client sends an OpenAI-compatible request (`/v1/chat/completions`, embeddings and so on) with a\n   *virtual* key.\n2. The gateway authenticates the key.\n3. It resolves the requested model alias to one or more deployments.\n4. It applies policy: budget remaining, requests and tokens per minute, guardrails.\n5. It picks a deployment by its routing strategy.\n6. It translates to the provider's native API and streams the response back.\n7. It records usage (tokens, computed cost, latency, key, tags) for logging and export.\n\nThe API is OpenAI-shaped *because* most SDKs already speak it, so adoption is changing a base URL and a key.\n\n### LiteLLM proxy vs Portkey\n| | LiteLLM proxy | Portkey |\n|---|---|---|\n| What | Open-source Python server (`litellm --config config.yaml`) plus a matching in-process `Router` class | Hosted gateway; the core gateway is also open source, written in TypeScript |\n| Behaviour defined in | YAML config file you run yourself | Declarative JSON configs: a `strategy` (fallback or load-balance) over `targets` |\n| Credentials | Keys referenced as `os.environ/VAR`; virtual keys per key, team or user | Provider credentials in Portkey's vault; clients reference them by an id (virtual keys, in Portkey's terms) |\n| State | Postgres for virtual keys, budgets and rate limits; Redis for rate-limit counters, cache entries and cooldowns across replicas | Managed by Portkey |\n| Per-target options | Retries, fallbacks, cooldowns in `router_settings` | Each target has its own virtual key and optional retry, cache (exact or semantic) and timeout settings |\n| Client side | OpenAI SDK pointed at the proxy | Config id or inline config in request headers |\n| Observability | Logging callbacks to tracing backends | Traces, cost and metadata tags in its dashboards; guardrails attach to configs |\n| Compliance | Prompts stay in your network | A third-party processor in your data path, unless you deploy the gateway in your own environment |\n\n**LiteLLM config sections:**\n- `model_list`: deployments, each an alias name plus `litellm_params` with provider and model.\n- `router_settings`: routing strategy, retries, fallbacks, cooldowns for failing deployments.\n- `litellm_settings`: caching, logging callbacks to tracing backends.\n- `general_settings`: the master key, the database.\n- **The same alias can point at several deployments,** which is how load-balancing and same-model failover\n  work.\n\n### Enforcing budgets and rate limits\nBudgets and limits are counters in shared state. LLM traffic makes them awkward because a request's cost is\nnot known until it finishes: the model decides the output length.\n\n1. **Check *before* the call:** requests per minute, and tokens per minute from the prompt's token count\n   plus `max_tokens` or an estimate.\n2. **Charge actual usage *after*** the response completes.\n\nTwo consequences:\n- **Budgets overshoot by whatever is in flight** when they run out, so a hard cap needs headroom or a\n  per-request ceiling enforced through `max_tokens`.\n- **Counters must live in Redis or similar** with several replicas, not in each process. Otherwise every\n  replica enforces the full limit on its own and the effective limit is multiplied by the replica count.\n\n### Routing among deployments\n- **Strategies:** LiteLLM offers simple shuffle, least-busy, latency-based and usage-based routing, among\n  others.\n- **Cooldowns:** a deployment that fails repeatedly is skipped for a period: a circuit breaker under another\n  name.\n- **Blind to quality:** these strategies only see what the gateway sees, latency and errors, never answer\n  quality.\n\n### Alternatives\n- **API-gateway vendors with AI plugins** (Kong AI Gateway).\n- **CDN-hosted gateways** (Cloudflare AI Gateway).\n- **Envoy AI Gateway,** built on Envoy, for teams already running it on Kubernetes.\n- **The choice axes** are the same as LiteLLM versus Portkey: where prompts flow, who operates it, which\n  providers and provider-specific features pass through intact, and fit with traffic infrastructure you\n  already run.\n- **The light end:** an in-process library such as the LiteLLM SDK's `Router` gives the same routing and\n  fallbacks with no extra hop, but no central enforcement: every service holds keys again and budgets are\n  per process.\n\n### What it centralises\n- Key custody.\n- Budgets and quotas per team or customer.\n- Rate-limit coordination.\n- Retries and fallbacks.\n- Caching.\n- PII redaction and guardrails.\n- Logging and cost attribution via tags.\n- **Model aliases:** applications call `fast` or `strong` while the platform team decides what those\n  resolve to, so migrations and deprecations become configuration changes rather than code changes.\n\n### Sharp edges\n- **Lowest-common-denominator API.** Provider-specific features (prompt-cache controls, extended reasoning\n  parameters, tool-call nuances, vision input formats) lag or are dropped unless passthrough is verified.\n- **A new single point of failure and a new hop.** Run several replicas per region and keep it close to\n  callers.\n- **Decide the database failure mode.** When its Postgres or Redis is unavailable: fail open on budget\n  checks, or fail closed and take every AI feature down with the database.\n- **Streaming must be forwarded without buffering,** or time-to-first-token suffers.\n- **Token counts across tokenisers are approximations.**\n- **Gateway logs contain prompts,** so they need a retention policy.\n- **Someone must own the config,** or aliases rot and fallbacks go untested.","example":"### LiteLLM config and the application call\nTwo deployments behind one alias, a stronger fallback, caching and logging. The application only needs the\nOpenAI SDK pointed at the proxy. Replace the model ids with current ones for your providers.\n\n```yaml\n# config.yaml: applications call \"fast\" or \"strong\"; the platform team decides what those mean\nmodel_list:\n  - model_name: fast\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/gpt-4o-mini\n      api_key: os.environ/OPENAI_API_KEY\n  - model_name: fast                          # second deployment of the same alias shares the traffic\n    litellm_params:\n      model: azure/gpt-4o-mini-eu             # azure/<your-deployment-name>\n      api_base: os.environ/AZURE_EU_BASE\n      api_key: os.environ/AZURE_EU_KEY\n  - model_name: strong\n    litellm_params:\n      model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\n      api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\nrouter_settings:\n  routing_strategy: simple-shuffle\n  num_retries: 2\n  fallbacks: [{\"fast\": [\"strong\"]}]           # when every \"fast\" deployment fails, try \"strong\"\nlitellm_settings:\n  cache: true                                 # exact-match response cache (Redis when configured)\n  success_callback: [\"langfuse\"]              # usage and cost per request to your tracing backend\ngeneral_settings:\n  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY\n  database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL       # virtual keys, teams and budgets live here\n```\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://litellm.internal:4000/v1\", api_key=\"sk-<support-team-virtual-key>\")\nr = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"fast\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise ticket #4821 in two lines.\"}],\n    extra_body={\"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"support-bot\", \"tenant:acme\"]}})   # tags drive cost attribution\nprint(r.choices[0].message.content)\n```\n\n- **The application never sees a provider key.**\n- **Moving `fast` to a different model** is a config change and a canary, not a deploy of every service.\n\n### Portkey config: fallback with retries\nThe same idea declared as a Portkey config. Check field names against the current Portkey docs; the shape\nis a `strategy` over `targets`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"strategy\": { \"mode\": \"fallback\" },\n  \"retry\": { \"attempts\": 2 },\n  \"targets\": [\n    { \"virtual_key\": \"openai-prod\", \"override_params\": { \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\" } },\n    { \"virtual_key\": \"anthropic-prod\", \"override_params\": { \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-4-20250514\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- **Fallback mode** tries targets in order; a load-balance strategy spreads traffic across targets instead.\n- **The client** sends this config (or its saved id) in a request header; provider keys stay in the vault.\n\n### Worked scenario: why counters overshoot\nIllustrative numbers for one team key.\n\n| Situation | Arithmetic | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Daily budget $100, 40 requests in flight when it runs out, `max_tokens` 4,000 at $15 per 1M output tokens | 40 × 4,000 × $15 / 1M = $2.40 | Overshoot of up to $2.40 in output tokens (plus those requests' input cost), bounded only because `max_tokens` caps each request |\n| Same, but no `max_tokens` ceiling | Unbounded per request | The \"hard\" cap is not hard |\n| 600 requests/min limit, 3 replicas with in-process counters | 3 × 600 | Effective limit 1,800 per minute; provider 429s while the gateway never trips |\n| Same, counters in Redis | One shared counter | Effective limit 600, as configured |","patterns":["**A virtual key per team or feature.** When more than one team calls models, issue each its own key through the gateway's key API with a budget, a budget period and RPM/TPM limits (LiteLLM's key generation accepts `max_budget`, `budget_duration`, `rpm_limit` and `tpm_limit`). Monitor spend and 429s per key; a leaked key is revoked alone.","**Shared state for every replica.** Before running more than one LiteLLM replica, point the router at Redis (`redis_host`, `redis_port`, `redis_password` in `router_settings`) so limits, cooldowns and cache are shared. Monitor gateway-side limit hits against provider 429s; a gap means counters are not shared.","**Cooldowns tuned to the provider.** Set how many failures put a deployment into cooldown and for how long (`allowed_fails` and `cooldown_time` in LiteLLM's `router_settings`). Monitor deployments in cooldown and the share of traffic each alias actually sends to each deployment.","**Fail open on soft budgets.** Decide explicitly what happens when Postgres is down; for internal traffic, let requests through and reconcile spend later (LiteLLM documents a general setting for allowing requests when the database is unavailable). Monitor database health and unreconciled spend.","**Streaming and health in CI.** After every gateway upgrade, compare time-to-first-token through the gateway with a direct call, and wire the proxy's liveness and readiness endpoints into the load balancer. Monitor added latency at p50 and p99 and gateway availability.","**Prompt-log retention.** Treat gateway logs as sensitive data: set a retention period, redact or omit message content where policy requires, and scope cache keys per tenant. Monitor log volume, retention jobs and cache hit rate per tenant."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant App as Application\n    participant GW as AI gateway\n    participant ST as Postgres and Redis\n    participant P as Provider deployment\n    App->>GW: POST /v1/chat/completions, model fast, virtual key\n    GW->>ST: authenticate key, check budget, RPM and TPM estimate\n    ST-->>GW: allowed\n    Note over GW: resolve alias fast to deployments, pick one by routing strategy\n    GW->>P: translated native request\n    P-->>GW: streamed tokens\n    GW-->>App: stream forwarded without buffering\n    GW->>ST: charge actual tokens and cost, with tags\n    Note over GW,P: on errors, retry, cool down the deployment, then fall back to strong","caption":"Follow one request: policy checks happen before the provider call using an estimate, and actual usage is\ncharged only after the response completes, which is why budgets can overshoot by what is in flight."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use an AI gateway instead of a shared client library?","a":"- **A library cannot enforce anything:** teams pin old versions, polyglot stacks need one library per\n  language, and keys still live in every service.\n- **Org-wide limits need shared state** that a library does not have, so five services sharing one quota\n  hit rate limits unpredictably.\n- **A network boundary applies policy to every caller,** whether or not they remembered: key custody,\n  budgets, logging and model aliases in one place."},{"q":"Provider 429s keep appearing but the gateway's own rate limits never trip. What is wrong?","a":"- **Most likely per-replica counters:** with 3 replicas each enforcing 600 requests per minute in process,\n  the effective limit is 1,800.\n- **Fix:** move counters, cooldowns and cache to Redis so every replica shares one counter, and the\n  configured limit becomes the real one.\n- **Verify:** gateway-side limit hits should now rise as provider 429s fall; the gap between them is the\n  signal."},{"q":"When would you not put a gateway in front of model calls?","a":"- **For a single application,** where an in-process router such as the LiteLLM SDK's `Router` gives routing\n  and fallbacks with no extra hop.\n- **When a provider-specific feature is core** (prompt-cache controls, extended reasoning parameters) and the\n  unified API does not pass it through intact.\n- **When you cannot operate it well:** a single replica in another region adds latency and a company-wide\n  single point of failure."},{"q":"How do you make a budget cap actually hard when cost is unknown until the response ends?","a":"- **Check before, charge after:** pre-check using prompt tokens plus `max_tokens`, then charge actual usage\n  when the response completes.\n- **Bound the overshoot with `max_tokens`:** 40 in-flight requests at 4,000 tokens and $15 per 1M output\n  tokens can overshoot by at most $2.40; without a ceiling the cap is not hard.\n- **Leave headroom** below the true limit, and reserve fail-closed for hard compliance limits on untrusted\n  keys."},{"q":"How would you choose between the LiteLLM proxy and Portkey for a regulated customer?","a":"- **Where prompts flow decides first:** a self-hosted LiteLLM proxy keeps prompts in your network, while hosted\n  Portkey is a third-party processor unless you deploy its gateway in your own environment.\n- **Who operates it:** LiteLLM means running Postgres, Redis and upgrades yourself; Portkey gives dashboards\n  and declarative configs on day one.\n- **Check passthrough** of the provider features the product depends on before committing to either."}],"tradeoffs":["**Central control vs an extra hop.** One place for keys, budgets and logs, against one more network hop and a shared failure point; the balance tips badly with a single replica or a different region from callers.","**Cheap migrations vs lagging features.** Aliases make provider migrations cheap, but the unified API lags provider features; it flips when a provider-specific capability such as cache controls is core to your product and is not passed through.","**Hosted vs self-hosted.** A hosted gateway removes operations and gives dashboards on day one; a self-hosted LiteLLM keeps prompts in your VPC but makes you run Postgres, Redis and upgrades. Compliance usually decides.","**Central policy vs app control.** A platform team can move traffic without touching applications, at the cost of application teams losing fine control; a single app with unusual needs may be better served in-process.","**One-line caching vs context blindness.** Gateway-level caching is a one-line win but context-blind; its safety depends entirely on keys and tags scoping entries per tenant."],"pitfalls":["**A fail-closed dependency on the gateway's database.** Symptom: every AI feature in the company goes down when Postgres restarts for maintenance.","**Buffering streamed responses in the proxy.** Symptom: time-to-first-token jumps from hundreds of milliseconds to seconds right after adoption.","**A shared cache without tenant scoping.** Symptom: a cached answer containing one customer's data served to another, which is an incident, not a bug.","**Assuming feature parity across providers.** Symptom: provider-specific parameters silently dropped and cost or quality regressing after a migration that \"worked\".","**One master key shared by all applications.** Symptom: zero cost attribution and an unbounded blast radius when the key leaks.","**Nobody owning the configuration.** Symptom: dozens of aliases, half pointing at deprecated models, with fallbacks that have never been exercised.","**Rate-limit counters per replica instead of in shared state.** Symptom: provider 429s and budget overruns while the gateway's own limits never trip, worsening each time a replica is added."],"when":"- **Adopt a gateway** once more than one team or more than one provider is involved, or as soon as finance\n  or compliance asks for spend by team and a list of what is sent where.\n- **Skip it for a single application:** an in-process router (LiteLLM's SDK or a thin client) is lighter.\n- **Choose the LiteLLM proxy** when you want an open-source component inside your own network and can\n  operate a small Python service with Postgres and Redis.\n- **Choose Portkey** when hosted observability and declarative configs matter more and your data rules\n  allow a processor.\n- **Measure:** added latency at p50 and p99, gateway availability, cache hit rate and spend per key.\n- **Interviews:** say what a gateway centralises and what it cannot do, which is make outputs correct.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Points the SDK at the gateway, calls aliases rather than model ids, tags requests for attribution, and debugs with gateway logs instead of five provider dashboards.","architect":"Decides placement per region, fail-open versus fail-closed policy, config ownership, and the boundary between what belongs in the gateway (routing, keys) and in the application (prompt logic).","fde":"Deploys a gateway inside the customer's cloud to satisfy key-custody and egress policies, and uses its per-key reports to show each business unit its own spend."},"check":{"q":"A platform team proposes routing all LLM traffic through a single LiteLLM proxy in one region,\nbacked by one Postgres instance, and failing requests whenever the budget check cannot run \"so budgets\nare never exceeded\". Critique the design.","a":"Three problems. A single replica in one region is a company-wide single point of failure and adds\ncross-region latency for every service elsewhere; run at least two replicas per region behind a load\nbalancer, with Redis for shared counters. Failing closed on the budget check couples every AI feature to a\ndatabase's uptime for a soft financial control; budgets should fail open with asynchronous\nreconciliation and alerts, reserving fail-closed for hard compliance limits on untrusted keys. Finally,\nthe proposal says nothing about operations: verify streaming passthrough, test the fallback chains, set a\nretention policy for logged prompts, assign an owner for aliases, and document an emergency bypass. The\ngateway is the right idea; the single-instance, fail-closed shape is what needs to change."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which concern is an AI gateway least able to solve?","options":["Attributing spend to teams and features","Failing over to another provider during an outage","Making a feature's outputs correct for its task","Keeping provider keys out of application code"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A gateway controls where requests go and what is recorded about them. Output quality depends on prompts, retrieval, models and evaluation, which live in the application and its eval harness, not at the network boundary."},{"q":"What most distinguishes the LiteLLM proxy from Portkey?","options":["Portkey only supports OpenAI models","LiteLLM is an open-source Python proxy you run with a YAML config; Portkey is primarily a hosted gateway driven by declarative configs, with an open-source core","LiteLLM cannot do fallbacks or load balancing","Portkey requires you to operate your own Postgres"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both offer routing, fallbacks, caching and observability across many providers. The real difference is operating model and where your data flows: self-hosted Python with your own database versus a hosted service with dashboards."},{"q":"Why do applications call model aliases such as \"fast\" and \"strong\" instead of concrete model ids?","options":["Aliases reduce the number of tokens billed","Aliases make model outputs deterministic","Aliases let the platform team remap, load-balance and migrate models by configuration without changing application code","The OpenAI API specification requires aliases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Indirection is what makes deprecations and migrations cheap: the mapping from alias to deployments changes in one place and can be canaried, while the application code stays untouched."}]},{"key":"system-design/model-fallback-strategies","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale"],"why":"- **Model endpoints fail in several distinct ways:** outages and hard errors (5xx, connection resets),\n  throttling (429, sometimes with a `Retry-After`), latency spikes that blow the deadline when the provider\n  is overloaded, capability changes (a deprecated or retired model id, behaviour shifted by a silent\n  update), and regional capacity limits or exhausted quota while another region has capacity. Each reaches\n  the user as an error or a spinner that never resolves.\n- **The arithmetic needs a second path.** If your availability target is higher than the provider's, two\n  paths that each succeed 99.5% of the time give 1 − 0.005² = 99.9975% *if their failures are\n  independent*.\n- **They rarely are.** Two resellers of the same upstream model, two deployments in one cloud region, or\n  two keys on one organisation quota fail together. The real gain is set by how independent the rungs are,\n  so independence is the property to design for.\n- **\"Retry with backoff\" makes real incidents worse.** It only covers transient blips on the same\n  endpoint. In an incident every client retries, load on the failing provider multiplies, and each user\n  waits through several timeouts before seeing an error.\n- **\"Add a second provider\" fails quietly.** The backup returns malformed JSON, ignores the tool schema, or\n  truncates at a smaller context window, so the error rate looks fine while the product silently breaks.","how":"### Retry vs fallback vs degradation\n| Mechanism | What changes | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| *Retry* | Nothing: repeats the call on the same target | Same endpoint after jittered backoff |\n| *Fallback* | The target, keeping the function | Another region, provider or model |\n| *Degradation* | The function itself | Cache, non-AI path, honest unavailability |\n\nConfusing the three is how teams end up with a fallback chain slower than the outage it was meant to hide.\n\n### Classify the failure first\nEvery error maps to one action; the mapping is the heart of the design.\n- **Retry the same target** for 429 (honour `Retry-After`), 5xx, connection errors and timeouts on\n  idempotent calls: once or twice, with jittered backoff, never unbounded.\n- **Fail over** on repeated 5xx or timeouts, an open circuit, a 404 for a retired model id, or regional\n  capacity errors.\n- **Neither** for 400 invalid request, 401, content-policy refusals and context-too-long. These recur\n  identically on every target, so failing over multiplies latency and cost. They say nothing about the\n  target's health, so they must not count towards its circuit breaker.\n- **Escalate on invalid output.** A response that fails schema validation after one repair attempt is a\n  legitimate reason to try the next rung, usually a stronger model. This is the cascade idea applied to\n  reliability. Count it separately from error-triggered fallback, because it signals a prompt or\n  model-fit problem rather than an outage.\n\n### The ladder, ordered by behavioural distance\n1. **Same model, another deployment or region:** identical behaviour, different capacity.\n2. **Same model through another provider** (a cloud platform serving the same weights): identical weights,\n   possibly different limits and features.\n3. **Same family, adjacent version:** small drift in style and formatting.\n4. **Comparable model from another vendor:** prompts, tool-call formats, structured output and tokeniser\n   limits must all be re-validated.\n5. **A smaller model:** a quality drop, but still the same function.\n6. **Hand off to graceful degradation:** cache, non-AI path, honest unavailability.\n\nOrder *because* each rung down changes behaviour more; you want the first rung that works to be the one\nusers cannot tell from normal.\n\n### Deadlines, not timeouts\n- **Each attempt has its own timeout,** and all attempts fit inside one user-facing deadline.\n- **Without that,** a 10 s primary timeout plus a 10 s fallback is a 20 s worst case nobody signed off.\n- **Pass the remaining budget down the ladder** and skip rungs that cannot complete in time.\n\n### Circuit breakers\n1. **Closed:** per target, count failures in a window.\n2. **Open:** after N failures the target is skipped for a cooldown.\n3. **Half-open:** a single probe decides whether to close it.\n\nWithout a breaker, every request during an outage waits the full primary timeout *before* falling back.\nLatency equals timeout plus fallback for the whole incident, and the failing provider keeps receiving\nyour traffic.\n\n### Hedging for latency\n- **What:** Dean and Barroso's hedged request sends the same call to a second target after the p95 delay\n  and takes whichever answers first.\n- **Cost:** it trims the tail for roughly 5% extra calls.\n- **Only for idempotent calls,** and never once a stream has been committed to the user.\n- **Switch it off when the breaker shows the primary is overloaded,** or it doubles the load that caused\n  the problem.\n\n### Equivalence is a test, not an assumption\n- **Validate outputs against a schema** regardless of which model served them.\n- **Keep per-model prompt variants** when formats differ.\n- **Send a small continuous slice (around 1%) through each fallback** so it is exercised in daily traffic.\n- **Run game days** where the primary is blocked on purpose.\n- **Tag every response with its serving target and alert when the fallback share rises,** otherwise the\n  primary can be down for a week and the only signal is the bill.\n\n### Side effects and streams\n- **A retry or failover after a tool call executed** (an email sent, a record written) duplicates the side\n  effect. Tool calls need idempotency keys; agent state needs checkpoints so the fallback resumes after\n  the action rather than replaying it.\n- **A stream that fails midway restarts on the fallback** with the partial output discarded; models do not\n  reliably continue each other's text.\n\n### Capacity and cost\n- **Secondary rate limits:** failing all traffic over hits them unless quota was provisioned in advance;\n  you cannot buy it during the incident.\n- **Larger fallback models** are a cost spike.\n- **Both belong in the runbook** before the first outage.","example":"### Fallback ladder with breakers and a shared deadline\nPer-target circuit breakers (closed, open, half-open with a single probe), error classification and one\ndeadline. `call_model`, `ModelError` (with an HTTP `status`) and `ServiceDegraded` are the adapter types\nyour client layer provides; the point is the control flow.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, time\n\nclass Breaker:                       # closed -> open (after N failures in window) -> half-open (one probe)\n    def __init__(self, fails=5, window=30, cooldown=60):\n        self.fails, self.window, self.cooldown = fails, window, cooldown\n        self.errors, self.open_until, self.probing = [], 0.0, False\n    def allow(self):\n        now = time.time()\n        if now < self.open_until: return False          # open: skip this target without waiting\n        if self.open_until and self.probing: return False   # half-open: one probe is already in flight\n        if self.open_until: self.probing = True         # cooldown over: let exactly one probe through\n        return True\n    def record(self, ok):\n        now = time.time()\n        if self.probing:                                # half-open: the probe alone decides\n            self.probing = False\n            self.open_until, self.errors = (0.0, []) if ok else (now + self.cooldown, [])\n            return\n        self.errors = [t for t in self.errors if now - t < self.window] + ([] if ok else [now])\n        if len(self.errors) >= self.fails:\n            self.open_until, self.errors = now + self.cooldown, []\n\nFAILOVER_ON = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504}   # target-side failures; same-target retries live in call_model\nLADDER = [(\"primary-us\", 3.0), (\"primary-eu\", 3.0), (\"vendor-b-equivalent\", 6.0)]   # (target, timeout s)\nbreakers = {t: Breaker() for t, _ in LADDER}\n\nasync def complete(messages, deadline=10.0):\n    start, last_err = time.time(), None\n    for depth, (target, timeout) in enumerate(LADDER):\n        remaining = deadline - (time.time() - start)\n        if remaining <= 0 or not breakers[target].allow():\n            continue                                    # out of budget, or breaker open: skip this rung\n        try:\n            resp = await asyncio.wait_for(call_model(target, messages), timeout=min(timeout, remaining))\n            breakers[target].record(ok=True)\n            return {\"served_by\": target, \"fallback_depth\": depth, **resp}\n        except ModelError as e:\n            if e.status not in FAILOVER_ON:             # 400/401/policy: the request is bad, not the target,\n                breakers[target].record(ok=True)        # so it must not trip the breaker...\n                raise                                   # ...and it would fail identically on every rung\n            breakers[target].record(ok=False); last_err = e\n            await asyncio.sleep(random.uniform(0, 0.2))  # jitter before the next rung\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            breakers[target].record(ok=False); last_err = \"timeout\"\n    raise ServiceDegraded(last_err)                     # hand off to the graceful-degradation layer\n```\n\n- **`served_by` and `fallback_depth` go into metrics and traces;** the alert on fallback depth greater than\n  zero tells you the primary is unhealthy before customers do.\n- **Client errors are recorded as healthy,** because a burst of malformed requests from one buggy caller\n  must not open the breaker and push everyone else's traffic to a worse rung.\n- **The per-rung timeouts (3 s, 3 s, 6 s) sum to more than the 10 s deadline,** so the third rung gets only\n  what is left: if both primaries time out, it has just under 4 s of its 6 s. A rung that cannot finish in\n  that time is better skipped (`remaining <= 0` is the crude version of that check).\n- **Release the half-open probe slot on every exit path** in a production breaker, including cancellation\n  when the client disconnects, or a target can stay skipped indefinitely.\n\n### Availability when rungs share a cause\nPure Python: how much of the 99.9975% survives when some failures are shared.\n\n```python\ndef both_fail(p_each, common_share):\n    \"\"\"P(both rungs fail) when a share of each rung's failures comes from a shared cause.\"\"\"\n    q = p_each * common_share                     # shared-cause outage: takes down both rungs\n    r = (p_each - q) / (1 - q)                    # independent failures, so each rung still fails p_each\n    return q + (1 - q) * r * r\n\nfor share in (0.0, 0.1, 0.5, 1.0):\n    print(f\"shared cause {share:>4.0%}: availability {1 - both_fail(0.005, share):.4%}\")\n# 0%: 99.9975%   10%: 99.9480%   50%: 99.7494%   100%: 99.5000% (the second rung bought nothing)\n```\n\n### Hedged request\nStart the primary; if it has not answered by the hedge delay (set it near your p95), start the secondary\nand take the first success.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def hedged(call, primary, secondary, hedge_after):\n    \"\"\"Start primary; if no answer by hedge_after seconds, also start secondary. First success wins.\"\"\"\n    tasks = {asyncio.create_task(call(primary))}\n    done, _ = await asyncio.wait(tasks, timeout=hedge_after)\n    if not done:                                         # slow tail: send the hedge\n        tasks.add(asyncio.create_task(call(secondary)))  # (a fast error is the ladder's job, not this)\n    errors = []\n    while tasks:\n        done, tasks = await asyncio.wait(tasks, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED)\n        for t in done:\n            if t.exception() is None:\n                for p in tasks: p.cancel()               # stop the loser\n                return t.result()\n            errors.append(t.exception())\n    raise errors[-1]\n\nasync def fake(target):                                  # demo: primary stalls, secondary is quick\n    await asyncio.sleep(2.0 if target == \"primary\" else 0.3)\n    return f\"answer from {target}\"\n\nprint(asyncio.run(hedged(fake, \"primary\", \"secondary\", hedge_after=0.8)))   # answer from secondary, ~1.1 s\n```\n\n### Worked incident: a 40-minute primary outage\nIllustrative traffic of 50 requests per second, `primary-us` hard down, breaker defaults from the ladder\ncode (5 failures, 60 s cooldown).\n\n| | No breaker | With breaker |\n|---|---|---|\n| Requests sent to the dead primary | 50 × 2,400 s = 120,000 | ~5 to open it if it fails fast (up to ~150 if it times out, since 3 s × 50/s are in flight before any timeout lands), then one probe per 60 s cooldown: about 40 more |\n| Added latency per request | The full 3 s primary timeout, every request | None once open; only probes pay it |\n| Load on the failing provider | All your traffic, plus retries | Near zero |\n| Signal | Error rate looks flat | `fallback_depth` > 0 alert fires as soon as traffic shifts to the next rung |","patterns":["**Declare the ladder in the gateway.** When several services share providers, put fallbacks in gateway config rather than app code: LiteLLM's `router_settings` takes `fallbacks`, and it also has separate fallback lists for context-window and content-policy errors. Monitor fallback rate per rung and success rate after fallback.","**Keep a live slice on every rung.** Route around 1% of normal traffic to each fallback continuously with a weighted deployment or a hash of the request id. Monitor schema pass rate and judged quality per serving target, so a broken fallback shows up on a quiet Tuesday, not during an outage.","**Game-day the primary.** In staging, then production off-peak, block the primary on purpose (an egress rule or an alias pointed at an unreachable endpoint) and watch the ladder take over. Monitor time-to-detect, latency by rung, error rate and on-call actions taken.","**Pre-provision and watch secondary headroom.** Size secondary quota for 100% failover before launch and load-test it. Read the provider's rate-limit response headers (OpenAI's `x-ratelimit-remaining-requests`, Anthropic's `anthropic-ratelimit-requests-remaining`) and alert when headroom on the secondary drops.","**Propagate the deadline.** Pass the absolute deadline from the edge to every service (a request header or context value), and compute each rung's timeout as `min(rung_timeout, remaining)`. Monitor rungs skipped for lack of budget and end-to-end p99 against the deadline.","**Idempotency keys on side-effecting tools.** Generate one key per logical action, store it with the result, and make the tool return the stored result on a repeat. Monitor duplicate-key hits during incidents; a spike shows the ladder replaying actions."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with one user-facing deadline\"] --> N{\"Next rung: budget left and breaker allows?\"}\n    N -->|\"no, skip rung\"| MORE{\"More rungs?\"}\n    N -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Call target, timeout = min of rung timeout and remaining\"]\n    C -->|\"success\"| OK[\"Return, tagged with served_by and fallback_depth\"]\n    C -->|\"400, 401, policy refusal, context too long\"| STOP[\"Raise: same error on every rung, breaker not tripped\"]\n    C -->|\"408, 429, 5xx, timeout\"| F[\"Record failure on this breaker, jitter\"]\n    F --> MORE\n    MORE -->|\"yes, next rung is further in behaviour\"| N\n    MORE -->|\"no\"| D[\"ServiceDegraded: hand off to graceful degradation\"]","caption":"One request walking the ladder: the breaker and the remaining deadline decide whether a rung is tried, the\nerror class decides between failing over and stopping, and the bottom of the ladder hands off to\ndegradation."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a retry, a fallback and graceful degradation?","a":"- **Retry** repeats the call on the same target after jittered backoff; it only covers transient blips.\n- **Fallback** changes the target while keeping the function: another region, provider or model.\n- **Degradation** changes the function itself: a cache, a non-AI path or honest unavailability. Confusing them\n  produces a fallback chain slower than the outage it was meant to hide."},{"q":"Two providers each succeed 99.5% of the time. What availability do you get with both?","a":"- **If failures are independent:** 1 − 0.005² = 99.9975%.\n- **They rarely are:** two resellers of the same upstream model, one cloud region or one organisation quota\n  fail together; with half the failures shared, availability falls to about 99.75%.\n- **So design for independence** of rungs, not just their count."},{"q":"Design the fallback ladder for an agent that sends emails and writes records.","a":"- **Order by behavioural distance:** same model in another region, same weights via another provider, then a\n  re-validated model from another vendor, then degradation.\n- **One deadline:** each attempt's timeout fits inside the user-facing budget, passed down the ladder.\n- **Protect side effects:** idempotency keys on tool calls and checkpointed agent state, so a failover resumes\n  after the email instead of sending it twice."},{"q":"When is hedging a bad idea?","a":"- **When the primary is overloaded,** because a hedge doubles load exactly when the provider is struggling;\n  disable it when the breaker indicates overload.\n- **For non-idempotent calls,** where two concurrent attempts duplicate side effects.\n- **Once a stream is committed** to the user, since models do not reliably continue each other's text."},{"q":"During an outage, latency rose to primary timeout plus fallback time for every request. What was missing?","a":"- **A per-target circuit breaker:** without one, each request waits the full primary timeout before falling\n  back, for the whole incident.\n- **With a breaker** (5 failures, 60 s cooldown in the example), the dead primary receives a few requests\n  to open it (about 5 on fast errors, up to one timeout's worth of traffic if it hangs) and then one probe\n  per cooldown, instead of 120,000 over 40 minutes at 50 requests per second.\n- **Also check** that client errors are not counted, so one buggy caller cannot open the breaker."}],"tradeoffs":["**Availability vs output consistency.** Consistency falls as the ladder descends to other vendors. For audited or regulated outputs, restrict the ladder to same-model rungs and degrade honestly after that.","**Shorter tails vs load during overload.** Hedged requests cut tail latency for a few per cent extra calls, yet double load exactly when the provider is struggling; disable hedging whenever the breaker indicates overload.","**Longer ladder vs worst-case latency.** More rungs raise the chance of success but stretch the worst case; with a tight deadline a short ladder with a breaker beats a long one.","**Cross-vendor resilience vs data exposure.** Cross-vendor fallback sends data to a second processor; it flips from asset to liability when that vendor is not on the approved list for the data class involved.","**Idle quota cost vs failover that works.** Pre-provisioned secondary quota costs money while idle, but without it the fallback fails with 429s at the exact moment it is needed."],"pitfalls":["**A fallback never exercised in normal traffic.** Symptom: the first real outage reveals expired credentials, a wrong model id or a schema mismatch on the secondary.","**Retrying or failing over after a side effect.** Symptom: duplicate emails, tickets or payments during provider incidents.","**No circuit breaker.** Symptom: every request during an outage takes primary timeout plus fallback time, and the failing provider is hammered by your retries.","**Silent fallback with no metric.** Symptom: discovering the primary has been down for days from the invoice or from a slow drift in answer quality.","**Failing over on non-retryable errors.** Symptom: a single bad request produces identical 400s from three providers with triple the latency and cost.","**Ignoring context-window and limit differences.** Symptom: context-length errors appear only on long requests and only when the fallback is active."],"when":"- **Always:** every production call should have at least the first two rungs and a documented ladder.\n- **Depth depends on** the SLO, the audit constraints on outputs and the cost you can absorb during an\n  incident.\n- **Decide per use case:** the error classification, the deadline and the breaker thresholds; provision\n  secondary quota before launch.\n- **Measure:** fallback rate by rung, success rate after fallback, latency by rung, the cost delta during\n  failover and the quality of fallback-served responses on your eval.\n- **Interviews:** distinguish retry, fallback and degradation cleanly, then cover error classification,\n  breakers, deadline propagation and equivalence testing; that sequence is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements error classification, breakers and deadline propagation, tags responses with the serving target, and writes the game-day test that blocks the primary on purpose.","architect":"Defines the ladder per use case under governance constraints, provisions secondary quota and hedging policy, and sets the SLO the fallback path itself must meet.","fde":"Builds the ladder from models and regions the customer has approved, often inside their cloud platform, and runs a failover drill with them before go-live."},"check":{"q":"Your primary provider had a 40-minute outage. The fallback to a second vendor \"worked\" and the\nerror rate stayed flat, but the next morning support reports a spike in malformed outputs and several\ncustomers received duplicate emails from the agent. What went wrong, and how do you fix it?","a":"Two independent failures. First, the fallback model was never validated for these prompts: its\ntool-call format or JSON adherence differs, so outputs that passed through unvalidated were malformed.\nFix with schema validation that runs regardless of model, per-model prompt variants where formats\ndiffer, a permanent small slice of traffic through the fallback, and a quality metric split by serving\ntarget. Second, the agent retried or failed over after a tool had already executed, replaying the\nsend-email step. Fix with idempotency keys on tool calls, a rule that non-idempotent steps are never\nretried blindly, and checkpointed agent state so the fallback resumes after the completed action. Add an\nalert on fallback share so someone is watching during the outage, not the morning after."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which error should not trigger failover to another provider?","options":["A 503 from the primary endpoint","A timeout after the per-attempt budget expired","A 400 invalid-request error or a content-policy refusal","A 429 after the Retry-After period was honoured once"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Invalid requests and policy refusals are properties of the request, not the provider, so every rung returns the same error; failing over only multiplies latency and cost. The other three are capacity or transient failures that a different target can resolve."},{"q":"What does a per-target circuit breaker add to a fallback ladder?","options":["It lowers the token cost of fallback calls","It skips a failing target immediately instead of waiting out its timeout on every request, and stops hammering it","It guarantees identical outputs across targets","It removes the need for retries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a breaker, each request during an outage pays the primary's full timeout before falling back, and the retries add load to a provider that is already struggling. The breaker turns a slow failure into a fast one."},{"q":"Which statement about hedged requests is correct?","options":["They halve the cost of the request","They guarantee the p99 equals the p95","They trim tail latency for a few per cent extra calls and must be disabled when the primary is overloaded","They are safe for calls with non-idempotent side effects"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sending a second request after the p95 delay helps only the slow tail and costs roughly the fraction of requests that exceed that delay. During provider overload the extra load makes things worse, and duplicated side effects make hedging unsafe for non-idempotent calls."}]},{"key":"system-design/graceful-degradation","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Nygard, 2007 — Release It!"],"why":"- **Most AI features enhance a product that already worked:** search, support, editors, dashboards. A\n  model incident, an exhausted rate limit, a runaway cost day or a traffic spike will happen.\n- **If the feature's failure breaks the workflow around it,** an enhancement becomes a new failure point\n  and lowers the whole product's availability. Users who could have found the document themselves now see\n  a spinner.\n- **The naive responses each miss something.**\n  - \"Retry and show an error\" makes the user wait and then gives them nothing.\n  - \"Fall back to another model\" handles a provider outage, not overload, budget exhaustion or a latency\n    SLO the fallback also misses.\n  - \"Turn the feature off\" is one binary switch, so the smallest incident costs the whole feature.\n- **AI adds a failure the others ignore:** a response that was produced but should not be trusted,\n  because the grounding check failed or the judge rejected it. Show less and say why; do not fail.\n- **AI adds an axis to SRE practice.** Load shedding and priority classes came from site reliability\n  engineering; AI adds quality levels, because one request can be answered well, adequately, or not at\n  all, at different costs. Degradation decides those levels before the incident.","how":"### Define the ladder per feature\n| Level | What the user gets | Typical contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| **0, full** | The best answer | Strong model, tools, reranking, a verifier |\n| **1, reduced** | Visibly still an answer; cheaper and faster | Smaller model, fewer retrieved passages, no reranker, lower `max_tokens`, agent loops capped at one step |\n| **2, cached or precomputed** | A known-good answer | Cached answer to a similar question, precomputed summary, FAQ entry, scoped to the same tenant |\n| **3, non-AI** | The product as it was before the feature | Keyword search results, a template, the raw documents, a form |\n| **4, honest unavailability** | A way to proceed | A clear message, the rest of the product working, a human handoff, a link, try later |\n\nFor each level write down what the user sees, what it costs, and who gets it.\n\n### Triggers\n- **Dependency health:** breaker state, error rate, p95 latency.\n- **Load:** queue depth, concurrency.\n- **Budget burn:** spend today against the cap.\n- **Rate-limit headroom.**\n- **Quality signals:** a failed grounding check demotes *this* response to sources-only.\n- **A human-operated kill switch.**\n\n### Hysteresis\n- **Evaluate triggers where the decision is made.**\n- **Step down quickly on a bad signal; step back up only after sustained health,** *because* flapping\n  between levels is worse for users than staying one level down.\n- **Concretely:** separate enter and exit thresholds plus a minimum dwell time. Illustrative numbers: enter\n  Reduced when p95 exceeds 4 s; leave only after p95 has stayed under 2.5 s for ten minutes.\n- **The gap between 4 s and 2.5 s** stops a signal hovering near one threshold from toggling the level on\n  every sample.\n\n### Budget triggers: projected burn, not spend so far\n| Trigger | When it fires on a heavy day | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `spent + current_rate × hours_left > cap` | Mid-morning | Step down one level gently |\n| `spent ≥ cap` | Late in the day | Every user drops straight to the cached level for the rest of it |\n\n### Shed by priority, isolate by class\n1. **Classify requests:** interactive versus batch, paying versus free, critical versus nice-to-have.\n2. **Shed batch and low tiers first.**\n3. **Reserve capacity** for critical interactive traffic in its own bulkhead, so a batch backfill can never\n   starve the chat box.\n4. **Shed at admission, before any tokens are spent.** A request abandoned after generation started has\n   already paid for the work it throws away, and under overload that waste is exactly the capacity you\n   were short of.\n\n### Deadlines and partial results\n- **Give each request a deadline** and return what you have when it expires: retrieved passages without\n  the synthesis, or a streamed partial answer with a visible marker.\n- **Streaming makes partial output useful** rather than confusing.\n- **Partial answers are dangerous where a half-statement misleads;** there, sources-only is the right\n  degraded form.\n\n### Honesty in the interface\n- **Label degraded output:** \"Showing matching documents; the AI summary is unavailable\".\n- **Never present a Level 1 answer as Level 0** when the quality difference matters, since users act on\n  outputs and silent degradation transfers the risk to them.\n\n### Keep the degraded paths alive\n- **Unused paths rot:** the non-AI path's index goes stale, its credentials expire, its flag is removed.\n- **Exercise them:** send a small share of traffic through each level routinely, run synthetic checks, and\n  hold game days.\n- **Every level sits behind a flag** that flips without a deploy, and the runbook names the flags.\n\n### Measure\n- Time spent at each level and requests served per level.\n- User outcomes per level (completion rate, handoff rate).\n- Time to recover to full.\n- **A good SLO:** \"Percentage of sessions served at Level 0\".","example":"### Support-answer endpoint with a degradation ladder\nA background task refreshes `h`, a health snapshot, every few seconds; the handler picks a level, enforces a\ndeadline, and steps down rather than raising when a stage fails. `keyword_or_vector_search`, `generate`,\n`semantic_cache_lookup` and `ModelError` are your adapters.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom enum import IntEnum\n\nclass Level(IntEnum):\n    FULL = 0; REDUCED = 1; CACHED = 2; NON_AI = 3; UNAVAILABLE = 4\n\ndef current_level(h):                            # h: health snapshot refreshed by a background task\n    if h.kill_switch:                                           return Level.NON_AI\n    if h.budget_used >= 1.0 or h.llm_breaker_open:              return Level.CACHED\n    if h.budget_used >= 0.8 or h.llm_p95_ms > 4000 or h.queue_depth > 200:\n        return Level.REDUCED\n    return Level.FULL\n\nasync def answer(q, user, h, deadline_s=6.0):\n    level = current_level(h)\n    if user.tier == \"free\" and h.queue_depth > 50:              # shed low priority first\n        level = max(level, Level.REDUCED)\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        docs = await keyword_or_vector_search(q, k=20 if level == Level.FULL else 5)\n    except Exception:                                           # even the non-AI path is down\n        docs, level = [], Level.UNAVAILABLE\n    if level <= Level.REDUCED:\n        full = level == Level.FULL\n        try:\n            remaining = deadline_s - (time.monotonic() - t0)\n            text = await asyncio.wait_for(\n                generate(\"strong\" if full else \"small\", q, docs if full else docs[:3],\n                         max_tokens=600 if full else 200), remaining)\n            return {\"level\": int(level), \"answer\": text, \"sources\": docs[:5],\n                    \"label\": None if full else \"Reduced-quality answer\"}\n        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, ModelError):\n            level = Level.CACHED                                # step down, do not raise\n    if level == Level.CACHED:\n        hit = await semantic_cache_lookup(q, tenant=user.tenant)\n        if hit:\n            return {\"level\": 2, \"answer\": hit.answer, \"sources\": hit.sources, \"label\": \"Cached answer\"}\n        level = Level.NON_AI\n    if level == Level.NON_AI:\n        return {\"level\": 3, \"answer\": None, \"sources\": docs[:5],\n                \"label\": \"AI assistant unavailable; showing matching documents\"}\n    return {\"level\": 4, \"answer\": None, \"sources\": [], \"label\": \"Search is temporarily unavailable\"}\n```\n\n- **The `level` field in every response is the metric:** a histogram of it over time shows whether the\n  product has been quietly running degraded.\n- **Hysteresis lives in the background task.** `current_level` is stateless for brevity; the task that\n  builds `h` should hold a level until its exit condition has persisted for the dwell time, so every\n  request in a window sees the same level.\n- **Each step down is cheaper than the one above,** and a failure inside a level moves the request down\n  rather than retrying, so an incident lowers spend instead of raising it.\n\n### Hysteresis controller for the background task\nRunnable, pure Python, using the illustrative 4 s / 2.5 s / ten-minute thresholds.\n\n```python\nclass Hysteresis:\n    \"\"\"Enter REDUCED fast when p95 > enter_ms; leave only after p95 < exit_ms for dwell_s straight.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, enter_ms=4000, exit_ms=2500, dwell_s=600):\n        self.enter_ms, self.exit_ms, self.dwell_s = enter_ms, exit_ms, dwell_s\n        self.reduced, self.healthy_since = False, None\n\n    def update(self, p95_ms, now_s):\n        if p95_ms > self.enter_ms:\n            self.reduced, self.healthy_since = True, None      # step down immediately\n        elif self.reduced and p95_ms < self.exit_ms:\n            self.healthy_since = self.healthy_since or now_s\n            if now_s - self.healthy_since >= self.dwell_s:\n                self.reduced, self.healthy_since = False, None  # step up after sustained health\n        elif self.reduced:\n            self.healthy_since = None                           # in the gap: hold the level, reset dwell\n        return \"REDUCED\" if self.reduced else \"FULL\"\n\nh = Hysteresis()\nsamples = [3000, 4200, 3800, 4100, 2400, 3000, 2300] + [2000] * 10   # one sample per minute\nprint(\" \".join(h.update(p, 60 * i)[0] for i, p in enumerate(samples)))\n# F R R R R R R R R R R R R R R R F : no flapping around 4 s; full only after 10 healthy minutes\n```\n\nA single 4 s threshold on the same samples would flip F R F R F F F, which users read as randomness.\n\n### Worked scenario: projected-burn budget trigger\nIllustrative: daily cap $500, a heavy day where spend reaches $150 by 10:00 and runs at $40 per hour.\n\n| Trigger | Fires at | What users get |\n|---|---|---|\n| Projected: 150 + 40 × 14 h left = $710 > $500 | 10:00 | Step to Reduced; if the smaller model halves the rate, 150 + 20 × 14 = $430 and the cap holds |\n| Spend so far: $150 + $40/h reaches $500 | about 18:45 | Everyone drops straight to Cached for the rest of the day |","patterns":["**Levels as runtime flags.** Read the forced level and the kill switch per request from your feature-flag service or a config key, never from code, so mitigation is a flag flip, not a deploy. Name each flag in the runbook. Monitor flag changes with who and when, and time from alert to flip.","**Admission-time bulkheads.** Give each request class its own concurrency pool (for example a separate semaphore or worker pool for batch with a hard cap) and reject over-limit batch work with 503 and a `Retry-After` before any model call. Monitor shed rate per class and interactive p95 during batch runs.","**Projected-burn budget job.** Every few minutes compute `spent + current_rate × hours_left` per feature and tenant, and write the resulting level into the health snapshot. Monitor projected versus actual end-of-day spend and hours spent at each budget-driven level.","**Synthetic checks per level.** Run a canary query against each level on a schedule by forcing the level with a test-only header or flag, and fail the check if the non-AI index is stale or a credential is expired. Monitor canary success per level, not only for Level 0.","**Level-mix SLO.** Emit the served level as a metric label on every response and set an SLO on the share of sessions at Level 0, alerting on burn rate. Monitor the level histogram per tenant, so one noisy tenant's degradation is visible."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Full\n    Full --> Reduced: p95 over 4 s, budget at 80 percent, queue over 200\n    Reduced --> Full: p95 under 2.5 s for ten minutes\n    Full --> Cached: breaker open or budget spent\n    Reduced --> Cached: model timeout or error on this request\n    Cached --> Reduced: breaker closed and budget recovered\n    Cached --> NonAI: no tenant-scoped cache hit\n    Full --> NonAI: kill switch flipped\n    NonAI --> Unavailable: search itself is down\n    Unavailable --> NonAI: search recovers\n    NonAI --> Full: kill switch cleared and health sustained","caption":"The ladder as states: each trigger steps down at once, a failure inside a level moves the request one level\ndown, and the only way back up to Full is sustained health for the dwell time."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is a model fallback not enough on its own to keep an AI feature available?","a":"- **A fallback changes the provider, not the function:** it handles a provider outage, but not overload,\n  budget exhaustion or a latency SLO the fallback also misses.\n- **Degradation changes the function** to something cheaper and simpler (smaller model, cache, non-AI path,\n  honest message), so an incident lowers spend instead of raising it.\n- **AI adds a trust failure** too: a produced answer that failed its grounding check should be demoted to\n  sources-only, not shown."},{"q":"Users report answers flipping between fast and slow, short and full, within one session. What is wrong?","a":"- **No hysteresis:** a single threshold near the signal's typical value toggles the level on every sample,\n  which users read as randomness.\n- **Fix:** separate enter and exit thresholds plus a dwell time, for example enter Reduced above a p95 of\n  4 s and leave only after ten minutes under 2.5 s.\n- **Compute the level in one background task** so every request in a window sees the same level."},{"q":"Why trigger on projected budget burn rather than spend so far?","a":"- **Spend so far fires too late:** on a heavy day at $40 per hour from $150 at 10:00, a $500 cap is hit around\n  18:45 and everyone drops straight to Cached.\n- **Projected burn fires at 10:00:** 150 + 40 × 14 = $710 exceeds the cap, so stepping to Reduced early (halving\n  the rate gives $430) keeps the cap without a cliff."},{"q":"When would you not build a full degradation ladder?","a":"- **Offline batch work** usually needs only retry-later, since no user is waiting.\n- **When the AI feature is the product,** no non-AI path exists, so degrade within AI levels and then be\n  honest.\n- **In medical, legal or financial contexts,** skip partial synthesis as a level and return sources only,\n  because a half answer can mislead."},{"q":"How do you prove each degraded level actually works before an incident?","a":"- **Exercise it routinely:** send a small share of traffic through each level and run synthetic checks that\n  fail on a stale non-AI index or an expired credential.\n- **Make every level a runtime flag** named in the runbook, so mitigation is a flag flip, not a deploy.\n- **Hold game days** and track time per level, outcomes per level and time to recover to full."}],"tradeoffs":["**Survival vs code-path cost.** The product survives incidents, but you maintain several code paths and an eval for each. When the AI feature *is* the product, degrade within AI levels and then be honest, since no non-AI path exists.","**Trust vs visible degradation.** Honest labelling preserves trust but shows degradation to users and competitors; where outputs are acted on, visibility is the correct default and the trade is rarely worth reversing.","**Protecting paying users vs the funnel.** Priority shedding protects paying and critical users at the expense of the free tier, which may be the acquisition funnel; if growth is the KPI, shed batch work before shedding free users.","**Partial results vs misleading halves.** Partial results at the deadline beat nothing, but a half answer can mislead; in medical, legal or financial contexts return sources only rather than a truncated synthesis.","**Eager triggers vs permanent degradation.** Eager triggers save cost and latency, but set too low they keep the product one level down most of the day without anyone noticing; hysteresis thresholds need the same review as the SLO."],"pitfalls":["**Degraded paths never exercised.** Symptom: the non-AI path fails with an expired credential or stale index at the exact moment of the incident.","**Silent degradation with no time-in-level metric.** Symptom: weeks of reduced answers discovered from quality complaints or a bill that dropped for no known reason.","**Flapping between levels.** Symptom: a user gets a fast reduced answer, then a slow full one, then a cached one within a single session, which reads as randomness.","**Degrading to something more expensive,** such as retries or a larger fallback model. Symptom: cost rises during the incident instead of falling.","**A kill switch that requires a deploy.** Symptom: a 40-minute mitigation for a 2-minute decision.","**Triggers measured globally when load is per tenant.** Symptom: one noisy tenant degrades everyone, or nobody is degraded while that tenant's queue grows unbounded."],"when":"- **Use for** any user-facing AI feature with an SLO; design the ladder alongside the non-functional\n  requirements, because the levels shape the architecture (what stays deterministic, where caches live,\n  which flags exist).\n- **Skip the ladder for** offline batch work, which usually needs only retry-later.\n- **Agree degraded behaviour before go-live** with product and, in customer deployments, with the customer.\n- **Measure:** time per level, outcomes per level and recovery time; set an SLO on the share of sessions\n  served at full quality.\n- **Interviews:** distinguish degradation from fallback, then describe the ladder, triggers with\n  hysteresis, priority shedding, honest labelling and the discipline of exercising the degraded paths.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements levels as flagged code paths with deadlines, emits the level on every response, and keeps a test per level in CI so the non-AI path cannot rot unnoticed.","architect":"Writes the ladder and priority classes into the NFRs, decides what \"acceptable degraded\" means per feature, and owns the kill-switch and hysteresis policy.","fde":"Agrees with the customer what their users will see when the model is down, configures the tenant-level triggers, and runs the drill before launch."},"check":{"q":"A customer says \"the assistant must never show an error\". Design the degradation for a support\nassistant and say what you would push back on.","a":"Propose a ladder: full RAG with the strong model; reduced with a small model and three passages;\ncached answers scoped to the tenant; search results with canned links; and a human handoff form.\nTriggers come from breaker state, p95 latency, queue depth and the daily budget, with hysteresis so the\nlevel steps down fast and recovers slowly; free-tier and batch traffic shed first; every level labelled\nin the interface and recorded as a metric. Push back on \"never\": at the bottom of the ladder the product\nmust show a clear message and a path to a human, because an unlabelled, ungrounded answer is worse than\nan honest unavailability and the customer will own the consequences of it. Replace the absolute with an\nSLO, for example the share of sessions served at full quality, and agree the drill that proves each level\nworks."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a fallback and graceful degradation?","options":["Fallbacks are automatic, degradation is manual","A fallback keeps the same function on a different target; degradation changes the function to a reduced, honest form","Fallbacks apply to models, degradation applies only to databases","They are the same thing with different names"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Switching provider or region preserves what the user gets. Degradation steps down to a smaller model, a cache, a non-AI path or a clear message. Both can be automatic and both apply to any dependency."},{"q":"Why do degradation triggers need hysteresis?","options":["To reduce the cost of the health checks","To make the full level recover as fast as possible","To stop the system flapping between levels, stepping down quickly but stepping up only after sustained health","To ensure free-tier users are always degraded first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A trigger that flips on every noisy sample produces alternating fast and slow, reduced and full answers inside one session. Asymmetric thresholds keep the system stable one level down until the signal has genuinely recovered."},{"q":"Which practice most determines whether degradation actually works during an incident?","options":["Choosing the largest available fallback model","Increasing timeouts so more requests complete","Routinely exercising every degraded path and controlling levels with flags that need no deploy","Adding more retries before stepping down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unused paths rot and deploy-gated switches are slow. Routine traffic through each level, synthetic checks and runtime flags are what make the ladder real; bigger models, longer timeouts and more retries typically make the incident worse."}]},{"key":"system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic prompt caching","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: GPTCache"],"why":"- **Hits are nearly free; misses are not.** A hit returns in milliseconds and costs almost nothing; a miss\n  takes seconds and bills every token.\n- **Prompts are mostly repeated content.** A system prompt, tool definitions, few-shot examples and\n  retrieved documents can be thousands of tokens identical for every user of a feature. Caching is the\n  biggest lever on cost and latency after model choice.\n- **\"Hash the prompt and cache the response\" fails in AI-specific ways:**\n  - Outputs are sampled, so caching only makes sense when you want the same answer, and the key must\n    include the sampling parameters.\n  - Prompts embed context: an identical question from two tenants, or from one tenant a week apart, can\n    need different correct answers.\n  - Semantic near-duplicates look like hits but differ in one decisive detail: a negation, a date, an\n    account number.\n  - Staleness is numerous and silent: the provider updates the model, someone edits the prompt template, a\n    document is re-ingested, the embedding model changes, an external tool's data moves.\n- **Without a strategy you land in one of two bad places:** caching nothing and paying for every repeated\n  token, or caching eagerly and serving stale or wrong answers that cost more trust than the tokens saved.\n- **Both famous hard problems are one problem here.** Naming things and invalidating caches merge: the name\n  of the cache entry is the list of everything the answer depends on.","how":"### The layers at a glance\n| Layer | Caches | Key | Goes stale when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Prefix / prompt | Computation (KV cache of a shared prefix) | Exact token prefix | Anything before or within the span changes |\n| 2. Exact response | Whole responses | Model, params, messages, tools, format, scope | Any key input changes |\n| 3. Semantic | Answers to similar queries | Query embedding within a scope | Sources change, or a paraphrase is a different question |\n| 4. Embedding | Vectors | Embedding model id + normalised text | Embedding model changes |\n| 5. Retrieval and tool | Doc ids, tool results, agent steps | Index version; tool name + args | Index changes; TTL expires |\n\n### Layer 1: prefix and prompt caching (caching computation)\n- **What:** a provider or self-hosted engine reuses the KV cache for a byte-identical token prefix across\n  requests, so the shared part's prefill is not recomputed.\n- **Key = the exact prefix.** Any change *before or within* the cached span invalidates everything after\n  it, because each cached key and value depends on all earlier positions.\n- **Layout rule:** static content first (system prompt, tools, few-shot, long documents), dynamic content\n  last, stable ordering, and never a timestamp or the user's name near the top.\n- **Pricing (vendor- and model-specific, and it changes):** cached input tokens bill at a lower rate with\n  short time-to-live windows; some providers charge a premium for the cache *write*; most enforce a\n  minimum cacheable prefix of roughly a thousand tokens or more, so a short system prompt alone may never\n  cache.\n- **Engines** such as vLLM offer automatic prefix caching across requests that share a prefix.\n- **Metric:** cached-token share in usage reports, checked after every change to prompt assembly, because a\n  regression is silent: requests keep succeeding and only the bill moves.\n\n### Layer 2: exact response cache\n- **Key** = hash of model id and version, sampling parameters, the full normalised message list, tool\n  schemas, response format, and the tenant or user scope whenever the prompt contains private context.\n- **Value** = the response plus its usage.\n- **Only for deterministic intent:** temperature 0, or a product that accepts identical answers to\n  identical requests.\n- **Hit rates:** low in open-ended chat; high in form-like, classification and extraction features, where\n  identical inputs recur.\n\n### Layer 3: semantic cache\n1. Embed the query.\n2. Search cached queries within the same scope (tenant, prompt version, index version).\n3. Return the cached answer when similarity exceeds a threshold.\n\n- **Gain:** a hit rate exact matching cannot reach. **Risk:** a paraphrase that is actually a different\n  question.\n- **Mitigations:** a high threshold; scope keys; excluding or entity-keying queries with numbers, dates,\n  names or negations; a cheap verifier on borderline hits (\"does this cached answer address this\n  query?\"); a sampled false-hit audit.\n- **Economics:**\n\n```\nsavings = hit_rate × (cost_miss − cost_hit) − false_hit_rate × cost_of_a_wrong_answer − embedding cost per lookup\n```\n\nThe last two terms are what people forget.\n\n### Layer 4: embedding cache\n- **Key** = hash of embedding model id plus normalised text.\n- **Cheap and high-value** in ingestion (unchanged chunks are not re-embedded on re-index) and for repeated\n  queries.\n- **A new embedding model invalidates everything,** which is fine *because* you must re-embed the whole\n  index anyway; version the index.\n\n### Layer 5: retrieval and tool caches\n- **Query → document ids,** keyed by index version.\n- **External tool results** with a TTL set by data volatility (exchange rates seconds, a product catalogue\n  hours).\n- **Memoised agent steps** keyed by tool name and arguments.\n\n### Read and write patterns\n| Pattern | How | Trade-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cache-aside** (most AI response caches) | App looks up the key, calls the model on a miss, writes the result | Cache stays optional (an outage means slower, not broken), at the price of stampedes |\n| **Read-through** (a gateway cache, effectively) | Callers do not know it exists | Convenient, and why it cannot see tenants or document versions nobody put in the request |\n| **Precomputation** | Batch job answers the few hundred most frequent questions; summaries written at ingestion | Moves model cost off the request path; suits predictable question sets |\n| **Stale-while-revalidate** | Serve an expired entry while one background call refreshes it | A bounded extra window of staleness for never making a user wait on a hot key; wrong where stale means incorrect, such as prices or permissions |\n\n- **Eviction under a memory cap** is usually LRU or LFU. Both ignore that entries differ greatly in what\n  they cost to recompute, so a long-context answer is worth more cache space than a short classification.\n\n### Invalidation strategies\n1. **Versioned keys.** Include model id, prompt template version, index version and embedding model id in\n   every key. Changing any yields a cold cache with no purge logic at all. Simplest and most robust; the\n   cost is cold misses after each deploy, which a replay of recent queries can pre-warm.\n2. **TTL.** Bounds staleness per data class; refresh ahead of expiry for hot keys; jitter expiries so they\n   do not align.\n3. **Event-driven purge.** When a document changes, purge the answers that used it.\n   - Needs a reverse index from document id to cache keys, which the generation step must write.\n   - Precise, more complex, and required when a deleted document must stop appearing in answers (erasure\n     requests).\n   - **Race:** a generation that read the old document can finish *after* the purge and write a stale\n     answer back. Close it by storing the version (a content hash) of each document an answer used, and\n     treating a hit as a miss when any of those versions is no longer current.\n4. **Scope isolation.** Tenant and user in the key wherever the prompt carries private context; a shared\n   cache across trust boundaries is a leak, not an optimisation.\n5. **Do not cache failures.** Errors, refusals, truncated streams and low-judge-score answers are not\n   cached, or an outage gets remembered after it ends.\n6. **Stampede control.** Single-flight locking or request coalescing on popular keys, so a thousand\n   simultaneous misses produce one model call.\n\n### Where each layer lives\n- **Prefix caching:** in the provider or engine.\n- **Exact and semantic caches:** in a gateway (convenient but context-blind) or in the application (knows\n  tenants and document versions; usually the better place).\n- **Observe per layer:** hit rate, cost saved and latency saved, sampled false-hit rate, and the age of\n  served entries.","example":"### Exact-match cache in Redis\nVersioned, tenant-scoped keys, single-flight on misses, a rule against caching partial or refused answers,\nand a reverse index so the ingestion pipeline can purge every cached answer that used a changed document.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time, redis\nr = redis.Redis()\nVERSIONS = {\"prompt\": \"support-v14\", \"index\": \"kb-2026-10-19\", \"embed\": \"text-embed-v2\"}\n\ndef exact_key(tenant, model, params, messages):\n    body = json.dumps({\"v\": VERSIONS, \"t\": tenant, \"m\": model, \"p\": params, \"msgs\": messages},\n                      sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))      # canonical form: same input, same hash\n    return \"resp:\" + hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()\n\ndef cached_complete(tenant, model, messages, params={\"temperature\": 0}, ttl=6 * 3600, tries=10):\n    k = exact_key(tenant, model, messages=messages, params=params)\n    if (hit := r.get(k)):\n        return json.loads(hit) | {\"cache\": \"exact\"}\n    if not r.set(k + \":lock\", \"1\", nx=True, ex=30):              # single-flight: one caller fills the key\n        if tries == 0:\n            return call_model(model, messages, **params) | {\"cache\": \"bypass\"}\n        time.sleep(0.2)\n        return cached_complete(tenant, model, messages, params, ttl, tries - 1)\n    try:\n        out = call_model(model, messages, **params)\n        if out[\"finish_reason\"] == \"stop\" and not out.get(\"refusal\"):   # never cache partial or refused\n            r.set(k, json.dumps(out), ex=ttl)\n            for doc_id in out.get(\"source_doc_ids\", []):             # reverse index for event-driven purge\n                r.sadd(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\", k)\n                r.expire(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\", ttl)\n        return out | {\"cache\": \"miss\"}\n    finally:\n        r.delete(k + \":lock\")\n\ndef on_document_updated(tenant, doc_id):                         # called by the ingestion pipeline\n    keys = r.smembers(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\")\n    if keys:\n        r.delete(*keys)\n    r.delete(f\"deps:{tenant}:{doc_id}\")\n```\n\n- **Bumping `VERSIONS[\"prompt\"]` at deploy time** cold-starts the cache without any purge code.\n- **The reverse index handles what versioning cannot:** a single document changing under an unchanged\n  prompt.\n- **Simplification 1, the lock:** it is released unconditionally, so a model call that outlives the lock's\n  30 s expiry deletes a lock another caller now holds. Store a random token and delete only if it still\n  matches (a small Lua compare-and-delete).\n- **Simplification 2, the purge race:** described above; it needs the document versions stored with each\n  answer and checked on read. Both fixes follow.\n\n### Hardening: token lock and version-checked reads\nThe two fixes, as drop-in helpers. Capture document versions at *retrieval* time, before generating, so an\nanswer built from an old document carries the old version.\n\n```python\nimport json, uuid, redis\nr = redis.Redis()\n\nRELEASE = r.register_script(\"\"\"\nif redis.call('get', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('del', KEYS[1]) end\nreturn 0\n\"\"\")                                                          # compare-and-delete, atomic in Redis\n\ndef acquire(k, ttl=30):\n    token = uuid.uuid4().hex                                  # unique per caller\n    return token if r.set(k + \":lock\", token, nx=True, ex=ttl) else None\n\ndef release(k, token):\n    RELEASE(keys=[k + \":lock\"], args=[token])                 # deletes the lock only if it is still ours\n\ndef read_versions(tenant, doc_ids):                           # call at RETRIEVAL time, before generating\n    vals = r.mget([f\"docver:{tenant}:{d}\" for d in doc_ids])  # ingestion writes a content hash per doc\n    return {d: (v.decode() if v else None) for d, v in zip(doc_ids, vals)}\n\ndef store(k, out, versions, ttl):\n    r.set(k, json.dumps(out | {\"doc_versions\": versions}), ex=ttl)\n\ndef get_if_current(tenant, k):\n    hit = r.get(k)\n    if not hit:\n        return None\n    entry = json.loads(hit)\n    used = entry.get(\"doc_versions\", {})\n    if used and read_versions(tenant, list(used)) != used:    # a source changed since generation: a miss\n        return None\n    return entry\n```\n\n### Worked economics: is the semantic cache paying?\nIllustrative numbers plugged into the savings formula; similarity thresholds are examples, not\nrecommendations.\n\n```python\ndef semantic_savings(hit_rate, false_hit_rate, cost_miss, cost_hit, cost_wrong, embed_cost):\n    \"\"\"Expected $ saved per request (rates are fractions of all requests).\"\"\"\n    return hit_rate * (cost_miss - cost_hit) - false_hit_rate * cost_wrong - embed_cost\n\n# Illustrative: $0.01 per model call, $0.0001 per cache hit, $0.00002 to embed the query,\n# $0.50 for a wrong answer (a support escalation). Thresholds trade hit rate for false hits.\nfor threshold, hit, false_hit in ((0.97, 0.30, 0.003), (0.92, 0.45, 0.012)):\n    s = semantic_savings(hit, false_hit, 0.01, 0.0001, 0.50, 0.00002)\n    print(f\"threshold {threshold}: hit {hit:.0%}, false hits {false_hit:.1%} -> \"\n          f\"net {s * 100_000:+,.0f} USD per 100k requests\")\n# threshold 0.97: hit 30%, false hits 0.3% -> net +145 USD per 100k requests\n# threshold 0.92: hit 45%, false hits 1.2% -> net -156 USD per 100k requests\n```\n\n| Threshold | Hit rate | False-hit rate | Net per 100k requests |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.97 (strict) | 30% | 0.3% | +$145 |\n| 0.92 (loose) | 45% | 1.2% | −$156: more hits, but wrong answers cost more than the tokens saved |","patterns":["**Static-first prompts with explicit cache points.** Put system prompt, tools and long documents first; on Anthropic mark the end of the static part with `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`, while OpenAI caches long shared prefixes automatically. Monitor `cache_read_input_tokens` (Anthropic) or `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` (OpenAI) as a share of input after every prompt change.","**Prefix caching on self-hosted engines.** Turn on vLLM's automatic prefix caching (the `enable_prefix_caching` engine option; recent releases enable it by default) and keep shared prefixes byte-identical across requests. Monitor the engine's prefix-cache hit rate and time to first token.","**Version bump plus warm-up replay.** Put every dependency version in the key, and after a deploy replay the most frequent recent queries against the new version before shifting traffic. Monitor hit rate in the first hour after deploy and p95 latency during the cold window.","**Erasure-safe purge.** Write the reverse index and document versions at generation time, call the purge from the same ingestion hook that deletes from the vector index, and check versions on read. Monitor purge counts per erasure request and any hit whose source document no longer exists.","**Sampled false-hit audit.** Send a small share of semantic hits (say 1%) to a judge or reviewer that asks whether the cached answer addresses the new query. Monitor false-hit rate per threshold and per feature, and tighten the threshold when it rises.","**Jittered TTLs per data class.** Set TTL by volatility (seconds for prices, hours for catalogue data) and add random jitter (for example up to 10% of the TTL) so popular keys do not expire together. Monitor latency and cost spikes at TTL multiples and the age of served entries."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Request: tenant, model, params, messages\"] --> K[\"Build key with model, prompt, index and embed versions plus tenant\"]\n    K --> E{\"Exact hit with current doc versions?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| R1[\"Return cached answer\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| S{\"Semantic hit above threshold, same scope?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes, borderline verified\"| R2[\"Return cached answer, labelled\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| L[\"Single-flight lock, call model with static-first prompt\"]\n    L -->|\"prefix reuse in provider or engine\"| M[\"Model response\"]\n    M --> C{\"Complete, not refused?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| W[(\"Store answer, doc versions, reverse index\")]\n    C -->|\"no\"| R3[\"Return without caching\"]\n    D[\"Document updated or erased\"] -.->|\"purge dependents via reverse index\"| W","caption":"The request path through the response caches, cheapest check first, and the two invalidation routes:\nversions in the key make old entries unreachable, while document events purge through the reverse index."},"interview":[{"q":"What goes into the cache key for an LLM response, and why each part?","a":"- **Model id and version, and sampling parameters,** because outputs are sampled and a provider update or\n  temperature change means a different correct response.\n- **The full normalised messages, tool schemas and response format,** because any of them changes the\n  answer.\n- **Prompt template, index and embedding versions, plus tenant or user scope** whenever the prompt carries\n  private context, so a deploy cold-starts the cache and no answer crosses a trust boundary."},{"q":"Prompt caching is enabled but the bill did not change. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the cached-token share** in usage reports, such as `cache_read_input_tokens` on Anthropic or\n  `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` on OpenAI.\n- **If it is near zero, look at the top of the prompt:** a timestamp, the user's name or unstable ordering\n  changes the prefix, invalidating everything after it.\n- **Check the minimum cacheable prefix:** most providers need roughly a thousand tokens or more, so a short\n  system prompt alone may never cache."},{"q":"When would you not use a semantic cache?","a":"- **For personalised or transactional queries,** where an answer depends on the user, an account or a live\n  value.\n- **When a wrong answer is expensive:** at $0.50 per wrong answer, loosening the threshold from 0.97 to 0.92\n  turns +$145 into −$156 per 100k requests in the illustrative example.\n- **For queries with numbers, dates, names or negations,** unless they are excluded or entity-keyed, since a\n  one-detail paraphrase is a different question."},{"q":"A user must be erased. How do you make sure the cache stops answering from their document?","a":"- **Event-driven purge:** generation writes a reverse index from document id to cache keys, and the same\n  ingestion hook that deletes from the vector index purges those keys.\n- **Close the in-flight race:** store each answer's document versions and treat a hit as a miss when any\n  version is no longer current, because a generation that read the old document can finish after the\n  purge.\n- **TTL alone is not enough,** because it bounds staleness but never eliminates it."},{"q":"Popular keys cause periodic latency and cost spikes. What is happening and what do you change?","a":"- **A stampede on expiry:** many simultaneous misses on one hot key each call the model.\n- **Single-flight locking or request coalescing** turns a thousand misses into one call; use a token lock with\n  compare-and-delete so a slow call cannot release another caller's lock.\n- **Jitter TTLs** so popular keys do not expire together, or use stale-while-revalidate where staleness is\n  not incorrectness."}],"tradeoffs":["**Hit rate vs false hits.** Looser keys and lower similarity thresholds raise both together; the right point depends on what a wrong answer costs, which differs between an FAQ bot and a billing assistant.","**Savings vs staleness.** Longer TTLs save more and serve staler data; acceptable with event-driven purge for the content that matters, dangerous when TTL is the only invalidation you have.","**Simple versioning vs cold starts.** Versioned keys are simple and safe but every deploy cold-starts the cache; when the cache is what meets the latency SLO, add a warm-up replay or a brief dual-version window.","**Prefix hits vs layout freedom.** Prefix caching needs a static-first layout, which constrains where personalisation can go; it flips only in the rare design where per-user content must precede the shared instructions.","**Global vs per-tenant cache.** A global cache maximises hits and is a leak surface; a per-tenant cache is safer with a lower hit rate. The trust boundary, not the hit rate, decides.","**Precise purge vs cheap TTL.** Event-driven purge is precise but requires dependency tracking storage and hooks in ingestion; TTL is cheap but can only bound staleness, never eliminate it."],"pitfalls":["**Caching across tenants or users.** Symptom: a user sees another customer's data inside an answer, a security incident rather than a bug.","**Dynamic content at the top of the prompt.** Symptom: cached-token share near zero while prompt caching is \"enabled\" and the bill is unchanged.","**Semantic false hits.** Symptom: confident wrong answers whose logs show a high-similarity hit on a question that differed by a negation or a number.","**Model or prompt version missing from the key.** Symptom: half of users see the old behaviour for hours after a prompt change, and A/B results are contaminated.","**Caching errors, refusals or partial streams.** Symptom: users still receive an error message long after the provider recovered.","**Stampedes on expiry.** Symptom: periodic latency and cost spikes aligned with TTL multiples.","**Purging without guarding against in-flight generations.** Symptom: a stale answer reappears minutes after a purge that logged success, typically when a document is edited during busy hours.","**A deleted document still answered from cache.** Symptom: a compliance finding after an erasure request that the index honoured and the cache did not."],"when":"- **Prefix caching:** always worth having; mostly a matter of prompt layout.\n- **Exact response cache:** pays off for form-like, classification and extraction traffic with\n  deterministic settings.\n- **Semantic cache:** suits high-volume FAQ-like traffic where a measured false-hit rate is tolerable;\n  avoid it for personalised or transactional queries.\n- **Embedding cache:** belongs in every ingestion pipeline.\n- **Measure:** hit rate per layer, cost and latency saved, sampled false-hit rate and the age of served\n  entries; treat erasure requests as a cache concern.\n- **Interviews:** lead with the key. List what the answer depends on, show how versions make invalidation\n  trivial, and explain when you still need event-driven purge.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the versioned key function, scoping, single-flight and metrics, and lays prompts out static-first so provider prefix caching actually hits.","architect":"Decides which layers exist and where they live, the staleness budget per data class, the cross-tenant policy and the purge obligations that compliance imposes.","fde":"Tunes thresholds and TTLs to the customer's data volatility, demonstrates the cost saving with before-and-after usage, and verifies that erasure requests purge cached answers."},"check":{"q":"You added a semantic cache and cost fell by 30%. Two weeks later a customer reports that the\nassistant stated a policy that changed last week. Diagnose and redesign.","a":"The cache served a stale answer, and there are several candidate causes to check in the logs: a\nTTL longer than the policy document's change cycle, no event-driven purge when the document was\nre-ingested, an index version missing from the cache scope so the old answer survived the re-index, or a\nsemantic hit on a paraphrase of a question about the updated section. The redesign: put the index\nversion in the scope key so re-indexing cold-starts the relevant entries; have generation record the\ndocument ids it used and purge dependents on document update; set TTLs per content class, short for\npolicy; verify borderline semantic hits with a cheap check or exclude queries mentioning dates and policy\nnames; sample hits for a false-hit rate; and show the source freshness on cached answers so users can\nsee what the answer was based on."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the simplest robust way to invalidate every cached response when the prompt template changes?","options":["Flush the whole cache by hand after each deploy","Reduce the TTL to one minute","Include the prompt template version in every cache key","Rely on semantic similarity to notice that the prompt changed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Versioned keys make the old entries unreachable the moment the version changes, with no purge code and no risk of forgetting. A short TTL still serves stale answers for a minute and destroys the hit rate; manual flushes get skipped."},{"q":"Why does placing a timestamp at the top of the system prompt hurt?","options":["It increases the token count significantly","It changes the prefix on every request, so provider or engine prefix caching never matches","Models ignore timestamps and it is wasted space","Providers reject prompts that contain dates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefix caching requires a byte-identical prefix from the first token. A changing value near the top invalidates the entire shared span after it, which shows up as a cached-token share near zero."},{"q":"Which query pair is the biggest false-hit risk for a semantic cache?","options":["\"How do I cancel my subscription?\" and \"how do i cancel my subcription\" (typo)","\"How do I cancel my subscription?\" and \"How do I stop my subscription being cancelled?\"","\"What is your refund policy?\" and \"Tell me about refunds\"","\"Reset my password\" and \"I forgot my password\""],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second pair is nearly identical in surface form and embedding space but opposite in intent, so a cached answer to one is harmful for the other. The other pairs are genuine paraphrases, which is exactly what a semantic cache is meant to match."}]},{"key":"system-design/rag-architecture","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Cormack et al., 2009 — Reciprocal Rank Fusion outperforms Condorcet and individual Rank Learning Methods","Liu et al., 2023 — Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts"],"why":"- **Weights are a frozen public snapshot.** A model answers from its weights and its context window.\n  The weights do not contain your pricing table, last week's incident report or the customer's\n  contract, and cannot be told that user A may see document X and user B may not.\n- **Fine-tuning does not fix it.** It teaches style and format reliably and facts unreliably, is stale\n  the moment a document changes, and has no notion of provenance or per-user access, so you can\n  neither cite a source nor revoke one.\n- **Stuffing the context window stops scaling.** Right for a handful of pages (see the\n  long-context-vs-RAG lesson), but cost and latency scale with input tokens on every request: a 500-page\n  knowledge base read on every question is unaffordable even when it fits.\n- **More context can mean worse answers.** Attention quality degrades when relevant material is buried\n  in the middle of a very long prompt (the lost-in-the-middle effect), and access control still has to\n  happen somewhere before the prompt is built.\n- **Retrieval solves selection.** Pick the few passages that matter for this query, for this user, right\n  now, and let the model read and synthesize. The architecture makes that selection fast, fresh,\n  permission-aware and measurable.","how":"### The offline pipeline: documents to index\n1. **Ingest.** Connectors pull documents from sources (wikis, tickets, object storage, databases) with\n   metadata: owner, ACL, timestamps, source URL. Ingestion is event- or schedule-driven *because*\n   freshness is a property of this path and nothing downstream can repair a stale index.\n2. **Parse and normalize.** PDFs, HTML and slides become clean text with structure preserved (headings,\n   tables). Most silent quality loss happens here; garbage in here is unrecoverable at query time.\n3. **Chunk.** Split text into retrieval units, typically a few hundred tokens, with overlap or\n   parent–child linking. Chunks are the unit of retrieval *because* an embedding summarizes one idea\n   well and a whole document badly, and you want to pay for a paragraph of context, not a chapter.\n4. **Embed and index.** Each chunk gets a dense vector and usually a sparse (BM25) representation,\n   stored with the chunk text and metadata in a store that supports approximate nearest-neighbour search\n   plus filtering.\n   - Record the embedding model version with every vector *because* vectors from different models are\n     not comparable, so a model upgrade means a re-index.\n\n### The online pipeline: query to answer\n1. **Query understanding.** Optionally rewrite the query: expand acronyms, resolve \"it\" from the\n   conversation, split multi-part questions. The raw user message is often a poor search query.\n2. **Retrieve.** Run dense and sparse search with metadata and ACL filters applied *inside* the search,\n   not after, *because* filtering after top-k can leave a user with zero permitted results and leaks the\n   existence of forbidden ones. Fuse the ranked lists (see below).\n3. **Rerank.** A cross-encoder scores (query, chunk) pairs jointly and reorders the top 50–100\n   candidates. First-stage retrieval optimizes recall cheaply; the reranker buys precision for the\n   handful of chunks you will actually pay to put in the prompt.\n4. **Assemble context.** Select the top chunks under a token budget, drop near-duplicates, order them\n   deliberately (most relevant first or last, never buried), and attach citation handles.\n5. **Generate.** Instruct the model to answer only from the provided passages and to cite them;\n   structured output can enforce the citation format. A grounding check (does each claim map to a\n   passage?) can run as a post-step.\n6. **Observe.** Log the query, retrieved ids, scores, the final prompt and the answer under one trace\n   id. Without this you cannot distinguish \"retrieval missed\" from \"model ignored the passage\", and\n   those are fixed in different places.\n\n### Fusing ranked lists: reciprocal rank fusion\nReciprocal rank fusion is the common default:\n\n`score(d) = Σ_lists 1 / (k + rank_list(d))` with k ≈ 60.\n\n- **It fuses ranks, not raw scores,** *because* a BM25 score and a cosine similarity live on different\n  scales and cannot be added.\n- **Agreement beats a single strong vote.** With k = 60, a chunk ranked 1st by dense search only\n  scores 1/61 ≈ 0.0164; a chunk ranked 3rd in both lists scores 2/63 ≈ 0.0317. Agreement between two\n  independent retrievers is exactly the behaviour you want from fusion.\n- **The large k flattens the curve,** so rank 1 versus rank 5 matters little and presence in a list\n  matters a lot.\n\n### Single-shot versus iterative retrieval\n| | Single-shot | Iterative (agentic RAG, or decompose chain) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retrievals per question | one | many |\n| Right for | most lookups | multi-hop questions |\n| Latency and cost | predictable | multiplied per question |\n\n- **Why single-shot fails on multi-hop.** In \"which of our customers on the legacy plan were affected\n  by last week's incident?\" the second hop's query depends on the first hop's answer, and no single\n  embedding of the original question finds both.\n- **Two fixes.** Expose `search` as a tool and let a model call it in a loop (agentic RAG), or run a\n  fixed decompose → retrieve-per-sub-question → merge chain.\n- **Route to them sparingly:** only when a classifier or the first pass signals that one retrieval was\n  not enough.\n\n### Where the boundaries go\n- **Retrieval is its own service** with a typed interface, roughly `search(query, user, filters, k)\n  -> [chunk]`, *because* it is shared by chat, agents and batch jobs, has its own scaling profile (CPU-\n  and IO-bound, cacheable), and is the component you evaluate independently with recall@k.\n- **The index is derived, never authoritative.** Source systems stay the source of truth and the index\n  is a rebuildable cache, which makes re-chunking and re-embedding a routine job rather than a migration.\n\n### Sizing intuition (illustrative)\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Corpus | 50,000 documents × 20 chunks each = 1,000,000 chunks |\n| One 1024-dimension fp32 vector | 4 KB |\n| Raw vectors | about 4 GB, before the ANN index and the chunk text |\n| Prompt evidence | retrieve 50 candidates, keep 8 chunks of roughly 300 tokens ≈ 2,400 tokens |\n\n- **pgvector is comfortable at this scale;** a dedicated vector database earns its keep one or two\n  orders of magnitude later.\n- **Evidence cost is fixed per question regardless of corpus size,** which is the whole economic\n  argument for retrieval over stuffing.","example":"### The online path in code\nHybrid retrieval with reciprocal rank fusion, a cross-encoder reranker, and token-budgeted context\nassembly with the ACL filter inside the search. The `store`, `reranker`, `embed` and `log_trace` calls\nstand in for your vector database, BM25 index, embedding model and tracing; the pipeline shape is what\nmatters.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef rrf(ranked_lists, k=60):\n    \"\"\"Reciprocal rank fusion: ids near the top of any list win.\"\"\"\n    score = defaultdict(float)\n    for lst in ranked_lists:\n        for rank, chunk_id in enumerate(lst, start=1):\n            score[chunk_id] += 1.0 / (k + rank)\n    return sorted(score, key=score.get, reverse=True)\n\ndef retrieve(query, user, store, reranker, k_dense=40, k_sparse=40, budget_tokens=2400):\n    acl = {\"tenant\": user.tenant, \"groups\": user.groups}          # filter inside the search\n    dense = store.vector_search(embed(query), k=k_dense, filters=acl)\n    sparse = store.bm25_search(query, k=k_sparse, filters=acl)\n    candidates = rrf([dense, sparse])[:60]\n    chunks = store.fetch(candidates)                                # text + metadata\n    scores = reranker.score(query, [c.text for c in chunks])       # joint (query, chunk) scoring\n    ordered = [c for _, c in sorted(zip(scores, chunks), key=lambda p: -p[0])]\n    context, used = [], 0\n    for c in ordered:                                               # dedupe step omitted for brevity\n        if used + c.tokens > budget_tokens:\n            break\n        context.append(c); used += c.tokens\n    return context\n\ndef answer(query, user, store, reranker, llm):\n    ctx = retrieve(query, user, store, reranker)\n    passages = \"\\n\\n\".join(f\"[{i+1}] ({c.source}) {c.text}\" for i, c in enumerate(ctx))\n    prompt = (\"Answer using only the passages below and cite passage numbers. \"\n              \"If the passages do not contain the answer, say so.\\n\\n\"\n              f\"{passages}\\n\\nQuestion: {query}\")\n    out = llm(prompt)\n    log_trace(query=query, chunk_ids=[c.id for c in ctx], answer=out)   # one trace id per request\n    return out\n```\n\n### Runnable: why fusion rewards agreement\nPure Python, no dependencies. Chunk `a` is 1st in dense only; chunk `b` is 3rd in both lists.\n\n```python\ndef rrf_scores(ranked_lists, k=60):\n    s = {}\n    for lst in ranked_lists:\n        for rank, cid in enumerate(lst, start=1):\n            s[cid] = s.get(cid, 0.0) + 1.0 / (k + rank)\n    return dict(sorted(s.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]))\n\ndense  = [\"a\", \"x\", \"b\", \"y\"]\nsparse = [\"p\", \"q\", \"b\", \"z\"]\nfor cid, sc in rrf_scores([dense, sparse]).items():\n    print(cid, round(sc, 4))\n# b 0.0317   <- 3rd in both lists: 2/63\n# a 0.0164   <- 1st in one list only: 1/61\n# p 0.0164\n# ...\n```\n\n### Worked incident: contractors get \"no information\"\nSame question, two user groups, post-filtering in place (global top-k = 8, then ACL filter). Numbers\nare illustrative.\n\n| Step | Employee query | Contractor query |\n|---|---|---|\n| Global top-8 retrieved | 8 employee-only HR chunks; public policy ranks 11th | same 8 chunks |\n| ACL filter after top-k | 8 permitted | 0 permitted |\n| Answer | correct, cited | \"I don't have information on that\" |\n| After pushing ACL into search | unchanged | top-8 over permitted docs, public policy now in the set |","patterns":["**ACL predicate inside the vector query.** When multiple tenants or groups share an index, put the filter in the same query as the similarity order (in pgvector, a `WHERE tenant_id = ...` clause next to `ORDER BY embedding <=> ...`). With HNSW and selective filters, enable pgvector's iterative index scans (0.8.0+, `SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order`) so a filter cannot starve top-k. Monitor zero-result rate per user group.","**Versioned embeddings with blue/green re-index.** Store the embedding model name on every row, build the new index side by side, run the golden query set against both, then switch the read alias. Monitor recall@k before and after cutover and keep the old index until it holds.","**Golden query set as a CI gate.** Keep labeled (query, expected chunk ids) pairs per domain and compute recall@k and reranker precision on every chunking, embedding or prompt change. Fail the build on a regression beyond a threshold you set. Monitor faithfulness separately on sampled live traffic.","**One trace per request.** Emit query, rewritten query, retrieved ids and scores, final prompt and answer under one trace id (for example as OpenTelemetry span attributes). Review traces tagged by user feedback and classify each failure as \"retrieval missed\" or \"model ignored the passage\".","**Index lag and deletion tracking.** Drive ingestion from source change events where possible and propagate deletes as tombstones. Monitor lag as source `updated_at` minus indexed time, and alert when deleted documents still appear in citations."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source systems\"] --> I[\"Ingest and parse\"]\n    I --> CH[\"Chunk\"]\n    CH --> E[\"Embed dense and sparse\"]\n    E --> X[(\"Index: vectors, BM25, ACL metadata\")]\n    Q[\"User query\"] --> R[\"Query rewrite\"]\n    R -->|\"dense and sparse search, ACL filter inside\"| X\n    X -->|\"RRF fused candidates\"| RR[\"Cross-encoder rerank\"]\n    RR -->|\"top chunks under token budget\"| A[\"Assemble context with citations\"]\n    A --> G[\"LLM answers from passages\"]\n    G --> T[\"Trace: ids, scores, prompt, answer\"]","caption":"The left branch is the offline pipeline that builds the index; the right branch is the online query\npath, which meets the index and continues down to the answer. Note that the ACL filter runs inside the\nsearch, before fusion and reranking."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use retrieval instead of fine-tuning the model on your documents?","a":"- **Fine-tuning teaches style, not facts.** It is reliable for format and tone, unreliable for facts,\n  and stale the moment a document changes.\n- **It has no provenance or access control.** You cannot cite a fine-tuned fact or revoke it for one\n  user, while retrieval selects permitted passages per request and attaches citation handles."},{"q":"Users say answers are wrong. How do you tell a retrieval failure from a generation failure?","a":"- **Start from the trace.** Pull the logged query, retrieved chunk ids, scores and final prompt for\n  the bad answers under their trace id.\n- **Right passage absent:** a retrieval problem; check recall@k on the golden set, parsing quality and\n  chunking before touching the prompt.\n- **Right passage present but ignored or blended:** a generation or context-assembly problem; check\n  ordering, the token budget and near-duplicate chunks crowding the evidence."},{"q":"Why fuse dense and sparse results with reciprocal rank fusion rather than adding their scores?","a":"- **Scores live on different scales.** A BM25 score and a cosine similarity cannot be added, so RRF\n  uses ranks: `score(d) = Σ 1 / (k + rank)` with k ≈ 60.\n- **It rewards agreement.** A chunk ranked 3rd in both lists scores about 0.0317, beating a chunk\n  ranked 1st in one list only at about 0.0164."},{"q":"When would you not build a RAG pipeline?","a":"- **Small, stable knowledge:** a long system prompt with prompt caching is simpler than an index.\n- **Structured data questions:** give the model a SQL or API tool instead of embedding rows.\n- **Behaviour rather than knowledge:** if the gap is tone or format, change the prompt or fine-tune;\n  retrieval only fixes what the model does not know."},{"q":"Roughly how big is the index for 50,000 documents, and what does each question cost in evidence?","a":"- **Vectors:** 50,000 documents × 20 chunks is 1,000,000 chunks; at 4 KB per 1024-dimension fp32\n  vector that is about 4 GB before the ANN index and chunk text, comfortable for pgvector.\n- **Evidence per question:** retrieve 50 candidates, keep 8 chunks of about 300 tokens, roughly 2,400\n  tokens, and that cost stays fixed as the corpus grows."},{"q":"How do you upgrade the embedding model without a recall outage?","a":"- **Vectors from different models are not comparable,** so every vector must be re-embedded; mixing\n  them collapses recall for one half of the corpus.\n- **Blue/green re-index:** build the new index beside the old one, run the golden query set against\n  both, switch the read alias, and keep the old index until recall@k holds in production."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fresh, citable answers vs an extra hop.** You get fresh, citable, permission-aware answers but pay a retrieval hop on every request. When the corpus is small and static enough to fit the window cheaply, long context wins and the index is overhead.","**Hybrid plus rerank vs operational load.** Answer quality rises measurably, but two more models and services must be operated. If queries are dominated by exact identifiers (SKUs, error codes), sparse retrieval alone may be enough.","**Small chunks vs lost context.** Small chunks improve retrieval precision while losing surrounding context; parent–document retrieval buys both at the cost of a second lookup and more prompt tokens.","**Derived index vs eventual consistency.** A rebuildable index makes re-embedding routine, but consistency with sources is eventual. Where staleness is a correctness bug (prices, permissions) you need synchronous invalidation or a live lookup tool instead.","**Separate retrieval service vs a network boundary.** Independent scaling and evaluation, at the cost of a network boundary and a contract that must be versioned."],"pitfalls":["**Filtering by ACL after retrieval.** Symptom: users get \"no results\" for questions the system clearly has documents about, or worse, a forbidden title appears in a citation. Fix: filter inside the search.","**Treating parsing as solved.** Symptom: retrieval returns the right document but its chunk text is a mangled table, so the model answers confidently and wrongly. Fix: inspect parsed output per source type.","**Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing everything.** Symptom: a sudden recall collapse that only affects documents ingested after the upgrade, or only those before it. Fix: re-embed the whole corpus into a new index and cut over, never mix model versions.","**Judging by end-to-end answer quality alone.** Without recall@k on a labeled query set you cannot tell retrieval failures from generation failures, and you end up tuning prompts to fix an index problem. Fix: measure retrieval and generation separately.","**Adding chunks to paper over retrieval misses.** Symptom: cost per question climbs while faithfulness falls, because the right passage is now buried among near-misses and the model blends them. Fix: improve recall (parsing, chunking, hybrid, rerank) instead of widening the context.","**Letting the index become the system of record.** Hand-edited chunks, data that exists nowhere else. Symptom: a re-chunking project that nobody dares to run. Fix: write corrections back to the source."],"when":"- **Use when:** answers must come from content that is private, changing, large or access-controlled,\n  and users need citations they can check.\n- **Avoid when:** the knowledge is small and stable (a long system prompt with prompt caching is\n  simpler), the task is precise querying of structured data (give the model a SQL or API tool), or the\n  \"knowledge\" is really behaviour (prompt or fine-tune).\n- **Measure:** retrieval and generation separately: recall@k and reranker precision on a golden query\n  set, plus faithfulness of answers to the retrieved passages.\n- **Day to day:** two deployables with different cadences, indexing jobs and a query service, sharing\n  one contract.\n- **Interviews:** draw both pipelines, place the ACL filter and the reranker correctly, and explain why\n  the index is derived rather than authoritative.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the chunk-embed-index jobs and the query service, wires hybrid search and reranking, and owns the retrieval eval harness that catches regressions before users do.","architect":"Decides the service boundary, the vector store (pgvector versus dedicated), the freshness and consistency model, and how tenancy and ACLs are enforced inside search.","fde":"Maps a customer's document sources, permissions and update frequency onto the pipeline, and explains why parsing quality and ACL propagation dominate the project timeline."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that the assistant answers policy questions correctly for most employees but\ntells contractors \"I don't have information on that\", even for public policies. Where in the RAG\narchitecture would you look first, and why?","a":"The pattern, correct for one group and empty for another on the same public content, points at\naccess control interacting with retrieval, not at the model. The most likely cause is post-filtering:\nthe search returns a global top-k, then ACL filtering removes everything contractors cannot see, and\nbecause employees' private documents outrank the public policy, contractors are left with nothing. The\nfix is to push the ACL predicate into the search query so top-k is computed over permitted documents\nonly. I would verify by logging retrieved ids before and after filtering for a contractor query, and\ncheck that public policies carry the correct ACL metadata at ingestion, since a mis-tagged document in\nthe offline pipeline would produce the same symptom."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the index treated as derived data rather than the system of record?","options":["Vector databases cannot store the original text","It keeps re-chunking and re-embedding a safe, repeatable rebuild instead of a data migration","Regulations prohibit storing documents in more than one place","Derived indexes are faster to query than authoritative ones"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If sources remain authoritative, the index can be dropped and rebuilt whenever chunking or the embedding model changes. Making it authoritative turns every retrieval improvement into a risky migration."},{"q":"Where should per-user access control be applied in the query path?","options":["After generation, by redacting the answer","After top-k retrieval, by removing forbidden chunks","Inside the retrieval query, so top-k is computed over permitted documents only","In the embedding model, by training it on permitted documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Filtering after top-k can leave a user with zero permitted results and still reveals what was ranked. Pushing the predicate into search keeps recall for permitted documents and leaks nothing."},{"q":"What does a reranker add that first-stage hybrid retrieval does not?","options":["Higher recall across the whole corpus","Joint scoring of each (query, chunk) pair, buying precision for the few chunks that enter the prompt","Access-control filtering","Freshness, by re-fetching documents from their sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"First-stage retrieval scores query and chunk independently for speed and recall. A cross-encoder reads them together and is more accurate, but is too slow to run over the whole corpus, so it is applied only to the shortlist."}]},{"key":"system-design/agentic-architecture","sources":["Yao et al., 2022 — ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents"],"why":"- **Some tasks have no fixed path.** \"Resolve this support ticket\", \"find out why this deploy failed\",\n  \"reconcile these two ledgers\" take a different number of steps each time, the next step depends on\n  what the previous one revealed, and the space of paths is too large to enumerate as branches.\n- **A fixed prompt chain ossifies.** Classify, extract, look up, draft is predictable and testable, and\n  often right (see workflows vs agents). It fails on unanticipated branches: two lookup candidates, a\n  document in an unexpected language, a ticket that is really three. Each becomes another hard-coded\n  branch.\n- **\"Give the model every tool and let it run\" fails the other way.** Demos come quickly and incidents\n  shortly after: nothing bounds steps or cost, a crash loses everything because state lived in a Python\n  list, a production write tool is one bad call from damage, and nobody can reconstruct what happened.\n- **The discipline in between.** Let the model decide *what* to do next; build a system that decides\n  what it is *allowed* to do, how long it may keep going, what happens when it fails, and how every step\n  is recorded.","how":"### The core loop\n1. **Build the context:** instructions, task, state, recent observations.\n2. **Call the model** with a set of tool definitions.\n3. **Branch.** A tool call: validate the arguments, execute the tool, append the result as an\n   observation, repeat. A final answer: stop.\n\n- **ReAct underneath.** ReAct-style interleaving of reasoning and acting is the pattern; every agent\n  framework wraps this loop plus the boundaries below.\n- **Plan-and-execute variant.** The model writes an explicit plan first, runs steps against it, and\n  re-plans when an observation contradicts it. It costs an extra call but gives the runtime something to\n  checkpoint, show a human and measure progress against, which a pure ReAct loop does not.\n\n### The cost shape of the loop\nEach step re-sends the accumulated context, so input tokens grow with the *sum* of context sizes, not\nthe final size.\n\n```\ntotal_input = Σ(3k + i·1k) for i = 0…11 ≈ 102k input tokens over 12 steps   (final context only 14k)\n```\n\nThat near-quadratic growth (illustrative numbers) is why the architecture:\n- **caps steps;**\n- **stores large tool outputs as references** instead of pasting them;\n- **keeps the stable prefix byte-identical** (instructions, tool definitions) so provider prompt caching\n  can discount it.\n\n### The layers around the loop\n| Layer | Owns | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Interface | where tasks enter and events leave | runs are long; HTTP requests held open are fragile |\n| 2. Orchestration / runtime | loop, budgets, retries, checkpoints | runs must resume after restarts |\n| 3. Model gateway | routing, rate limits, caching, cost | steps multiply model calls |\n| 4. Tool layer | typed contracts, credentials, approvals | confidence is not permission |\n| 5. State and memory | task state, artifacts | context is the scarcest resource |\n| 6. Observability and audit | one event per step | evaluation and incident review |\n\n1. **Interface layer.** Tasks enter via chat UI, API, event or schedule. For anything over a few seconds\n   it is asynchronous: accept the task, return a run id, stream events, deliver the result later. Agents\n   are long-running *because* each step is a model call plus a tool call and the step count is unknown.\n2. **Orchestration and runtime layer.** Enforces step budget, token and cost budget, wall-clock timeout,\n   loop detection (same tool, same arguments, repeatedly), retries with backoff for transient tool\n   failures, and cancellation.\n   - It persists state after every step *because* a run that cannot resume after a worker restart will,\n     at scale, fail constantly for reasons unrelated to the task.\n   - Durable execution engines are one way to get this; a checkpoint store plus idempotent steps is\n     another.\n3. **Model gateway.** One place that calls models: routing, rate limiting, caching, cost accounting,\n   fallbacks. Agents multiply model calls by the number of steps, so cost control lives here, not in\n   prompts.\n4. **Tool layer.** Each tool is a typed contract: schema-validated inputs, structured outputs, an\n   explicit side-effect class (read-only versus consequential), and credentials scoped to that tool.\n   - **The agent never holds credentials;** the tool adapter does.\n   - **Consequential tools route through an approval gate,** human or policy, *because* the model's\n     confidence is not a permission.\n   - **Code-executing and browsing tools run in a sandbox** *because* their inputs may come from untrusted\n     content, which makes prompt injection a system-level threat rather than a prompt-level one.\n5. **State and memory.** Task state (plan, completed steps, artifacts) is separate from conversation\n   history and long-term memory, external and addressable by run id. Large tool outputs are stored as\n   artifacts and referenced *because* raw tool results are the fastest way to exhaust context.\n6. **Observability and audit.** Every step emits a structured event: model input hash, tool name,\n   arguments, result size, latency, cost, under a run id and tenant id. This makes trajectory evaluation\n   and incident review possible.\n\n### Where control flow should be code, and where model\n- **Model for judgment about content:** \"which document is relevant\".\n- **Code for guarantees:** \"may this user issue refunds above 500\" is enforced in the tool layer\n  regardless of what the model asks.\n- **Mixed systems are the production norm:** a deterministic workflow calls an agent for one open-ended\n  step, or an agent calls a deterministic sub-workflow as a tool.\n\n### Stopping\nEvery agent needs four explicit terminators; without all four the failure mode is not a wrong answer\nbut an unbounded one.\n1. **A final-answer action.**\n2. **A maximum number of steps.**\n3. **A budget.**\n4. **A stuck detector.**\n\nRecord each terminal state distinctly (`done`, `budget_exhausted`, `stuck`, `awaiting_approval`,\n`cancelled`) *because* they demand different follow-ups: a retry, a human, or a bug report.","example":"### A minimal runtime that shows the boundaries\nA budget, schema-validated tools with a side-effect flag, an approval gate that interrupts rather than\nblocks, and a checkpoint after every step. `call_model` is whatever model API you use and returns either\na tool call or a final answer; `validate` is a JSON Schema check.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nTOOLS = {   # name -> (input schema, needs_approval(args), implementation(args, idem_key))\n    \"search_tickets\": ({\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"q\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"q\"]},\n                       lambda a: False, lambda a, key: tickets.search(a[\"q\"])),\n    \"issue_refund\":   ({\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"ticket\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                        \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"exclusiveMinimum\": 0, \"maximum\": 5000}},\n                        \"required\": [\"ticket\", \"amount\"]},\n                       lambda a: a[\"amount\"] > 500,          # policy threshold, enforced in code\n                       lambda a, key: payments.refund(a[\"ticket\"], a[\"amount\"], idempotency_key=key)),\n}\n\ndef execute(run_id, state, name, args):\n    if name not in TOOLS:\n        return {\"error\": f\"unknown tool {name}\"}               # an observation, not a crash\n    schema, needs_approval, fn = TOOLS[name]\n    validate(args, schema)                                     # rejects amount > 5000 outright\n    if needs_approval(args) and not approvals.granted(run_id, name, args):\n        return \"PAUSE\"\n    try:\n        return fn(args, f\"{run_id}:{state['step']}\")         # same key on retry -> no double refund\n    except TransientError as e:\n        return {\"error\": str(e), \"retryable\": True}           # the model decides: retry or give up\n\ndef run(run_id, task, store, max_steps=12, budget_usd=0.50):\n    state = store.load(run_id) or {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}], \"step\": 0, \"cost\": 0.0}\n    while state[\"step\"] < max_steps and state[\"cost\"] < budget_usd:\n        if state.get(\"pending\"):                              # resuming after a pause: no new model call\n            msg, name, args = state[\"pending\"]\n        else:\n            reply = call_model(state[\"messages\"], tools=[{\"name\": n, \"schema\": t[0]} for n, t in TOOLS.items()])\n            state[\"cost\"] += reply.cost_usd\n            if reply.final is not None:\n                store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"done\", \"answer\": reply.final}); return reply.final\n            msg, name, args = reply.as_message(), reply.tool_name, reply.tool_args\n        result = execute(run_id, state, name, args)\n        if result == \"PAUSE\":\n            store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"awaiting_approval\", \"pending\": [msg, name, args]})\n            return None                                        # interrupt; an approval event re-invokes run()\n        state.pop(\"pending\", None)\n        state[\"messages\"] += [msg, {\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": name, \"content\": json.dumps(result)[:4000]}]\n        state[\"step\"] += 1\n        store.save(run_id, state)                              # checkpoint: a crash here loses nothing\n    store.save(run_id, {**state, \"status\": \"budget_exhausted\"})\n```\n\nThree boundaries are visible:\n- **Hard cap in the schema.** `maximum: 5000` rejects a larger refund however persuasively the model\n  argued for it.\n- **Policy threshold in code, gate as interrupt.** Above 500 needs approval. The gate saves the exact\n  pending call instead of holding a thread while a human decides; on resume the approved call runs\n  without asking the model again, so the human approved precisely what executes.\n- **Idempotency key from run id and step.** A worker that crashes after the refund but before the\n  checkpoint re-executes the step without paying twice.\n\nWhat the sketch leaves out, so you do not copy it as-is:\n- **Validation errors should be observations.** Here `validate` raises and ends the run; in production\n  catch the error and return it like the unknown-tool case, so the model can fix its arguments.\n- **One status covers two caps.** Hitting `max_steps` and hitting `budget_usd` both record\n  `budget_exhausted`; record them separately if you alert on them separately.\n\n### Walking one run through the runtime\n| Step | What happens | Status after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | `search_tickets` (read-only) runs | running |\n| 1 | model proposes a 750 refund; the predicate fires | `awaiting_approval`, zero further cost |\n| — | approver accepts; `run()` is re-invoked | running |\n| 1 (resume) | refund executes with key `run_id:1`, no new model call | running |\n| 2 | model writes the final answer | `done` |\n\n### Runnable: what offloading tool output saves\nPure Python, illustrative numbers. Same 12-step run, with raw tool output pasted (1k per step) versus\nartifacts stored by reference with a short summary (0.2k per step).\n\n```python\ndef loop_input_tokens(start_k, growth_k, steps):\n    sent = [start_k + i * growth_k for i in range(steps)]\n    return sum(sent), sent[-1]\n\nfor label, growth in [(\"raw output in context\", 1.0), (\"artifacts by reference\", 0.2)]:\n    total, last = loop_input_tokens(3, growth, 12)\n    print(f\"{label:24s} total={total:6.1f}k  last step={last:4.1f}k\")\n# raw output in context    total= 102.0k  last step=14.0k\n# artifacts by reference   total=  49.2k  last step= 5.2k\n```","patterns":["**Durable execution for the loop.** When runs last minutes to hours or touch money, run each model and tool call as a recorded step in a durable engine (for example a Temporal workflow with one activity per call) or a checkpoint store keyed by run id. Monitor runs resumed after restarts and runs stuck in a non-terminal state.","**Idempotency keys derived from run and step.** Pass `f\"{run_id}:{step}\"` to every consequential API that accepts one (payment providers such as Stripe take an `Idempotency-Key` header). Monitor duplicate side effects per thousand runs; it should be zero.","**Approval as interrupt, not wait.** Persist the pending call, notify the approver, end the worker, and resume from an approval event that executes the stored call verbatim. Monitor approval latency, the approval rate per tool, and runs that expire in `awaiting_approval`.","**Budgets enforced in the runtime and gateway.** Set max steps, max cost and a wall-clock timeout per run, plus a repeated-call stuck detector. Alert on the fraction of runs ending `budget_exhausted` or `stuck` per task type, because a rise usually means a tool or prompt regression.","**Cache-friendly stable prefix.** Put instructions and tool definitions first and keep them byte-identical across steps, so provider prompt caching can discount them. Monitor the cached-token share of input tokens that the provider reports in usage data."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Build context: instructions, task, state\"] --> B{\"Step or cost budget left?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| BX[\"budget_exhausted\"]\n    B -->|\"yes\"| M[\"Call model via gateway\"]\n    M -->|\"final answer\"| D[\"done\"]\n    M -->|\"tool call\"| V[\"Validate args against schema\"]\n    V --> G{\"Consequential and not yet approved?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Save pending call: awaiting_approval\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| X[\"Execute tool with idempotency key\"]\n    X --> K[(\"Checkpoint state by run id\")]\n    K -->|\"next step\"| C\n    P -.->|\"approval event resumes stored call\"| X","caption":"One step of the runtime loop. Follow the tool-call branch: validation, the approval gate and the\ncheckpoint all sit in code around the model, and every exit lands in a distinct terminal state."},"interview":[{"q":"What is an agentic architecture, and how does it differ from a prompt chain?","a":"- **Definition:** the model chooses the next action at runtime, observes the result and repeats, while\n  the system around it owns tools, state, budgets, approvals and tracing.\n- **Versus a chain:** a chain fixes the path in code, which is predictable and testable but ossifies on\n  unanticipated branches; an agent handles paths you cannot enumerate, at the cost of predictability."},{"q":"How would you stop an agent from issuing a refund it should not?","a":"- **Enforce in the tool layer, not the prompt.** A schema cap such as `maximum: 5000` rejects the call\n  outright, and a code predicate (amount above 500) routes it to approval.\n- **Gate as an interrupt.** Save the exact pending call, end the worker, and on approval execute that\n  stored call without asking the model again, so the human approves precisely what runs.\n- **Scope credentials to the adapter** so the agent never holds them, and a prompt-injected document\n  cannot borrow broader permissions."},{"q":"Why does agent cost grow faster than the context size suggests, and what do you do about it?","a":"- **Each step re-sends the accumulated context,** so input tokens follow the sum of context sizes: 12\n  steps starting at 3k and growing 1k per step send about 102k input tokens for a 14k final context.\n- **Levers:** cap steps, store large tool outputs as artifacts by reference (about 49.2k in the same\n  illustrative run at 0.2k growth), and keep the stable prefix byte-identical for prompt caching."},{"q":"When would you not build an agent?","a":"- **Known steps:** if the path is known in advance, a deterministic workflow is cheaper, faster and far\n  easier to evaluate.\n- **Tight latency or ungateable risk:** when a wrong action is costly and you cannot put an approval\n  gate in front of it, keep control flow in code and call a model only for one bounded judgment."},{"q":"Which terminal states do you record, and why keep them distinct?","a":"- **The four terminators** are a final-answer action, a step cap, a budget and a stuck detector;\n  without all four the failure is an unbounded run, not a wrong answer.\n- **Distinct states** (`done`, `budget_exhausted`, `stuck`, `awaiting_approval`, `cancelled`) map to\n  different follow-ups: a retry, a human, or a bug report, and their rates are what you alert on."}],"tradeoffs":["**Flexibility vs predictability.** Flexibility on unknown task paths costs predictability and testability. When the path is known in advance, a deterministic workflow is cheaper, faster and far easier to evaluate.","**Checkpointed state vs a write per step.** Externalized state makes runs resumable and auditable but adds a store write per step; for sub-second, read-only agents an in-memory loop is acceptable.","**Approval gates vs latency and human load.** Gates stop damage from bad tool calls but add delay and reviewer work; what counts as consequential is a product decision, not a technical one.","**Many tools vs selection accuracy.** More tools broaden what the agent can do while lowering tool-selection accuracy and inflating context. Narrow tool sets per task, or sub-agents, win once the list grows large (the threshold is model-dependent, so measure tool-selection accuracy rather than assume a number).","**Async with streamed events vs client complexity.** Fits long runs and survives restarts, but clients must handle partial results, resumption and cancellation."],"pitfalls":["**Keeping run state in process memory.** Symptom: runs vanish on deploys or autoscale events, and users who retry double the side effects. Fix: checkpoint by run id after every step.","**No budget or step cap.** Symptom: a single stuck run consumes a day's token budget while repeating the same failing tool call. Fix: all four terminators plus distinct terminal states.","**Trusting the model to enforce permissions** (\"only refund if the policy allows\"). Symptom: a prompt-injected document convinces the agent to call a write tool, which then succeeds. Fix: enforce in the tool layer.","**Letting the model re-propose an action after approval.** Symptom: an approved 750 refund executes as 800, because the second model call differed. Fix: execute the stored pending call.","**Pasting full tool outputs into context.** Symptom: cost grows step over step and quality falls as the window fills with raw JSON. Fix: store artifacts and reference them.","**Treating a demo loop as the architecture.** Symptom: the first production requirement (resume, audit, cancel, approve) requires a rewrite. Fix: build the runtime boundaries (external state, budgets, gates, events) before adding tools."],"when":"- **Use when:** the number and order of steps genuinely depend on intermediate results, tools exist to\n  act on those results, and you can define \"done\" well enough to evaluate it.\n- **Prefer a workflow when:** steps are known, latency must be tight, or the cost of a wrong action is\n  high and you cannot gate it.\n- **Measure:** task completion rate, steps and cost per task, tool-call success rate, and the fraction of\n  runs that hit budgets or require approval.\n- **Day to day:** decisions about which tools are read-only, what the step budget is, and where state\n  lives.\n- **Interviews:** draw the layers, explain why state must be external, and argue where model judgment\n  ends and code-enforced guarantees begin.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the loop with budgets and checkpoints, writes tool adapters with schemas and scoped credentials, and instruments every step for tracing and cost accounting.","architect":"Defines the runtime boundary, the tool permission model, the sync-versus-async contract with clients, and which decisions are code-enforced rather than model-made.","fde":"Fits the agent into a customer's identity, approval and audit requirements, and negotiates which actions may run autonomously versus with a human in the loop."},"check":{"q":"A team's agent works well in demos, but in production about 5% of runs disappear without a\nresult and some customers report duplicate actions. What architectural gaps would you suspect, and how\nwould you fix them?","a":"Both symptoms point at in-process state and non-idempotent side effects. Runs that disappear are\nmost likely killed by deploys, autoscaling or worker crashes while the loop's state lived in memory; with\nno checkpoint there is nothing to resume, and no event stream shows where it stopped. Duplicates come\nfrom the obvious reaction: the client or a retry wrapper re-submits the task, and consequential tools\nexecute again. The fix is to externalize state by run id and checkpoint after every step so a worker can\nresume rather than restart, make tool execution idempotent with a key derived from run id and step,\nroute consequential tools through an approval or at least a dedupe layer, and emit structured events so\ndisappearing runs become visible as \"interrupted at step n\" instead of silence."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should agent run state be persisted outside the process after every step?","options":["Because model APIs require state to be stored before each call","So a worker crash, deploy or autoscale event interrupts a run instead of losing it, and the run can resume","To reduce the number of tokens sent to the model","Because tool results cannot be held in memory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agent runs span many model and tool calls over seconds to hours. Anything that lives only in process memory is lost on restart; a checkpoint per step turns that loss into a resumable interruption."},{"q":"Where should the rule \"refunds above 500 require approval\" be enforced?","options":["In the system prompt, so the model knows the policy","In the model's reasoning, by asking it to double-check amounts","In the tool layer, by schema validation and an approval gate that runs regardless of what the model requested","In the client, by warning the user after the refund"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model may be wrong or manipulated through its inputs. Guarantees belong in code the model cannot bypass; the prompt can mention the policy, but the tool layer must enforce it."},{"q":"When is a deterministic workflow a better choice than an agent?","options":["Whenever more than one tool is involved","When the sequence of steps is known in advance and predictability matters more than handling unanticipated paths","When the task involves reading documents","Never, because agents can emulate any workflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agents trade predictability and cost for flexibility on unknown paths. If the path is known, that flexibility buys nothing and you pay for it in latency, cost and harder evaluation."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-agent-systems","sources":["Wu et al., 2023 — AutoGen: Enabling Next-Gen LLM Applications via Multi-Agent Conversation","Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents","Anthropic, 2025 — How we built our multi-agent research system"],"why":"- **A single agent degrades as the task grows.** Its context fills with tool definitions, instructions\n  for every sub-case and the accumulated trace, so the useful signal is diluted. Tool selection worsens\n  as the list lengthens, since the model must pick among many similar schemas.\n- **Privileges accumulate.** An agent that both reads untrusted web content and holds write credentials\n  to internal systems is a prompt-injection path by construction. And one loop is sequential, even when\n  the task has ten independent branches.\n- **A bigger prompt and longer window only delay it.** That buys some headroom, then hits the same walls,\n  now more expensively per step.\n- **Splitting everything is just as common and more damaging.** Each agent re-reads context the others\n  had, each hand-off loses information, each boundary is a place to disagree, and success becomes the\n  product of the parts: five sequential stages at 95% each give roughly 0.95⁵ ≈ 77% end to end.\n- **The deliberate version splits for a named gain:** isolation of context (each worker sees only its\n  sub-task), isolation of privilege (the agent reading untrusted input has no write tools), parallelism,\n  or specialization (small, sharp instructions and evals). A split that buys none of these is overhead.","how":"### Topologies and when each fits\n| Topology | Shape | Fits when | Watch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Supervisor (orchestrator–workers) | one agent decomposes, dispatches, synthesizes | sub-tasks must be derived from the task | supervisor holds the plan; workers hold the detail |\n| Pipeline | agents in sequence (research, draft, review) | stages need distinct skills, outputs checkable between them | compounding-error arithmetic |\n| Parallel fan-out with aggregation | agents on independent partitions (document, region, hypothesis), then merge | breadth-first work | the aggregator's context budget is the limit |\n| Evaluator–optimizer (critic) | one produces, one critiques against explicit criteria, loop | quality is checkable but hard to get right first time | loop until the critic passes or a budget ends |\n| Peer hand-off | agents transfer control by conversation (triage to billing specialist) | conversational routing | each specialist needs its own persona and tools |\n\n### What crosses the boundary\n- **Send a typed task, not the transcript.** Passing the full transcript is the default in many\n  frameworks and the main source of cost and confusion. A typed task (goal, constraints, relevant\n  artifacts, output schema) makes workers cheap and testable.\n- **Return structured, compact results** *because* the coordinator's context is the scarce resource and\n  raw worker traces will exhaust it.\n- **Pass large intermediates by reference** through a shared artifact store, *because* copying them\n  through model context costs tokens on both sides and degrades attention.\n\n### Compression is what a sub-agent buys\n| Quantity (illustrative) | Value |\n|---|---|\n| One worker reads | 40k tokens of pages and tool output |\n| One worker returns | a 400-token structured finding |\n| Supervisor reasons over (five workers) | 2k tokens of distilled evidence |\n| Instead of | 200k of raw material that would not fit, or would fit and drown the signal |\n\n- **The price:** the total token bill is the sum over all agents, so multi-agent runs cost several times\n  a single agent's. Anthropic's write-up of its research system reports on the order of 15× the tokens\n  of a chat interaction.\n\n### Reliability arithmetic: why topology matters\nCompounding is about *undetected* errors.\n- **Pipeline: a product.** Stage 3 receives stage 2's output and usually cannot tell a fluent wrong\n  answer from a right one, so success is roughly the product p₁·p₂·…·pₙ.\n- **Fan-out with an all-workers join: also a product** (five workers at 0.9 each: 0.9⁵ ≈ 0.59). Two\n  properties change the arithmetic:\n  1. **Retry alone.** Workers are independent, so with one retry each a worker succeeds with probability\n     1 − 0.1² = 0.99, and all five with 0.99⁵ ≈ 0.95.\n  2. **Quorum join.** Synthesize if at least k of n succeeded.\n- **Both need detectable failure,** which is why workers return a schema with an explicit \"not found\" or\n  low confidence rather than prose, and why pipelines need a validator between stages before any retry\n  logic helps.\n- **Cap retries.** Retries assume independent failures; a worker that fails because the sub-question is\n  unanswerable will fail again.\n\n### Coordination state\n- **One task table keyed by run id** knows which sub-tasks exist, their status and their results.\n- **Coordinator: model or code.** A model is flexible and derives the plan; code is predictable with a\n  fixed fan-out. Mixed is common: code owns the fan-out and join, a model owns what each worker does.\n- **Termination at two levels** *because* a worker can loop forever just as a single agent can, and the\n  coordinator must also stop when the task is done, over budget, or when further delegation stops\n  improving the result.\n\n### Privilege and policy across agents\n- **Per-agent tool sets and credentials,** scoped to the role.\n- **A policy layer enforces it independently of prompts:** the web researcher cannot call `send_email`\n  even if a page it read tells it to.\n- **Delegation must not escalate:** a worker is never granted more than the coordinator had for this\n  tenant and user.\n\n### Observability across agents\n- **One run id, parent–child spans,** exactly like distributed tracing across services.\n- **Without it,** debugging means reconstructing a conversation among five participants from five logs;\n  with it, you can see which worker produced the wrong fact and what it was given.\n\n### Protocols\n- **Across teams or vendors:** an interoperability protocol (A2A is the orientation point) standardizes\n  discovery, task hand-off and status.\n- **Inside one codebase:** a typed function call is simpler and sufficient.","example":"### Supervisor with parallel read-only workers and a quorum\nWorkers get a narrow, read-only tool set, run in parallel with a per-worker budget, and return compact\nstructured results. `agent_run` is the single-agent loop from the agentic-architecture lesson,\n`call_model` stands in for your model API, and `task_table` is the persisted coordination state.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\n\nREAD_ONLY = [\"search_docs\", \"fetch_page\"]            # workers never receive write tools\nFAILED = {\"answer\": None, \"sources\": [], \"confidence\": 0.0}\n\nasync def worker(run_id, subtask, budget_usd=0.10, retries=1):\n    for attempt in range(retries + 1):               # independent worker -> retry it alone\n        try:\n            result = await agent_run(\n                run_id=f\"{run_id}/{subtask['id']}/{attempt}\",   # child span under the parent run\n                system=\"Answer the sub-question using the tools. Return JSON: {answer, sources, confidence}. \"\n                       \"Use answer=null if the tools do not support an answer.\",\n                task=json.dumps(subtask), tools=READ_ONLY, max_steps=6, budget_usd=budget_usd)\n            out = json.loads(result)\n            if out.get(\"answer\"):\n                return {\"id\": subtask[\"id\"], **out}\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, TimeoutError):\n            pass                                     # malformed or slow: a detectable failure\n    return {\"id\": subtask[\"id\"], **FAILED}\n\nasync def supervise(run_id, question, max_workers=5):\n    plan = call_model(f\"Split into at most {max_workers} independent sub-questions as JSON \"\n                      f\"[{{id, question, why}}]. Question: {question}\").json[:max_workers]\n    task_table.create(run_id, [p[\"id\"] for p in plan])                  # coordination state, persisted\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(worker(run_id, p) for p in plan))  # parallel fan-out\n    for r in results:\n        task_table.mark(run_id, r[\"id\"], \"done\" if r[\"answer\"] else \"failed\")\n    evidence = [r for r in results if r[\"answer\"] and r[\"confidence\"] >= 0.5]\n    if len(evidence) < max(1, (len(plan) + 1) // 2):                    # quorum: a majority must succeed\n        return {\"status\": \"insufficient_evidence\", \"results\": results}\n    synthesis = call_model(\"Synthesize an answer with citations from these findings only. Name any \"\n                           \"sub-question that has no finding:\\n\" + json.dumps(evidence)\n                           + f\"\\n\\nQuestion: {question}\").text\n    return {\"status\": \"ok\", \"answer\": synthesis, \"sub_results\": results}\n```\n\nThe choices that matter:\n- **Typed sub-task in, schema out.** Workers never see the supervisor's transcript and never return a\n  trace.\n- **Detectable failure.** Null answer, bad JSON or timeout, so a worker can be retried in isolation.\n- **Quorum before synthesis.** The supervisor declines to synthesize when most workers failed instead of\n  producing a confident answer from thin evidence. `(n + 1) // 2` is a strict majority only for odd n;\n  for four workers it accepts two, so use `n // 2 + 1` if you need a majority.\n\n### Worked scenario: a data-residency question across three markets\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Plan | three sub-questions, one per market |\n| Workers 1 and 2 | each return a finding with sources |\n| Worker 3 | returns null twice (first attempt and retry) |\n| Quorum `max(1, (3 + 1) // 2)` | 2 required, 2 found: synthesize |\n| Synthesis | told to say the third market is unanswered rather than fill it in |\n\n### Runnable: compare topologies before you build one\nPure Python. Plug in your measured per-stage success rates.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\ndef pipeline(p, n):            # every stage must succeed, errors undetected\n    return p ** n\n\ndef with_retry(p, retries):    # independent, detectable failures only\n    return 1 - (1 - p) ** (retries + 1)\n\ndef quorum(p, n, k):           # at least k of n workers succeed\n    return sum(comb(n, i) * p**i * (1 - p)**(n - i) for i in range(k, n + 1))\n\nprint(round(pipeline(0.95, 5), 2))                # 0.77  five-stage pipeline\nprint(round(pipeline(0.9, 5), 2))                 # 0.59  fan-out, join needs all five\nprint(round(pipeline(with_retry(0.9, 1), 5), 2))  # 0.95  ...with one retry per worker\nprint(round(quorum(0.9, 5, 3), 3))                # 0.991 ...or a 3-of-5 quorum, no retries\n```","patterns":["**Code-owned fan-out, model-owned workers.** When the decomposition is a known shape (one worker per document, region or tenant), let code create sub-tasks and join them, and bound concurrency (for example an `asyncio.Semaphore`, or child workflows in a durable engine). Monitor per-worker failure and retry rates by sub-task type.","**Tool allowlist per agent role, enforced at dispatch.** The tool registry checks the calling agent's role, tenant and user before executing, regardless of what the prompt says. Monitor denied tool calls per agent; a spike on a reading agent is a likely injection attempt.","**Schema-validated worker returns with an explicit null.** Validate every worker result against a JSON Schema and treat invalid output as a failure, not as text to synthesize. Monitor the null and invalid-output rate per sub-task type, because it is your detectable-failure signal.","**One trace, one span per agent.** Use the parent run id as the trace and each worker attempt as a child span carrying tokens, cost and tool calls (for example OpenTelemetry spans). Monitor total tokens per run and the share consumed by each agent.","**Single-agent baseline in the eval suite.** Run the multi-agent design and a single-agent baseline on the same eval set on every significant change. Monitor task success, total tokens, wall-clock and per-stage failure attribution, and drop the split if it stops paying."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Question\"] --> S[\"Supervisor: plan sub-questions\"]\n    S --> TT[(\"Task table by run id\")]\n    S -->|\"typed sub-task\"| W1[\"Worker 1: read-only tools\"]\n    S -->|\"typed sub-task\"| W2[\"Worker 2: read-only tools\"]\n    S -->|\"typed sub-task\"| W3[\"Worker 3: read-only tools\"]\n    W1 -->|\"answer, sources, confidence\"| J{\"Quorum met?\"}\n    W2 -->|\"answer, sources, confidence\"| J\n    W3 -->|\"null after retry\"| J\n    J -->|\"yes\"| SY[\"Synthesize with citations, name gaps\"]\n    J -->|\"no\"| IE[\"insufficient_evidence\"]","caption":"The supervisor topology with read-only workers. Look at what crosses each arrow: a typed sub-task\ngoes out, a compact schema comes back, and synthesis happens only after the quorum check."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a multi-agent system, and what makes a split worth its coordination cost?","a":"- **Definition:** several model loops, each with its own instructions, tools and context, coordinated\n  by a topology such as supervisor, pipeline, fan-out, critic loop or peer hand-off.\n- **A split must buy a named gain:** context isolation, privilege isolation, parallelism or\n  specialization, because every boundary adds re-read context, lossy hand-offs and new places to fail."},{"q":"A multi-agent research run returns a confident but wrong fact. How do you find where it came from?","a":"- **Open the run's trace.** One run id with parent–child spans per worker attempt shows which worker\n  produced the fact and exactly which typed sub-task and tool results it was given.\n- **Check the join.** If the supervisor synthesized over a null or low-confidence result, the bug is the\n  quorum or confidence filter, not the worker.\n- **Without shared tracing** you are reconstructing a five-party conversation from five logs, which is\n  why the run id is non-negotiable."},{"q":"Five parallel workers each succeed 90% of the time. How reliable is the run, and how do you raise it?","a":"- **All-workers join:** 0.9⁵ ≈ 0.59, a product just like a pipeline.\n- **One retry per worker:** each succeeds with 1 − 0.1² = 0.99, so all five with 0.99⁵ ≈ 0.95.\n- **3-of-5 quorum, no retries:** about 0.991. Both levers need detectable failure (an explicit null or\n  schema-invalid output), and retries assume failures are independent."},{"q":"How do you stop a web-reading agent from being prompt-injected into taking actions?","a":"- **Privilege isolation:** the agent that reads untrusted pages gets read-only tools only, so a page\n  saying \"send this email\" has nothing to call.\n- **Policy outside the prompt:** the tool registry checks role, tenant and user at dispatch, and\n  delegation never grants a worker more than the coordinator had for that tenant and user."},{"q":"When should code own the coordination instead of a model supervisor?","a":"- **Code when the decomposition is known:** one worker per document, region or tenant is predictable,\n  cheap and easy to test.\n- **Model when sub-tasks must be derived from the task,** accepting a less predictable plan; the common\n  mix is code owning fan-out and join while a model owns what each worker does."}],"tradeoffs":["**Parallel wall-clock vs total tokens.** Parallel workers cut wall-clock time for breadth-first tasks, but total tokens rise because each worker rebuilds context; for narrow sequential tasks one agent is cheaper.","**Narrow tool sets vs routing risk.** Per-agent tool sets improve selection accuracy and contain blast radius, at the cost of a coordinator that must route correctly; mis-routing becomes the new failure mode.","**Testable stages vs compounding errors.** Pipelines make each stage independently testable, but errors compound multiplicatively. Add checks between stages or shorten the chain when end-to-end reliability matters more than modularity.","**Model supervisor vs code fan-out.** A model-driven supervisor adapts its plan to the task; a code-driven fan-out is predictable and cheap. Use the model only where the decomposition is genuinely unknown in advance.","**Artifacts by reference vs a shared store.** References keep contexts small but require a shared store with access control; for tiny results inline JSON is simpler."],"pitfalls":["**Splitting along an org chart or for \"cleanliness.\"** No isolation, parallelism or specialization gain. Symptom: higher cost and latency than the single-agent baseline with no quality gain. Fix: keep one agent until you can name the gain.","**Forwarding full transcripts between agents.** Symptom: worker contexts that are mostly the supervisor's history, and costs that grow with every added agent. Fix: send a typed sub-task.","**A worker that reads untrusted content holding write tools.** Symptom: an action nobody asked for, traced back to instructions inside a fetched page. Fix: read-only tools for readers, enforced at dispatch.","**Synthesizing over failed or low-confidence workers.** Symptom: fluent final answers whose citations point at sub-results that said \"not found\". Fix: filter on the explicit null and confidence, and apply a quorum before synthesis.","**No shared run id across agents.** Symptom: an incident where five logs disagree about what happened and nobody can say which agent introduced the error. Fix: one trace per run, one child span per agent."],"when":"- **Use when you can name the gain:** independent branches that can run in parallel, a privilege boundary\n  separating reading untrusted input from acting, or a single agent whose tool list or instruction set\n  has outgrown one context.\n- **Avoid when:** the goal is to look modular, to fix a weak single agent, or the task is a short\n  sequential chain.\n- **Measure:** against the single-agent baseline: task success, total tokens, wall-clock, and per-stage\n  failure attribution.\n- **Day to day:** contract design between agents, and tracing work.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"why not one agent\", how you prevent compounding errors and privilege\n  escalation, and how you would debug a wrong final answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements typed hand-offs, per-agent tool scopes and budgets, parallel fan-out with a join, and parent–child tracing so every worker's steps roll up under one run.","architect":"Decides whether a split is justified, chooses the topology, owns the privilege model across agents, and sets the aggregate budget and termination policy.","fde":"Maps customer workflows onto agents with the right data access each, and explains to stakeholders why the system refuses to answer when workers fail rather than guessing."},"check":{"q":"Your single research agent handles 20 tools and answers in about 90 seconds with 80% task\nsuccess. A colleague proposes five specialist agents. How would you decide whether that is an\nimprovement, and what would you watch for?","a":"First I would name what the split buys. If sub-questions are independent, parallel workers can\ncut wall-clock substantially, since latency becomes the slowest worker rather than the sum; if 20 tools are hurting tool selection, five agents with four tools each\nmay raise accuracy; if one tool reads untrusted web pages, separating it from write tools is a security\ngain. Then I would run both designs on the same eval set and compare task success, total tokens, latency\nand failure attribution per stage, because the multi-agent version will cost more tokens and introduce\nrouting and synthesis errors that did not exist before. I would watch for compounding: a pipeline of five\n95% stages is roughly 77% end to end, so the topology should be parallel with a join rather than\nsequential where possible, because independent workers can be retried alone and joined with a quorum,\nprovided their failures are detectable through a structured \"not found\". I would also check that the\nbaseline's failures are actually the kind the split fixes: if the single agent fails because it lacks\ndata, five agents will lack it too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which gain does NOT by itself justify splitting one agent into several?","options":["Running independent sub-tasks in parallel","Separating an agent that reads untrusted content from one holding write credentials","Making the architecture diagram look more modular","Reducing a tool list that has grown past what one context selects from accurately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multi-agent systems add coordination cost, duplicated context and compounding errors. The split must buy parallelism, privilege isolation or specialization; modularity for its own sake is pure overhead."},{"q":"Why should a worker agent receive a typed sub-task instead of the supervisor's full transcript?","options":["Workers cannot parse long messages","It keeps worker context small and focused, makes workers testable in isolation, and stops cost growing with every added agent","Transcripts contain secrets that workers must not see","Typed tasks are required by agent protocols"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forwarding transcripts means every worker pays for context it does not need, and the supervisor's noise dilutes its instructions. A typed task defines a contract that can be evaluated independently."},{"q":"A five-stage agent pipeline has stages that each succeed about 95% of the time. What is the approximate end-to-end success rate, and what does it imply?","options":["About 95%, since the stages are independent","About 77%, so long sequential chains need intermediate checks or fewer stages","About 99%, because later stages correct earlier errors","About 50%, because errors always double at each hand-off"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sequential stages multiply: 0.95 to the fifth power is roughly 0.77, because a later stage cannot usually detect an earlier stage's fluent error. Compounding is why pipelines need validation between stages, and why independent parallel workers, retried alone and joined by quorum, fare better."}]},{"key":"system-design/agent-orchestration","sources":["Anthropic, 2024 — Building Effective Agents","Official docs: LangGraph"],"why":"- **Two dependent calls already make a program.** Add a second call that depends on the first, a tool\n  whose result feeds a third, and a user who may approve something midway: the steps are spread across\n  model outputs, and something must own ordering, state, retries, concurrency and interruption.\n- **A plain Python function becomes an accidental orchestrator.** A tool times out at step four: redo\n  steps one to three, with their side effects? The user closes the tab during approval: where is the run?\n  Traffic triples: which steps can run concurrently? What did the agent do for last Tuesday's ticket:\n  which log line was that? Each answer bolts on another ad-hoc mechanism, untested and undocumented.\n- **Letting the model orchestrate everything is wrong for fixed steps.** Right for genuinely open-ended\n  steps; wrong for steps that must happen (validation, logging, a compliance check), must not happen\n  twice (payments), or must happen in a fixed order. Model-chosen paths are also the hardest to evaluate.\n- **A deliberate layer makes control flow explicit.** State is a typed object, steps are nodes,\n  transitions are fixed edges or router decisions, and a runtime adds persistence, events and policies.\n  As with any explicit state machine, you can inspect, resume, test and reason about what is written down,\n  and you can do none of that with control flow buried in prompts.","how":"### State, nodes, edges\n- **State:** one object per run (the task, messages, intermediate results, counters).\n- **Node:** a function from state to updated state: call the model, call a tool, run a sub-agent, ask a\n  human.\n- **Edges:** static (A always leads to B) or conditional, decided by a router. Routers can be pure code\n  (`if state.score < 0.7`), a classifier, or the main model's tool choice.\n- **Why this shape:** it makes the possible paths enumerable, *because* you can draw it, test each node\n  in isolation, and know where loops can occur.\n\n### The orchestration patterns, from most to least deterministic\n| # | Pattern | How | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Prompt chaining | fixed sequence, each step checks the previous output | the task decomposes cleanly and predictably |\n| 2 | Routing | classify, then dispatch to a specialized path (prompt, model or tool set) | inputs fall into distinct categories |\n| 3 | Parallelization | run independent nodes and join (sectioning), or run one node several times and vote (ensembling) | latency matters and branches are independent |\n| 4 | Orchestrator–workers | a model decides sub-tasks at runtime; code runs the fan-out and join | sub-tasks cannot be known in advance |\n| 5 | Evaluator–optimizer | generate, evaluate against explicit criteria, revise, bounded by a budget | quality is checkable |\n| 6 | Autonomous agent loop | the model picks the next node every step until done | the path is unknown and feedback is trustworthy |\n\n- **Each step down trades predictability and cost for flexibility,** so the right system usually mixes\n  them: a routed, chained outer workflow with an autonomous loop for the one step that needs it.\n\n### Runtime responsibilities\nThe orchestrator owns cross-cutting concerns *because* putting them in nodes means every node\nreimplements them differently.\n- **Persistence.** Checkpoint state after each node so a run resumes at the node where it stopped. An\n  interrupt is a checkpoint plus a status; resumption is loading it and continuing, which is what makes\n  human-in-the-loop possible.\n- **At-least-once, never exactly-once.** A crash *after* a node finishes but *before* its checkpoint is\n  written re-runs that node on resume. That single fact is why side-effecting nodes need idempotency\n  keys and model-calling nodes should be safe to repeat.\n- **Policies.** Step and cost budgets, per-node timeouts, retry with backoff for transient failures, and\n  idempotency keys for nodes with side effects so a retry does not repeat them.\n- **Concurrency.** Fan-out and join primitives with limits, so parallel branches do not exhaust rate\n  limits. Parallel writes to one state field need an explicit merge rule (append to a list, take the\n  max, last-writer-wins); LangGraph calls these reducers. Without one, the branch that finishes last\n  silently overwrites the others.\n- **Events.** A structured stream per run (node started, model called, tool result, waiting for\n  approval, done) that clients subscribe to, *because* long runs need progress and operators need traces.\n- **Cancellation.** A cooperative stop signal checked between nodes, with in-flight tool calls either\n  finished or compensated.\n\n### Request-driven versus event-driven orchestration\n| | Request-driven | Event-driven |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fits | short runs | runs that wait (approvals, slow tools, scheduled follow-ups) |\n| How a run advances | inside one request | when an event arrives |\n| Workers | hold the run | stateless; durable state is in the store |\n\n- This is where agent orchestration meets workflow engines such as Temporal: the graph is the same, the\n  executor is a durable one.\n\n### Orchestration versus choreography\n- **Orchestration:** one component holds the graph and tells each step when to run (everything above).\n- **Choreography:** no central owner; each agent or service reacts to events on a bus and emits its own.\n  It couples components less and scales organisationally, but the flow exists only implicitly in the sum\n  of subscriptions, so \"where is run 42 and why did it stop?\" has no single answer.\n- **For LLM applications** steps are non-deterministic and budgets and approvals are enforced per run, so\n  a central orchestrator per run is the usual choice. Choreography fits the boundaries *between* runs and\n  systems (a finished run emits an event another team's pipeline consumes).\n\n### Frameworks\n- **Graph libraries** such as LangGraph: the state graph, checkpointing and interrupts.\n- **Vendor agent SDKs:** the autonomous loop with hand-offs.\n- **Durable execution engines:** the event-driven executor.\n- **What matters more than the abstraction:** making state explicit and keeping runtime concerns out of\n  the nodes.","example":"### A small graph executor with an approval interrupt\nTyped state, nodes as functions, a static edge, a code router, a model-driven node, a checkpoint after\nevery node, and an interrupt resumed by calling `run` again with the same id. `call_model`, `mail` and\n`store` are stand-ins.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field, asdict\n\n@dataclass\nclass State:\n    ticket: str\n    draft: str = \"\"\n    score: float = 0.0\n    critique: str = \"\"\n    approved: bool | None = None\n    step: int = 0\n    status: str = \"running\"\n    history: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\ndef draft_reply(s):\n    s.draft = call_model(f\"Draft a reply to: {s.ticket}\\nFix these issues if any: {s.critique}\").text; return s\ndef grade(s):        # evaluator: structured output, so the router can branch on a number\n    g = call_model(f\"Policy compliance of this draft as JSON {{score: 0-1, critique}}:\\n{s.draft}\").json\n    s.score, s.critique = float(g[\"score\"]), g[\"critique\"]; return s\ndef approval(s):     # interrupt node: nothing to compute until a human acts\n    if s.approved is None: s.status = \"waiting_approval\"\n    elif not s.approved:   s.status = \"rejected\"\n    return s\ndef send(s):         mail.send_idempotent(key=f\"{s.ticket}:send\", body=s.draft); s.status = \"done\"; return s\n\nNODES = {\"draft_reply\": draft_reply, \"grade\": grade, \"approval\": approval, \"send\": send}\nEDGES = {\"draft_reply\": \"grade\",                                                          # static edge\n         \"grade\": lambda s: \"draft_reply\" if s.score < 0.7 and s.step < 6 else \"approval\",  # code router\n         \"approval\": lambda s: \"send\" if s.approved else None,\n         \"send\": None}\n\ndef run(run_id, store, max_steps=10):\n    s = store.load(run_id) or State(ticket=store.task(run_id))\n    node = store.cursor(run_id) or \"draft_reply\"\n    while node and s.step < max_steps:\n        s = NODES[node](s); s.step += 1; s.history.append(node)\n        if s.status != \"running\":                                   # interrupted, rejected or done\n            store.save(run_id, asdict(s), cursor=node); break\n        edge = EDGES[node]; node = edge(s) if callable(edge) else edge\n        store.save(run_id, asdict(s), cursor=node)                  # checkpoint after every node\n        emit(run_id, node=s.history[-1], score=s.score)\n    return s\n```\n\n### Tracing one ticket through it\n| Step | Node | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `draft_reply` | first draft |\n| 2 | `grade` | returns 0.55 with the critique \"promises a refund date\"; router loops back |\n| 3 | `draft_reply` | redrafts using the critique |\n| 4 | `grade` | returns 0.82; router goes to `approval` |\n| 5 | `approval` | finds `approved is None`, sets `waiting_approval`; run returns with cursor on `approval`, no worker held |\n| — | (hours later) | approval service writes `approved=True, status=\"running\"` and calls `run` again |\n| 6–7 | `approval` → `send` | loads the checkpoint, re-enters `approval`, routes to `send`, finishes |\n\n- **Crash after `send` sends but before its checkpoint:** resume re-runs `send`, and the idempotency key\n  `ticket:send` turns the second send into a no-op.\n- **Key choice in the sketch:** `f\"{s.ticket}:send\"` is built from the ticket text, so two tickets with\n  identical text would collide. In production derive it from the run id (`f\"{run_id}:send\"`).\n- **Two independent bounds on the revise cycle, one per level:** the `s.step < 6` guard in the router and\n  `max_steps` in the loop.\n\n### Runnable: why parallel branches need a reducer\nPure Python. Three branches finish in random order and merge into one state field.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random\n\nasync def branch(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(random.random() / 100)        # branches finish in any order\n    return {\"findings\": [f\"{name}: result\"]}\n\ndef last_writer_wins(state, update):\n    return {**state, **update}\n\ndef append_reducer(state, update):\n    return {**state, \"findings\": state[\"findings\"] + update[\"findings\"]}\n\nasync def fan_out(merge):\n    state = {\"findings\": []}\n    for coro in asyncio.as_completed([branch(n) for n in (\"web\", \"docs\", \"tickets\")]):\n        state = merge(state, await coro)\n    return state\n\nprint(len(asyncio.run(fan_out(last_writer_wins))[\"findings\"]))  # 1, and which one varies run to run\nprint(len(asyncio.run(fan_out(append_reducer))[\"findings\"]))    # 3\n```","patterns":["**Database-backed checkpointer keyed by thread.** When runs must survive restarts or wait on humans, compile the graph with a persistent checkpointer (in LangGraph, a Postgres checkpointer) and pass a stable `thread_id` per run in the config. Monitor run counts by status and runs with no checkpoint write for longer than their expected duration.","**Approval as an interrupt, resumed by event.** Pause in the approval node (LangGraph offers an `interrupt()` call, resumed later by invoking the graph with `Command(resume=...)` on the same thread), return immediately, and resume from the approval service's event. Monitor time spent waiting, approval and rejection rates, and expired approvals.","**Declared reducers for every fan-out field.** Any state key written by parallel branches gets an explicit merge rule, for example `Annotated[list, operator.add]` in a LangGraph state type. Monitor in tests: assert the joined result contains one entry per branch.","**Idempotency keys on every side-effecting node.** Derive the key from run id and node (for example `f\"{run_id}:send\"`) and pass it to the downstream API, because node execution is at-least-once. Monitor duplicate side effects detected by the downstream system.","**Per-run event stream to clients and traces.** Stream node started, tool result, waiting and done events to the UI (for example over server-sent events) and to tracing under the run id. Alert on running runs whose last event is older than a threshold you set: they are stuck."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Ticket arrives\"] --> D[\"draft_reply: model call\"]\n    D -->|\"static edge\"| G[\"grade: model returns score and critique\"]\n    G -->|\"score below 0.7 and step below 6\"| D\n    G -->|\"otherwise\"| A[\"approval: interrupt node\"]\n    A -->|\"approved is None\"| W[(\"Checkpoint: waiting_approval\")]\n    W -.->|\"approval event calls run again\"| A\n    A -->|\"approved\"| S[\"send with idempotency key\"]\n    A -->|\"rejected\"| R[\"rejected\"]\n    S --> DN[\"done\"]","caption":"The graph from the example below. Static edges are fixed in code, the router after `grade` is a code\ndecision on the score, and every node writes a checkpoint so the approval wait holds no worker."},"interview":[{"q":"What is agent orchestration, and why not let the model drive every step?","a":"- **Definition:** the control layer that decides what runs next (model call, tool, sub-agent or human\n  step), with what state, and what happens on failure.\n- **Why not the model everywhere:** steps that must happen, must not happen twice or must run in a\n  fixed order belong in code, and model-chosen paths are the hardest to evaluate."},{"q":"Walk me through the pattern ladder. Which would you pick for a support inbox with three ticket types?","a":"- **The ladder:** prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator–workers, evaluator–optimizer,\n  then the autonomous agent loop; each step down trades predictability and cost for flexibility.\n- **For distinct ticket types, routing:** classify, then dispatch to a specialized prompt, model or tool\n  set, and confine an autonomous loop to the one path that genuinely needs it."},{"q":"A fan-out of three searches sometimes returns only one branch's findings. What is wrong?","a":"- **Missing reducer.** Parallel branches write the same state key with no merge rule, so the branch that\n  finishes last silently overwrites the others, varying run to run.\n- **Fix:** declare an explicit merge rule that keeps every branch (append to a list, or take the max for\n  a score), for example `Annotated[list, operator.add]` in a LangGraph state type, rather than the\n  implicit last-writer-wins, and assert one entry per branch in tests."},{"q":"Why must side-effecting nodes be idempotent even with checkpoints after every node?","a":"- **Execution is at-least-once, never exactly-once.** A crash after a node finishes but before its\n  checkpoint is written re-runs that node on resume.\n- **So** derive a key from run id and node, as in `ticket:send`, and pass it downstream so the second\n  execution is a no-op; model-calling nodes should simply be safe to repeat."},{"q":"When would you choose choreography over a central orchestrator?","a":"- **Orchestrator per run** for LLM applications, because budgets and approvals are enforced per run and\n  \"where is run 42 and why did it stop?\" needs one answer.\n- **Choreography between runs and systems:** a finished run emits an event another team's pipeline\n  consumes, which couples components less and scales organisationally."},{"q":"What would you monitor to catch stuck or misbehaving runs in production?","a":"- **Status mix:** run completion versus interruption versus budget exhaustion, plus retries per run.\n- **Per-node latency and failure rates,** so a regression points at one node rather than the whole run.\n- **Staleness alert:** running runs whose last event or checkpoint is older than their expected\n  duration are stuck, and the event stream says which node they are in."}],"tradeoffs":["**Explicit graphs vs adaptability.** Graphs make paths enumerable and testable, but every new behaviour is a code change; a model-driven loop adapts without redeploys at the price of unpredictable paths.","**Per-node checkpoints vs latency.** Checkpointing enables resume and human interrupts but adds a store round-trip per node; for sub-second, side-effect-free chains, in-memory execution is fine.","**Parallel branches vs rate limits and cost.** They cut latency but multiply concurrent model calls, so rate limits and cost spikes arrive sooner. Cap fan-out and reserve it for genuinely independent work.","**Policies in the runtime vs a critical dependency.** Budgets, retries and idempotency in the runtime keep nodes simple, but the runtime must itself be reliable and well understood.","**Framework speed vs lock-in.** A framework accelerates the first version while shaping your state and persistence model; migrating a framework-specific graph to a durable, event-driven executor later can be a rewrite."],"pitfalls":["**Hiding control flow in prompts** (\"after you search, always validate, then...\"). Symptom: runs that skip required steps and no way to assert they happened. Fix: make required steps nodes.","**Retrying a side-effecting node without an idempotency key.** Symptom: duplicate emails, tickets or payments after a transient failure. Fix: a key from run id and node, passed to the downstream API.","**Unbounded evaluator–optimizer or self-correction loops.** Symptom: a run that revises forever at increasing cost with no termination. Fix: bound at the router and the loop.","**Human approval as a blocking call in a worker.** Symptom: held connections, worker exhaustion and runs lost when the process restarts. Fix: checkpoint, return, resume on event.","**Parallel branches writing one state key without a reducer.** Symptom: a fan-out of three searches whose joined result contains only one branch's findings, varying run to run. Fix: declare a reducer.","**No per-run event stream.** Symptom: users see a spinner for minutes and operators cannot say which node a stuck run is in. Fix: emit structured events per node under the run id."],"when":"- **Use when:** a feature has more than one model call with dependencies, any tool with side effects, or\n  any step that waits on a human or an external system.\n- **Choose:** the most deterministic pattern that handles the task, and confine model-driven control flow\n  to steps that need judgment.\n- **Measure:** per-node latency and failure rates, run completion versus interruption versus budget\n  exhaustion, and retries per run.\n- **Day to day:** deciding what is a node, where checkpoints go, and what is idempotent.\n- **Interviews:** describe the pattern ladder from chaining to autonomous loop, say which runtime concerns\n  the orchestrator owns, and explain how an approval interrupt works without blocking a worker.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines nodes, routers and state types, adds checkpoints and idempotency keys, and emits the event stream that clients and traces consume.","architect":"Chooses where control flow is code versus model, whether execution is request- or event-driven, and which engine or framework owns persistence and policies.","fde":"Adapts the graph to a customer's approval chains and systems, and explains why a human step pauses the run rather than holding a connection open."},"check":{"q":"A team built an agent that handles refund requests end to end. Compliance now requires that every\nrefund above a threshold is approved by a person, sometimes hours later. How would you change the\norchestration, and what would break if you simply added a blocking approval call?","a":"A blocking call holds a worker and a connection for hours, exhausts the worker pool under load,\nand loses the run entirely if the process restarts, so it is not an option. I would make the run's state\nexplicit and persisted, add an approval node that checkpoints the state with status \"waiting_approval\"\nand returns, and make the run event-driven: the approval decision arrives as an event that loads the\ncheckpoint and resumes at the next node. The refund-executing node gets an idempotency key so a resumed\nor retried run cannot pay twice, the threshold check is code in the router rather than a prompt\ninstruction, and the event stream shows the run as waiting so support can see why it is pending. If runs\ncan wait days, a durable workflow engine is the natural executor."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason to represent an agent application as explicit state plus nodes and edges?","options":["Models produce better answers when the prompt describes a graph","It makes the possible paths enumerable, so you can test nodes, resume runs and know where loops can occur","Graphs execute faster than sequential function calls","It removes the need for a model to make any decisions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Control flow buried in prompts or local variables cannot be inspected, tested or resumed. An explicit graph can be drawn, checkpointed and reasoned about, while still leaving judgment calls to the model at router nodes."},{"q":"How should a human approval step be implemented in an orchestrated run?","options":["Block the worker thread until the human responds","Ask the model to wait and poll for the decision","Checkpoint the state with a waiting status, return, and resume from the checkpoint when the approval event arrives","Restart the run from the beginning once approved"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Approvals can take hours. Holding a worker wastes capacity and loses the run on restart; a checkpoint plus event-driven resumption costs nothing while waiting and survives deploys."},{"q":"Which pattern fits when sub-tasks cannot be known until the task is inspected, but you still want predictable fan-out and join behaviour?","options":["Prompt chaining","Orchestrator–workers, with a model deciding the sub-tasks and code running the fan-out and join","Routing to a fixed set of specialists","A single autonomous agent loop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Orchestrator–workers places model judgment exactly where it is needed, deciding the decomposition, while the concurrency, limits and aggregation stay deterministic in code."}]},{"key":"system-design/workflow-orchestration","sources":["Official docs: Temporal","Official docs: Apache Airflow","Official docs: Prefect"],"why":"- **Multi-step processes all need the same machinery.** Remember which steps completed, retry failures\n  without repeating successes, wait (timer, human, callback) without holding a process open, run on a\n  schedule or event, and expose state. Teams starting from a cron job and a script reinvent this, badly,\n  one incident at a time.\n- **The naive job-plus-status-row breaks at five steps.** It needs per-step status, partial retries,\n  idempotency for every side effect, resume after a worker dies between steps, and a dashboard.\n- **The subtle failure is a wrong guarantee.** A job retried after a crash re-executes \"send email\"\n  because the crash came after the send and before the status write. Exactly-once is impossible across\n  systems; you get a durable record of attempted steps plus idempotent steps, which is what\n  orchestrators provide.\n- **AI systems make this acute.** Ingestion is multi-stage, scheduled and expensive to redo (parsing and\n  embedding a million chunks). Agent runs take minutes to hours, wait for approvals, call flaky tools and\n  must not repeat consequential actions. Different shapes, which is why the three tools coexist rather\n  than compete.","how":"### The three at a glance\n| | Airflow | Prefect | Temporal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit | scheduled DAG of coarse tasks | Python flow calling tasks | workflow code calling activities |\n| Graph | static plan | ordinary code, data-dependent | ordinary code, deterministic |\n| Recorded state | task instance states | task run states and persisted results | full event history |\n| Recovery | retry the task for its interval | re-run flow, skip cached tasks | replay code against history |\n| Fits | periodic batch, backfills | runtime-shaped pipelines | long-lived stateful runs, human waits |\n\n### Airflow: scheduled DAGs of tasks\n- **Model.** Airflow (started at Airbnb, now an Apache project) defines work as a directed acyclic graph\n  of tasks in Python.\n- **Execution.** A scheduler evaluates DAGs against their schedule, creates task instances per run and\n  hands them to an executor (local, Celery, Kubernetes) that runs them on workers; a metadata database\n  records every task state.\n- **Coarse, idempotent tasks.** Run this SQL, launch this Spark job, call this API, exchanging only small\n  values through XCom, *because* the DAG is a scheduling plan, not a data-flow program.\n- **Data intervals make retries safe.** Each DAG run is bound to a *data interval* (its logical date).\n  Well-written tasks read only that interval's data and overwrite only that interval's output, so\n  re-running \"2024-03-14\" replaces that day's partition instead of appending a duplicate.\n- **Strengths:** retries, SLAs, backfills over past dates and a mature UI. Fits nightly re-embedding,\n  daily data-quality checks, hourly connector syncs.\n- **Fits poorly** when the graph depends on runtime data or a run must wait on a human for days.\n\n### Prefect: dynamic Python dataflows\n- **Model.** A flow is a Python function and tasks are decorated functions called from it, so loops,\n  conditionals and fan-out driven by runtime data are ordinary code rather than a static graph.\n- **Hybrid execution.** The orchestration layer (Prefect server or Cloud) tracks states, retries, task\n  result caching, schedules and concurrency limits, while your workers execute the code, *because* data\n  and credentials then never leave your infrastructure.\n- **Fits** the many AI pipelines whose number of documents, branches and parameters are discovered as\n  the flow runs.\n- **Still a pipeline tool.** A flow run can be paused or suspended for human input, but there is no\n  replayable event history. Recovery is by retrying tasks and re-running the flow function while skipping\n  tasks whose results were persisted under a cache key; a suspended run resumes by executing the flow\n  from the top this way.\n- **Works well** when tasks are coarse and results persisted; poorly for many small stateful steps that\n  must survive anything for weeks, which is not its design centre.\n\n### Temporal: durable execution\nTemporal (descended from Uber's Cadence) makes ordinary code durable.\n1. **Workflow and activities.** A *workflow* function orchestrates calls to *activities*, the functions\n   that do side effects: call an LLM, write a database row, send an email.\n2. **Event history.** The server records every event (started, activity scheduled, activity completed\n   with its result, timer fired, signal received) in an append-only history.\n3. **Replay.** Workers poll task queues and execute workflow code. When a worker crashes, another picks\n   up the workflow and *replays* its code against the history; completed activity results come from\n   history instead of re-execution, so the code deterministically reaches where it stopped and\n   continues.\n\n- **Determinism rule.** No wall-clock reads, random numbers or direct I/O in workflow code, only\n  activities, timers and the SDK's side-effect APIs, *because* replay must reproduce the original\n  decisions. Changing workflow code with runs in flight needs versioning (patch markers in the code).\n- **Activity controls.** Retry policies, timeouts (schedule-to-start, start-to-close) and heartbeats for\n  long tasks.\n- **Workflow powers.** Sleep for days with no process alive, receive *signals* (an approval arrived),\n  answer *queries* (which step are you on), start child workflows, and run indefinitely by\n  continuing-as-new to keep history bounded.\n- **Costs.** The determinism discipline, a server to run or buy, and small payloads (pass large data by\n  reference), because every activity input and output lands in history.\n\n### The same crash, three recovery models\nA worker crashes in the middle of step 3 of a five-step run.\n\n| Tool | What it knows | What happens | Correctness rests on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Airflow | task instances | tasks 1–2 are `success`; task 3 is marked failed (or found as a zombie and failed) and retried as a whole unit | task 3 idempotent for its interval |\n| Prefect | task runs and persisted results | retry re-enters the flow from the top; steps 1–2 return cached results, step 3 runs again | cacheable tasks |\n| Temporal | every decision the workflow made | a new worker replays; steps 1–2 return recorded results instantly; step 3's activity is retried after its timeout | deterministic workflow code |\n\n- **The finer the recorded state, the smaller the unit of redo,** and the more discipline the code must\n  obey.\n\n### Which fits an AI system where\n- **Nightly re-chunk and re-embed with backfill over past days:** **Airflow**.\n- **Parse, enrich and index whatever arrived in the last hour, branching per document type:** **Prefect**\n  (or Airflow with dynamic task mapping).\n- **An agent run calling tools over an hour, pausing for a manager's approval until Monday, never issuing\n  a refund twice, inspectable mid-run:** **Temporal**.\n- **A ten-second chain of three model calls inside a request:** none of them; an in-process orchestrator\n  is enough.\n\n### What all three give you, and what none of them do\n- **All three give:** persistence of step state, retries, scheduling, a UI and observability.\n- **None make your steps idempotent, and none guarantee exactly-once side effects:** a retry after a\n  crash between \"effect done\" and \"result recorded\" re-runs the step.\n- **So:** every activity or task that acts on the outside world needs an idempotency key derived from the\n  run and the step.","example":"### Temporal: document extraction with a durable approval wait\nActivities carry the side effects and retry policies; the workflow carries only deterministic\norchestration; the approval wait is a durable signal rather than a blocked thread. `blob_store`, `db` and\n`llm_extract` stand in for your own clients.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom temporalio import activity, workflow\nfrom temporalio.common import RetryPolicy\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def extract_fields(doc_id: str) -> dict:\n    text = await blob_store.read_text(doc_id)            # large payloads stay out of workflow history\n    return await llm_extract(text)                        # non-deterministic work belongs in an activity\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def write_record(doc_id: str, fields: dict) -> None:\n    await db.upsert(key=f\"extract:{doc_id}\", value=fields)   # idempotent by key: safe to retry\n\n@workflow.defn\nclass DocumentExtraction:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.approved: bool | None = None\n\n    @workflow.signal\n    def approve(self, decision: bool) -> None:            # delivered durably whenever it arrives\n        self.approved = decision\n\n    @workflow.run\n    async def run(self, doc_id: str) -> str:\n        fields = await workflow.execute_activity(\n            extract_fields, doc_id, start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(minutes=5),\n            retry_policy=RetryPolicy(maximum_attempts=4, initial_interval=timedelta(seconds=5)))\n        if fields.get(\"confidence\", 1.0) < 0.8:\n            try:   # durable wait: no worker is held; survives restarts and deploys\n                await workflow.wait_condition(lambda: self.approved is not None, timeout=timedelta(days=3))\n            except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n                return \"approval_timed_out\"\n            if not self.approved:\n                return \"rejected\"\n        await workflow.execute_activity(write_record, args=[doc_id, fields],\n                                        start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=30))\n        return \"written\"\n\n# Worker(client, task_queue=\"extraction\", workflows=[DocumentExtraction], activities=[extract_fields, write_record])\n```\n\n### Walking one low-confidence document through replay\nThe server's history for the run, simplified:\n\n| Event | Recorded |\n|---|---|\n| (1) | workflow started with `doc_id` |\n| (2) | activity `extract_fields` scheduled |\n| (3) | activity started, recorded with attempt 2: the server retried a provider 503 per the retry policy (intermediate attempts are not written as separate events) |\n| (4) | activity completed with result `{..., \"confidence\": 0.62}` |\n| (5) | a 3-day timer started for the wait; no worker holds anything now |\n| — | a deploy restarts every worker overnight |\n| (6) | next morning a reviewer sends the `approve(True)` signal |\n\nA fresh worker replays `run` from the top:\n1. `execute_activity(extract_fields)` is matched to events 2–4 and returns the recorded fields without\n   calling the LLM.\n2. `wait_condition` is matched to the timer; the signal handler applies event (6), so the condition is\n   now true.\n3. The code reaches `write_record`, the first genuinely new command.\n\n- **The bug the rule prevents.** Had someone put `datetime.now()` in the workflow to stamp the record,\n  replay would compute a different value than the original run. The determinism rule and the SDK's\n  `workflow.now()` exist to prevent exactly this class of bug.\n\n### Airflow: an interval-scoped nightly re-embed\nA TaskFlow-style DAG (Airflow 2.x imports) where each run touches only its own day, so retries and\nbackfills overwrite rather than duplicate. `docs`, `embed` and `index` stand in for your clients.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nfrom airflow.decorators import dag, task\n\n@dag(schedule=\"@daily\", start_date=datetime(2024, 1, 1), catchup=True,\n     default_args={\"retries\": 3, \"retry_delay\": timedelta(minutes=5)})\ndef reembed_daily():\n    @task\n    def changed_ids(data_interval_start=None, data_interval_end=None):   # injected from the run context\n        return docs.changed_between(data_interval_start, data_interval_end)  # ids only: XCom stays small\n\n    @task\n    def reembed(doc_ids, ds=None):\n        index.overwrite_partition(ds, embed(doc_ids))   # overwrite this interval, never append\n\n    reembed(changed_ids())\n\nreembed_daily()\n```","patterns":["**Idempotency keys from workflow and step.** In every Temporal activity with an external effect, build the key from the workflow id and activity id (available from `activity.info()`) and send it to the downstream API. Monitor activity attempt counts per type; a rising average means retries, and each retry is a duplicate unless keyed.","**Patch-based versioning for in-flight runs.** Wrap changed workflow logic in `workflow.patched(\"change-id\")` so old histories replay the old branch and new runs take the new one; remove the patch once old runs finish. Monitor non-determinism errors after each deploy and the count of open runs on old versions.","**Continue-as-new for long-lived workflows.** For workflows that loop for weeks (an agent that wakes daily), call `workflow.continue_as_new(...)` with compact state before history grows large. Monitor history event count and size per workflow against the server's limits.","**Interval-scoped, overwrite-only Airflow tasks.** Read only `data_interval_start` to `data_interval_end` and overwrite that partition, with `catchup` set deliberately. Monitor row counts per partition before and after retries or backfills; any growth on a re-run is a bug.","**Persisted, cache-keyed Prefect tasks.** Give expensive tasks (parse, embed) `retries`, a cache key derived from their inputs (for example `cache_key_fn=task_input_hash`) and persisted results, so a re-run flow skips finished work. Monitor cache-hit rate on retried flow runs."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant W1 as Worker 1\n    participant S as Temporal server\n    participant W2 as Worker 2\n    W1->>S: schedule activity extract_fields\n    S->>W1: run extract_fields, retried per policy on a 503\n    W1->>S: activity completed with fields, confidence 0.62\n    W1->>S: start 3 day timer for the approval wait\n    Note over W1: deploy restarts every worker\n    S->>S: record approve signal from reviewer\n    S->>W2: workflow task with full event history\n    Note over W2: replay returns recorded fields, no LLM call\n    W2->>S: schedule write_record, the first new command\n    S->>W2: run write_record, idempotent by key","caption":"Temporal recovery for the extraction workflow in the examples. Watch the second worker: it replays\nfrom history, gets the extraction result without calling the LLM again, and only `write_record` is new\nwork."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain deterministic replay. Why does Temporal need it?","a":"- **Mechanism:** the server keeps an append-only event history; after a crash, a worker re-runs the\n  workflow code from the top and completed activity results come from history instead of re-execution.\n- **Why determinism:** replay only reaches the same point if the code makes the same decisions, so no\n  wall-clock reads, random numbers or direct I/O in workflow code; use activities and SDK APIs such as\n  `workflow.now()`."},{"q":"Place Airflow, Prefect and Temporal against three AI workloads.","a":"- **Nightly re-chunk and re-embed with backfill over past days:** Airflow, because data intervals make\n  retries and backfills overwrite one day's partition.\n- **Hourly parse-and-index branching per document type:** Prefect, because the graph is ordinary Python\n  shaped by runtime data (or Airflow with dynamic task mapping).\n- **An agent run waiting days for approval that must never refund twice:** Temporal, with durable\n  signals, timers and idempotent activities."},{"q":"After a deploy, in-flight workflows fail with non-determinism errors. What happened and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Cause:** the workflow code changed while runs were in flight, so replaying old histories against new\n  code produces different commands than were recorded.\n- **Fix:** wrap the change in `workflow.patched(\"change-id\")` so old histories take the old branch and new\n  runs the new one, then remove the patch once old runs finish; monitor open runs on old versions."},{"q":"A backfill doubled the rows for exactly the days that were retried. What is the bug?","a":"- **The task appends instead of overwriting its interval.** Airflow's retry safety rests on each run\n  reading only its `data_interval_start` to `data_interval_end` and overwriting that partition.\n- **Detect it** by comparing row counts per partition before and after retries or backfills; any growth\n  on a re-run is the bug."},{"q":"When would you not add a workflow orchestrator at all?","a":"- **Short chains inside a request:** a ten-second chain of three model calls needs only an in-process\n  orchestrator with good logging.\n- **The cost of adding one** is an operational component, a learning curve and, for Temporal, a server\n  to run or buy plus determinism discipline."},{"q":"Why keep document text and embeddings out of workflow history, and how do you bound history?","a":"- **Every activity input and output lands in history,** so megabyte payloads slow UIs, hit size limits\n  and fail runs on large documents; pass references to object storage instead.\n- **Long-lived loops** should call `workflow.continue_as_new(...)` with compact state; monitor history\n  event count and size per workflow against the server's limits."}],"tradeoffs":["**Airflow's static DAGs vs runtime-shaped graphs.** Predictable scheduling, backfills and a mature ecosystem, but once the graph depends on runtime data you are fighting the model; dynamic task mapping helps, Prefect removes the constraint.","**Prefect's plain Python vs long waits.** Fast to write and natural for data-dependent branching, but flows are expected to complete; a run that must wait weeks for an external event is not its design centre.","**Temporal's durability vs discipline.** Long-lived, stateful processes become crash-proof and resumable, in exchange for determinism in workflow code, versioning for in-flight runs, and operating or buying a server.","**Full history vs payload size.** Storing every activity input and output enables replay but caps payload sizes; passing blobs by reference keeps histories small at the cost of a second store to manage.","**Any orchestrator vs simplicity.** It adds an operational component and a learning curve; for a short chain inside a request, in-process orchestration with good logging wins."],"pitfalls":["**I/O, clock reads or random values inside Temporal workflow code.** Symptom: non-determinism errors on replay after a worker restart or a code deploy. Fix: move it into activities or SDK APIs.","**Assuming the orchestrator makes side effects exactly-once.** Symptom: duplicate emails or writes after a retry, fixed only by idempotency keys in the activity or task.","**Airflow for per-request or event-driven work.** Symptom: noticeable scheduler latency between tasks (it was built for batch, not milliseconds) and DAG files sprouting conditionals to fake dynamism. Fix: keep Airflow for scheduled batch and move event-driven work to Prefect, Temporal or in-process code.","**Airflow tasks that append rather than overwrite their interval's output.** Symptom: doubled rows for exactly the days that were retried or backfilled. Fix: overwrite the interval's partition.","**Megabytes of document text or embeddings through task results or workflow history.** Symptom: slow UIs, size limits being hit, and failed runs on large documents. Fix: pass references.","**Deploying changed workflow code with runs in flight and no versioning.** Symptom: those runs fail or take a different path on replay than they did originally. Fix: guard the change with `workflow.patched`, or let old runs drain on the old code first."],"when":"- **Use an orchestrator when:** a process has multiple steps with independent failure, runs longer than a\n  request, needs scheduling or backfill, or waits on humans or external systems.\n- **Pick Airflow** for scheduled batch with a fixed shape and a data-engineering team already running it.\n- **Pick Prefect** when the pipeline's shape is decided at runtime and you want plain Python.\n- **Pick Temporal** when runs are long-lived, stateful and must survive anything: production agent runs\n  and approval-gated business processes.\n- **Measure:** run success rate, retries per step, time spent waiting versus executing, and queue\n  latency.\n- **Interviews:** explain deterministic replay in one paragraph, say why exactly-once is still your job,\n  and place each tool against an AI pipeline and an agent run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent tasks and activities, keeps workflow code deterministic, sets retry policies and timeouts, and keeps large payloads in object storage rather than history.","architect":"Chooses the engine per workload class, decides what state lives in history versus a database, and sets versioning policy and operational ownership of the orchestrator.","fde":"Fits customer approval flows and nightly data syncs onto the engine the customer can operate, and explains replay and retries when they ask why a step ran twice."},"check":{"q":"An agent run calls an LLM activity, then a payment activity. A worker crashes immediately after\nthe payment succeeded but before the activity completion was recorded. What happens on Temporal, and\nwhat must you have done to be safe?","a":"Temporal replays the workflow on another worker from the event history. The LLM activity's result\nis in history, so it is not called again. The payment activity has a scheduled event but no completion\nevent, so once its timeout elapses it is retried according to its retry policy, and the payment code\nruns a second time. Temporal guarantees the workflow's progress is durable; it cannot know whether the\nexternal side effect happened. Safety therefore depends on the payment activity being idempotent: it\nmust use an idempotency key derived from the workflow id and step so the payment provider deduplicates\nthe second call. This is the general rule for every orchestrator: durable progress plus idempotent steps\nis the achievable form of exactly-once."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must Temporal workflow code be deterministic?","options":["Because the server executes workflow code and cannot run arbitrary Python","Because a workflow is recovered by replaying its code against recorded history, which only works if the code makes the same decisions each time","Because activities cannot be retried otherwise","Because determinism is required for the UI to display progress"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Durable execution rebuilds in-memory state by re-running the workflow function and feeding it recorded results. Any divergence, from clocks, randomness or I/O, breaks the match between code and history."},{"q":"Which workload is the poorest fit for Apache Airflow?","options":["Nightly re-embedding of a document corpus with backfill","Hourly data-quality checks on ingested tables","An agent run that pauses for a human approval that may take days","A weekly report-generation pipeline"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Airflow is a scheduler for batch DAGs with a fixed shape. Long-lived, event-driven runs that wait on humans belong to durable execution engines such as Temporal."},{"q":"What does a workflow orchestrator NOT provide, regardless of which one you choose?","options":["Persisting which steps have completed","Retrying failed steps with backoff","Exactly-once execution of side effects against external systems","A view of where each run currently is"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Orchestrators make progress durable, but a crash between performing a side effect and recording its completion leads to a retry. Idempotent steps remain the application's responsibility."}]},{"key":"system-design/memory-architecture","sources":["Park et al., 2023 — Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior","Packer et al., 2023 — MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems"],"why":"- **Every model call starts from zero.** The model knows only what the application puts in the prompt.\n  Fine for one question; for an assistant running over weeks, preferences, decisions, half-finished\n  tasks and facts from earlier sessions vanish unless something carries them forward.\n- **Carrying the whole conversation forward fails.** Cost and latency grow linearly with history,\n  because every turn re-reads all previous turns; the window eventually fills; quality drops before that\n  as facts get buried among hundreds of turns of chatter. And nothing crosses sessions, devices or agents.\n- **Embedding every utterance and retrieving by similarity also fails.** It returns stale and\n  contradictory facts side by side (\"user lives in Berlin\" from last year, \"moved to Lisbon\" from last\n  week), cannot update or delete, retrieves what the user told a different agent, and cannot answer\n  \"what did we decide\", because decisions are not textually similar to the question that needs them.\n- **Explicit stores and policies replace both.** The operating-system picture: a small, expensive working\n  set (the context window), a larger persistent store, and a controller deciding what to page in and out.\n- **The \"what\" is the design problem.** Remember too little and the assistant is amnesiac; too much and\n  it is expensive, confused and a privacy liability.","how":"### Memory types and where each lives\n| # | Type | Holds | Lives in | Answers |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Working memory | system prompt, current task, recent turns, retrieved items | the context window, rebuilt per call by code | this call |\n| 2 | Session state | full transcript and task state of one conversation or run | database keyed by conversation id | this session |\n| 3 | Episodic memory | past sessions, outcomes, decisions, with timestamps | retrievable store | \"what did we do last time\" |\n| 4 | Semantic memory | distilled facts and preferences | small, structured where possible, updated in place | \"prefers concise answers\", \"account tier: enterprise\", \"project X uses Postgres\" |\n| 5 | Procedural memory | instructions, tool-usage notes, corrections | prompts, skills or configuration, usually after human review | how to behave next time |\n| 6 | External knowledge | documents, databases | RAG index (see the RAG architecture lesson) | not memory |\n\n- **The model never stores anything itself;** working memory is assembled by code each call.\n- **Session state is the source of truth** *because* the context is a view of it, not the other way\n  around; compaction summarizes it for the prompt without deleting it.\n- **Keep knowledge separate from memory** *because* memory is per user and mutable while knowledge is\n  shared and versioned.\n\n### The four operations\n1. **Write: decide what is worth keeping.**\n   - The model calls a `remember` tool explicitly; or\n   - a background extractor reads the session after each turn or at session end and proposes facts; or\n   - the application records structured events (purchases, settings changes) directly.\n   - Extraction at write time keeps reads cheap *because* a read happens on every call and a write only\n     when something changes.\n2. **Read: build the working set.** The last *n* turns verbatim, a rolling summary of older turns,\n   semantic facts for this user that match the task, and episodic items ranked by relevance, recency and\n   importance. Scope filters (tenant, user, agent, purpose) apply inside the query, not after, for the\n   same reason as ACLs in RAG.\n3. **Update and consolidate.** New facts that contradict old ones must supersede them, not sit beside\n   them; duplicates merge; many episodes collapse into one summary.\n   - **Embedding similarity is a poor contradiction detector:** \"lives in Berlin\" and \"moved to Lisbon\"\n     may not be close vectors, while \"likes Python\" and \"dislikes Python\" are.\n   - **Typed slots** (`home_city`, `answer_style`) for facts that drive behaviour: an update is an upsert\n     by key, so supersession is deterministic.\n   - **Model adjudication for free text:** retrieve the few nearest memories and ask a model to choose\n     add, update, delete or no-op against them.\n   - **Run consolidation in the background** *because* it needs a model call and should not sit in the\n     request path.\n4. **Forget.** Time-to-live for episodic items, explicit deletion on user request, purpose limits\n   (memory collected for support is not used for marketing), and tenant off-boarding. Deletion must reach\n   every derived copy, including embeddings and summaries, which is why memory rows need lineage back to\n   their source events.\n\n### A read policy, concretely\nFrom the generative-agents line of work, score each candidate as a weighted sum, then take the top *k*\nunder a token budget:\n\n`score = w_rel·sim(q, m) + w_rec·exp(−age/τ) + w_imp·importance`\n\n- **Terms:** relevance (embedding similarity to the current task), recency (exponential decay on age),\n  importance (a score assigned at write time), each normalized to [0, 1] so the weights mean what they\n  say.\n- **Two stages at scale:** an ANN search fetches a few hundred candidates by relevance and the composite\n  score reranks only those, *because* computing recency and importance over every row is wasted work.\n- **Weights and τ are product decisions:** a coding assistant weights recency (yesterday's branch\n  matters, last quarter's does not); a CRM assistant weights importance (the contract renewal date\n  matters however old it is).\n\n### Who controls paging\n- **Code-controlled:** the application decides what enters the context, as above.\n- **Model-controlled (MemGPT line of work):** the OS analogy made literal; the model gets tools to read,\n  write and evict memory and decides when to page items in and out. Same operations, controller moved\n  into the model.\n- **The trade:** flexibility on open-ended tasks, at the cost of predictability, extra calls, and a model\n  that sometimes forgets to remember.\n- **Common production mix:** a code-driven baseline read plus model-callable memory tools for the rest.\n\n### Scoping and isolation\n- **Every memory row carries** tenant id, user id, the agent or purpose it was collected for, a source\n  event id and timestamps.\n- **Cross-user leakage is the most embarrassing failure here.** It happens when the key is the agent\n  instead of the user, or when a shared summary is built across users.\n\n### Budget arithmetic (illustrative)\n| Design | Per call | Over a 200-turn conversation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Verbatim replay (roughly 150 tokens per turn) | about 30,000 tokens at turn 200 | Σ 150·t for t = 1…200 ≈ 3.0M input tokens |\n| Bounded: last 10 turns (about 1,500 tokens) + 400-token summary + 800 tokens of memories | under 3,000 tokens, flat | at most about 0.6M |\n\n- **A tenfold per-call reduction that stays flat** as the conversation grows.\n- **Linear-per-call growth is quadratic in total,** because verbatim replay re-sends every earlier turn\n  on every call. That flat line is the architectural goal.","example":"### A memory layer with the tiers wired together\nA session store as the source of truth, a write policy that extracts facts after each turn and\nsupersedes contradictions (by slot for typed facts, by model adjudication for free text), and a read\npolicy that assembles a bounded working set using relevance, recency and scope. `llm`, `embed`, `cosine`\nand `store` stand in for your model, embedding model and database.\n\n```python\nimport math, time\n\nRECENT_TURNS, SUMMARY_EVERY, MEM_BUDGET_TOKENS = 10, 10, 800   # summary cadence <= recent window: no turn falls between them\n\ndef build_context(user, tenant, convo_id, query, store, w_rel=1.0, w_rec=0.5, w_imp=0.3):\n    turns = store.turns(convo_id)                                    # session state: full transcript\n    recent, older = turns[-RECENT_TURNS:], turns[:-RECENT_TURNS]\n    summary = store.summary(convo_id) if older else \"\"\n    q, now = embed(query), time.time()\n    cands = store.memories(tenant=tenant, user=user, purpose=\"assistant\")   # scope inside the query\n    def score(m):\n        recency = math.exp(-(now - m.updated_at) / (7 * 86400))     # decays over about a week\n        return w_rel * cosine(q, m.vec) + w_rec * recency + w_imp * m.importance\n    picked, used = [], 0\n    for m in sorted(cands, key=score, reverse=True):\n        if used + m.tokens > MEM_BUDGET_TOKENS: break\n        picked.append(m); used += m.tokens\n    return {\"summary\": summary, \"memories\": [m.text for m in picked], \"recent\": recent}\n\ndef after_turn(user, tenant, convo_id, store):\n    turns = store.turns(convo_id)\n    if len(turns) % SUMMARY_EVERY == 0:                              # compaction: summarize, never delete\n        store.set_summary(convo_id, llm(f\"Update this summary:\\n{store.summary(convo_id)}\\n\\n\"\n                                        f\"New turns:\\n{turns[-SUMMARY_EVERY:]}\").text)\n    facts = llm(\"Extract durable facts as JSON [{slot or null, text, importance 0-1}] \"\n                f\"from the user's own words only:\\n{turns[-2:]}\").json   # not from tool output\n    for f in facts:\n        common = dict(tenant=tenant, user=user, purpose=\"assistant\", source_event=turns[-1].id)\n        if f[\"slot\"]:                                                # typed fact: upsert by key\n            store.upsert_slot(slot=f[\"slot\"], text=f[\"text\"], **common)\n            continue\n        near = store.nearest(tenant, user, embed(f[\"text\"]), k=3)    # free text: let a model adjudicate\n        op = llm(f\"New: {f['text']}\\nExisting: {[m.text for m in near]}\\n\"\n                 \"Reply JSON {op: add|update|noop, target_index}\").json\n        if op[\"op\"] == \"add\":\n            store.add_memory(text=f[\"text\"], importance=f[\"importance\"], **common)\n        elif op[\"op\"] == \"update\":                                   # supersede, keep lineage\n            store.supersede(near[op[\"target_index\"]].id, text=f[\"text\"], **common)\n```\n\n### Tracing the write policy over two turns and one preference\n| When | User says | Path | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| March | \"I'm based in Berlin\" | extractor emits `{slot: \"home_city\", text: \"Berlin\"}` | slot written |\n| June | \"we just moved to Lisbon\" | same slot, upsert | Berlin replaced, June turn recorded as source; no later read can surface both cities |\n| Any time | \"I like answers with code first\" | no slot; three nearest fetched, one is \"prefers short answers\" | adjudicator chooses `add`: the preferences are compatible |\n| Contrast | \"prefers short answers\" vs \"prefers detailed answers\" | a cosine threshold alone would merge them as a duplicate (keeping whichever text won) or keep both as a contradiction, depending on where it sat | the adjudicator sees a conflict and chooses `update` |\n\n### Runnable: the same read policy, two products\nPure Python. One coding memory and one CRM memory, scored under two weight profiles.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef score(sim, age_days, importance, w_rel, w_rec, w_imp, tau_days):\n    return w_rel * sim + w_rec * math.exp(-age_days / tau_days) + w_imp * importance\n\nmemories = {                       # (similarity, age in days, importance)\n    \"branch feature/rrf is checked out\": (0.6, 1, 0.3),\n    \"contract renews on 1 March\":        (0.5, 90, 0.9),\n}\nprofiles = {                       # (w_rel, w_rec, w_imp, tau_days)\n    \"coding assistant\": (1.0, 1.0, 0.2, 7),\n    \"CRM assistant\":    (1.0, 0.2, 1.0, 30),\n}\nfor name, w in profiles.items():\n    best = max(memories, key=lambda m: score(*memories[m], *w))\n    print(f\"{name:16s} -> {best}\")\n# coding assistant -> branch feature/rrf is checked out   (1.527 vs 0.68)\n# CRM assistant    -> contract renews on 1 March          (1.41 vs 1.093)\n```","patterns":["**Typed slots with a unique key.** For facts that drive behaviour or billing, store one row per `(tenant_id, user_id, slot)` with a unique constraint and write with an upsert (in Postgres, `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE`). Supersession becomes a database guarantee. Monitor contradiction rate in sampled working sets.","**Lineage-driven deletion with a canary test.** Every memory, embedding and summary records its source event ids; a purge is a lineage query that deletes rows and vectors and regenerates affected summaries. Run a scheduled test that plants a unique fact, purges it, and asserts no read surfaces it. Monitor time from request to completed purge.","**Consolidation off the request path.** Enqueue extraction and adjudication after each turn and run them in a background worker, so a slow model call never delays a reply. Monitor queue lag and memories per user, which should plateau rather than grow without bound.","**Two-stage scoped read.** ANN search with tenant, user and purpose filters inside the query fetches a few hundred candidates; the composite score reranks them under a token budget. Monitor working-set tokens per call and memory hit rate (retrieved items the answer actually used).","**Provenance-gated writes.** Extract only from the user's own turns and tag each memory with its source type, rejecting tool output, retrieved documents and web pages. Monitor memories created per session by source type; anything from a non-user source is an injection signal."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"User turn\"] --> SS[(\"Session store: full transcript\")]\n    SS -->|\"background job\"| X[\"Extract facts from user words only\"]\n    X -->|\"typed slot\"| U[\"Upsert by tenant, user, slot\"]\n    X -->|\"free text\"| AJ[\"Model adjudicates add, update, noop\"]\n    U --> M[(\"Memory store with lineage to source events\")]\n    AJ --> M\n    Q[\"Next call\"] --> R[\"Scoped read: tenant, user, purpose\"]\n    M --> R\n    SS -->|\"last 10 turns and rolling summary\"| W[\"Working set under token budget\"]\n    R -->|\"rank by relevance, recency, importance\"| W\n    W --> L[\"Model call\"]","caption":"The write path runs down from the user turn into the memory store, in the background after each turn;\nthe read path joins at the scoped read and rebuilds the working set for every call. Note that both paths\napply tenant, user and purpose scope, and that the transcript store stays the source of truth."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not just replay the whole conversation, or embed every utterance and retrieve by similarity?","a":"- **Replay grows without bound:** cost and latency rise with every turn, the window eventually fills,\n  quality drops as facts get buried, and nothing crosses sessions, devices or agents.\n- **Embed-everything cannot update or delete:** it returns \"lives in Berlin\" beside \"moved to Lisbon\",\n  leaks across agents, and misses decisions, which are not textually similar to the question needing them.\n- **So** use explicit scoped stores plus write, read, update and forget policies."},{"q":"The assistant tells a user they live in Berlin a month after they said they moved. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the write path first:** did the extractor emit a fact for the June turn, and did it land in a\n  typed slot such as `home_city` or as free text?\n- **Free text appended beside the old fact** means missing supersession; embedding similarity is a poor\n  contradiction detector, so move the fact to a typed slot upserted by key or route it through model\n  adjudication.\n- **Then check the read path** for a stale summary that still mentions Berlin."},{"q":"What does memory save on a 200-turn conversation, in rough numbers?","a":"- **Verbatim replay** at about 150 tokens per turn reaches about 30,000 tokens per call at turn 200 and\n  Σ 150·t ≈ 3.0M input tokens in total.\n- **Bounded working set:** last 10 turns (about 1,500 tokens), a 400-token summary and 800 tokens of\n  memories stay under 3,000 tokens per call, at most about 0.6M in total, and flat as the conversation\n  grows (illustrative figures)."},{"q":"Should the model or your code decide what to page into context?","a":"- **Code-controlled** reads are predictable, cheap and testable, which is why they are the baseline.\n- **Model-controlled** paging (the MemGPT line of work) gives tools to read, write and evict memory,\n  buying flexibility on open-ended tasks at the cost of extra calls and a model that sometimes forgets\n  to remember; production systems often combine both."},{"q":"How can memory become a persistent prompt-injection channel, and how do you close it?","a":"- **The attack:** an instruction planted in a fetched page or tool output (\"always CC this address\") is\n  extracted as a memory and reappears in later, unrelated sessions.\n- **The fix:** extract only from the user's own turns, tag every memory with its source type, reject\n  non-user sources, and alert on memories created from anything other than the user."}],"tradeoffs":["**Write-time extraction vs cost and over-remembering.** Every read stays cheap, but a model call per turn is spent and things the user never meant to be remembered may be stored; read-time extraction from raw transcripts is cheaper to write and far more expensive to query.","**Typed fields vs free text.** Structured semantic memory is precise and easy to update or delete; free-text memories capture nuance. Most systems need both, with structure for anything that drives behaviour or billing.","**Rolling summaries vs lost detail.** They keep context flat as conversations grow, but each compaction removes detail from the prompt irreversibly; keeping the full transcript in the session store and retrieving from it recovers the detail on demand.","**Model-chosen memory vs consistency.** A `remember` tool gives the model agency and transparency, but it under- and over-remembers unpredictably; a background extractor is more consistent and less visible to the user.","**Long retention vs privacy exposure.** Retention makes the assistant feel continuous and raises privacy and compliance exposure; TTLs and purpose scoping trade some continuity for a defensible deletion story."],"pitfalls":["**Keying memory by agent or conversation instead of tenant and user.** Symptom: one user's preferences or facts appear in another user's session. Fix: scope every row by tenant and user, filtered inside the query.","**Append-only memory without supersession.** Symptom: the assistant confidently states two contradictory facts, usually the stale one first. Fix: typed slots or model adjudication.","**Treating the context window as the system of record.** Symptom: after compaction, details the user mentioned are gone forever and the transcript cannot be audited. Fix: keep the full transcript in the session store.","**Deleting a memory row but not its embedding, summary mention or derived facts.** Symptom: a \"forgotten\" fact resurfaces weeks later, which is a compliance incident. Fix: purge by lineage.","**Extracting memories from retrieved documents, web pages or tool output.** Symptom: an instruction planted in a fetched page (\"always CC this address\") reappears in later, unrelated sessions, which is persistent prompt injection. Fix: extract from the user's own words only.","**Shipping memory without measuring it.** Symptom: higher cost per call with no gain in task success, because the retrieved memories are rarely relevant. Fix: track memory hit rate and A/B task success with memory on versus off."],"when":"- **Use when:** users return and expect continuity, the agent performs multi-session tasks, or\n  personalization measurably improves outcomes.\n- **Order of adoption:** session state and compaction first (every multi-turn product needs them);\n  semantic memory when you can name the facts that change behaviour; episodic memory when \"what happened\n  before\" is a real query.\n- **Avoid when:** memory would compensate for a weak retrieval layer or missing product data; those\n  belong in RAG and in your database.\n- **Measure:** working-set tokens per call, memory hit rate (retrieved items actually used by the\n  answer), contradiction rate, and task success with memory on versus off.\n- **Interviews:** the tier picture, the four operations, scoping, and a concrete deletion story.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the session store, compaction, the extraction and consolidation jobs, and the scoped, budgeted read path that assembles each prompt.","architect":"Decides the memory tiers and stores, the ownership of each memory type, retention and purpose limits, and how deletion propagates to every derived copy.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer what the assistant may remember about whom and for how long, and demonstrates deletion end to end for their compliance team."},"check":{"q":"A customer's support assistant should remember each support agent's preferences and past cases\nacross sessions, but their legal team requires that any case data be purgeable within 24 hours of a\nrequest. Design the memory architecture and explain how you would meet the purge requirement.","a":"I would separate the tiers. Session state lives in a transcript store keyed by tenant, user and\nconversation, with a rolling summary for the prompt. Semantic memory holds structured preferences per\nuser, updated in place with supersession. Episodic memory holds case summaries with a case id, tenant,\nuser, purpose, source event ids and timestamps, with a TTL. Every derived artifact, embeddings, summaries\nand extracted facts, records the source event ids it came from. A purge request for a case then becomes\na lineage query: find every row derived from events of that case, delete rows and vectors, and\nregenerate any summary that referenced them. Reads filter by tenant and user inside the query so nothing\ncrosses accounts. I would prove the purge with a test that plants a unique fact, purges, and shows that\nno retrieval, summary or extraction can surface it afterwards."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a session transcript store, rather than the context window, treated as the source of truth for a conversation?","options":["The context window cannot hold more than a few turns","Compaction removes detail from the prompt, so without a persistent transcript that detail is lost and cannot be retrieved or audited later","Models require transcripts to be stored before answering","Transcripts are cheaper to send than summaries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The prompt is a view built per call. Summarizing older turns keeps it small, but only works safely if the full record exists elsewhere to retrieve from and to audit."},{"q":"What problem does supersession in semantic memory solve?","options":["It reduces embedding dimensionality for faster search","It prevents the model from storing more than one fact per turn","It ensures a new fact that contradicts an old one replaces it, so the assistant does not retrieve both and state the stale one","It allows memories to be shared across tenants"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Append-only memory accumulates contradictions (\"lives in Berlin\", \"moved to Lisbon\"). Linking the new fact to the one it replaces keeps reads consistent and makes deletion traceable."},{"q":"Which design most directly prevents one user's memories from appearing in another user's session?","options":["Encrypting memory rows at rest","Using a separate embedding model per user","Keying every memory by tenant and user and applying those filters inside the retrieval query","Limiting memories to the last seven days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Leakage comes from wrong keys and post-filtering, not from encryption or recency. Scope must be part of the row and enforced where candidates are selected."}]},{"key":"system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","sources":["Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software","Vernon, 2013 — Implementing Domain-Driven Design","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"- **One word, three meanings.** Ask billing, support and sales what a \"customer\" is. Billing: a legal\n  entity with a payment method and invoices. Support: a person with a login and a ticket history. Sales:\n  an account with an owner, a pipeline stage and a renewal date. Each is right inside its own work and\n  wrong inside the others'.\n- **One Customer table for everyone fails.** Nobody fully understands it, a change for billing breaks\n  support, and every team must coordinate with every other team to ship anything.\n- **The enterprise canonical model fails too.** A committee defines the single true schema and everyone\n  conforms, but meaning is local: the model becomes a lowest common denominator with dozens of optional\n  fields, and the committee becomes the bottleneck.\n- **Splitting by technical layer or table recreates the coupling.** A \"customer service\" that owns the\n  Customer table for everyone just moves the same coupling behind an API.\n- **DDD models the inconsistency instead of fixing it.** Each area uses its own language consistently\n  inside a boundary; between boundaries, translation is explicit. Conway's observation (systems mirror\n  their builders' communication structures) is why it works: a boundary matching a team's responsibility\n  is one that team can own and change alone.\n- **AI raises the stakes.** An agent reaches across many contexts at once through tools, and retrieval\n  indexes mix documents written in several vocabularies; without drawn boundaries the agent inherits\n  every ambiguity and resolves it by guessing.","how":"### Ubiquitous language\n- **One vocabulary, everywhere in the area.** Developers and domain experts use the same terms, and so\n  does the code: class names, tool names, event names, prompts.\n- **The boundary test.** If a term means two things, you have found a boundary; if two terms mean one\n  thing, pick one.\n- **For AI products** this language also goes into system prompts, tool descriptions and evaluation\n  rubrics, so inconsistency there shows up directly as model errors.\n\n### Bounded contexts\n- **Definition.** The boundary inside which a model and its language are consistent. It owns its data,\n  its invariants and its storage; nothing outside writes to that storage directly.\n- **Draw by language and responsibility,** not by data volume or technology. Billing, Support and Sales\n  are three contexts, each with its own Customer, and that is correct.\n- **Deployment is a separate decision.** A context may be a service, a module in a modular monolith, or a\n  schema in one database; the boundary is about ownership and meaning.\n\n### Finding the boundaries\nBoundaries are discovered, not decreed. Map the business process with domain experts as a timeline of\ndomain events (event storming), then look for seams. Four signals mark one:\n\n| Signal | Example |\n|---|---|\n| A term changes meaning | \"customer\", \"active\", \"account\" |\n| Who decides changes | finance owns the dunning rule, support owns the escalation rule |\n| Rate of change differs | pricing rules change weekly, ledger rules almost never |\n| A set of rules must hold together at the same instant | order total and credit limit: that set belongs inside one context, so the seam runs around it |\n\n- **Wrong seam test:** when two candidate contexts must constantly ask each other questions to make any\n  decision, the seam is in the wrong place.\n\n### Subdomain classification\n| Subdomain | What it is | Investment |\n|---|---|---|\n| *Core* | where the business differentiates | most careful modelling, your best people |\n| *Supporting* | necessary but not differentiating | adequate, build simply |\n| *Generic* | authentication, invoicing | buy or borrow |\n\n- **For most AI products the core is the domain logic and the data, not the model call.** This\n  classification tells you where custom work belongs and where a vendor is fine.\n\n### Context mapping: how boundaries relate\n- **Shared kernel:** two contexts share a small model and change it together. Cheap and dangerous; keep\n  it tiny.\n- **Customer–supplier:** the downstream team's needs feed the upstream team's roadmap.\n- **Conformist:** downstream adopts the upstream model wholesale because it has no leverage, typical with\n  a vendor API.\n- **Anticorruption layer (ACL):** downstream translates the upstream model into its own at the boundary,\n  *because* a leaking foreign model corrupts your language one field at a time. The most important\n  pattern for AI integrations: the agent's context sees its own concepts, with adapters translating each\n  external system.\n- **Open host service with a published language:** upstream exposes a stable, documented interface in a\n  well-defined language for many consumers. A tool schema an agent calls is exactly a published language.\n- **Separate ways:** the contexts do not integrate at all. Sometimes correct.\n\n### Inside a context: aggregates, entities, value objects, domain events\n- **Aggregate.** A cluster of objects changed together under one transaction through one entry point (the\n  root), with invariants that hold at the end of every transaction. Example: an `Order` with its lines and\n  the invariant \"total must not exceed the approved credit of 10,000\".\n- **One entry point.** Lines change only through `order.add_line(...)`, *because* if code could insert a\n  line row directly, nothing would recheck the total.\n- **Keep aggregates small** as the true invariants allow, *because* the aggregate is the unit of locking\n  and a large one serializes unrelated edits.\n- **Reference other aggregates by id,** not by object, so loading an order does not drag in the customer.\n- **One aggregate per transaction;** the others catch up through *domain events* (\"OrderPlaced\",\n  \"InvoicePaid\"). Cross-context changes are always eventually consistent, *because* a single transaction\n  spanning contexts would couple their storage and their uptime. Here DDD hands over to event-driven\n  architecture and sagas.\n- **Entities vs value objects.** *Entities* have identity over time (the order is the same order after its\n  lines change). *Value objects* are defined by their attributes and are immutable (`Money(120, \"EUR\")`),\n  which makes them safe to share and pass across boundaries.\n- **Repository.** Loads and saves whole aggregates, one per aggregate type, which keeps the invariant check\n  from being bypassed by partial writes.\n\n### Applying it to an AI system\n- **One tool, or tool group, per bounded context,** speaking that context's published language; the\n  agent's tool list then mirrors the context map.\n- **Translation lives in tool adapters (the ACL), never in the prompt.** Asking the model to reconcile\n  three meanings of \"customer\" at runtime is asking it to redo your architecture per request.\n- **Scope retrieval indexes and memory per context** when vocabularies conflict, or tag them with the\n  context so the prompt can say which \"account\" a passage refers to.\n- **Consequential actions respect aggregate boundaries:** an agent step that must update two contexts is\n  a saga with compensation, not one transaction.\n- **Evals are per context,** written in that context's language by that context's experts.","example":"### Two contexts, one anticorruption layer, one published tool\nEach context has its own Customer; the support agent sees only its own concept. The translation from\nbilling's model happens in the adapter, not in the prompt. `billing_api`, `mapping_repo` and `tickets`\nstand in for the real clients.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom datetime import date\n\n# Billing context: its own language and storage, owned by the billing team\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass BillingAccount:\n    account_id: str\n    legal_name: str\n    plan: str                      # billing's codes: \"ent\", \"pro\", \"free\"\n    past_due_since: date | None\n\n# Support context: a different \"customer\", shaped for support work\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass SupportCustomer:\n    customer_id: str\n    display_name: str\n    tier: str                      # support's words: \"enterprise\", \"pro\", \"free\"\n    payment_blocked: bool\n    open_tickets: int\n\nPLAN_TO_TIER = {\"ent\": \"enterprise\", \"pro\": \"pro\", \"free\": \"free\"}\n\nclass BillingAdapter:              # anticorruption layer: billing model -> support language\n    def __init__(self, billing_api, mapping_repo):\n        self.api, self.map = billing_api, mapping_repo\n    def payment_status(self, customer_id: str) -> dict:\n        acct: BillingAccount = self.api.get_account(self.map.billing_id_for(customer_id))\n        blocked = acct.past_due_since is not None and (date.today() - acct.past_due_since).days > 14\n        if acct.plan not in PLAN_TO_TIER:            # fail loudly: a silent default corrupts meaning\n            raise UnmappedUpstreamValue(f\"billing plan {acct.plan!r}\")\n        return {\"payment_blocked\": blocked, \"tier\": PLAN_TO_TIER[acct.plan]}\n\ndef lookup_customer(customer_id: str, tickets, billing: BillingAdapter) -> SupportCustomer:\n    \"\"\"Published language for the agent: no billing field names leak into its context.\"\"\"\n    b = billing.payment_status(customer_id)\n    return SupportCustomer(customer_id=customer_id, display_name=tickets.name(customer_id),\n                           tier=b[\"tier\"], payment_blocked=b[\"payment_blocked\"],\n                           open_tickets=tickets.count_open(customer_id))\n\nTOOLS = [{\"name\": \"lookup_customer\", \"description\": \"Fetch the support view of a customer.\",\n          \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"customer_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                           \"required\": [\"customer_id\"]}}]\n```\n\n### Running one lookup, then an upstream change\n| Moment | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Lookup | billing returns `BillingAccount(\"B-881\", \"Acme GmbH\", \"ent\", 2026-09-30)` |\n| On 2026-10-20 | 20 days past due, more than 14, so the agent sees `SupportCustomer(tier=\"enterprise\", payment_blocked=True, open_tickets=3)` and never the words \"ent\" or `past_due_since` |\n| Billing launches a \"team\" plan, with a `.get(plan, \"free\")` default | every team-plan customer is silently treated as free tier: a wrong answer with no error anywhere |\n| Same launch, with the explicit map | the upstream change becomes a failing call and an alert on day one |\n\n- **The 14-day rule is a support-context rule,** so it lives in the support side's adapter, versioned and\n  tested there; neither billing nor the prompt needs to know it.\n- **The point of an ACL:** translation is a contract, and an unmapped value is a contract break.\n\n### Runnable: an aggregate that protects its invariant\nPure Python. `Money` is a value object; `Order` is the aggregate root and the only way to add lines.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Money:                                   # value object: immutable, equal by value\n    amount: Decimal\n    currency: str\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if other.currency != self.currency:\n            raise ValueError(\"currency mismatch\")\n        return Money(self.amount + other.amount, self.currency)\n\nclass CreditLimitExceeded(Exception):\n    pass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Order:                                   # aggregate root: the only way to change lines\n    order_id: str\n    customer_id: str                           # another aggregate, referenced by id\n    credit: Money\n    lines: list = field(default_factory=list)\n    events: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def total(self) -> Money:\n        t = Money(Decimal(0), self.credit.currency)\n        for _, price in self.lines:\n            t = t + price\n        return t\n\n    def add_line(self, sku: str, price: Money) -> None:\n        if (self.total() + price).amount > self.credit.amount:   # invariant checked on every change\n            raise CreditLimitExceeded(f\"{self.order_id} would exceed {self.credit.amount}\")\n        self.lines.append((sku, price))\n        self.events.append((\"OrderLineAdded\", self.order_id, sku))  # other contexts react later\n\norder = Order(\"O-1\", \"C-7\", Money(Decimal(10000), \"EUR\"))\norder.add_line(\"seats\", Money(Decimal(9880), \"EUR\"))\norder.add_line(\"support\", Money(Decimal(120), \"EUR\"))\nprint(order.total().amount)                    # 10000: equal to the credit is allowed\ntry:\n    order.add_line(\"extra\", Money(Decimal(1), \"EUR\"))\nexcept CreditLimitExceeded as e:\n    print(\"rejected:\", e)                      # rejected: O-1 would exceed 10000\n```","patterns":["**Storage ownership enforced by the database.** When contexts share one database, give each its own schema and database role, and grant no role write access to another context's schema. Other contexts read through an API or events. Monitor permission-denied errors from cross-schema access attempts.","**Strict ACL mappings with contract tests.** Translate every upstream enum through an explicit map that raises on unknown values, and run contract tests against the upstream sandbox in CI. Alert on unmapped value errors in production, because each one is an upstream change you have not modelled yet.","**Domain events through a transactional outbox.** Write the aggregate change and an outbox row in the same transaction; a relay publishes the row to the broker. Other contexts consume and update their own models. Monitor outbox lag and consumer lag per context.","**Tool groups that mirror the context map.** Register agent tools per context with that context's published language and owners, and run per-context evals written by its experts. Monitor tool-selection errors and eval scores by context, so a regression points at one owner.","**Coupling metrics on change.** Tag pull requests and releases by the contexts they touch, and track how many contexts a typical change spans and how often releases must be coordinated. A rising number means a seam is in the wrong place."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    AG[\"Support agent\"] -->|\"lookup_customer tool\"| SC[\"Support context: SupportCustomer\"]\n    SC --> ACL1[\"Billing adapter: anticorruption layer\"]\n    ACL1 -->|\"plan, past_due_since\"| BC[\"Billing context: BillingAccount\"]\n    SC --> ACL2[\"CRM adapter: anticorruption layer\"]\n    ACL2 --> CRM[\"Sales context: account and stage\"]\n    BC --> DB1[(\"Billing schema\")]\n    SC --> DB2[(\"Support schema\")]\n    BC -.->|\"domain event InvoicePaid\"| SC\n    ACL1 -.->|\"unmapped plan raises an alert\"| AL[\"On-call owner\"]","caption":"A context map for the support agent in the examples. The agent only speaks its own published language;\neach upstream model is translated in an adapter, and contexts change each other only through events."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a bounded context, and why not agree on one canonical customer schema?","a":"- **Definition:** the boundary inside which one model and its language are consistent; it owns its\n  data, invariants and storage, and nothing outside writes to that storage directly.\n- **Why not canonical:** meaning is local, so a single schema becomes a lowest common denominator with\n  dozens of optional fields, owned by a committee that becomes the bottleneck for every change."},{"q":"How would you find the context boundaries in a business you do not know yet?","a":"- **Event storming:** map the process with domain experts as a timeline of domain events, then look for\n  seams.\n- **Seam signals:** a term changes meaning, a different person decides changes, the rate of change\n  differs; rules that must hold together at the same instant belong inside one context.\n- **Wrong seam test:** if two candidate contexts must constantly ask each other questions to decide\n  anything, move the boundary."},{"q":"How big should an aggregate be, and what crosses aggregate boundaries?","a":"- **As small as the true invariants allow,** because the aggregate is the unit of locking; an `Order`\n  with its lines and a credit-limit invariant is one aggregate, the customer is another, referenced by id.\n- **One aggregate per transaction:** other aggregates and other contexts catch up through domain events\n  such as \"OrderPlaced\", published reliably with a transactional outbox."},{"q":"After billing launched a new plan, the agent started treating some customers as free tier. What went wrong?","a":"- **A catch-all default in the translation map:** `.get(plan, \"free\")` turned an unknown upstream value\n  into a wrong meaning with no error anywhere.\n- **Fix:** an explicit map that raises on unmapped values, contract tests against the upstream sandbox in\n  CI, and an alert on unmapped-value errors so the change surfaces on day one."},{"q":"When would you conform to an upstream model instead of building an anticorruption layer?","a":"- **Conform** when the upstream is tiny and stable or you have no leverage, as with many vendor APIs,\n  because translation code changes whenever either side changes.\n- **Build the ACL** when the foreign model would leak into your prompts, UI and tests, or when its\n  churn would otherwise become your churn.\n- **Skip DDD's ceremony** for a small product with one team and one language; one context is correct."},{"q":"An agent step must update records in two contexts. How do you make it safe?","a":"- **Not one transaction:** spanning contexts couples their storage and uptime, and leads to distributed\n  locks, timeouts under load and partial updates.\n- **Use a saga with compensation:** each context commits its own change and emits an event; if a later\n  step fails, a compensating action undoes the earlier one, and the agent sees eventual consistency."}],"tradeoffs":["**Separate models vs reconciliation.** Separate models per context remove cross-team coupling and keep each model small, but the same real-world thing exists in several places and must be reconciled through events or adapters.","**ACL vs maintenance.** An anticorruption layer protects your language from upstream churn, at the cost of a translation that changes whenever either side changes; for a tiny, stable upstream, conforming is cheaper.","**Eventual consistency vs disagreement windows.** Independent deployability and uptime come with windows where contexts disagree; when the business cannot tolerate that window, the boundary is probably drawn in the wrong place.","**Rich core model vs wasted rigor.** A rich core-domain model repays deep investment, while the same rigor on generic subdomains wastes it; misclassifying the core is the expensive mistake.","**One tool per context vs tool count.** Meanings stay clean and privileges scoped, but tools multiply; when contexts are many, a facade context that speaks the agent's language may be justified."],"pitfalls":["**One shared database schema behind several \"services\".** Symptom: a migration in one team's context breaks another team's deployment. Fix: one schema and role per context, with no cross-context writes.","**Contexts drawn by technical layer or by noun** (\"the customer service\"). Symptom: every feature touches every service and release trains must be coordinated. Fix: redraw by language and responsibility.","**An upstream model leaking through without an ACL.** Symptom: vendor field names and enums appear in your prompts, your UI and your tests. Fix: translate in an adapter at the boundary.","**Asking the model to reconcile conflicting vocabularies at runtime.** Symptom: answers mix the billing and support meanings of a term, and evals cannot agree on correctness. Fix: move translation into tool adapters.","**Translation tables with catch-all defaults.** Symptom: a new upstream enum value (a new plan, a new status) is quietly mapped to the wrong meaning and found weeks later through customer complaints rather than errors. Fix: raise on unmapped values and alert.","**Transactions that span contexts.** Symptom: distributed locks, timeouts under load, and partial updates nobody can compensate. Fix: one aggregate per transaction, with events or a saga across contexts."],"when":"- **Use when:** more than one team or vocabulary touches the system, an agent must integrate several\n  enterprise systems, or a shared schema has become the bottleneck for change.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a small product with one team and one language; one context is correct, so do\n  not fragment prematurely.\n- **Measure coupling:** how many contexts a typical change touches, how often cross-context releases must\n  be coordinated, and how many tool adapters contain translation versus pass-through.\n- **Day to day:** naming decisions, where a rule lives, and whether a new field belongs to you.\n- **Interviews:** draw a context map for a given business, place an agent and its tools on it, and explain\n  why an anticorruption layer belongs in the adapter rather than the prompt.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps each context's language consistent in code, tools and prompts, writes adapters that translate at the boundary, and publishes events instead of writing to another context's tables.","architect":"Draws the context map, classifies core versus supporting versus generic subdomains, chooses the relationship pattern per boundary, and decides where the agent sits on the map.","fde":"Discovers the customer's real vocabularies and system owners, maps each to a context and an adapter, and resolves \"which system is the source of truth\" before the agent is built."},"check":{"q":"You are building an agent that answers \"can this customer upgrade?\" using a CRM, a billing system\nand a support desk, each with its own notion of customer and status. Where do you put the logic that\nreconciles them, and why not in the prompt?","a":"I would treat the agent's own domain as a bounded context with its own small model, say an\nEligibility view with fields like tier, payment standing and open escalations, and build one adapter per\nupstream system acting as an anticorruption layer: the CRM adapter maps account stages to the agent's\ntier, the billing adapter maps past-due rules to payment standing, the support adapter counts\nescalations. The agent gets one tool in its own published language. Putting the reconciliation in the\nprompt would make the model re-derive the business rules on every request, from three vocabularies it\nonly partly understands, with no tests and no owner; a mapping change would be a prompt edit with\nunpredictable effects. In adapters the rules are code, owned by the team that owns each boundary,\nunit-tested, and versioned independently of the model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Billing, Support and Sales each define \"customer\" differently. What does domain-driven design recommend?","options":["Define one canonical Customer schema with every field and make all three conform","Keep a separate model per bounded context and translate explicitly at the boundaries","Store all customer data in one service that the other three call","Let the agent reconcile the differences at runtime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Meaning is local to each area of the business. A single model becomes a lowest common denominator owned by a committee; separate models with explicit translation let each team change independently."},{"q":"What is the role of an anticorruption layer when an agent integrates a vendor system?","options":["To scan vendor responses for malicious content","To translate the vendor's model into the agent's own language so foreign fields and semantics do not leak into prompts and code","To cache vendor responses for performance","To enforce rate limits on vendor calls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ACL protects a context's language. Without it, upstream names and enums spread through prompts, tests and UI, and every upstream change becomes your change."},{"q":"Why are changes that span two bounded contexts usually made eventually consistent via events rather than one transaction?","options":["Databases cannot run transactions across more than one table","Events are faster than transactions","A single transaction would couple the contexts' storage and availability, defeating the point of separate ownership","Domain-driven design forbids transactions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aggregates are the unit of transactional consistency; across contexts, independent deployability and uptime matter more than instantaneous agreement, so domain events and sagas carry the change."}]},{"key":"system-design/event-driven-architecture","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Fowler, 2017 — What do you mean by \"Event-Driven\"?","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Apache Kafka"],"why":"The naive design: the ingestion endpoint receives a PDF and, in the same request, calls the parser,\nchunker, embedder, index writer, notification service and analytics API. Three things go wrong as it grows.\n- **Temporal coupling.** The upload succeeds only if all six downstream services are up at that instant.\n  At 99.9% each, the chain is roughly 0.999⁶ ≈ 99.4% available, and its latency is the sum of six\n  latencies. The slowest or flakiest consumer sets the user's experience.\n- **Knowledge coupling.** Every new consumer (compliance wants a copy of every upload) means a producer\n  code change and redeploy, plus a new failure mode in its request path. The producer accumulates\n  reasons to change that have nothing to do with uploading.\n- **Lost history.** Once the request returns, the upload exists only as a row the producer chose to keep.\n  You cannot replay last Tuesday's uploads through a fixed chunker, because the calls were ephemeral.\n- **Events invert the dependency.** The producer only durably records the fact; consumers depend on the\n  event contract, the producer on nothing. Availability becomes \"can I write to the broker\" and history\n  becomes the log. That inversion, not the broker technology, is the idea.\n- **What it buys in AI systems.** You can add a billing meter, an eval sampler and an audit log to an\n  agent pipeline without touching the agent.","how":"### Events versus commands\n| | Event | Command |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grammar | past tense: `OrderPlaced`, `EmbeddingGenerated` | imperative: `ChargeCard` |\n| Mutability | immutable statement of fact, owned by the producer | a request that may be refused |\n| Addressee | none, no expected reply | exactly one handler |\n| Coupling | producer stays ignorant of consumers | re-introduces the coupling you were escaping |\n\n- **Why it matters:** an event implies no outcome the sender cares about; a command does.\n- **Smell:** publishing `SendWelcomeEmail` as an event is a remote procedure call with extra steps.\n\n### The envelope\nEvery event carries metadata separate from its payload:\n- **`event_id`** — globally unique, so consumers can deduplicate.\n- **`type` and `schema_version`** — so consumers can evolve independently.\n- **`occurred_at`** — when the fact happened.\n- **`aggregate_id`** — the entity it belongs to; this is the partition key (see below).\n- **`trace_id`** — for observability across hops.\n\nPayload styles (Fowler's names):\n- **Event notification** — thin: \"document 42 changed, fetch it\". Always fresh, but consumers call back.\n- **Event-carried state transfer** — a snapshot: \"document 42 now has these fields\". Removes the callback,\n  at the cost of larger messages and consumers holding copies of data that can go stale.\n\n### The broker and its guarantees\n| | Log-style (Kafka, Pulsar, Redpanda) | Queue-style (RabbitMQ, SQS) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Storage | appends to **partitions**, retained | message deleted once a consumer acknowledges it |\n| Consumers | each consumer group keeps its own **offset** | each message goes to one competing consumer |\n| Replay | yes: a new consumer starts from offset 0 and rebuilds state | no |\n| Best for | fan-out, history, rebuilds | simple work distribution |\n\n- **Ordering is per partition only.** Choose the partition key to be the thing whose order matters,\n  usually the aggregate id: all events for document 42 land in one partition in order, while documents 42\n  and 43 may interleave arbitrarily.\n- **Delivery is at least once in both styles.** A consumer that crashes after processing but before\n  acknowledging sees the message again, so consumers must be idempotent: record processed `event_id`s or\n  make the effect naturally repeatable.\n- **Exactly-once is built at the consumer, not given end to end.** Kafka's transactional producers cover\n  only Kafka-to-Kafka read-process-write loops; any effect outside the log (a database write, an HTTP\n  call) still needs idempotence.\n- **Partitions are the unit of parallelism.** Within one consumer group each partition is read by at most\n  one consumer at a time, so a topic with 12 partitions keeps at most 12 consumers of a group busy.\n- **Pick partition count up front.** It is a capacity decision, because adding partitions later changes\n  which partition a key hashes to and breaks per-key ordering across the change.\n\n### When a consumer cannot process an event\nA message that fails every time (malformed payload, a PDF that crashes the parser) is a **poison\nmessage**. How you retry it decides what you lose:\n\n| Strategy | Keeps per-key order? | Keeps partition flowing? | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Retry in place | yes | no | every later event on that partition, for every document hashed there, waits |\n| Retry topic or dead-letter queue (DLQ) | no | yes | a later event for the *same* key can overtake it (an `Updated` before its `Created`) |\n| Park the key | yes | yes | mark the key blocked, divert its later events aside too, release them in order once the head event is fixed or discarded |\n\nChoose by whether per-key order or partition throughput matters more for that event type, and decide it\nper topic, not by discovery in an incident.\n\n### Producing reliably\n1. The producer writes its own database row and publishes the event.\n2. Doing those as two separate operations is the **dual-write problem**: a crash between them loses the\n   event or emits one for a rolled-back transaction.\n3. The **transactional outbox** fixes it: write the event into the same database transaction, relay it\n   afterwards.\n\n### Choreography versus orchestration\n- **Choreography:** each service reacts to events and emits new ones; the workflow is emergent. Lighter\n  for two or three steps.\n- **Orchestration:** a dedicated component listens for events and issues commands to drive the steps.\n- **The switch point:** beyond a few steps nobody can answer \"where is order 42 right now\" without an\n  orchestrator or a very good trace. Multi-step agent workflows with compensation needs usually end up\n  orchestrated.\n\n### Flavours worth naming\n- **Event sourcing** makes the event log the system of record and rebuilds current state by folding events.\n- **CQRS** separates the write model from read models built from events.\n- Both are separate commitments with their own costs; \"event-driven\" does not require either.","example":"### Document ingestion as events\n`publish` and `consume` stand in for any broker client; what matters is the envelope, the partition key\nand the idempotent consumer.\n\n```python\nimport uuid, json, datetime as dt\n\ndef make_event(type_, aggregate_id, payload, trace_id, version=1):\n    return {\n        \"event_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()),      # dedupe key for consumers\n        \"type\": type_, \"schema_version\": version,\n        \"aggregate_id\": aggregate_id,       # partition key: per-document ordering\n        \"occurred_at\": dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n        \"trace_id\": trace_id, \"payload\": payload,\n    }\n\n# producer: the upload service knows nothing about who listens\ndef on_upload(doc_id, storage_uri, tenant_id, trace_id, publish):\n    ev = make_event(\"DocumentUploaded\", doc_id,\n                    {\"uri\": storage_uri, \"tenant_id\": tenant_id}, trace_id)\n    publish(topic=\"documents\", key=doc_id, value=json.dumps(ev))\n\n# consumer: embedder reacts, and tolerates redelivery. The vector store is not in the same\n# transaction as any dedupe table, so idempotence comes from the effect itself: chunk ids are\n# deterministic (doc_id:chunk_no), and re-indexing the same document upserts the same rows.\ndef on_document_uploaded(raw, chunk_and_embed, vector_upsert, publish):\n    ev = json.loads(raw)\n    doc_id = ev[\"aggregate_id\"]\n    chunks = chunk_and_embed(ev[\"payload\"][\"uri\"])          # list of (text, vector)\n    vector_upsert([{\"id\": f\"{doc_id}:{i}\", \"vector\": v, \"text\": t,\n                    \"tenant_id\": ev[\"payload\"][\"tenant_id\"]} for i, (t, v) in enumerate(chunks)])\n    chunk_count = len(chunks)\n    # crash here => redelivery re-embeds and overwrites identical ids (wasted work, no duplicates)\n    publish(topic=\"documents\", key=doc_id, value=json.dumps(\n        make_event(\"DocumentIndexed\", ev[\"aggregate_id\"],\n                   {\"chunks\": chunk_count}, ev[\"trace_id\"])))\n```\n\nWhat this design buys and where it is thin:\n- **New consumers are free.** A billing meter is one more consumer of `DocumentIndexed`; the upload service\n  and embedder do not change.\n- **Outages become lag.** If the embedder is down for an hour, events wait on the topic and are processed\n  in per-document order when it returns.\n- **Deterministic ids carry idempotence.** A random chunk id would add a second copy of the document on\n  every redelivery; the deterministic id turns redelivery into an overwrite.\n- **The downstream publish can be lost or duplicated.** That is acceptable only because the next consumer\n  is idempotent too; otherwise the embedder would need its own outbox.\n- **Gap:** if a re-upload produces *fewer* chunks, old high-numbered chunks survive. A real indexer deletes\n  `doc_id:*` ids beyond the new count or versions chunks by document revision.\n\n### Runnable: redelivery with and without an idempotent consumer\nSimulates a broker that redelivers after a crash, and shows why the dedupe key matters.\n\n```python\ndeliveries = [(\"e1\", \"doc42\", 5), (\"e2\", \"doc43\", 3), (\"e1\", \"doc42\", 5)]  # e1 redelivered\n\ndef naive(events):\n    billed = 0\n    for _eid, _doc, chunks in events:\n        billed += chunks                  # counts the redelivery twice\n    return billed\n\ndef idempotent(events):\n    seen, billed = set(), 0\n    for eid, _doc, chunks in events:\n        if eid in seen:                   # inbox check on event_id\n            continue\n        seen.add(eid)\n        billed += chunks\n    return billed\n\nprint(naive(deliveries), idempotent(deliveries))   # 13 8\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: synchronous chain vs events\n| Design | What must be up for an upload to succeed | Availability (99.9% each) | Upload latency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Six synchronous calls | producer + 6 services | 0.999⁷ ≈ 99.3% | sum of 7 latencies |\n| Event on a broker | producer + broker | 0.999² ≈ 99.8% | producer write + one broker append |\n\nThe six consumers still do the same work, but their outages show up as consumer lag instead of failed\nuploads (illustrative arithmetic, assuming independent failures). The 99.4% in \"Why it exists\" counts\nonly the six downstream services; counting the producer too gives the 99.3% here.","patterns":["**Outbox relay instead of dual writes.** When a service changes its database and must emit an event, write the event row in the same transaction and relay it with a poller or change-data capture (for example Debezium reading the outbox table). Monitor outbox backlog age; alert when the oldest unpublished row exceeds your freshness budget.","**Idempotent producer settings on Kafka.** Set `acks=all` and `enable.idempotence=true` (the default since Kafka 3.0, but set it explicitly) so the producer's own send retries do not duplicate or reorder within a partition. This does not make consumers exactly-once; monitor producer retry and error rates.","**Schema registry with compatibility checks.** Register every event schema and enforce a compatibility mode (for example backward compatibility in Confluent Schema Registry) in CI, so a breaking payload change fails the build instead of a consumer. Monitor schema versions in use per consumer.","**Lag-based alerting per consumer group.** Track lag per partition (Kafka's `kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe` prints it; managed services export it as a metric). Alert on lag that grows over minutes, and on one partition lagging while others are at zero, the poison-message shape.","**Bounded retries then DLQ.** On SQS set a redrive policy with `maxReceiveCount` (for example 5) to a dead-letter queue; on Kafka route to a retry topic after N attempts. Page on DLQ depth above zero for topics where order or completeness matters, and keep a replay tool."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Upload request\"] --> P[\"Upload service\"]\n    P -->|\"one DB transaction\"| DB[(\"Document row + outbox row\")]\n    DB --> R[\"Outbox relay\"]\n    R -->|\"key = document id\"| T[(\"documents topic, partitioned\")]\n    T --> E[\"Embedder group\"]\n    T --> B[\"Billing meter group\"]\n    T --> A[\"Audit log group\"]\n    E -->|\"DocumentIndexed\"| T\n    E -.->|\"after N failures\"| DLQ[(\"Dead-letter topic\")]\n    E --> V[(\"Vector store, deterministic ids\")]","caption":"The producer's only dependency is the outbox and the broker; each consumer group reads the same\npartitioned log at its own offset, and failures divert to a dead-letter topic instead of blocking it."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between an event and a command, and why does it matter for coupling?","a":"- **An event is a past-tense fact with no addressee.** `DocumentUploaded` is owned by the producer, and\n  any number of consumers may react or ignore it, so the producer never changes when a consumer is added.\n- **A command is an instruction to one handler.** `ChargeCard` expects an outcome and may be refused, so\n  the sender depends on that handler existing and behaving.\n- **Why it matters:** publishing a command dressed as an event keeps all the coupling of a remote call and\n  adds broker latency and at-least-once duplicates on top."},{"q":"Users report that deleted documents reappear in search results. The pipeline is event-driven. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect ordering first.** Ordering holds only within a partition; if `DocumentDeleted` and\n  `DocumentUploaded` were keyed differently, the indexer can apply the delete before the upload.\n- **Check the keys and offsets.** Confirm both event types use the document id as the partition key, and\n  look for redeliveries of an old upload after the delete (a rebalance or a retry topic can replay it).\n- **Fix:** key every event by aggregate id, and make the indexer reject events older than the version it\n  already holds, so a late upload cannot resurrect a deleted document."},{"q":"When would you not use an event-driven design?","a":"- **When the caller needs the result now.** If the user must see the answer in the same interaction, a\n  synchronous call (or a stream) is simpler than waiting on a downstream completion event.\n- **When there is one consumer and a short workflow.** A broker adds schemas, lag, idempotency and DLQs\n  for no decoupling benefit.\n- **When you cannot trace across services yet.** Without propagated trace ids, an emergent multi-hop flow\n  becomes undebuggable; build tracing first or keep the flow orchestrated."},{"q":"A topic has 12 partitions and one consumer group is falling behind. You add 20 more consumers. What happens?","a":"- **At most 12 consumers in the group do work.** Each partition is read by at most one consumer of a group\n  at a time, so the other 20 sit idle.\n- **Real levers:** speed up per-event processing, batch work inside the consumer, or plan a larger\n  partition count, accepting that adding partitions remaps keys and breaks per-key order across the change.\n- **Check for a poison message too.** One partition lagging while the rest are at zero means a retry loop,\n  not a capacity problem."},{"q":"How do you get an event published reliably when the service also writes to its database?","a":"- **Name the dual-write problem.** Writing the row and publishing separately can lose the event or publish\n  one for a rolled-back transaction if the process crashes between them.\n- **Use a transactional outbox.** Insert the event into an outbox table in the same transaction as the\n  row, then relay it with a poller or change-data capture.\n- **Expect duplicates downstream.** The relay can publish twice after a crash, so consumers still\n  deduplicate on `event_id`; monitor the age of the oldest unpublished outbox row."}],"tradeoffs":["**Producer independence vs the synchronous answer.** The uploader cannot tell the user \"indexed, ask away\" unless something waits for `DocumentIndexed`. This flips back toward a synchronous call when the user needs the result in the same interaction.","**Replay vs long-lived contracts.** Replayable logs rebuild indexes and backfill new consumers, but every event schema becomes a public contract. Once events are retained for months, a breaking payload change needs versioning and dual-reading consumers.","**Choreography vs visibility.** Small decoupled services, yet end-to-end visibility degrades with each hop. Past roughly four or five steps, or whenever compensation is needed, orchestration pays for itself.","**Event-carried state vs freshness.** Snapshots remove callbacks and survive producer outages, at the cost of duplicated data that drifts; thin notifications stay fresh but create a thundering herd of reads on the producer when a hot event fans out.","**At-least-once vs exactly-once.** At-least-once with idempotent consumers is robust and cheap but pushes deduplication state into every consumer; exactly-once brokers trade throughput and operational complexity for it."],"pitfalls":["**Commands disguised as events.** `SendEmailRequested` with exactly one intended handler. Symptom: producers break when \"their\" consumer changes, so the coupling never left. Fix: send a command, or publish the underlying fact.","**Dual writes without an outbox.** Rows exist with no corresponding event, usually noticed as \"the index is missing documents that are in the database\" after a deploy or crash. Fix: transactional outbox.","**Assuming global ordering.** A consumer that processes `DocumentDeleted` before `DocumentUploaded`, because the two were keyed differently, resurrects deleted content in the index. Fix: key by aggregate id.","**Non-idempotent consumers.** Duplicate embeddings, double-counted billing and repeated notifications appear after any consumer restart or broker rebalance. Fix: inbox table or deterministic ids.","**Event soup.** Dozens of topics with no owner, no schema registry and no documented consumers. Symptom: nobody dares delete or change anything, and behaviour is discovered by reading consumer code. Fix: one owning team per topic, every schema in a registry, and a catalogue of known consumers.","**Treating the broker as infinitely fast.** A slow consumer accumulates lag silently until a dashboard shows events processed hours after they occurred. Fix: consumer lag as a first-class alert.","**Retrying a poison message in place forever.** Symptom: lag climbing on one partition while every other partition of the group sits at zero, and the same offset looping in the error log. Fix: bounded retries, then a DLQ or a parked key, chosen per topic."],"when":"- **Use when:** more than one party cares about a fact, consumers may be down while producers are up, you\n  need to replay history, or the producer's latency budget cannot absorb the consumers' work.\n- **Typical AI uses:** ingestion pipelines (upload, parse, chunk, embed, index); agent run telemetry fanned\n  out to billing, evaluation sampling and audit; model-output moderation that must not block the response.\n- **Avoid when:** the caller needs the result now, there is exactly one consumer and the workflow is short,\n  or the team has no way to trace a flow across services yet.\n- **Measure:** consumer lag, redelivery rate and dead-letter volume; those three numbers tell you whether\n  the architecture is healthy.\n- **Interviews:** state the dual-write problem unprompted, explain per-partition ordering, and say\n  \"at-least-once plus idempotent consumers\" before anyone asks about exactly-once.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs event envelopes, chooses partition keys, writes idempotent consumers with an inbox table, and wires dead-letter queues and lag alerts for every consumer group.","architect":"Decides which facts become published contracts, who owns each event's schema, where choreography ends and orchestration begins, and which flows must remain synchronous.","fde":"Integrates with a customer's existing bus (often Kafka or SQS), maps their events onto the product's ingestion, and explains why the index trails the source system by seconds."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants uploaded contracts to be parsed, chunked, embedded and indexed, with an audit\nrecord per stage and a Slack notification when indexing completes. The current implementation does all\nof this inside the upload request and times out on large files. Redesign it with events and explain\nwhat guarantees each stage has.","a":"The upload handler stores the file, writes the document row and, in the same transaction, an\noutbox row for `DocumentUploaded` keyed by document id; a relay publishes it, so the user gets a fast\n\"received\" response and the event cannot be lost. Parser, chunker, embedder and indexer are consumer\ngroups on the same topic chain, each emitting its own completion event; keying every event by document\nid keeps per-document order. Audit and Slack are additional consumers of those events, so they add no\nlatency and no failure modes to the pipeline. Every consumer is idempotent because the broker delivers\nat least once: stages whose effect is a database write record the event id in an inbox table in the same\ntransaction, and the indexer, whose effect lands in a vector store outside that transaction, upserts\nchunks under deterministic ids so a redelivery overwrites rather than duplicates. The\nnotification consumer in particular must be idempotent, or every restart re-pings Slack. Failures go to\na dead-letter topic with alerts; lag per consumer group is the health metric. If the customer also needs\n\"ask questions immediately after upload\", the UI waits for `DocumentIndexed` via a push channel rather\nthan making indexing synchronous."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most clearly distinguishes an event from a command in a message-driven system?","options":["Events are JSON while commands use a binary encoding","An event states an immutable fact with no addressee; a command instructs one handler and implies an expected outcome","Events are always delivered exactly once and commands at least once","Commands can be replayed from the log but events cannot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The difference is semantic, not technical. A fact (\"OrderPlaced\") lets the producer ignore consumers; an instruction (\"ChargeCard\") binds sender and handler and reintroduces coupling. Encoding and delivery guarantees are independent of which one you publish."},{"q":"You need every event about the same document processed in order. What is the correct mechanism on a partitioned log?","options":["Enable \"global ordering\" on the topic","Use a single consumer for the whole topic","Partition by document id so all events for one document share a partition","Add a sequence number and have consumers sort incoming events by it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Partitioned logs guarantee order only within a partition, so the partition key must be the entity whose order matters. A single consumer sacrifices throughput and still breaks if the producer spread the events across partitions; client-side sorting cannot know when the sequence is complete."},{"q":"After a deploy, some rows exist in the orders table with no corresponding event on the bus. What is the likely cause?","options":["The broker's retention period expired","Consumers were not idempotent and dropped duplicates","The service wrote the row and published the event as two separate operations and crashed in between","The partition key was wrong, so events landed on an unread partition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is the dual-write problem: a database commit and a broker publish cannot be made atomic. A crash between them loses the event. The transactional outbox writes the event in the same database transaction and relays it afterwards, so an event exists if and only if the row does."}]},{"key":"system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)","Official docs: FastAPI (background tasks)"],"why":"- **AI work outlives connections.** A classic web request completes in tens of milliseconds, so holding\n  the connection is free. An LLM completion takes seconds, a tool-using agent run minutes, a batch of a\n  thousand document summaries an hour.\n- **Every hop has an idle timeout you do not control.** Load balancers and reverse proxies commonly\n  default to around a minute (the exact value varies by product); mobile networks drop connections when\n  the device changes cells. On timeout the client sees an error and retries while the server is still\n  working, so the same expensive run executes twice, tokens are paid twice, and side effects (a posted\n  comment, a charged card) happen twice.\n- **Capacity scales with run time, not work.** By Little's law, in-flight requests ≈ arrival rate × time\n  in system. At 10 requests per second averaging 30 seconds, the fleet holds about 300 open connections,\n  each with a goroutine, thread or coroutine, buffers and partial state. Double the run time and the count\n  doubles though useful work is unchanged; a deploy that restarts an instance drops every run it held.\n- **Synchronous work is lost on restart.** If the process restarts mid-request the run vanishes; the client\n  only learns the socket closed. There is no record the work existed, so nothing to resume.\n- **\"Raise every timeout to ten minutes\" only hides it.** Long-lived connections pile up, one deploy drops\n  them all, and the client still cannot tell \"slow\" from \"dead\". The real fix separates *accepting* work\n  from *doing* it, which is what asynchronous execution means.","how":"### Two meanings of \"asynchronous\"\n- **Async I/O inside a process** (`asyncio`, Node's event loop) lets one thread wait on many sockets\n  instead of blocking per request. It raises how many connections a server can hold but does not change\n  the interaction model; the language modules cover it.\n- **This lesson** is the *interaction* model between caller and service: does the caller wait for the\n  result or not?\n\n### The three shapes at a glance\n| Shape | Caller's state lives in | Fits | Breaks when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Synchronous | the connection | work well inside every timeout, caller needs the answer to continue | p99 nears the shortest timeout |\n| Streaming | the connection, with partial results | a person watching, seconds to about a minute | reconnects without resume re-run the generation |\n| Asynchronous job | a durable job record | long, retried, restart-surviving or unattended work | sub-second work, where it is pure overhead |\n\n### The synchronous shape\n- Client sends a request, server does the work, response carries the result.\n- **Failure semantics:** an error means the result was not delivered, *not* that the work did not happen.\n  That is why even synchronous calls with side effects need an idempotency key.\n\n### Streaming: synchronous with incremental delivery\n- Server-sent events or chunked responses push partial results as produced (tokens, tool-call progress).\n- Time-to-first-byte drops from full generation time to prefill time, which makes a 40-second answer\n  tolerable to a person.\n- The connection is still held, so timeouts and retries still apply. A reconnecting client needs a way to\n  resume (\"give me events after sequence 812\") or it re-runs the generation.\n\n### The asynchronous shape\n1. **Submit.** The client sends the request with a client-generated idempotency key. The server validates\n   it, persists a job record (`status=queued`, inputs, key, tenant, created_at) and returns `202 Accepted`\n   with a job id and a status URL.\n   - Only the job-store write must succeed inside the request, so it is fast, and the work survives a\n     restart *because* it is durable before anyone starts on it.\n   - A repeated key returns the existing job. A repeated key with a *different* body is rejected (`409`\n     or `422`), because returning the old job would hand the client a result for a request it did not make.\n2. **Execute.** A worker claims the job from a queue (Redis, SQS, Postgres with `SKIP LOCKED`), marks it\n   `running` with a lease or heartbeat, does the work, and writes the result and `succeeded` or `failed`.\n   The lease matters because a worker that dies mid-job must have its job reclaimed by another worker\n   after the lease expires; without it the job stays `running` forever.\n3. **Deliver.** The client learns the outcome by one of:\n\n| Channel | Strength | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Polling `GET /jobs/{id}` | simple, works through any proxy | costs requests |\n| Webhook the server calls | efficient | server must retry; receiver must verify and deduplicate |\n| Push (SSE or WebSocket) subscribed to the job | best UX for interactive clients | holds a connection |\n\n- **Status model:** a small state machine, `queued → running → succeeded | failed | cancelled`, often with\n  a `progress` field and an `attempt` counter.\n- **Retention:** results need a policy; a job store that keeps every result forever becomes the biggest\n  table in the system.\n\n### Cancellation is a request, not a guarantee\n- Detached work no longer stops when the browser tab closes, so the API needs `POST /jobs/{id}/cancel`.\n- The server can only set `cancel_requested`. The worker checks it between steps (between tool calls,\n  between document batches) and stops at the next safe point, *because* interrupting mid-step can leave a\n  half-applied side effect.\n- Effects already performed stay performed: a cancelled agent run may still have created a ticket.\n  Cancelling multi-step work with effects is a saga question (compensate or leave), and the final status\n  should say which steps completed.\n\n### The hybrid: respond if fast, otherwise hand back a job\n- Many APIs accept a `wait` budget. If the job finishes within, say, 20 seconds, the result returns inline;\n  otherwise the job id does.\n- Client code handles both outcomes. Short requests stay simple; long ones are not fragile.\n\n### Backpressure\n- An asynchronous front door accepts work faster than workers drain it, so the queue is where overload\n  becomes visible.\n- **Bound it:** reject submissions with `429` or `503` when queue depth or estimated wait exceeds a\n  threshold, and expose that wait estimate to clients.\n- An unbounded queue turns an outage into a multi-hour backlog that keeps failing after the cause is fixed.","example":"### FastAPI job service: submit, worker, status\nSubmit returns `202` and a job id, a worker drains a queue, a status endpoint reports progress. The\nin-memory queue and dict are for illustration; production uses a durable queue and a table so jobs\nsurvive restarts.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, uuid\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Response\n\njobs: dict[str, dict] = {}              # production: a table with status, result, attempt, lease\nqueue: asyncio.Queue[str] = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=1000)   # bounded => backpressure\n\nasync def run_analysis(text: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(5)              # stands in for a multi-second LLM/agent run\n    return f\"{len(text.split())} words\"\n\nasync def worker():\n    while True:\n        job_id = await queue.get()\n        jobs[job_id][\"status\"] = \"running\"\n        try:\n            jobs[job_id][\"result\"] = await run_analysis(jobs[job_id][\"input\"])\n            jobs[job_id][\"status\"] = \"succeeded\"\n        except Exception as e:          # production: retry with backoff, then dead-letter\n            jobs[job_id].update(status=\"failed\", error=str(e))\n        queue.task_done()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app):\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker()); yield; task.cancel()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\n@app.post(\"/analyses\", status_code=202)\nasync def submit(body: dict, response: Response):\n    job_id = body.get(\"idempotency_key\") or str(uuid.uuid4())\n    if job_id in jobs and jobs[job_id][\"input\"] != body[\"text\"]:\n        raise HTTPException(409, \"idempotency key reused with a different body\")\n    if job_id not in jobs:              # a retried submit returns the same job\n        jobs[job_id] = {\"status\": \"queued\", \"input\": body[\"text\"]}\n        try:\n            queue.put_nowait(job_id)\n        except asyncio.QueueFull:\n            del jobs[job_id]; raise HTTPException(503, \"busy, retry later\")\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/analyses/{job_id}\"\n    return {\"job_id\": job_id, \"status\": jobs[job_id][\"status\"]}\n\n@app.get(\"/analyses/{job_id}\")\nasync def status(job_id: str):\n    if job_id not in jobs:\n        raise HTTPException(404)\n    return jobs[job_id]\n```\n\nTrace one request:\n1. `POST {\"text\": \"...\", \"idempotency_key\": \"k1\"}` returns `202` with `Location: /analyses/k1` in a few\n   milliseconds.\n2. A `GET` one second later shows `running`.\n3. The client's network drops and it re-sends the same `POST`; it gets the same job, still `running`,\n   rather than a second five-second run. A full queue is refused immediately rather than growing silently.\n4. A `GET` after five seconds returns `succeeded` with the result.\n\nWhat the sketch omits is exactly what production needs:\n- **Durability:** a restart loses `jobs` and the queue, so both must be durable.\n- **Leases:** a crashed worker leaves the job `running` forever, so claims need a lease.\n- **Cross-instance dedupe:** a key held only in memory cannot deduplicate across instances, so the key\n  needs a unique constraint in the job table.\n\n### Runnable: pick the shape from p99 and Little's law\n```python\ndef in_flight(rate_per_s, avg_seconds):\n    return rate_per_s * avg_seconds            # Little's law: L = lambda * W\n\ndef shape(p99_s, smallest_timeout_s, human_watching):\n    if p99_s < 0.5 * smallest_timeout_s and p99_s <= 5:\n        return \"synchronous\"\n    if human_watching and p99_s < smallest_timeout_s:\n        return \"stream\"\n    return \"async job\"\n\nprint(in_flight(10, 30), in_flight(10, 60))    # 300 600 open connections\nprint(shape(2, 60, True))                      # synchronous\nprint(shape(40, 60, True))                     # stream\nprint(shape(120, 30, False))                   # async job\n```\nThe thresholds (half the timeout, 5 seconds) are illustrative defaults to tune, not standards.\n\n### Production snippet: claiming a job with a lease in Postgres\n```sql\nUPDATE jobs\nSET status = 'running', attempt = attempt + 1,\n    lease_until = now() + interval '60 seconds'\nWHERE id = (\n  SELECT id FROM jobs\n  WHERE status = 'queued'\n     OR (status = 'running' AND lease_until < now())   -- reclaim from a dead worker\n  ORDER BY created_at\n  LIMIT 1\n  FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\n)\nRETURNING id, input;\n```\n`SKIP LOCKED` lets many workers poll the same table without blocking on each other's rows; the worker\nextends `lease_until` as a heartbeat while it runs.","patterns":["**`202` plus `Location` plus `Retry-After`.** For any endpoint whose p99 nears a proxy timeout, return `202 Accepted` with a `Location` status URL and a `Retry-After` hint so clients poll at a sane interval. Monitor the ratio of status polls to submits; a high ratio is the cue for push delivery.","**Idempotency key with a unique constraint.** Store the client key in the job table under a unique index scoped by tenant, plus a hash of the request body to detect reuse with a different body. Monitor retried submits versus unique jobs.","**Leases and a reaper.** Workers set `lease_until` on claim and extend it on heartbeat; expired leases are reclaimed (as in the SQL above) with an `attempt` cap before dead-lettering. Alert on jobs whose `attempt` hits the cap and on the age of the oldest `running` job.","**Resumable streams.** For SSE, give each event an `id:` so a reconnecting browser sends the standard `Last-Event-ID` header and the server replays from there instead of re-running generation. Monitor reconnect rate and duplicate-generation count.","**Signed webhooks with retries.** Sign the payload with an HMAC over a timestamp and body, retry with exponential backoff for a bounded window, and include the job id so receivers deduplicate. Monitor delivery failure rate per receiver.","**Bounded queue with load shedding.** Reject with `429` or `503` when queue depth or estimated wait exceeds the budget. Track queue depth, oldest-job age and estimated wait as first-class metrics."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Client\n    participant A as API\n    participant J as Job store\n    participant W as Worker\n    C->>A: POST /analyses with idempotency key\n    A->>J: insert job, status queued\n    A-->>C: 202 Accepted, Location /analyses/k1\n    W->>J: claim job with lease, status running\n    C->>A: GET /analyses/k1\n    A-->>C: status running\n    W->>J: write result, status succeeded\n    C->>A: GET /analyses/k1\n    A-->>C: status succeeded with result","caption":"The asynchronous shape: the request only has to persist a job, a worker does the work under a lease, and\nthe client learns the outcome by polling (or a webhook or push stream) instead of holding a connection."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"asynchronous\" mean in API design, and how is it different from async I/O?","a":"- **Interaction model, not runtime.** An asynchronous API accepts work, returns a job id (`202 Accepted`)\n  and delivers the result later; the caller does not hold a connection while the work runs.\n- **Async I/O is inside one process.** `asyncio` or Node's event loop lets one thread wait on many sockets,\n  which raises connection capacity but still keeps the caller waiting on each request.\n- **Why it matters:** async I/O does not survive a restart or a proxy timeout; a durable job record does."},{"q":"Your fleet serves 10 requests per second of agent runs averaging 30 seconds. How many connections are open, and what changes if runs double?","a":"- **Little's law:** in-flight ≈ arrival rate × time in system = 10 × 30 = 300 open connections.\n- **Doubling run time doubles it to 600** with no extra useful work, and a deploy that restarts an instance\n  drops every run that instance held.\n- **The async shape breaks the link.** Submits finish in milliseconds, so connections track request rate,\n  and in-flight work lives in the job store where a restart cannot erase it."},{"q":"Users see spinners that never resolve on a job-based endpoint. What do you look for?","a":"- **Jobs stuck in `running`.** A worker that crashed without a lease or heartbeat leaves its job claimed\n  forever; query the age of the oldest `running` job.\n- **Fix with leases.** Set `lease_until` on claim, extend it while working, and let other workers reclaim\n  expired leases, capping `attempt` before dead-lettering.\n- **Also check delivery.** A webhook that failed without retries, or a client that stopped polling, looks\n  identical from the user's side; compare job status in the store with what the client last saw."},{"q":"When would you keep an endpoint synchronous even though you have a job system available?","a":"- **When p99 is comfortably inside every timeout.** Sub-second or few-second work gains nothing from a job\n  store, worker fleet and polling logic except latency and moving parts.\n- **When the caller needs the answer to continue.** A classifier gating the next step of a request is\n  simpler as a synchronous call with an idempotency key and a tight timeout.\n- **Hybrid when in doubt.** Offer a `wait` budget so fast cases return inline and slow ones return a job id."},{"q":"How do you make a streaming chat response survive a dropped connection?","a":"- **Number the events.** Give each SSE event an `id:`; a reconnecting browser sends `Last-Event-ID`.\n- **Keep the generation server-side.** The server buffers the run's events by sequence so it can replay\n  everything after the last id instead of starting a new, paid generation.\n- **Measure** reconnect rate and duplicate-generation count; a rising duplicate count means resume is not\n  working."}],"tradeoffs":["**Simplicity vs timeout ceiling.** Synchronous calls are simple to build, test and reason about, but their reliability is bounded by the shortest timeout in the path; once p99 work time approaches it, the shape must change.","**Durability vs machinery.** Asynchronous jobs survive restarts and absorb bursts, but add a job store, a worker fleet, a status API and client polling or callback logic; for sub-second work that is pure overhead.","**Perceived latency vs held connections.** Streaming gives the best perceived latency for a human, yet holds connections, complicates retries and resumption, and offers nothing to a machine caller that only wants the final answer.","**Polling vs webhooks.** Polling is proxy-friendly and stateless but wastes requests and adds up to one interval of latency; webhooks are efficient but make you responsible for retries, signatures and the receiver's uptime.","**Hybrid wait vs two response shapes.** \"Wait up to N seconds\" keeps simple cases simple, but clients must handle both shapes, and a poorly chosen N recreates the timeout problem it was meant to avoid."],"pitfalls":["**Raising timeouts layer by layer instead of changing the shape.** Symptom: duplicate runs and double token spend after clients retry requests the server was still processing.","**Accepting jobs without an idempotency key.** Every client retry of the submit creates a new job, visible as bursts of identical jobs seconds apart.","**Workers with no lease or heartbeat.** A worker crash leaves jobs in `running` indefinitely and users see spinners that never resolve.","**Unbounded queues.** An upstream outage builds a backlog that takes hours to drain, during which fresh requests wait behind stale ones nobody still wants.","**Fixed short polling from thousands of clients.** Status endpoints become the top traffic source and the database behind them the bottleneck; add push delivery or backoff.","**Results kept forever.** No TTL and no size limit make the jobs table the largest and slowest table and its backups the longest.","**Treating a disconnect or cancel as \"the work stopped\".** Symptom: tickets or emails produced by runs the UI reports as cancelled, because the worker never checked the cancel flag or the effect had already happened."],"when":"Decide from the p99 of the work, not the median, against the smallest timeout in the path and how long a\nhuman will wait.\n- **Synchronous:** work that finishes within a few seconds at p99.\n- **Stream:** several seconds to roughly a minute with a person watching.\n- **Asynchronous job:** anything longer, anything that must survive a restart, anything with retries and\n  compensation, and anything with no human waiting (batch summarisation, re-indexing, nightly evaluations).\n- **Measure:** time-to-first-byte, end-to-end completion time, queue depth and the ratio of retried\n  submits to unique jobs.\n- **Day to day:** whether an endpoint returns `200` with a body or `202` with a `Location` header, and\n  whether your agent framework exposes a `run` call or a `submit` plus `events` pair.\n- **Interviews:** name the three shapes, justify each with a timeout or human-attention argument, and\n  mention idempotency keys and leases without being prompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the `202` + status endpoint pair, adds idempotency keys to submits, implements worker leases and retries, and streams tokens over SSE for interactive paths.","architect":"Sets the latency budget per interaction, chooses queue technology and delivery channel, and defines the job lifecycle, retention and backpressure policy as platform standards.","fde":"Fits the product's job model to the customer's integration style, often replacing a synchronous call that times out through their gateway with polling or a signed webhook."},"check":{"q":"A customer's workflow calls your agent synchronously through their API gateway, which times out at\n30 seconds. Agent runs take 5 to 120 seconds. They report intermittent \"failures\" and duplicate tickets\nbeing created in their system. Diagnose the problem and propose a design.","a":"The gateway closes the connection at 30 seconds, so every run longer than that looks like a failure\neven though the agent keeps working and eventually creates the ticket; the customer's retry then starts a\nsecond run that creates a second ticket. Two changes are needed. First, change the shape: `POST /runs`\npersists the run and returns `202` with a run id inside the gateway's timeout, a worker executes it, and\nthe customer polls `GET /runs/{id}` or receives a signed webhook on completion. Second, make the ticket\ncreation idempotent by deriving its key from the run id and the step, so even a genuine retry cannot\ncreate two tickets. Workers need leases so a crash reclaims the run, the queue should be bounded with a\nclear `503` on overload, and run results need a retention window. If the customer wants progress, offer\nan SSE stream keyed by run id rather than lengthening the gateway timeout."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does raising the load balancer timeout from 60 s to 600 s not fix an endpoint whose agent runs take up to five minutes?","options":["Load balancers cannot be configured above 60 seconds","The work still vanishes on a restart, retries still duplicate it, and connection count grows with run time","Longer timeouts increase token cost per request","HTTP/1.1 does not permit responses slower than 60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A longer timeout hides the symptom but keeps the synchronous shape: in-flight runs have no durable record, a client or proxy retry re-executes them, and each instance holds connections proportional to run time. Separating submission from execution addresses all three."},{"q":"A client retries a job submission after a network error and the server creates a second identical job. What was missing?","options":["A longer polling interval","A streaming response instead of a job id","A client-supplied idempotency key that the server uses to return the existing job","A webhook so the client would not have retried"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The submit request itself can be duplicated by the network, so the server must recognise a repeat. An idempotency key stored with the job lets the retry return the original job id instead of enqueuing new work. Delivery channel and polling cadence do not affect submission duplicates."},{"q":"When is streaming the right choice over a background job?","options":["A person is watching and the work finishes within the connection's timeout budget","The work must survive a server restart","The caller is a batch process that only needs the final answer","The result must be delivered to several consumers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Streaming improves perceived latency for a human reader while keeping a synchronous connection, so it fits interactive work that still completes within timeouts. Durability, machine callers and fan-out all point to a persisted job with polling, push or events."}]},{"key":"system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","sources":["Garcia-Molina & Salem, 1987 — Sagas","Gray & Reuter, 1992 — Transaction Processing: Concepts and Techniques","Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"- **No shared transaction across systems.** \"Place order\" reserves stock in inventory, charges the card\n  through a payment provider, creates a shipment in logistics and records the order, each in its own\n  database. If the charge succeeds and the shipment fails, the customer has paid for nothing, and there is\n  no shared transaction to roll back.\n- **Two-phase commit (2PC) is blocking.** A coordinator asks every participant to *prepare* (write durably,\n  hold locks, promise it can commit), then tells them to *commit* once all vote yes. If the coordinator\n  crashes after prepare, participants must hold their locks until it returns, *because* deciding alone\n  could disagree with its decision. Rows stay locked for the whole outage.\n- **2PC excludes the riskiest participants.** Stripe, Slack, Jira, a vector database and an LLM provider\n  expose no \"prepare\", so the step with the most consequential side effect is exactly the one that cannot\n  join. Availability compounds too: every participant must be up at the same moment, the temporal coupling\n  event-driven designs try to remove.\n- **The saga designs the undo explicitly.** Proposed in 1987 for long-lived database transactions, it\n  accepts that there is no global rollback. Deciding what \"undo a charge\" means, and when undo is no longer\n  possible, is the real content of the pattern.","how":"### Where 2PC still lives\n2PC is not dead; it moved inside systems that can make its weak point safe.\n\n| Variant | How it copes with the coordinator problem | Limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spanner-style databases | 2PC across shards, but each participant and the coordinator is a replicated consensus group; a new leader takes over with the decision already in its log | only inside that database |\n| XA standard | exposes 2PC across a database and a broker that both implement it | works inside one organisation's infrastructure; excludes every SaaS API |\n| Three-phase commit | adds a pre-commit round to avoid blocking under crash failures | can decide inconsistently under network partitions, so rarely deployed |\n\n- **The lesson:** atomic commit across participants is available when you own all of them and replicate the\n  coordinator. A business process across vendors is not that case.\n\n### Structure\n1. **Local transactions.** A saga is a sequence T₁ … Tₙ; each Tᵢ commits on its own, in its own service.\n2. **Compensations.** Every Tᵢ that may need undoing has a compensating transaction Cᵢ.\n3. **Failure path.** If Tₖ fails, the saga runs Cₖ₋₁, Cₖ₋₂, … C₁ in reverse order.\n4. **Saga atomicity.** The end state is either \"all Tᵢ committed\" or \"every committed Tᵢ compensated\".\n5. **Durability.** Each local commit plus a persisted saga log of which step the saga reached, *because* the\n   coordinator may crash between steps and must resume rather than restart.\n   - The log records intent before each step (`started charge_card`) and the outcome after\n     (`done charge_card`).\n   - On recovery, `started` with no `done` is the unknown-outcome case below: resolve it by querying the\n     participant with the step's idempotency key, never by assuming either answer.\n\n### Compensation is semantic, not physical\n- **A rollback restores bytes; a compensation is a new business action** that cancels the effect.\n\n| Step | Compensation | Visible to the customer? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Charge card | refund (not deleting the charge) | yes, on the statement |\n| Reserve stock | release reservation | no |\n| Send email | none; at best send a correction | yes |\n\n- **This is why step ordering matters more in sagas than in transactions.**\n\n### Try-Confirm-Cancel (TCC)\nSplit a step into a reservation and a confirmation so it is cheaper to undo:\n1. **Try** places a hold that expires on its own (a seat hold, a card authorisation).\n2. **Confirm** turns it into the real effect at the end of the saga.\n3. **Cancel** releases it.\n\n- **Why it helps:** most compensations become \"let the hold lapse or release it\", invisible to the\n  customer, *because* nothing irreversible happened until confirm.\n- **Cost:** the participant must support holds, and a hold's expiry must outlast the slowest path to confirm.\n\n### Compensatable, pivot and retriable steps\n| Class | Meaning | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Compensatable | can be undone | seat hold, stock reservation |\n| Pivot | the first action with no undo; after it the saga can no longer be abandoned | non-refundable booking, message delivered to a customer |\n| Retriable | after the pivot; guaranteed to eventually succeed if retried | record the order, send the confirmation |\n\n- **Charges are often the pivot.** Many payment flows treat the successful charge as the pivot even though\n  refunds exist, *because* a refund is visible on the statement and costs fees.\n- **The ordering rule:** put every step that might fail for business reasons (validation, stock check,\n  fraud score) *before* the pivot, so compensation is rarely needed.\n- **After the pivot:** make everything idempotent and retried forever, *because* you have promised the\n  customer the outcome.\n\n### No isolation\n- **Partial results are visible.** Local transactions commit immediately, so other sagas and readers see\n  intermediate state. Garcia-Molina and Salem's original paper already states this; Richardson summarises\n  sagas as \"ACD\": atomicity, consistency and durability without isolation.\n- **Lost update:** another saga modifies the same order between a step and its compensation.\n- **Dirty read:** a dashboard shows an order as paid that is about to be refunded.\n\nCountermeasures named in Richardson's treatment:\n\n| Countermeasure | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Semantic lock** | write `status = PENDING` so other logic treats the record as in flight |\n| **Commutative updates** | debit/credit rather than set-balance |\n| **Pessimistic view** | reorder steps to shrink the window |\n| **Re-reading values** | re-check before acting |\n| **Version** check | reject stale writes |\n\n### Coordination styles\n- **Choreography:** each service listens for the previous step's event and emits its own; failure events\n  trigger compensation. No central component, but the saga's logic is scattered across services.\n- **Orchestration:** a saga orchestrator holds the state machine, sends commands, waits for replies and runs\n  compensations. Beyond a handful of steps, or whenever someone must answer \"which step is order 42 stuck\n  on\", orchestration wins.\n- **Durable-execution engines** (Temporal and similar) are orchestrators with the saga log built in: your\n  code is replayed from the log after a crash, and the compensation list is ordinary code.\n\n### Unknown outcomes\n- **A timeout is not a failure.** The charge may or may not have happened.\n- **Compensating blindly is wrong** because it can refund a charge that never occurred.\n- **Query first:** ask the participant with the step's idempotency key before deciding, which means every\n  step must be issued with such a key. Timeouts become a reconciliation step, not an error branch.\n\n### Agents are sagas\n- An agent that creates a Jira ticket, posts to Slack and emails a customer is executing T₁, T₂, T₃ with no\n  transaction.\n- **Tool adapters** should expose compensations where they exist (close ticket, delete message).\n- **The orchestrator** should place irreversible tools after a pivot, and **approval gates** belong exactly\n  at the pivot.","example":"### Orchestrator with compensations and a pivot\nSteps run forward, each with an idempotency key derived from the saga id and step name, and committed\nsteps are compensated in reverse on failure. The persisted log is a list here; in production it is a table\nor a durable-execution engine's history.\n\n```python\nimport logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"saga\")\nNOOP = lambda ctx, idempotency_key: None      # step had no side effect worth undoing\nIRREVERSIBLE = None                            # step cannot be undone: this is the pivot\n\nclass Step:\n    def __init__(self, name, action, compensate):\n        self.name, self.action, self.compensate = name, action, compensate\n\ndef run_saga(saga_id, steps, ctx, saga_log):\n    done = []                                        # steps whose action committed\n    for step in steps:\n        key = f\"{saga_id}:{step.name}\"               # same key on every retry of this step\n        saga_log.append((\"started\", step.name))      # intent first: recovery sees in-flight steps\n        try:\n            ctx[step.name] = step.action(ctx, idempotency_key=key)\n            saga_log.append((\"done\", step.name)); done.append(step)\n        except Exception as e:   # production: on a timeout, query the participant by key first\n            if any(s.compensate is IRREVERSIBLE for s in done):   # pivot passed\n                raise RuntimeError(f\"cannot abandon saga; retry {step.name} forward\") from e\n            log.warning(\"step %s failed: %s; compensating\", step.name, e)\n            for prev in reversed(done):\n                prev.compensate(ctx, idempotency_key=f\"{saga_id}:{prev.name}:undo\")\n                saga_log.append((\"undone\", prev.name))\n            return \"compensated\"\n    return \"completed\"\n\ndef fraud_check(ctx, idempotency_key):\n    if not fraud.passes(ctx[\"customer\"]):            # flights, fraud, payments, hotel, mail: service clients\n        raise ValueError(\"declined\")\n\nsteps = [   # business failures first, the irreversible step as late as possible\n    Step(\"hold_seat\",   lambda c, idempotency_key: flights.hold(c[\"flight\"], key=idempotency_key),\n                        lambda c, idempotency_key: flights.release(c[\"hold_seat\"], key=idempotency_key)),\n    Step(\"fraud_check\", fraud_check, NOOP),\n    Step(\"charge_card\", lambda c, idempotency_key: payments.charge(c[\"amount\"], key=idempotency_key),\n                        lambda c, idempotency_key: payments.refund(c[\"charge_card\"], key=idempotency_key)),\n    Step(\"book_hotel\",  lambda c, idempotency_key: hotel.book(c[\"stay\"], key=idempotency_key), IRREVERSIBLE),\n    Step(\"send_itinerary\", lambda c, idempotency_key: mail.send(c[\"employee\"], key=idempotency_key), NOOP),\n]\n```\n\n- **Declined charge:** if `charge_card` is declined, the seat hold is released and the saga ends as\n  `compensated`.\n- **Failure after the pivot:** if `send_itinerary` fails after the non-refundable hotel is booked, the\n  orchestrator refuses to compensate and the caller re-runs the saga from the log.\n- **Why the re-run is safe:** the idempotency keys make the hold, charge and booking no-ops on the second\n  pass, so only the email is actually retried.\n\n### Runnable: reverse compensation with idempotent undo\nSelf-contained fakes for a payment provider and an inventory service. The shipment step fails for a\nbusiness reason, and each compensation is deliberately run twice to show that keyed undo is safe to retry.\n\n```python\nclass Payments:\n    def __init__(self): self.charges, self.refunds = {}, {}\n    def charge(self, amount, key):\n        self.charges.setdefault(key, amount)        # same key => same charge, no double charge\n        return key\n    def refund(self, charge_id, key):\n        self.refunds.setdefault(key, self.charges[charge_id])   # same key => one refund\n\nclass Stock:\n    def __init__(self): self.held = {}\n    def reserve(self, sku, key): self.held[key] = sku; return key\n    def release(self, hold_id, key): self.held.pop(hold_id, None)\n\ndef ship(order, key):\n    raise ValueError(\"address rejected\")            # business failure, outcome known\n\npay, stock = Payments(), Stock()\nsaga, done = \"order-42\", []\nsteps = [(\"reserve\", lambda: stock.reserve(\"sku-1\", f\"{saga}:reserve\"),\n                     lambda r: stock.release(r, f\"{saga}:reserve:undo\")),\n         (\"charge\",  lambda: pay.charge(30, f\"{saga}:charge\"),\n                     lambda r: pay.refund(r, f\"{saga}:charge:undo\")),\n         (\"ship\",    lambda: ship(\"order-42\", f\"{saga}:ship\"), None)]\ntry:\n    for name, act, comp in steps:\n        done.append((name, act(), comp))\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    for name, result, comp in reversed(done):      # undo in reverse order\n        comp(result); comp(result)                  # run twice: a retried compensation\n    print(\"compensated:\", e)\nprint(stock.held, pay.charges, pay.refunds)\n# compensated: address rejected\n# {} {'order-42:charge': 30} {'order-42:charge:undo': 30}   one refund, not two\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: where the failure lands decides the outcome\nThe travel saga above, failing at each step in turn.\n\n| Failure point | Committed so far | What the orchestrator does | Customer sees |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fraud check declines | seat hold | release hold | nothing |\n| Charge declined | seat hold | release hold | a declined payment |\n| Charge times out | seat hold, charge unknown | query payments by `order:charge_card` key, then refund or continue | nothing extra, if reconciled |\n| Hotel API fails cleanly | hold, charge | refund, release hold | a refund on the statement |\n| Itinerary email fails | hold, charge, hotel (pivot) | retry the email forward, never compensate | a delayed email |","patterns":["**Durable-execution workflow as the saga log.** When a saga has more than a few steps, write it as a workflow in an engine such as Temporal: after each activity succeeds, append its compensation to a list, and in the failure handler run that list in reverse. Configure per-activity retry policies (bounded attempts before the pivot, effectively unbounded after it). Monitor failed and long-running workflows.","**Authorise now, capture later.** Use the payment provider's two-step flow (for example a Stripe PaymentIntent created with `capture_method=\"manual\"`, captured at the end of the saga) so the charge is a TCC hold until confirm. Monitor authorisations nearing expiry, because a slow approval can outlive them.","**Provider idempotency keys on every step.** Send the saga-derived key (`{saga_id}:{step}`) as the provider's idempotency key (Stripe accepts an `Idempotency-Key` header) and as the lookup for reconciliation after timeouts. Monitor the count of steps resolved by reconciliation.","**Semantic lock plus version column.** Write `status = PENDING` on the aggregate when the saga starts and guard compensations with `UPDATE ... WHERE id = $1 AND version = $2`, so a compensation cannot silently overwrite a human's edit. Monitor rejected stale writes and records stuck in `PENDING`.","**Stuck-saga reaper and repair queue.** A scheduled job finds sagas whose last log entry is older than a threshold, resumes them, and after N attempts routes them to a human queue. Track saga completion rate, compensation rate and manual-repair count."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Start saga, write log\"] --> T1[\"T1: hold seat\"]\n    T1 --> T2[\"T2: fraud check\"]\n    T2 --> T3[\"T3: charge card\"]\n    T3 --> G{\"Approval gate\"}\n    G -->|\"approved\"| P[\"Pivot: book hotel\"]\n    P --> R[\"Retriable: send itinerary\"]\n    R -->|\"fails\"| R\n    R --> D[\"Completed\"]\n    T2 -->|\"declined\"| C1[\"C1: release seat\"]\n    T3 -->|\"declined\"| C1\n    T3 -.->|\"timeout\"| Q[\"Query participant by key\"]\n    C1 --> X[\"Compensated\"]","caption":"An orchestrated saga: business checks come first, the pivot is gated, failures before the pivot unwind in\nreverse, and failures after it are retried forward."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a rollback and a compensation?","a":"- **A rollback restores bytes.** Inside one database, the uncommitted change simply never becomes visible.\n- **A compensation is a new business action.** A refund cancels a charge's effect but is itself visible on\n  the statement, may cost fees, and can fail and need retrying.\n- **Why it matters:** because compensations are visible and imperfect, sagas order steps so that the\n  irreversible pivot comes last and most failures happen before anything needs undoing."},{"q":"Why not just use two-phase commit across your services?","a":"- **It blocks.** If the coordinator crashes after prepare, participants hold locks until it recovers, since\n  deciding alone could contradict it.\n- **It needs every participant to speak the protocol.** Payment providers, ticketing SaaS and LLM APIs have\n  no prepare phase.\n- **Where it does fit:** inside one system that replicates the coordinator (Spanner-style shards) or across\n  XA-capable components you own."},{"q":"Finance reports a customer was refunded for a charge that never happened. How did your saga do that?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the charge step timed out and the orchestrator treated the timeout as a failure,\n  running compensations without knowing the outcome.\n- **Confirm in the saga log:** a `started charge_card` with no `done`, followed by `undone` entries.\n- **Fix:** on timeout, query the provider by the step's idempotency key, then either continue forward or\n  compensate; make the refund call keyed too so a retry cannot refund twice."},{"q":"An admin edits an order while a saga is compensating it, and the edit disappears. What is going on and what do you change?","a":"- **No isolation.** Sagas commit each step immediately, so the compensation ran on a stale view and\n  overwrote the admin's write (a lost update).\n- **Semantic lock:** mark the order `PENDING` while the saga runs so the admin UI blocks or warns.\n- **Version check:** compensations update with `WHERE version = $expected` and re-read on conflict instead\n  of blindly writing the old state back."},{"q":"Where would you put a human approval gate in an agent that files a ticket, charges a card and emails a customer?","a":"- **At the pivot.** Classify each tool call: ticket creation is compensatable (close it), the charge may be\n  treated as the pivot because refunds are visible, and the delivered email has no undo.\n- **Order around it:** run validation and compensatable steps first, gate before the first irreversible\n  action, and retry everything after it forward.\n- **Persist the agent's decisions** in the saga log, because re-running the LLM on retry can produce\n  different arguments than the ones the compensation expects."}],"tradeoffs":["**Independent availability vs visible intermediate state.** Sagas keep every service available on its own and need no shared lock manager, but they expose intermediate state to other readers. When an invariant must never be observably violated, keep those writes inside one database and one aggregate instead.","**2PC atomicity vs blocking and reach.** 2PC gives true atomicity with no application-level compensation code, yet it blocks on coordinator failure and needs every participant to speak the protocol, which excludes external APIs and most SaaS systems.","**Orchestration vs choreography.** Orchestration centralises visibility and compensation logic at the cost of a component every service depends on. Choreography removes that dependency but spreads the state machine across teams; it flips toward orchestration as step count or compensation complexity grows.","**Explicit compensations vs actions with no undo.** Designing compensations forces the business to decide what \"undo\" means, which is valuable clarity, but some actions have no undo; for those the only levers are ordering and approval gates.","**Forward-only retry vs a human escape hatch.** Retrying forward after the pivot guarantees completion without refunds, provided post-pivot steps are idempotent and the retry budget is effectively unbounded, which requires a dead-letter path to a human when a participant stays down."],"pitfalls":["**Compensations that are not idempotent.** A retried refund refunds twice, discovered when finance reconciles payouts. Fix: key every compensation (`{saga_id}:{step}:undo`).","**Compensating on timeout without querying the participant.** Refunds issued for charges that never happened, or shipments created twice, visible as unexplained negative balances. Fix: reconcile by key.","**An irreversible step placed early.** When a later validation fails there is nothing to compensate it with, and the customer has an email or a ticket for an order that does not exist. Fix: move it to the pivot position.","**Ignoring isolation.** A second saga (or a human in the admin panel) edits the record mid-flight and the compensation overwrites the change, surfacing as \"the system reverted my edit\". Fix: semantic lock and version check.","**No saga log or heartbeat.** The orchestrator crashes between steps, nobody resumes, and orders sit in `PENDING` until a customer complains. Fix: persisted log plus a reaper.","**Holds that expire before the saga confirms them.** Sagas that wait on a human approval or a slow partner fail at confirm with \"hold not found\", and the seat or authorisation is gone. Fix: size expiry to the slowest confirm path.","**Treating the LLM as a step that can be redone freely.** Re-running a non-deterministic step on retry produces different tool arguments, so the compensation keyed to the first attempt no longer matches. Fix: persist the LLM's output in the log and replay it."],"when":"- **Use when:** one business operation must update more than one service, database or external system and\n  can legitimately fail part-way.\n- **Avoid when:** all the data can live in a single database and a local transaction suffices, which is the\n  better design more often than microservice enthusiasm suggests.\n- **Before choosing:** write down each step's compensation and its idempotency key. A step with neither is\n  your pivot and belongs as late as possible, possibly behind a human approval.\n- **In practice:** a workflow definition in an orchestrator, a `compensate` method on a tool adapter, or a\n  `status` column with `PENDING` values that other code must respect.\n- **Measure:** saga completion rate, compensation rate, time stuck per step and the count of sagas needing\n  manual repair.\n- **Interviews:** state why 2PC is unsuitable across heterogeneous services, define compensation versus\n  rollback, and bring up the lack of isolation and the pivot step without prompting.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements steps and compensations with idempotency keys, persists saga state, and writes reconciliation queries for steps whose outcome is unknown after a timeout.","architect":"Decides aggregate boundaries so that most invariants stay inside one transaction, classifies steps as compensatable, pivot or retriable, and picks orchestration versus choreography.","fde":"Maps a customer's cross-system process (CRM, billing, ticketing) onto a saga, identifies which of their systems cannot be undone, and places approval gates before those actions."},"check":{"q":"An agent books travel for an employee: it holds a flight seat, charges the corporate card, books a\nnon-refundable hotel through a partner API, and files an expense record. Order the steps, identify the\npivot, and describe what happens if the hotel API times out.","a":"Everything that can fail for business reasons goes first: validate policy, hold the seat\n(compensation: release the hold). The hotel booking is non-refundable, so it is the pivot and must come as\nlate as possible; the card charge is refundable, so it can precede the hotel. Order: policy check, seat\nhold, card charge, hotel booking, then retriable steps: confirm the seat and file the expense. If the\nhotel call times out, the outcome is unknown; the orchestrator must query the partner with the booking's\nidempotency key before deciding. If the booking exists, the saga continues forward and retries the\nremaining steps until they succeed. If it does not, the agent may retry the booking with the same key,\nor compensate by refunding the charge and releasing the hold. Because the hotel is irreversible and\ncostly, an approval gate belongs immediately before it, and the saga log must persist each step so a\ncrashed worker resumes rather than rebooking."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is two-phase commit rarely used to coordinate a payment provider, a ticketing SaaS and an internal database?","options":["2PC cannot guarantee atomicity across more than two participants","External services do not expose a prepare phase, and 2PC blocks participants if the coordinator fails after prepare","2PC requires all participants to use the same database engine","2PC is only defined for read-only transactions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"2PC needs every participant to vote in a prepare phase and hold locks until the decision arrives; SaaS APIs offer no such phase, and a coordinator crash leaves prepared participants blocked. Atomicity across many participants is exactly what 2PC does provide where it applies."},{"q":"In a saga, what does \"no isolation\" mean in practice?","options":["Steps cannot run on different machines","Compensations must run in the same order as the original steps","Other transactions can observe and act on the intermediate state between a step and its compensation","Each local transaction must use READ UNCOMMITTED"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each local transaction commits immediately, so a saga's partial results are visible system-wide until it completes or compensates. Countermeasures such as semantic locks and version checks exist precisely because of this; compensations run in reverse order, not the same order."},{"q":"A step after the pivot fails repeatedly. What should the orchestrator do?","options":["Run compensations for all earlier steps, including the pivot","Abort the saga and leave the current state as is","Keep retrying the step forward, because post-pivot steps must be idempotent and eventually succeed","Restart the saga from the first step with a new saga id"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once the pivot has committed the saga has promised its outcome; earlier steps may be uncompensatable. Post-pivot steps are designed to be retriable and idempotent so the saga completes forward, with a dead-letter path to a human only when a participant stays unavailable."}]},{"key":"system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","sources":["Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium (outbox event router)"],"why":"A service cannot atomically commit a database row and publish a message, yet every producer must commit\nthe order row *and* tell the world. All three naive orderings are wrong:\n- **Commit, then publish.** The process crashes, is OOM-killed or loses its broker connection after the\n  commit and before the publish. The row exists; the event never happens, so the search index, billing\n  meter and audit log never hear about the order. A silent failure: nothing errors, data is just missing.\n- **Publish, then commit.** The event is out; the commit fails on a constraint violation or a deadlock and\n  rolls back. Consumers now act on an order that does not exist.\n- **Publish inside the transaction, before commit.** The consumer queries the producer for details and\n  finds nothing, because the transaction has not committed yet; or it later rolls back and the event is a\n  phantom.\n- **The frequency is not academic.** With a millisecond crash window, 1,000 transactions per second and an\n  illustrative one restart per day, you lose about one event per day. At scale that is \"the index is missing\n  a few hundred documents and nobody knows which\". Retrying in application code fails when the process\n  itself is gone, and two-phase commit needs a broker that supports it (most do not) and blocks on\n  coordinator failure.\n- **The way out:** use the one atomic primitive you already have, the local database transaction, and make\n  publishing a separate, retryable, idempotent step that reads from committed state.","how":"### The outbox table\n- **Columns:** `id` (monotonic), `aggregate_type`, `aggregate_id`, `event_type`, `payload`, `created_at`,\n  `published_at`, alongside the business tables.\n- **Same transaction:** when the application commits a business change it inserts the event row *in the\n  same transaction*, so the database guarantees both the order and its event row are durable, or neither.\n- **Event as data:** the event is modelled as data, not as a side effect, *because* data is the only thing\n  the transaction can protect.\n\n### The relay\nA separate process moves rows from the outbox to the broker.\n\n| | Polling publisher | Log tailing (CDC) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | loop runs `SELECT ... FROM outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY id LIMIT n FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes, marks published (or deletes) | a connector such as Debezium reads the write-ahead log and forwards committed outbox inserts |\n| Ordering | `id` order per relay | commit order |\n| Latency | polling interval | milliseconds, no polling |\n| Cost | polling latency and load on the table | operating a CDC pipeline and granting it replication access |\n| Strength | simple, database-agnostic; `SKIP LOCKED` lets several relays run without double-claiming rows | single commit-ordered log |\n\n- **The polling loop** claims a batch with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes each row, then marks it\n  published (or deletes it).\n- **Query unpublished rows, never a high-water mark.** Ids come from a sequence assigned at insert time,\n  but transactions commit in a different order, so row 101 can be visible while row 100's transaction is\n  still open. A relay that advances \"last id published\" past 101 never returns for 100, and that event is\n  lost without any error. Selecting by `published_at IS NULL` is deliberate.\n- **At-least-once, never exactly-once.** The relay can crash after publishing and before marking the row,\n  so it publishes that row again on restart. That is acceptable precisely *because* the inbox exists on the\n  other side.\n\n### Ordering\n- **Per-aggregate order:** publish with `aggregate_id` as the partition key, and have the relay process rows\n  in `id` order.\n- **Several polling relays:** order across relays is not guaranteed unless rows are partitioned among\n  relays by aggregate as well. CDC avoids the problem by reading a single commit-ordered log.\n\n### Housekeeping\n- **The outbox grows by one row per event forever** unless pruned. Delete or archive rows after publication\n  with a lag that covers your debugging needs.\n- **Index it:** a table with hundreds of millions of published rows makes the `WHERE published_at IS NULL`\n  scan slow unless a partial index exists.\n\n### The inbox table\nEvery message handler runs inside one database transaction that:\n1. **Inserts the message's id first** into an `inbox` table with a unique constraint\n   (`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`).\n2. **Checks the result.** If the insert affects zero rows, the message was already handled and the handler\n   commits nothing.\n3. **Performs the business effect** and commits both together.\n\nWhy each choice:\n- **Inbox insert first** also handles two copies arriving concurrently on different consumer instances: in\n  Postgres the second insert waits on the unique index until the first transaction commits, then finds the\n  conflict and does nothing. The unique constraint, not application logic, serialises the duplicates.\n- **Same transaction** means a crash cannot leave the effect applied but unrecorded, or recorded but\n  unapplied.\n- **Key on the producer's `event_id`,** not the broker's delivery id, *because* a redelivery carries the\n  same event with a new delivery id.\n- **Variant:** store the full message in the inbox first, acknowledge to the broker, and process from the\n  inbox asynchronously. This decouples receipt from processing and gives the consumer its own durable\n  queue, at the cost of another relay-like loop.\n\n### What the pair does and does not guarantee\n- **Does:** outbox plus inbox plus an at-least-once broker gives effectively-once effects from the\n  producer's database to the consumer's database.\n- **Does not:** make effects outside the consumer's database idempotent. If the handler calls an external\n  API, sends an email or invokes a model, a duplicate that slips in between the effect and the commit\n  still repeats that call. Those effects need their own idempotency key at the external boundary.","example":"### Postgres outbox, relay and inbox\nTables and the three code paths: the producer writes order and outbox row in one transaction, a polling\nrelay publishes with `SKIP LOCKED`, and a consumer deduplicates through the inbox. Uses psycopg 3; the\n`broker.publish` call stands in for a Kafka or SQS client.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE outbox (\n  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, aggregate_id TEXT NOT NULL, event_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  payload JSONB NOT NULL, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(), published_at TIMESTAMPTZ);\nCREATE INDEX outbox_unpublished ON outbox (id) WHERE published_at IS NULL;\nCREATE TABLE inbox (event_id UUID PRIMARY KEY, handled_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now());\n```\n\n```python\nimport json, uuid, psycopg\n\ndef place_order(conn, order):                       # producer: one transaction, two inserts\n    with conn.transaction():\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO orders (id, total) VALUES (%s, %s)\", (order[\"id\"], order[\"total\"]))\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\",\n                     (order[\"id\"], \"OrderPlaced\",\n                      json.dumps({\"event_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), **order})))\n\ndef relay_once(conn, broker, batch=100):            # N copies won't double-claim, but can reorder\n    with conn.transaction():\n        rows = conn.execute(\"\"\"SELECT id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload FROM outbox\n                               WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY id LIMIT %s\n                               FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\"\"\", (batch,)).fetchall()\n        for id_, agg, etype, payload in rows:\n            broker.publish(topic=\"orders\", key=agg, value=json.dumps({\"type\": etype, **payload}))\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE outbox SET published_at = now() WHERE id = %s\", (id_,))\n    return len(rows)   # crash after publish, before commit => republish: at-least-once\n\ndef handle_order_placed(conn, msg):                 # consumer: effect + inbox in one transaction\n    with conn.transaction():\n        cur = conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO inbox (event_id) VALUES (%s) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\",\n                           (msg[\"event_id\"],))\n        if cur.rowcount == 0:\n            return \"duplicate\"                      # already handled: commit nothing new\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO revenue_ledger (order_id, amount) VALUES (%s, %s)\",\n                     (msg[\"id\"], msg[\"total\"]))\n    return \"handled\"\n```\n\n- **Relay crash:** kill the relay between `broker.publish` and the `UPDATE` and the row is published twice;\n  the consumer's second `INSERT INTO inbox` conflicts, `rowcount` is zero, and the ledger is written\n  exactly once.\n- **Glossed detail 1, locks during publish:** the relay publishes while holding row locks, so a slow broker\n  stretches the transaction, which is why real relays use small batches and a short publish timeout.\n- **Glossed detail 2, ordering:** with several relays running, two events for the same order can be claimed\n  by different relays and published in the wrong order, so either run one relay per partition of\n  aggregates or use CDC.\n\n### Runnable: relay crash and inbox dedupe with SQLite\nThe same three paths with the standard library only (SQLite 3.24+ for `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`). The relay\n\"crashes\" after publishing, the restart republishes, and the ledger still has one row.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3, json, uuid\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\", isolation_level=None)\ndb.executescript(\"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE orders (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, total INT);\nCREATE TABLE outbox (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, aggregate_id TEXT, payload TEXT, published_at TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE inbox (event_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE ledger (order_id TEXT, amount INT);\n\"\"\")\n\ndef place_order(oid, total):                        # both rows or neither\n    with db:\n        db.execute(\"BEGIN\")\n        db.execute(\"INSERT INTO orders VALUES (?, ?)\", (oid, total))\n        ev = {\"event_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"id\": oid, \"total\": total}\n        db.execute(\"INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, payload) VALUES (?, ?)\", (oid, json.dumps(ev)))\n\ndef relay(broker, crash_before_mark=False):         # at-least-once publication\n    for id_, payload in db.execute(\"SELECT id, payload FROM outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY id\").fetchall():\n        broker.append(payload)\n        if crash_before_mark:\n            return                                  # simulated crash: row stays unpublished\n        db.execute(\"UPDATE outbox SET published_at = datetime('now') WHERE id = ?\", (id_,))\n\ndef handle(payload):                                # inbox insert first, then effect, one transaction\n    msg = json.loads(payload)\n    with db:\n        db.execute(\"BEGIN\")\n        cur = db.execute(\"INSERT INTO inbox VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\", (msg[\"event_id\"],))\n        if cur.rowcount == 0:\n            return \"duplicate\"\n        db.execute(\"INSERT INTO ledger VALUES (?, ?)\", (msg[\"id\"], msg[\"total\"]))\n        return \"handled\"\n\nbroker = []\nplace_order(\"o1\", 30)\nrelay(broker, crash_before_mark=True)              # published, then \"crashed\"\nrelay(broker)                                      # restart republishes the same row\nprint([handle(m) for m in broker])                 # ['handled', 'duplicate']\nprint(db.execute(\"SELECT count(*), sum(amount) FROM ledger\").fetchone())   # (1, 30)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the high-water-mark relay that loses one event forever\nTwo transactions take ids 100 and 101 but commit in the opposite order.\n\n| Time | Transaction A (id 100) | Transaction B (id 101) | High-water-mark relay | `published_at IS NULL` relay |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| t1 | inserted, still open | inserted and committed | sees 101, publishes, mark = 101 | sees 101, publishes it |\n| t2 | commits | | queries `id > 101`: never sees 100 | sees 100 unpublished, publishes it |\n| Result | | | event 100 lost, no error | both published |","patterns":["**CDC with Debezium's outbox event router.** When polling latency or table load becomes a problem, tail the WAL with Debezium and apply its outbox event router transform (`io.debezium.transforms.outbox.EventRouter`) so outbox rows are routed to topics by aggregate type and keyed by aggregate id. Monitor connector lag and replication slot growth, because a stopped connector makes Postgres retain WAL.","**Outbox-lag alert from one query.** Export `SELECT now() - min(created_at) FROM outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL` as a metric and alert when it exceeds the freshness budget; it catches a dead relay long before consumers notice.","**Small batches, short publish timeout.** Keep the relay's `LIMIT` small and set a short broker publish timeout so a slow broker does not hold row locks for long. Watch relay transaction duration and rows published per second.","**Wake the poller with LISTEN/NOTIFY.** In Postgres, issue `NOTIFY` in the producing transaction (it is delivered on commit) and have the relay `LISTEN`, keeping a slow poll as the safety net. This cuts polling latency without hammering the table.","**Batched retention jobs.** Delete published outbox rows and old inbox rows in small batches on a schedule, keeping inbox rows longer than the broker's maximum redelivery delay. Monitor table size and dead tuples.","**Idempotency keys at external boundaries.** Where a handler calls an email or payment API, pass the `event_id` as the provider's idempotency key, or move the call after commit with its own retry loop. Track duplicate-send complaints and provider-side dedupe hits."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"place_order\"] -->|\"one transaction\"| DB[(\"orders + outbox\")]\n    DB -->|\"published_at IS NULL\"| R[\"Relay: poll or CDC\"]\n    R -->|\"key = aggregate_id\"| K[(\"Broker, at-least-once\")]\n    R -->|\"mark published\"| DB\n    K --> H[\"Consumer handler\"]\n    H -->|\"INSERT inbox ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\"| I{\"Inserted a row?\"}\n    I -->|\"yes\"| E[(\"Effect + inbox row, one commit\")]\n    I -->|\"no\"| D[\"Duplicate: commit nothing\"]\n    H -.->|\"external call needs own key\"| X[\"Email, API, model\"]","caption":"The producer writes the order and its event in one transaction; the relay may publish twice, and the\nconsumer's inbox, committed with the effect, turns the duplicate into a no-op."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the dual-write problem and why can't you fix it with retries?","a":"- **Definition:** a service writes its database and publishes to a broker as two separate operations, and\n  no ordering of the two is safe; a crash between them loses the event or emits one for a rolled-back\n  change.\n- **Retries do not help** because the failure that matters is the process dying; there is nothing left to\n  run the retry.\n- **The fix** moves the event into the database transaction (the outbox) and makes publishing a separate,\n  retryable step reading committed rows."},{"q":"Your relay uses \"select rows with id greater than the last published id\". Events go missing rarely and permanently. Why?","a":"- **Sequence order is not commit order.** A long transaction can hold id 100 while id 101 commits; the relay\n  publishes 101, advances its mark, and never looks at 100 again.\n- **Symptom:** rare gaps that no retry fills, with no error anywhere.\n- **Fix:** select `WHERE published_at IS NULL` with a partial index, or switch to CDC, which reads the log in\n  commit order."},{"q":"When would you skip the inbox table?","a":"- **When the effect is naturally idempotent,** such as an upsert keyed by aggregate id or a vector upsert\n  with deterministic chunk ids; a redelivery overwrites identical data.\n- **When the effect is outside your database,** the inbox does not protect it anyway; put an idempotency\n  key on the external call instead.\n- **Keep it** for ledgers, counters and \"send once\" effects, where a duplicate changes the result."},{"q":"Polling relay or CDC: how do you choose, and what do you monitor?","a":"- **Polling** is database-agnostic and easy to run; it costs polling latency and table load, and several\n  relays need `SKIP LOCKED` plus partitioning by aggregate to keep order.\n- **CDC** (for example Debezium on the WAL) gives millisecond latency and commit ordering, but needs\n  replication access and a connector to operate.\n- **Monitor** the age of the oldest unpublished outbox row for either; for CDC also watch replication slot\n  lag, because a stalled connector makes the database retain WAL."},{"q":"Two copies of the same message arrive at two consumer instances at the same moment. What stops a double effect?","a":"- **The unique constraint on `event_id`.** Both handlers insert into the inbox first; in Postgres the second\n  insert blocks on the unique index until the first transaction commits.\n- **Then it conflicts** and `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` affects zero rows, so the second handler commits nothing.\n- **Why insert first:** checking with a `SELECT` before the effect would let both pass the check; the\n  database index serialises the duplicates, not application logic."}],"tradeoffs":["**Guarantee vs per-event cost.** You gain a hard guarantee that events match committed state, but every event costs a database write and a relay hop, adding tens of milliseconds with polling (less with CDC) compared with publishing directly.","**Polling vs CDC.** A polling relay is trivial to run anywhere; CDC gives lower latency and commit ordering but requires replication privileges and a connector to operate. Polling flips to CDC once event volume or latency sensitivity makes table scans costly.","**Inbox vs naturally idempotent effects.** The inbox makes any handler idempotent regardless of its logic, yet adds a write and a growing table per consumer. When the effect is naturally idempotent (an upsert keyed by aggregate id) the inbox is redundant and can be skipped.","**Retention vs bloat.** Keeping outbox and inbox rows longer eases debugging and widens the dedupe window, but table size and index bloat grow linearly; retention must at least exceed the broker's maximum redelivery delay.","**Coupling to the database vs reach.** The pattern couples publishing to the service's database schema and transaction, which is the point, but services without a relational store (or using a database per request) need a different mechanism, such as a broker with transactional producers."],"pitfalls":["**Publishing from a trigger or ORM \"after commit\" hook that calls the broker directly.** Still a dual write; shows up as occasional missing events after deploys and failovers.","**Multiple polling relays without `SKIP LOCKED` or partitioning.** The same event is published several times in a burst and per-aggregate order breaks, seen as out-of-order status transitions downstream.","**Deduplicating on the broker's message or receipt id.** Redeliveries carry new ids and sail past the inbox, so duplicates reappear exactly when the broker retries. Use the producer's `event_id`.","**A relay that tracks \"last published id\".** Events from transactions that committed out of sequence order are skipped forever, showing up as rare, permanent gaps that no retry ever fills.","**An unbounded outbox with no partial index.** Relay latency creeps from milliseconds to seconds as the unpublished scan degrades.","**Treating the inbox as protection for external calls.** The handler emails the customer, crashes before commit, the redelivery emails again, and support hears about duplicate messages.","**Huge payloads per event.** Outbox rows bloat the primary database and the WAL, slowing every unrelated transaction, which argues for thin events with an id to fetch."],"when":"- **Use the outbox when:** a service persists state in a transactional database and must publish messages\n  about that state, which is nearly every service in an event-driven system: order services,\n  document-ingestion services recording upload rows, agent runtimes persisting step results while emitting\n  telemetry.\n- **Use the inbox when:** the consumer's effect is not naturally idempotent (ledgers, counters, \"send once\"\n  notifications, calls that append rather than upsert).\n- **Skip both when:** there is no database in the loop, the broker itself is the system of record, or a lost\n  message is genuinely harmless.\n- **Measure:** outbox lag (oldest unpublished row age), relay throughput, duplicate rate at the inbox and\n  table sizes.\n- **Interviews:** raise the dual-write problem yourself, sketch the outbox with `SKIP LOCKED` or CDC, and\n  state plainly that the result is at-least-once plus consumer-side deduplication rather than exactly-once\n  delivery.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds the outbox insert to the same transaction as the business write, runs the relay as a separate process, and guards every handler with an inbox insert keyed by event id.","architect":"Standardises the outbox and inbox schemas, event envelope and relay technology across services, and decides where CDC replaces polling.","fde":"Recognises dual writes in a customer's integration code (webhook handlers that update their CRM then call the product) and introduces an inbox so redelivered webhooks do not double-process."},"check":{"q":"Your ingestion service writes a document row and then publishes `DocumentUploaded` to Kafka. After\na Kubernetes node failure, 37 documents exist in Postgres with no chunks in the vector index. Explain the\ncause and redesign the write path so it cannot recur, including what happens on relay crashes.","a":"The service committed rows and was killed before the publish calls completed, a textbook dual\nwrite; nothing retried because the process was gone. Redesign: insert the `DocumentUploaded` event into\nan outbox table inside the same transaction as the document row, so the pair is atomic. A relay, either a\npolling loop using `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` or Debezium tailing the WAL, publishes unpublished rows to\nKafka keyed by document id and marks them published. If the relay crashes after publishing and before\nmarking, it republishes, so the indexer must be idempotent. If the chunks live in Postgres (pgvector),\nthe indexer inserts the event id into an inbox table in the same transaction as writing the chunks and\nskips the work on conflict. If the vector store is a separate system, no shared transaction exists, so\nidempotence has to come from the effect: deterministic chunk ids such as `doc_id:chunk_no` and upserts,\nso a republished event overwrites instead of duplicating. Backfilling the 37 missing\ndocuments is now a one-off insert of outbox rows for them. Add an alert on the age of the oldest\nunpublished outbox row and on inbox duplicate rate."},"quiz":[{"q":"What exactly does the transactional outbox guarantee?","options":["Each event is delivered to consumers exactly once","An event row is durable if and only if the business transaction that created it committed","Events are delivered to consumers in global order","Consumers never see duplicates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The outbox moves the event into the same local transaction as the state change, which is the only atomicity the database can give. Publication by the relay is still at-least-once, so duplicates and consumer-side deduplication remain part of the design."},{"q":"Why must the inbox deduplicate on the producer's event id rather than the broker's delivery id?","options":["Delivery ids are too long to index efficiently","Producers never generate ids, so the broker id is the only option","A redelivered message carries the same event id but may carry a new delivery id","The broker id changes when the consumer group rebalances, but the event id also changes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deduplication must identify \"the same event\", and only the producer-assigned id is stable across redeliveries, relay republishes and consumer rebalances. Keying on a per-attempt delivery id lets exactly the duplicates you wanted to catch through."},{"q":"The consumer handler sends an email and then commits the inbox row. A redelivery arrives after a crash between the two. What happens?","options":["The inbox prevents the second email because the first send was recorded","The email is sent twice, because the inbox only protects effects inside the consumer's transaction","The broker detects the duplicate and drops it","The first email is automatically recalled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outbox and inbox give effectively-once effects between databases; an external side effect that happens before the commit is outside the transaction and repeats on redelivery. External calls need their own idempotency key at the provider or must be moved after the commit with their own at-least-once loop."}]},{"key":"system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Gray, 1978 — Notes on Data Base Operating Systems","Official docs: Apache Kafka (idempotent producer and transactions)","Official docs: Apache Flink (checkpointing and exactly-once sinks)"],"why":"- **Everyone eventually asks \"can you guarantee this runs exactly once?\"** The honest answer needs the\n  reasoning, *because* the marketing answer (\"our broker supports exactly-once\") hides where the guarantee\n  ends.\n- **A consumer has three failure points.** It reads, applies the effect, then acks:\n\n| Crash point | Result on redelivery |\n|---|---|\n| Before the effect | safe to redeliver |\n| After the effect, before the ack | redelivery duplicates the effect |\n| After the ack | fine |\n\n- **The middle case is unavoidable** because \"apply effect\" and \"send ack\" are two operations on two\n  systems. Acking *before* the effect just moves the crash window and loses the message instead. A\n  protocol can only choose **at-most-once** (ack first, may lose) or **at-least-once** (ack last, may\n  duplicate).\n- **The two generals problem makes it exact.** Jim Gray's framing: two parties exchanging messages over a\n  channel that can drop them can never be certain both know the outcome, no matter how many confirmations\n  they send, *because* the last confirmation could always be the one that was lost. A sender without an\n  ack cannot tell whether the message or the ack was lost.\n- **\"Make the network reliable\" does not escape it.** TCP gives exactly-once *within a connection*; once a\n  connection breaks and the client retries on a new one, the server sees the request twice. A request\n  that timed out may have been processed, and the client does not know. An LLM-calling service is in this\n  state several times an hour.\n- **So reframe:** nobody needs the *message* exactly once; they need the *effect* exactly once. That is\n  achievable with conditions, and the conditions are this lesson.","how":"### Effectively-once: the recipe\nTake at-least-once delivery and make processing idempotent:\n1. **Deduplication.** The receiver records each processed message id (an inbox table, a Redis set with\n   TTL; on the broker side, Kafka dedupes producer retries by per-partition sequence number) and skips\n   repeats.\n   - Works for any effect, but only if the record and the effect commit atomically, which is why the inbox\n     row lives in the same database transaction as the business write.\n2. **Natural idempotency.** The effect is defined so applying it twice equals applying it once:\n   `SET status='paid'`, an upsert keyed by order id, `PUT` rather than `POST`.\n   - No dedupe state is needed, but the effect must be an absolute state rather than a delta:\n     `balance += 10` is not idempotent; `balance = 110 WHERE version = 7` is.\n3. **Store the input position with the effect.** A consumer writing to a database saves the source offset\n   (topic, partition, offset) in the same transaction as its output, and on restart seeks to the stored\n   offset instead of trusting the broker's committed one.\n   - The offset *is* the dedupe record: a message is reflected in both the output and the stored offset, or\n     in neither.\n   - Works only when one consumer owns each partition's offset row; it is the usual way to get exactly-once\n     from a log into a relational sink.\n\n### What Kafka's \"exactly-once semantics\" actually covers\n- **Idempotent producer.** Attaches a producer id and a per-partition sequence number to every batch; the\n  broker rejects a batch whose sequence it already appended, so a producer retry after a lost ack does not\n  create a duplicate record.\n- **Transactions.** Extend this to read-process-write loops entirely inside Kafka: read from topic A,\n  produce to topic B, and commit input offsets in the same transaction, so output records and the offset\n  advance together or not at all.\n- **`read_committed` consumers** never see records from aborted transactions.\n- **Genuine exactly-once,** but only for stream processing whose input and output are both Kafka topics.\n- **The boundary:** if the processing step calls a payment API or writes to Postgres, those effects are\n  outside the transaction and at-least-once reasoning returns.\n- **Flink has the same shape.** Checkpoint-based exactly-once restores operator state consistently from the\n  last checkpoint; sinks are exactly-once only when idempotent or supporting a two-phase-commit protocol.\n\n### Where it breaks: the limitations\n- **External side effects.** Any effect in a system outside the transaction (HTTP APIs, email, file storage,\n  a model call) is at-least-once unless that system accepts an idempotency key and deduplicates for you.\n  Payment providers do; most internal services and most LLM endpoints do not.\n- **Finite dedupe windows.** Ids are kept for a window (a table retention, a TTL, Kafka's producer-session\n  state); a redelivery after the window is treated as new. The window must exceed the longest possible\n  redelivery delay, including the dead-letter queue's replay time.\n- **Key stability.** Dedupe needs the same key on every attempt; a fresh UUID per retry defeats it. Create the\n  key once, before the first attempt, and persist it; for agent steps derive it from `(run_id, step_id)`,\n  never from the attempt number or the current time.\n- **Non-deterministic processing.** Replaying a model call yields a different output and possibly different\n  tool arguments. Idempotency then means *memoisation*: store the output under the key on first success and\n  return it on every later attempt. Durable-execution engines do exactly this.\n- **Unknown outcomes.** After a timeout, dedupe alone does not tell you whether the effect happened. The\n  only resolution is to query the receiver by key (reconciliation) before retrying or compensating.\n- **Ordering across keys.** Dedupe is per key; two different messages that must apply in order are a\n  separate problem, solved by partitioning, not by idempotency.\n- **Cost.** Transactional producers add coordinator round trips and reduce throughput; inbox tables add a\n  write and an index per message. Exactly-once is paid per message, so apply it only where duplicates hurt.\n- **Human-visible effects.** A duplicate notification cannot be deduplicated after the fact; the dedupe has\n  to sit before the send, and the send must be the last thing before the commit, not the first.\n\n### A boundary test\nFor each effect a handler produces, ask: **does the dedupe record commit atomically with this effect?**\n\n| Answer | Guarantee | What else it needs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Yes | exactly-once within the dedupe window | a window longer than any redelivery delay |\n| No | at-least-once | an idempotency key honoured by the receiver, a memoised result, or acceptance of duplicates |","example":"### Agent step: email a model-written draft at most once\nAn agent step must email a customer a model-written draft at most once per `(run_id, step_id)`, even though\nthe worker can be killed at any instruction and the model is non-deterministic. The step is split into\nphases, each idempotent on its own, with the state persisted between them.\n\n```python\nimport psycopg   # conn opened with autocommit=True: each statement commits on its own\n\n# CREATE TABLE step_results (step_key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, draft TEXT NOT NULL,\n#                            status TEXT NOT NULL);           -- 'drafted' | 'sent'\n\ndef load(conn, key):\n    return conn.execute(\"SELECT status, draft FROM step_results WHERE step_key = %s\",\n                        (key,)).fetchone()\n\ndef email_draft_once(conn, run_id, step_id, llm, mailer, prompt, to):\n    key = f\"{run_id}:{step_id}\"                    # stable across every retry, never per attempt\n    row = load(conn, key)\n    if row is None:                                # phase 1: generate once, persist before acting\n        draft = llm.complete(prompt)\n        conn.execute(\"\"\"INSERT INTO step_results (step_key, draft, status)\n                        VALUES (%s, %s, 'drafted') ON CONFLICT (step_key) DO NOTHING\"\"\",\n                     (key, draft))\n        row = load(conn, key)                      # if a racing worker inserted first, use ITS draft\n    status, draft = row\n    if status == \"sent\":\n        return draft                               # replay: no model call, no send\n    mailer.send(to=to, body=draft, idempotency_key=key)   # phase 2: provider dedupes on key\n    conn.execute(\"UPDATE step_results SET status = 'sent' WHERE step_key = %s\",\n                 (key,))                           # phase 3: mark sent\n    return draft\n```\n\nWalk the kill points:\n\n| Killed | What the retry does | Visible effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| During the model call or before the insert | calls the model again | none; wasted tokens, nothing was sent |\n| After the insert, before the send | finds `drafted`, skips the model, sends the *stored* draft | the same text whichever attempt sends it |\n| After the send, before the update | sends again with the same key; the provider suppresses the duplicate | none, as long as the provider's key retention outlasts the retry delay |\n| After the update | returns immediately | none |\n| Two workers racing on one key | both may call the model; `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` keeps the first draft | both send that draft with one key |\n\n- **The single residual duplicate** is the third kill point with a provider that ignores idempotency keys.\n  No ordering of our code can remove it; that is the two-generals result showing up in practice.\n\n### Runnable: at-most-once vs at-least-once vs dedupe\nA broker redelivers until it sees an ack, and the consumer crashes between effect and ack 1% of the time.\nOnly the dedupe record committed with the effect gives zero losses and zero duplicates.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef run(mode, n=10_000, p_crash=0.01, seed=7):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    applied = {}                     # message id -> times its effect was applied\n    processed = set()                # dedupe record, committed together with the effect\n    for mid in range(n):\n        while True:                  # the broker redelivers until it sees an ack\n            crash = rng.random() < p_crash\n            if mode == \"at-most-once\":           # ack first, then apply\n                if not crash:\n                    applied[mid] = applied.get(mid, 0) + 1\n                break                            # acked either way: a crash loses it\n            if mode == \"dedupe\" and mid in processed:\n                break                            # duplicate: ack without re-applying\n            applied[mid] = applied.get(mid, 0) + 1\n            if mode == \"dedupe\":\n                processed.add(mid)               # same transaction as the effect\n            if not crash:\n                break                            # ack sent; a crash here means redelivery\n    return n - len(applied), sum(c - 1 for c in applied.values())\n\nfor mode in (\"at-most-once\", \"at-least-once\", \"dedupe\"):\n    lost, dupes = run(mode)\n    print(f\"{mode:14} lost={lost:4} duplicates={dupes:4}\")\n# at-most-once   lost= 106 duplicates=   0\n# at-least-once  lost=   0 duplicates= 106\n# dedupe         lost=   0 duplicates=   0\n```\n\nThe simulation's dedupe record is in memory; in a real system it survives only if it commits in the same\ntransaction as the effect.","patterns":["**Kafka-to-Kafka with transactions.** For pipelines whose input and output are both topics, set `processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2` in Kafka Streams (or, with a plain client, a `transactional.id` on the producer and `isolation.level=read_committed` on consumers). Monitor transaction abort rate and end-to-end latency, which rises with commit interval.","**Offsets stored in the sink.** For a log-to-Postgres consumer, write the output rows and the `(topic, partition, offset)` row in one transaction, and on startup seek each assigned partition to the stored offset. Monitor the gap between stored offsets and the broker's committed offsets.","**Provider idempotency keys from stable ids.** Pass `{run_id}:{step_id}` as the idempotency key to payment and messaging providers that honour one (Stripe accepts an `Idempotency-Key` header), and check the provider's documented key retention against your longest retry delay. Track keys per logical operation; more than one means keys are being generated per attempt.","**Memoised step results.** Persist every model output and tool result in a `step_results` table (or use a durable-execution engine that records them) before acting on it. Monitor replays served from memo versus fresh model calls.","**Reconciliation for unknown outcomes.** A scheduled job finds steps left in an in-between state (for example `drafted` older than a threshold), queries the receiver by key, and repairs. Track the count of unknown-outcome timeouts and how many reconciliation actually changed.","**Dedupe window sized to the DLQ.** Set inbox retention or dedupe TTL longer than the maximum time a message can sit in a dead-letter queue before replay. Plot the age distribution of redeliveries against the window."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant B as Broker\n    participant C as Consumer\n    participant D as Consumer DB\n    participant X as External API\n    B->>C: deliver event e1\n    C->>D: one transaction: effect + inbox row e1\n    C--xB: ack lost, consumer crashes\n    B->>C: redeliver e1\n    C->>D: insert inbox row e1\n    D-->>C: conflict, already processed\n    C->>B: ack, no second effect\n    Note over C,X: a call to X before the commit is outside the transaction\n    C->>X: send with idempotency key run:step","caption":"Why the ack cannot be trusted: the effect lands, the ack is lost, the broker redelivers, and only a dedupe\nrecord committed with the effect turns the second delivery into a no-op."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between exactly-once delivery and exactly-once processing?","a":"- **Delivery** is about the message crossing the network once; it is impossible over a lossy channel\n  because a sender without an ack cannot tell a lost message from a lost ack.\n- **Processing (effect)** is about the outcome being applied once; it is achievable with at-least-once\n  delivery plus dedupe or idempotent effects.\n- **The condition:** the dedupe record must commit atomically with the effect, and only within the dedupe\n  window."},{"q":"Your team enabled Kafka exactly-once, yet customers were charged twice during a rebalance. Explain.","a":"- **The guarantee stops at Kafka.** Transactions make input offsets and output records atomic; the payment\n  API call inside the handler is outside that transaction.\n- **During a rebalance** the partition moves to another consumer before the offset commit, so the new owner\n  reprocesses the message and calls the API again.\n- **Fix:** send a stable idempotency key derived from the event or `(run_id, step_id)` to the payment\n  provider, and reconcile by key on timeouts."},{"q":"When would you deliberately accept duplicates instead of building dedupe?","a":"- **When the effect is naturally idempotent,** such as an upsert into a search index or a vector store with\n  deterministic ids: a duplicate is a wasted write.\n- **When dedupe costs more than the duplicate,** for example a metrics counter where a rare double count is\n  within tolerance.\n- **Never for human-visible or financial effects;** a duplicate email or charge cannot be undone after the\n  fact."},{"q":"How long should your dedupe window be, and what happens if it is too short?","a":"- **Longer than the longest redelivery delay,** including how long a message can sit in a dead-letter queue\n  before someone replays it.\n- **Too short:** a DLQ replay a day later finds its id expired and is processed as new, a duplicate that\n  looks like a fresh event.\n- **Measure** the age distribution of redeliveries and compare its tail with the window."},{"q":"How do you make a retried LLM step produce the same downstream behaviour?","a":"- **Memoise, do not regenerate.** Persist the model output under a stable `(run_id, step_id)` key on first\n  success and return it on every later attempt.\n- **Temperature zero is not enough;** it is not guaranteed deterministic across runs, and different output\n  means different tool arguments and mismatched compensations.\n- **A deliberate regenerate** is a new key and a product decision, not a retry."}],"tradeoffs":["**Idempotent consumers vs broker transactions.** At-least-once with idempotent consumers is cheap, broker-agnostic and robust, but pushes dedupe state and key discipline into every consumer; broker-level transactions remove that burden only for broker-to-broker pipelines.","**Kafka transactions vs throughput.** True exactly-once inside Kafka costs throughput and latency from coordinator round trips; for pipelines whose sink is a database or an API they add cost without extending the guarantee.","**Long vs short dedupe windows.** Long windows catch late redeliveries but grow storage and slow the lookup; short windows are cheap but let a replayed dead-letter message through as a fresh event.","**Memoisation vs fresh outputs.** Memoising non-deterministic outputs makes agent replays reproducible and avoids repeated model spend, yet freezes a possibly poor output; a deliberate \"regenerate\" must use a new key, which is a product decision, not a retry.","**Accepting duplicates vs dedupe machinery.** Sometimes duplicates are the right trade: for an idempotent upsert into a search index, a duplicate costs a wasted write, cheaper than any dedupe machinery."],"pitfalls":["**Believing the broker's \"exactly-once\" flag covers the HTTP call inside the handler.** Symptom: duplicate charges or double-posted messages that appear only during broker rebalances or deploys.","**Generating the idempotency key per attempt** (`uuid4()` inside the retry loop). Duplicates persist despite \"having idempotency keys\", visible as several keys for one logical operation in the provider's logs.","**A dedupe TTL shorter than the dead-letter replay delay.** Messages replayed from the DLQ a day later are processed again because their ids have expired.","**Recording the processed id in a different store (Redis) from the effect (Postgres).** A crash between the two writes loses the effect or lets the duplicate through, and the inconsistency is rare enough to be blamed on \"flakiness\" for months.","**Retrying a timed-out model or tool call without memoisation.** Each retry produces a different answer and the downstream saga compensations no longer match what was actually done.","**Treating delta updates as idempotent.** `credits -= 1` applied twice is wrong, discovered when customer balances drift from the ledger."],"when":"Decide per effect, not per system. Classify each side effect a handler or agent step produces:\n- **Naturally idempotent:** upserts, absolute state.\n- **Deduplicable in the same transaction:** inbox or result table.\n- **Idempotent at the receiver:** payment and messaging providers that honour keys.\n- **Duplicate-tolerant:** a duplicate costs only a wasted write.\n- **None of those:** only these need the heavyweight options, broker transactions for Kafka-to-Kafka flows\n  or a reconciliation job that detects and repairs duplicates after the fact.\n- **Measure:** duplicate rate at each dedupe point, the age distribution of redeliveries against your\n  window, and the number of unknown-outcome timeouts needing reconciliation.\n- **Day to day:** the `idempotency_key` parameter on every tool adapter and the `step_results` table in the\n  agent runtime.\n- **Interviews:** explain why delivery cannot be exactly-once, define effectively-once via atomic dedupe\n  plus effect, and name the boundary where the guarantee ends.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Derives stable idempotency keys from run and step ids, memoises model outputs per step, and keeps dedupe records in the same transaction as the effect.","architect":"Classifies every external effect by its idempotency class, sets dedupe-window policy, and decides where broker transactions are worth their throughput cost.","fde":"Audits a customer's webhook and integration handlers for the \"exactly-once\" assumption, and negotiates idempotency-key support with their downstream systems before go-live."},"check":{"q":"A product manager asks you to guarantee that an agent's \"post summary to the customer's Slack\nchannel\" step happens exactly once per run, given that workers can be preempted at any moment. What can\nyou promise, and how would you build it?","a":"Promise exactly-once *effect* within a stated window, not exactly-once delivery. Build it in\nphases keyed by `(run_id, step_id)`: first generate the summary and persist it in a `step_results` row,\nso a preempted worker on retry reuses the same text instead of calling the model again; then call Slack\nwith that key as the idempotency token if their API honours one, and if it does not, record a \"sent\"\nmarker only after the call returns and accept that a preemption between the post and the marker can\nproduce a duplicate. Reduce that window by making the marker write the very next operation, and add a\nreconciliation that searches the channel for the summary's correlation id before any retry. State the\nresidual risk explicitly: with a provider that lacks idempotency keys, the duplicate probability equals\nthe probability of a crash between Slack accepting the post and our marker committing, a window of one\nresponse round trip plus one write, which is small but not zero."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can no protocol over a lossy network guarantee exactly-once delivery?","options":["Because TCP limits retransmissions to a fixed count","Because a sender without an acknowledgement cannot distinguish a lost message from a lost ack, so it must choose between risking loss and risking duplication","Because clocks on the two sides are never synchronised","Because brokers do not persist messages to disk by default"],"answer":1,"explanation":"This is the two generals argument: certainty about the other side's state would require a final confirmation that itself could be lost. Retries resolve toward at-least-once; acking first resolves toward at-most-once. Clock sync and persistence are separate concerns."},{"q":"A Kafka Streams job with transactions enabled reads orders, calls a payment API, and writes receipts to another topic. What is exactly-once?","options":["The payment API call, because the transaction wraps the whole handler","The offset commit and the receipt records, but not the payment API call","Nothing, because transactions only work with a single topic","Everything, provided consumers use read_committed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kafka transactions make input offsets and output records atomic inside Kafka. The payment call is an external effect outside the transaction, so it is at-least-once and needs its own idempotency key at the provider. read_committed affects what consumers see, not external calls."},{"q":"Which change makes a retried agent step produce the same downstream behaviour as the first attempt?","options":["Lower the model temperature to zero","Add a longer timeout before retrying","Persist the step's output under a stable (run_id, step_id) key and return it on replay","Generate a new idempotency key for each retry so the provider accepts it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memoising the first successful output under a stable key is what makes a non-deterministic step idempotent; the replay reads the stored result instead of recomputing. Temperature zero is not guaranteed deterministic across runs, and per-retry keys defeat deduplication entirely."}]},{"key":"system-design/distributed-systems","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Lamport, 1978 — Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distributed System","Fischer, Lynch & Paterson, 1985 — Impossibility of Distributed Consensus with One Faulty Process","Ongaro & Ousterhout, 2014 — In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm"],"why":"- **One machine is predictable.** A function call returns or the process is dead; a write happens or the\n  program crashes; the operating system gives you a consistent clock and shared memory.\n- **Systems leave that world for three reasons.** Capacity (one box cannot hold the model, the index and the\n  traffic), availability (one box is one failure domain), and geography (users and data live in many\n  places, and the model provider is on someone else's network anyway). Model API, vector store, queue,\n  agent workers and customer tools are all separate machines.\n- **\"It worked or it crashed\" disappears.** Send a request to a model endpoint and get no reply within 30\n  seconds. The request was lost; it arrived and is queued; it was processed and the response was lost; the\n  server crashed mid-way; or your own process paused for garbage collection and the response sits in a\n  buffer. Each demands a different reaction, and the only evidence is the absence of a reply.\n- **Single-machine assumptions become outages.** Peter Deutsch's \"fallacies of distributed computing\": the\n  network is reliable, latency is zero, bandwidth is infinite, topology does not change, there is one\n  administrator.\n- **Both naive poles fail.** \"Add retries and bigger timeouts\" turns invisible failures into duplicate\n  effects and overload: five layers retrying three times each fan one failure into up to 4⁵ ≈ 1,000\n  attempts. \"Make everything strongly consistent\" costs round trips, and with even one crashed process no\n  deterministic protocol can guarantee agreement terminates in an asynchronous network (the FLP result).","how":"### System models: what you assume can go wrong\nEvery protocol is correct only under a stated model, so name it before trusting a design.\n\n| Axis | Model | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Faults | **Crash-stop** | a node halts and never returns |\n| Faults | **Crash-recovery** | it halts and comes back, possibly losing in-memory state but keeping its disk; the realistic case for a restarted pod |\n| Faults | **Byzantine** | nodes may lie; relevant to blockchains and adversarial settings, almost never inside one company's backend |\n| Timing | **Synchronous** | known bounds on delay and pauses, which real networks do not give you |\n| Timing | **Asynchronous** | no bounds at all; the setting of the FLP result |\n| Timing | **Partially synchronous** | bounds hold most of the time but can be violated for unknown periods |\n\n- **Practical systems assume crash-recovery and partial synchrony,** and split their guarantees:\n  - **Safety** (nothing bad ever happens, such as two leaders committing conflicting entries) must hold\n    under any timing.\n  - **Liveness** (something good eventually happens, such as a leader being elected) is promised only while\n    the network behaves.\n- **This is how Raft coexists with FLP:** timeouts drive elections and can stall progress, but never make two\n  nodes commit different values.\n\n### Failure detection is a timeout\n- **Machines do not announce their deaths.** A heartbeat or request with no answer within *T* is the only\n  signal, and it conflates a dead node, a partitioned network, an overloaded node and a paused process.\n- **Choose *T* from measured latency** (p99 plus margin), not a default.\n- **Propagate a deadline** down the call chain, so a request with 2 seconds left does not launch a 10-second\n  sub-call.\n- **A timeout means \"outcome unknown\",** never \"did not happen\". Retries therefore need idempotency keys,\n  jittered backoff and a budget.\n- **Callers need circuit breakers** so a dead dependency fails fast instead of tying up every thread.\n\n### Clocks lie\n- **Wall clocks drift and NTP corrects them with jumps,** so timestamps from two nodes cannot order events\n  reliably at millisecond granularity.\n- **Use monotonic clocks for durations.**\n- **Use causality for ordering:** Lamport's logical clocks (and vector clocks, version numbers, log offsets)\n  order events by what they could have influenced, not by when a clock said they happened.\n- **Last-writer-wins by wall clock silently discards writes** when the \"earlier\" writer had the faster clock.\n\n### Replication\nCopying data to several nodes buys availability and read throughput.\n\n| Style | How writes flow | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Leader-based**, synchronous followers | one leader ships writes to followers and waits | slower writes, durable across nodes |\n| **Leader-based**, asynchronous followers | leader ships without waiting | **replication lag**: a follower read can return stale data |\n| **Multi-leader** and **leaderless** | writes accepted in many places | must resolve conflicts |\n\n- **Quorums (leaderless, Dynamo-style):** with *N* replicas, writing to *W* and reading from *R* such that\n  *W + R > N* guarantees at least one replica in every read saw the latest write. That is how availability\n  is traded against quorum size.\n- **Example:** *N = 3, W = 2, R = 2* tolerates one replica down for both reads and writes.\n- **Overlap is necessary but not sufficient** for \"always read the latest\":\n  - concurrent writes still need a rule to pick a winner;\n  - a write that reached fewer than *W* replicas is reported as failed yet is not rolled back on the\n    replicas it did reach;\n  - \"sloppy\" quorums that accept writes on fallback nodes during outages break the overlap.\n- **Quorum reads give probably-fresh data,** not linearizability, unless the system adds more machinery.\n\n### Partitioning\n- **When data outgrows one node it is split by key.** The partition key decides which operations stay local\n  (cheap, atomic) and which span partitions (expensive, no atomicity without extra protocol).\n- **Hot keys** (one tenant, one viral document) overload a single partition no matter how many exist, which\n  is why tenant id is often a poor sole key for an AI platform.\n\n### Consensus and leases\n- **Some decisions need agreement:** who is the leader, what is the next log entry, who holds a lock. Raft\n  solves this with terms, leader election and majority-replicated logs.\n- **2f + 1 nodes tolerate f failures,** *because* any two majorities overlap in at least one node. The\n  majority that elects a new leader always contains a node holding every committed entry, and Raft's voting\n  rule refuses any candidate whose log is behind the voter's, so committed entries survive leader changes.\n\n| Cluster size | Failures tolerated |\n|---|---|\n| 3 | 1 |\n| 4 | 1 (adds cost without adding tolerance) |\n| 5 | 2 |\n\n- **Packaged consensus:** etcd and Consul (Raft) and ZooKeeper (its own Zab protocol, same majority\n  principle) let applications store small, critical state (configuration, leadership, locks) and keep bulk\n  data elsewhere, *because* each consensus write costs a majority round trip.\n- **Locks and leadership are leases** with an expiry, since a holder that dies must not hold the lock forever.\n- **The subtle part:** a holder may *believe* it still has the lease after it expired (it paused, or its clock\n  lagged). A **fencing token**, a number that increases with each grant and that the protected resource\n  checks, rejects writes from the stale holder.\n\n### The CAP and PACELC framing\n- **During a partition** a replicated system must choose: refuse some requests to stay consistent, or serve\n  them and diverge (CAP).\n- **Outside partitions** it trades latency against consistency (PACELC): every coordinated write costs a\n  round trip to a quorum.\n- **Practical reading:** strong consistency is bought per operation, so systems make a few operations strongly\n  consistent (allocating an id, taking a lock, debiting a quota) and the rest eventually consistent.\n\n### Seeing it\n- **No single stack trace exists,** so correlation ids and distributed tracing are not optional; they are how\n  a \"request\" is reconstituted from spans that ran on different machines.","example":"### Lease lock with fencing tokens\nThe canonical illustration of why \"the lock holder\" is not a reliable concept. A worker takes the lease to\nre-index a document, pauses longer than the lease, and a second worker takes over; the storage layer\nrejects the first worker's late write because its token is stale.\n\n```python\nimport time, threading\n\nclass LeaseLock:                       # stand-in for etcd/ZooKeeper/Redis-with-care\n    def __init__(self, ttl): self.ttl, self.token, self.holder, self.expires = ttl, 0, None, 0.0\n    def acquire(self, who):\n        if self.holder is None or time.monotonic() > self.expires:\n            self.token += 1; self.holder, self.expires = who, time.monotonic() + self.ttl\n            return self.token         # strictly increasing fencing token\n        return None\n\nclass IndexStore:                      # the protected resource checks the token\n    def __init__(self): self.max_token, self.data = 0, {}\n    def write(self, token, doc_id, chunks):\n        if token < self.max_token:\n            raise PermissionError(f\"stale token {token} < {self.max_token}\")\n        self.max_token = token; self.data[doc_id] = chunks\n\nlock, store = LeaseLock(ttl=0.5), IndexStore()\n\ndef worker(name, pause):\n    tok = lock.acquire(name)\n    if tok is None: return\n    time.sleep(pause)                  # simulates a GC pause or a slow embedding call\n    try:\n        store.write(tok, \"doc-42\", f\"chunks by {name} (token {tok})\")\n        print(f\"{name}: wrote with token {tok}\")\n    except PermissionError as e:\n        print(f\"{name}: rejected ({e})\")\n\na = threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(\"A\", 1.0)); a.start()   # holds lease, then stalls\ntime.sleep(0.7)                                                   # lease has expired by now\nb = threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(\"B\", 0.0)); b.start()   # takes token 2, writes\na.join(); b.join()\nprint(store.data[\"doc-42\"])            # B's chunks survive; A's late write was fenced off\n```\n\n- **Without the token check,** worker A's write would land after B's and silently overwrite a newer index\n  with older chunks.\n- **Nothing in the logs would show a lock violation,** because A really did hold the lock when it started.\n\n### Runnable: retry amplification and quorum overlap\nTwo numbers interviewers ask for: how retries multiply across layers, and which quorums are guaranteed to\noverlap (checked by brute force over every write set and read set).\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\ndef attempts(retries_per_layer, layers):\n    return (retries_per_layer + 1) ** layers      # each layer multiplies the attempts below it\n\ndef always_overlap(n, w, r):                      # does every read set meet every write set?\n    nodes = range(n)\n    return all(set(ws) & set(rs)\n               for ws in combinations(nodes, w) for rs in combinations(nodes, r))\n\nprint(attempts(3, 5), attempts(3, 1), attempts(1, 5))      # 1024 4 32\nfor n, w, r in [(3, 2, 2), (5, 3, 3), (5, 2, 2), (5, 1, 4)]:\n    print(f\"N={n} W={w} R={r}  W+R>N={w + r > n}  overlap={always_overlap(n, w, r)}\")\n# N=3 W=2 R=2  W+R>N=True  overlap=True\n# N=5 W=3 R=3  W+R>N=True  overlap=True\n# N=5 W=2 R=2  W+R>N=False  overlap=False\n# N=5 W=1 R=4  W+R>N=False  overlap=False\n```\n\n- **Retrying at one layer only** (3 retries, 1 layer) caps the fan-out at 4 attempts instead of 1,024.\n\n### Worked scenario: propagating a deadline through an agent request\nA chat request arrives with a 10-second budget (illustrative numbers). Each hop passes on what is left\nrather than its own default timeout.\n\n| Hop | Spent | Remaining | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gateway and auth | 0.2 s | 9.8 s | continue |\n| Retrieval (vector store) | 0.8 s | 9.0 s | continue |\n| First model call times out at its 6 s share | 6.0 s | 3.0 s | outcome unknown; do not start another 6 s call |\n| Fallback: smaller model with a 2.5 s cap | 2.0 s | 1.0 s | answer returned |\n| Without deadline propagation | 6 s + a fresh 6 s retry | negative | client already gave up; the retry's tokens are wasted |","patterns":["**Deadline propagation on every hop.** Carry the remaining budget with the request (gRPC deadlines do this natively; over HTTP pass it in a header or context) and set each client's timeout to the smaller of its own cap and what is left. Monitor timeout rate per dependency and requests that start with under a minimum budget.","**One retry layer with a budget.** Retry at a single layer, with jittered exponential backoff (for example `tenacity` with `wait_random_exponential` and `stop_after_attempt`), the same idempotency key on every attempt, and a cap on the fraction of traffic that is retries. Monitor the retry ratio; a rising ratio during an incident is the storm forming.","**Circuit breakers on slow dependencies.** Open on a failure or timeout rate over a window, not on one timeout, and fail fast or fall back while open. Track the fraction of requests that hit an open breaker.","**Leadership in etcd or ZooKeeper, with fencing.** Run singleton jobs behind a consensus-backed lease and use a monotonically increasing value from the store (for example the lock key's creation revision in etcd) as the fencing token the protected resource checks. Alert on lock hold time approaching the lease TTL.","**Read-your-writes routing.** Send a user's reads to the primary for a short window after they write, or check replica lag (in Postgres, `pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp()` on the replica) before serving freshness-sensitive reads from it. Monitor replication lag per replica.","**Odd-sized consensus across three failure domains.** Deploy 3 or 5 voters spread across three zones, so losing one zone leaves a majority. Monitor leader elections per hour; frequent elections mean timeouts are tighter than the network."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Call with deadline\"] --> Q{\"Reply before deadline?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| OK[\"Use result\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| U[\"Outcome unknown\"]\n    U --> P[\"Lost, queued, processed, crashed or paused\"]\n    P --> B{\"Budget and deadline left?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Jittered backoff\"]\n    R -->|\"same idempotency key\"| C\n    B -->|\"no\"| K[\"Query by key or fail\"]\n    K -.->|\"failure rate high\"| CB[\"Circuit breaker opens\"]","caption":"What a caller can and cannot conclude: no reply means \"outcome unknown\", so every retry carries the same\nidempotency key and stays inside the deadline and retry budget, and repeated failure opens a breaker."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes a distributed system fundamentally harder than a single-machine program?","a":"- **Partial failure.** Some nodes or links fail or stall while others keep running, and there is no shared\n  view of which.\n- **Indistinguishable symptoms.** A missing reply could be a lost request, a queued one, a lost response, a\n  crash or a pause, so the caller must act without knowing the outcome.\n- **No shared clock or memory.** Ordering needs logical clocks or version numbers, and agreement needs a\n  protocol with round trips."},{"q":"One slow dependency at the bottom of a call chain took the whole platform down. Walk me through why.","a":"- **Retry amplification.** If five layers each retry three times, one failing call becomes up to 4⁵ ≈ 1,000\n  attempts against the dependency that is already slow.\n- **Thread and connection exhaustion.** Callers without short deadlines hold resources while waiting, so\n  upstream services run out of capacity too.\n- **Fix:** retry at one layer with a budget and jitter, propagate deadlines, and put a circuit breaker in\n  front of the dependency so callers fail fast."},{"q":"Why do consensus clusters use 3 or 5 nodes and not 4 or 6?","a":"- **Tolerance comes from majorities.** 2f + 1 nodes tolerate f failures, because any two majorities overlap.\n- **An even node adds cost, not tolerance.** Four nodes need three for a majority, so they still tolerate\n  only one failure, and a clean 2-2 split leaves no majority at all.\n- **Placement matters too:** spread voters over three failure domains so losing one keeps a majority."},{"q":"You use a quorum store with N = 3, W = 2, R = 2. Is every read guaranteed to see the latest write?","a":"- **Overlap yes, linearizability no.** W + R > N means every read set meets every write set in at least one\n  replica.\n- **But:** concurrent writes still need a winner rule, a failed write may persist on the replicas it reached,\n  and sloppy quorums on fallback nodes break the overlap.\n- **For a strict guarantee** route the operation through a consensus-backed or single-leader path."},{"q":"How would you stop two copies of a singleton scheduler from both acting?","a":"- **Leases alone cannot,** because a paused holder can act after its lease expired without knowing it.\n- **Add fencing:** each grant returns a strictly increasing token, and the resource the scheduler writes to\n  rejects tokens lower than the highest it has seen.\n- **Operate it:** keep the lease in etcd or ZooKeeper, and alert when hold time approaches the TTL."}],"tradeoffs":["**Replicas vs write latency or staleness.** Replicating raises availability and read capacity, but every extra replica either slows writes (synchronous) or widens the stale-read window (asynchronous). The choice flips toward synchronous when a stale read has a business cost, such as permissions.","**Consensus vs round trips.** Safe leadership and locks cost a majority round trip per write and a minimum of three nodes; worth it for metadata and coordination, wasteful for bulk data.","**Short vs long timeouts.** Short timeouts detect failures fast but misclassify slow nodes as dead and trigger needless retries; long ones waste capacity holding connections. The right value comes from the latency distribution, not from a default.","**Retries vs amplification.** Retries raise success probability for transient faults, yet without jitter, budgets and idempotency they amplify outages into retry storms and duplicate effects.","**Tenant partitioning vs hot spots.** Partitioning by tenant keeps each tenant's operations local and simplifies isolation, but concentrates a large tenant on one partition; compound keys spread load at the cost of cross-partition queries."],"pitfalls":["**Treating a timeout as \"the request did not happen\".** Symptom: duplicate side effects (two tickets, two charges, two emails) that cluster around latency spikes.","**Retrying at every layer** (client, gateway, service, SDK), so one failure fans out multiplicatively. Symptom: a dependency that stays overloaded long after its original fault cleared.","**Ordering events by wall-clock timestamps from different hosts.** Records appear to go backwards in time and last-writer-wins drops updates that users swear they made.","**Holding a distributed lock without a fencing token.** Rare corruption after GC pauses or network blips, impossible to reproduce and blamed on \"a race\".","**Reading from a replica immediately after writing to the leader.** Users report that their upload or setting \"disappeared\" and then reappears seconds later.","**Treating a quorum store with *W + R > N* as linearizable.** During a slow or partially failed write, one client reads the new value and a moment later another reads the old one, an anomaly that only appears under load or replica failure.","**Consensus-backed stores with an even number of nodes or across two data centres.** A clean split leaves neither side with a majority and the whole cluster unavailable."],"when":"You do not choose to build a distributed system; any product that calls a model API, keeps state in a\ndatabase and runs more than one process already is one. What you choose is how much coordination each\noperation gets.\n- **Default to:** local, idempotent, eventually consistent operations.\n- **Spend coordination on** the few invariants that must hold: quota debits, permission checks, idempotency\n  keys, leader election for singleton jobs.\n- **Measure:** timeout and retry rates per dependency, replication lag, lock hold times versus lease length,\n  and the fraction of requests that hit a circuit breaker.\n- **Day to day:** the deadline you pass to the model client, the idempotency key on every tool call, and the\n  choice of Postgres primary versus replica for a given read.\n- **Go deeper:** the reliability module covers the mitigation patterns (circuit breakers, bulkheads,\n  multi-region) in depth; the consistency lesson covers what reads may return.\n- **Interviews:** reason from partial failure: what a timeout can and cannot tell you, why retries need\n  idempotency, and why a lock needs a token.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets per-call deadlines, jittered retry budgets and idempotency keys on model and tool calls, and propagates trace context so a failed agent run can be reconstructed across services.","architect":"Decides which state needs consensus or strong consistency, how data is partitioned by tenant, and how many failure domains the system must survive, then budgets latency accordingly.","fde":"Diagnoses customer-side symptoms (duplicate records, stale reads, \"random\" timeouts) as partial-failure effects and adjusts timeouts, keys and read routing in their integration."},"check":{"q":"An agent platform runs one \"scheduler\" process that must be the only instance assigning jobs to\nworkers. The team uses a Redis key with a 30-second expiry as the lock and renews it every 10 seconds.\nTwice last month two schedulers ran at once for a few seconds. Explain how that happened and what to\nchange.","a":"The lock is a lease, and a lease holder cannot know it still holds the lease when it acts. If the\nscheduler paused for more than 30 seconds (a GC pause, a stalled Redis renewal, a VM migration, or a\nclock-related expiry on the Redis side), the key expired, a standby acquired it and started scheduling,\nand the original resumed believing it was still the leader until its next renewal failed. That window is\nexactly the overlap observed. Two changes close it. First, issue a strictly increasing fencing token with\nevery acquisition and make the job-assignment write (the protected resource) reject any token lower than\nthe highest it has seen, so the stale leader's assignments are refused rather than applied. Second, move\nleadership to a consensus-backed store (etcd, ZooKeeper) whose lease and token semantics are designed for\nthis, and alert on lock hold time approaching the TTL. Renewing more often helps only until the next pause\nlonger than the TTL."},"quiz":[{"q":"A call to the embedding service times out after 10 seconds. Which statement is correct?","options":["The embedding was not computed, so a retry is always safe","The embedding was computed, so the client should look it up instead of retrying","The outcome is unknown; a retry must carry an idempotency key or the client must query by key first","The service is down and the circuit breaker should open immediately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timeout only says that no reply arrived in time. The request may have been lost, queued, processed with a lost reply, or delayed by a pause on either side. Safe retries therefore need idempotency, and breakers open on a rate of failures, not on one timeout."},{"q":"With N = 5 replicas, which read/write quorum sizes guarantee a read sees the latest acknowledged write?","options":["W = 2, R = 2","W = 3, R = 3","W = 1, R = 4","W = 5, R = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Overlap requires W + R > N. With 3 and 3 the sets must share at least one replica, so a read sees the newest write. 2 + 2 = 4 does not exceed 5, 1 + 4 = 5 does not either, and a read of zero replicas returns nothing."},{"q":"Why is a monotonically increasing fencing token needed in addition to a lease-based lock?","options":["To make lock acquisition faster under contention","To replace the need for a lease expiry","Because a holder may act after its lease expired, and the resource must be able to reject that stale action","To order events across data centres by wall-clock time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A paused or partitioned holder cannot know its lease lapsed. The token lets the protected resource compare the acting holder's grant against the newest grant it has seen and refuse the stale one; the lease still handles dead holders, and tokens have nothing to do with wall clocks."}]},{"key":"system-design/data-consistency","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Herlihy & Wing, 1990 — Linearizability: A Correctness Condition for Concurrent Objects","Bailis et al., 2014 — Highly Available Transactions: Virtues and Limitations","Vogels, 2009 — Eventually Consistent"],"why":"- **One serializable database lets you forget this topic.** Every read sees every committed write, and\n  constraints keep invariants true.\n- **A second copy forces a decision** about what a reader may see, *because* copies cannot be updated at\n  the same instant. AI products are full of copies: a read replica behind the API, a Redis cache in front of\n  the database, a vector index built from documents, an agent memory store summarised from conversations,\n  a permissions service mirrored into index metadata, a data warehouse fed by nightly jobs.\n- **\"It will catch up in a second, nobody will notice\" fails visibly:**\n  - a user uploads a contract, asks about it, and hears \"I don't have that document\" because the index\n    refresh had not run;\n  - an admin revokes a contractor's folder access and the contractor still retrieves its chunks for thirty\n    seconds;\n  - an agent reads its memory on a replica, misses the note it just wrote, and repeats a tool call.\n- **Each is a named anomaly with a precise fix,** but only if you know which guarantee you need.\n- **\"Make everything strongly consistent\" fails on physics and economics.** A write must be acknowledged by\n  a quorum, or reads must go to the leader. Round trips cost roughly a millisecond within a region and\n  tens to over a hundred milliseconds across continents, and under a partition the system must refuse\n  requests. Paying that on every catalogue or chunk-embedding read is waste.\n- **The skill is choosing per invariant,** which requires knowing the ladder of guarantees and what each\n  one rules out.","how":"### Consistency inside one database: isolation\nConcurrent transactions are isolated to a configurable degree:\n\n| Level | Prevents | Still allows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Read committed** | dirty reads (seeing uncommitted data) | a row changing between two reads in the same transaction |\n| **Snapshot isolation** (Postgres's `REPEATABLE READ`) | non-repeatable reads and phantoms, via a frozen view as of transaction start | **write skew** |\n| **Serializable** | everything: the outcome equals some serial order | aborts under contention |\n\n- **Write skew:** two transactions each read a condition, both find it true, and both write, leaving the\n  invariant violated (two on-call doctors each see \"at least one other doctor on call\" and both go off duty).\n- **The decisive question:** does the invariant span rows that one transaction reads and another writes? If\n  so, you need serializable isolation or an explicit lock. Covered in depth in the backend-fundamentals\n  module.\n\n### Consistency across copies: the ladder\nOrdered strongest to weakest; each level is defined by the anomaly it forbids.\n\n| Level | Guarantee | Needed for | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Linearizable** | behaves as one copy; each operation takes effect at a single instant between start and end; once any client sees a write, every later read sees it | locks, leader election, unique-name registration, quota debits | quorum or leader round trip per operation; unavailable to the minority side of a partition |\n| **Sequential** | every client sees the same total order of writes, consistent with each client's program order, not necessarily real time | | cheaper than linearizable |\n| **Causal** | only causally related writes must be seen in order (a reply never appears before the message it answers); concurrent writes may be seen in different orders | most collaborative and conversational data | cheaper still |\n| **Session guarantees** | promises scoped to one client's session (below) | what users actually notice | cheap |\n| **Eventual** | if writes stop, all copies converge; no bound on *when*, no promise about reads meanwhile | analytics, recommendations, derived indexes, caches with TTLs | cheapest |\n\nSession guarantees, and how to provide them cheaply:\n- **Read-your-writes:** you see your own updates.\n- **Monotonic reads:** you never see time go backwards.\n- **Monotonic writes:** your writes apply in order.\n- **Writes-follow-reads.**\n- **Implementation:** route a session's reads to the leader for a short period after a write, or carry a\n  version token the replica must have caught up to before serving.\n\n### Converging needs a merge rule\n\"All copies converge\" hides a decision: when two copies accepted different writes to the same item\n(multi-leader replication, offline clients, two regions), something must pick the merged value.\n\n| Rule | How it converges | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Last-writer-wins** | keeps the highest timestamp; always converges *because* every replica applies the same rule | silently discards the other write; with wall-clock timestamps, can discard the genuinely later one |\n| **Version vectors** | detect that two writes were concurrent rather than ordered and keep both as siblings | the application must merge (two edits to a shared prompt template surface as a conflict) |\n| **CRDTs** | the merge is defined so any order of applying updates gives the same result | only for operations the type defines |\n\n- **CRDT examples:** a grow-only counter sums per-node counts; an observed-remove set keeps an add unless\n  the remove saw it; collaborative text editors use sequence CRDTs.\n- **What merging cannot do:** an invariant such as \"balance never below zero\" cannot be kept by merging\n  after the fact and needs coordination before the write.\n\n### Where lag comes from and how it is bounded\nReplication lag is the time between a commit on the leader and its visibility on a copy.\n\n| Copy | Typical lag |\n|---|---|\n| Asynchronous database replica | milliseconds to seconds, more under load |\n| Search and vector indexes | until the next periodic refresh (Elasticsearch's default refresh interval is one second) |\n| Caches | until invalidation or TTL |\n| Event-driven projections | queue depth |\n\n- **Bounding lag means measuring it** (leader LSN minus replica LSN, event timestamp minus processing time)\n  and routing sensitive reads around it.\n\n### Derived data and the permission problem\nA vector index is a *derived* copy of documents plus access metadata, which raises two questions:\n- **Freshness:** how long after a document changes or is deleted do stale chunks stay retrievable?\n  Deletion must propagate as an explicit tombstone event, and retaining embeddings of deleted documents is\n  a compliance issue, not only a quality issue.\n- **Authorisation:** if access control lists are copied into the index as filter metadata, a revocation is\n  eventually consistent, and the window is a leak.\n- **Robust design:** check permissions at query time against the authoritative store (a strongly\n  consistent read of a small set), or filter by document id against a fresh ACL lookup after retrieval.\n  Use index metadata only as a pre-filter for efficiency.\n\n### Choosing per invariant\nList each piece of state, name the invariant, and ask what a stale read costs.\n\n| State | Level | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Money, permissions, uniqueness, quotas, idempotency records | strong | in one place, read from the leader |\n| User-visible \"did my action take\" state | session guarantees | token or sticky routing |\n| Everything derived | eventual | with a measured and displayed lag (\"index updated 4 s ago\") |\n\n- **Domain-driven design says the same:** strong consistency inside an aggregate, eventual between\n  aggregates.","example":"### Read-your-writes with a Postgres LSN token\nRead-your-writes across a Postgres primary and an asynchronous replica. After a write commits, the API\nreturns a version token (the primary's write-ahead-log position at or after the commit record); later reads\ncarry it, and the replica is used only if it has replayed past that position, otherwise the read is routed\nto the primary.\n\n```python\nimport psycopg\n\ndef save_memory(primary, run_id, note):\n    with primary.transaction():\n        primary.execute(\"INSERT INTO agent_memory (run_id, note) VALUES (%s, %s)\", (run_id, note))\n    # read the LSN AFTER commit: the commit record is what makes the row visible on the replica,\n    # and it is written after any LSN observed inside the transaction\n    (token,) = primary.execute(\"SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn()::text\").fetchone()\n    return token                       # hand this back to the client as a 'consistency token'\n\ndef replica_is_fresh(replica, token):\n    (ok,) = replica.execute(\n        \"SELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= %s::pg_lsn\", (token,)).fetchone()\n    return bool(ok)\n\ndef read_memory(primary, replica, run_id, token=None):\n    conn = replica if (token is None or replica_is_fresh(replica, token)) else primary\n    return conn.execute(\"SELECT note FROM agent_memory WHERE run_id = %s ORDER BY id\",\n                        (run_id,)).fetchall()\n\n# Usage inside an agent step:\n#   token = save_memory(primary, run_id, \"user prefers formal tone\")\n#   notes = read_memory(primary, replica, run_id, token)   # never misses its own write\n# Reads with no token (another user's dashboard) go to the replica and accept lag.\n```\n\n- **Cost:** one extra round trip to the replica when the token is present, and a primary read only when the\n  replica is behind, which is exactly the small fraction of reads that would otherwise have been wrong.\n- **Cheaper variant:** route all reads of a session to the primary for a fixed window (a few seconds) after\n  any write. The price is some unnecessary primary load, and a guarantee that breaks if replica lag ever\n  exceeds the window.\n- **Monotonic reads for free:** with several replicas, if the client keeps the highest LSN it has *read* as\n  well as written and sends that, a request load-balanced to a more-lagged replica is redirected instead of\n  showing older data than the previous page did.\n\n### Runnable: last-writer-wins, a CRDT merge, and write skew\nThree anomalies in a few lines of pure Python: a skewed clock makes LWW drop the later write, a grow-only\ncounter converges in any merge order, and two snapshot-isolated quota checks both pass.\n\n```python\n# Last-writer-wins with a skewed clock. Real order: A writes at 9.0 s, then B at 10.0 s,\n# but A's clock runs 2 s fast, so A's write is stamped 11.0.\nwrites = [(\"A\", 9.0 + 2.0, \"tone=casual\"), (\"B\", 10.0, \"tone=formal\")]   # (node, stamp, value)\nprint(max(writes, key=lambda w: w[1])[2])     # tone=casual: the genuinely later write is lost\n\n# Grow-only counter CRDT: each node counts only its own increments.\ndef merge(x, y):                              # per-node max: commutative, associative, idempotent\n    return {k: max(x.get(k, 0), y.get(k, 0)) for k in x.keys() | y.keys()}\n\na, b = {\"A\": 3}, {\"B\": 2}                     # increments accepted on each side of a partition\nab, ba = merge(a, b), merge(b, a)\nprint(sum(ab.values()), ab == ba)             # 5 True: same value in either merge order\nprint(merge(ab, ab) == ab)                    # True: re-merging (a redelivery) changes nothing\n\n# Write skew under snapshot isolation: quota 100, usage 90, two requests add 8 each.\nusage_rows, quota = [90], 100\nsnapshot_t1, snapshot_t2 = sum(usage_rows), sum(usage_rows)    # both read before either commits\nif snapshot_t1 + 8 <= quota: usage_rows.append(8)               # T1 passes its check\nif snapshot_t2 + 8 <= quota: usage_rows.append(8)               # T2 passes its check too\nprint(sum(usage_rows))                        # 106: both committed, invariant broken\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: fixing the quota write skew\nQuota 100, current usage 90, two concurrent requests of 8 each (the last block above).\n\n| Approach | What happens | Final usage |\n|---|---|---|\n| Snapshot isolation, insert a usage row each | both snapshots show 90, both insert different rows, no write-write conflict | 106 (broken) |\n| Serializable isolation | one transaction aborts with a serialization failure and retries, then sees 98 and is rejected | 98 |\n| `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the tenant row before the check | the second request waits, then sees 98 and is rejected | 98 |\n| One counter, single atomic conditional `UPDATE` | the second update matches no row because 98 + 8 > 100 | 98 |","patterns":["**Consistency tokens from the WAL position.** Return `pg_current_wal_lsn()` after a write and compare with `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()` on the replica before serving that client's reads, as in the example. Monitor the fraction of token reads that fall back to the primary; a rising fraction means replicas are lagging.","**Replication lag as a first-class metric.** On the Postgres primary, `pg_stat_replication` reports per-replica lag (`replay_lag`); alert when it exceeds the product's promise, and take a replica out of read rotation when it does.","**Atomic conditional updates for quotas.** Keep usage as one counter and debit with `UPDATE quotas SET used = used + $1 WHERE tenant_id = $2 AND used + $1 <= quota_limit RETURNING used`; zero rows returned means over quota. Monitor rejected debits per tenant.","**Serializable with retry for multi-row invariants.** Run the transaction at `SERIALIZABLE` and retry on SQLSTATE `40001` (serialization failure) with a small bounded loop. Monitor the abort rate; a high rate is the cue to switch that path to an explicit lock or a single counter.","**Wait for index refresh on user-facing writes.** When the user will search right after uploading, index with Elasticsearch's `refresh=wait_for` on that request (or show \"indexing\" until the document's indexed event arrives) instead of shortening the refresh interval for everyone. Monitor indexing latency.","**Query-time ACL check after retrieval.** Pre-filter by index metadata, then drop candidates whose current permission, read from the authoritative store, denies the user. Measure revocation-to-enforcement time and report it."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"A piece of state\"] --> Q1{\"Stale read costs money or access?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| ST[\"Strong: leader read, serializable or lock\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2{\"User checks their own action?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| SE[\"Session: version token or sticky primary\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| EV[\"Eventual: derived copy\"]\n    EV --> M[\"Measure and show the lag\"]\n    ST -.->|\"spans rows\"| SK[\"Guard against write skew\"]","caption":"The per-invariant decision: ask what a stale read costs, then buy only the guarantee that cost justifies."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between the C in ACID and consistency in a replicated system?","a":"- **ACID's C is about invariants** inside one database: constraints and isolation keep rules such as\n  \"balance not negative\" true across transactions.\n- **Replication consistency is about which value a read returns** when several copies exist, from\n  linearizable (one-copy behaviour) down to eventual (copies converge sometime).\n- **Why the distinction matters:** a perfectly ACID primary can still serve stale reads from its replicas, so\n  you must pick a level for both."},{"q":"A user uploads a document, immediately asks about it, and the assistant says it has no such document. Diagnose and fix.","a":"- **Diagnosis:** the read hit a derived copy that had not caught up, typically the vector or search index\n  before its refresh, or a replica behind the primary.\n- **Fix for the user who wrote:** read-your-writes, by waiting for the document's indexed event (or a\n  wait-for-refresh write) before enabling questions, or by carrying a version token.\n- **Fix the UX too:** show \"indexing\" until it is searchable, rather than making all indexing synchronous."},{"q":"When would you not use serializable isolation?","a":"- **On hot contended rows,** where aborts and retries climb; a quota or counter is faster as one atomic\n  conditional `UPDATE` or behind a row lock.\n- **When the invariant lives in one row,** since snapshot isolation already catches write-write conflicts on\n  the same row.\n- **Use it** when an invariant spans rows that one transaction reads and another writes, the write-skew\n  shape, and you cannot restructure it into a single row."},{"q":"Your RAG index copies ACLs and syncs them every hour. What is the leak window, and how do you shrink it to near zero?","a":"- **The window is up to an hour plus sync time:** a revoked user can retrieve chunks until the next sync.\n- **Close it at query time:** after retrieval, check each candidate's current permission against the\n  authoritative store and drop denials; keep metadata as a pre-filter.\n- **Shrink the pre-filter lag** with change events, emit tombstones on deletion, and report\n  revocation-to-enforcement time as a metric."},{"q":"Two regions accept writes to the same prompt template while partitioned. How should the merge work?","a":"- **Not wall-clock last-writer-wins,** which silently drops one edit and may drop the genuinely later one if\n  clocks are skewed.\n- **Version vectors** detect that the edits were concurrent and keep both as siblings for an explicit merge\n  or a human choice.\n- **CRDTs** suit data whose merge can be defined up front (counters, sets, collaborative text), but cannot\n  protect invariants that need coordination before the write."}],"tradeoffs":["**Linearizable reads vs latency and availability.** They make correctness trivial to reason about, but each costs a leader or quorum round trip and disappears on the minority side of a partition. Once an operation's error cost is lower than its latency cost, drop to a weaker level.","**Session guarantees vs stateless routing.** They give users almost everything they notice at a fraction of the cost of global strong consistency, yet they require sticky routing or version tokens, which complicates stateless load balancing.","**Copied permissions vs query-time checks.** ACLs in index metadata make retrieval filters fast and self-contained, but turn every revocation into an eventually consistent leak window; query-time checks against the source are slower per request but close the window entirely.","**Serializable vs explicit locks.** Serializable isolation catches write skew automatically, but aborts rise with contention; for a hot counter or quota, an explicit row lock or an atomic single-statement update is both faster and simpler.","**Displaying lag vs hiding it.** Showing \"updated 4 s ago\" or \"indexing…\" costs UI work and admits imperfection, but converts a bug report into an understood state and lets you keep the cheap eventual path."],"pitfalls":["**Reading from a replica right after writing to the primary.** Users see their new document, setting or memory vanish and reappear, and the bug cannot be reproduced on a single-node dev setup.","**Treating snapshot isolation as serializable.** Two concurrent requests both pass a \"seat is free\" or \"usage is under quota\" check, each inserts its own row, both commit, and the invariant is found broken days later (a conflicting update to the *same* row would have been caught; disjoint writes are not).","**Enforcing authorisation only through copied index metadata.** A revoked user still retrieves content for the lag window, which audit logs reveal as access after revocation.","**Deleting source documents without a tombstone to derived stores.** Embeddings of deleted or redacted content keep surfacing in answers, sometimes months later.","**Assuming the cache is coherent with the database.** After a write, one request path reads the fresh row and another the stale cache entry, producing responses that contradict each other within one page load.","**Wall-clock last-writer-wins to merge concurrent updates across replicas.** An edit silently disappears and no error is ever logged."],"when":"You are always choosing a consistency level, whether you name it or not; the default of \"whatever the\nreplica returns\" is a choice.\n- **Make it explicit per invariant:** write down which state is strong, which has session guarantees, and\n  which is eventual with what bound.\n- **Encode the routing:** primary versus replica, query-time ACL versus metadata filter, synchronous versus\n  asynchronous indexing.\n- **Measure:** replication lag, index freshness lag and cache hit-on-stale rates, and alert when lag exceeds\n  what the product promises.\n- **Day to day:** the `read_from_primary=True` flag on a repository call, the tombstone event on document\n  deletion, and the ACL check after retrieval.\n- **Interviews:** place linearizable, causal, read-your-writes and eventual on one ladder with the anomaly\n  each forbids, explain write skew, and apply the ladder to a RAG index that mirrors permissions.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Routes post-write reads to the primary or carries version tokens, emits tombstones for deletions, and chooses isolation levels or locks for invariants that span rows.","architect":"Classifies every data store as source of truth or derived copy, assigns a consistency level and lag budget to each, and decides where permissions are enforced.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a document is searchable seconds after upload rather than instantly, and verifies that their access revocations propagate to the index within the agreed window."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks for a guarantee that when a user loses access to a SharePoint\nfolder, the assistant can never surface content from it. Your index stores ACLs as chunk metadata and\nsyncs them hourly. What do you tell them, and how do you redesign?","a":"The current design is eventually consistent with an hour-long leak window, which cannot meet\n\"never\". Redesign in layers. First, keep the metadata filter as a cheap pre-filter but add a query-time\nauthorisation check: after retrieval, look up the candidate documents' current permissions against the\nauthoritative source (SharePoint's API or a near-real-time mirror with a measured lag) and drop anything\nthe user cannot access now. That makes the guarantee as strong as the permission source, which is where\nthe customer already trusts it. Second, replace the hourly sync with change events or incremental\nsynchronisation so the pre-filter is seconds behind rather than an hour, and emit tombstones on document\ndeletion so chunks are removed, not merely hidden. Third, state the residual: the query-time check is\nonly as fresh as the permission source's own consistency, and a strongly consistent read there costs\nlatency per query, which you can bound by caching allow decisions for a few seconds at most. Measure\nrevocation-to-enforcement time and report it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which guarantee ensures a user always sees the memory note their agent wrote a moment ago, without requiring every read in the system to go to the leader?","options":["Linearizability for all reads","Read-your-writes, implemented by routing that session's reads to the primary or carrying a version token","Eventual consistency with a short TTL","Serializable isolation on the replica"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Read-your-writes is a session guarantee scoped to the writer, so only the writer's post-write reads pay for freshness. Global linearizability would solve it at far higher cost; eventual consistency gives no promise; isolation levels govern concurrent transactions, not replica staleness."},{"q":"Two concurrent requests each sum a tenant's usage rows, find the total below the quota, and each insert a new usage row, under snapshot isolation. What anomaly can occur?","options":["Dirty read","Non-repeatable read","Write skew: both see headroom in their snapshots and both insert, pushing the total over the quota","Lost update, because the second insert overwrites the first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each transaction reads a consistent snapshot in which the quota has headroom and then writes a *different* row; snapshot isolation only detects write-write conflicts on the same row, so both commit. The fix is serializable isolation, locking the tenant row (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) before the check, or keeping the usage as one counter updated by a single atomic conditional statement."},{"q":"Why is a query-time permission check more robust than relying on ACL metadata copied into a vector index?","options":["Metadata filters are too slow for vector search","Vector indexes cannot store string metadata","Copied ACLs are eventually consistent, so a revocation leaves a window in which content is still retrievable","Query-time checks make the index linearizable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The index is a derived copy, so its ACL metadata lags the source by the sync interval. Checking current permissions against the authoritative store at query time closes that window; the metadata filter stays useful as a pre-filter for efficiency, not as the enforcement point."}]},{"key":"system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","sources":["Fowler, 2015 — MonolithFirst","Newman, 2021 — Building Microservices (2nd ed.)","Richardson, 2018 — Microservices Patterns","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"- **Failure mode 1: the big ball of mud.** A monolith with no internal structure: any table joined from\n  anywhere, any function calls any other. Easy to deploy, impossible to change; every release needs a full\n  regression and nobody can say which code owns which data.\n- **Failure mode 2: the distributed monolith.** Services split too early or along the wrong lines. One\n  feature touches four repositories, releases ship in lock-step, and one service's schema change breaks two\n  others. You pay for the network (latency, retries, timeouts, serialisation, tracing) and get none of the\n  independence.\n- **Both naive answers skip the question.** Microservices answered failure mode 1 at many-team\n  organisations; failure mode 2 is smaller teams copying the result without the cause. \"Monoliths are\n  legacy\" and \"microservices are best practice\" both skip: *which boundaries need to be independently\n  deployable, scalable or owned, today?*\n- **AI systems sharpen it.** They genuinely mix different physics: a GPU-bound model server, a CPU-bound\n  orchestration API, bursty batch ingestion and embedding, long-running agent workflows. That tempts teams\n  to split everything on day one.\n- **What the modular monolith buys.** Internal boundaries stay sharp without paying the network tax until\n  a specific boundary earns it.","how":"### What a module boundary is inside one deployable\nFour properties, each chosen to make later extraction cheap:\n\n1. **A narrow public API.** Other modules import one facade (an `api.py`, an interface, a package's\n   exported surface) and nothing else, *because* a service boundary is exactly a public API over a\n   network. If the in-process API is already narrow, extraction is a transport change.\n2. **Owned data.** Each module owns its tables (a schema per module, or at minimum a naming and ownership\n   rule); no other module queries them directly, *because* a shared database is the coupling that makes\n   services inseparable later. Cross-module reporting goes through the owning module's API or an\n   event-fed read model.\n3. **Events for side effects.** When module A needs B to react (\"document ingested\", \"chat answered\"), A\n   publishes an in-process event instead of calling B's internals. The bus is a function call today and an\n   outbox plus broker tomorrow; the contract does not change.\n4. **Enforcement in CI.** Boundaries that live only in a wiki erode in weeks. `import-linter` (Python),\n   ArchUnit (JVM), Nx module-boundary lint rules (TypeScript) and Packwerk (Ruby) fail the build when a\n   module reaches into another's internals.\n\n**What you keep from one process:**\n- In-process calls cost microseconds, versus a network round trip of roughly a millisecond or more\n  (illustrative order of magnitude).\n- One transaction boundary when you need atomicity.\n- One build, one deploy, one log stream and one debugger.\n\n### What changes when a boundary becomes a service\nThe added problems are the same every time:\n\n| Problem | What it forces |\n|---|---|\n| **Network and partial failure** | Calls time out, succeed without the caller knowing, or get retried, so every mutating call needs idempotency, timeouts and circuit breakers |\n| **Data ownership becomes physical** | No shared database: an operation spanning two services becomes a saga with compensating actions, or an outbox-driven eventually consistent flow; joins become data pipelines |\n| **Contracts become versioned** | Callers deploy on their own schedule, so the API must evolve backward-compatibly; contract tests replace the compiler |\n| **Operational surface multiplies** | Per-service CI/CD, dashboards, alerts, on-call, secrets, distributed tracing with propagated correlation IDs, and usually a platform team to make it repeatable |\n\nNone of these are reasons never to split; they are the price, and it is paid **per boundary**.\n\n### The arithmetic of a hop\nThe price compounds rather than adding up linearly:\n\n- **Availability multiplies along a synchronous chain.** *n* services in series, each available a\n  fraction *a*, succeed roughly *aⁿ* of the time.\n  - Five services at 99.9% give 0.999⁵ ≈ 99.5%: the ~43 minutes of monthly downtime one service is allowed\n    becomes ~3.6 hours for the path.\n  - Real failures are correlated, so treat this as the shape rather than a forecast.\n  - Five modules in one process fail together but do not multiply.\n  - This is *why* service designs lean on async messaging, caches and fallbacks: each cuts a link out of\n    the synchronous chain.\n- **Fan-out amplifies tail latency.** A request that calls *n* services in parallel and waits for all is\n  slow whenever any one is.\n  - If each call exceeds its own p99 1% of the time, the request exceeds it with probability 1 − 0.99ⁿ,\n    about 10% at *n* = 10. The callees' p99 becomes the caller's p90 (Dean and Barroso's \"The Tail at\n    Scale\" is the classic treatment).\n  - Standard mitigations are timeouts with partial results and hedged requests, both extra code a modular\n    monolith never needs.\n\n### The reasons that justify paying it\nA boundary is worth a service when at least one holds and the modular monolith cannot deliver it:\n\n- **Different scaling or hardware profile.** A vLLM model server needs GPUs and scales on tokens per\n  second; the orchestration API scales on request concurrency on cheap CPU nodes. Co-deploying wastes the\n  GPU.\n- **Different failure domain.** An ingestion worker that can OOM on a 400-page PDF must not take the chat\n  API down with it.\n- **Different team and release cadence.** Conway's law is descriptive: when two teams own two things,\n  their interface becomes a service boundary whether planned or not. The \"inverse Conway manoeuvre\" runs it\n  the other way: shape teams around the boundaries you want, then split.\n- **Different security or compliance envelope.** A component handling raw PII or running untrusted agent\n  code may need its own network segment and credentials.\n- **Different runtime.** Python for the ML path and TypeScript for the product surface is a common and\n  legitimate split.\n\n**The middle option many teams miss: one codebase, several deployables.** The same artifact runs as\n`api`, `worker` or `scheduler` depending on its entrypoint. Processes are isolated and scale\nindependently, but there is one build, one set of contracts and no network between modules that do not\nneed it.\n\n### Extraction when it is time\nBecause the module already has a facade, owned data and events, extraction follows a fixed path:\n\n1. Put the facade behind an interface.\n2. Implement a remote client with the same interface.\n3. Route a percentage of traffic to the new service (strangler pattern).\n4. Move the tables.\n5. Delete the in-process implementation.\n\nContract tests written against the facade keep passing throughout.","example":"### Enforced module boundary in Python\n`chat` depends only on retrieval's public contract, side effects go through an in-process event bus, and\nCI forbids reaching into retrieval internals. Extracting retrieval later means writing a second\n`Retriever` implementation that speaks HTTP; `ChatService` does not change.\n\n```python\n# app/retrieval/api.py — the only module other packages may import from retrieval\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Passage:\n    doc_id: str\n    text: str\n    score: float\n\nclass Retriever(Protocol):                      # the contract; unchanged by extraction\n    def search(self, tenant_id: str, query: str, k: int = 8) -> list[Passage]: ...\n\ndef get_retriever() -> Retriever:\n    from .service import PgVectorRetriever      # private: schema, SQL, embedding client\n    return PgVectorRetriever()\n\n# app/chat/service.py — depends on the contract, never on retrieval internals\nfrom app.retrieval.api import Retriever, get_retriever\nfrom app.events import publish                  # in-process today; outbox + broker later\n\nclass ChatService:\n    def __init__(self, retriever: Retriever | None = None):\n        self.retriever = retriever or get_retriever()\n\n    def answer(self, tenant_id: str, question: str) -> str:\n        passages = self.retriever.search(tenant_id, question)\n        reply = compose_answer(question, passages)       # LLM call elided\n        publish(\"chat.answered\", {\"tenant_id\": tenant_id, \"passages\": len(passages)})\n        return reply\n\n# .importlinter — the build fails if chat imports retrieval's internals\n# [importlinter:contract:retrieval-is-private]\n# name = chat may only use retrieval.api\n# type = forbidden\n# source_modules = app.chat\n# forbidden_modules = app.retrieval.service\n# allow_indirect_imports = True\n```\n\n### Hop calculator: what a proposed split costs\nRun this before a design review to put numbers on a synchronous chain and a parallel fan-out.\n\n```python\nMINUTES_PER_MONTH = 30 * 24 * 60   # 43,200\n\ndef chain(per_service: float, hops: int) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    avail = per_service ** hops                       # independent failures: the optimistic shape\n    downtime_h = (1 - avail) * MINUTES_PER_MONTH / 60\n    return avail, downtime_h\n\ndef fanout_slow(n: int, p_slow: float = 0.01) -> float:\n    return 1 - (1 - p_slow) ** n                      # P(at least one callee beyond its p99)\n\nfor hops in (1, 3, 5, 10):\n    a, h = chain(0.999, hops)\n    print(f\"{hops:>2} hops: {a:.3%} available, ~{h:.1f} h/month down, \"\n          f\"fan-out slow {fanout_slow(hops):.1%}\")\n#  1 hops: 99.900% available, ~0.7 h/month down, fan-out slow 1.0%\n#  3 hops: 99.700% available, ~2.2 h/month down, fan-out slow 3.0%\n#  5 hops: 99.501% available, ~3.6 h/month down, fan-out slow 4.9%\n# 10 hops: 99.004% available, ~7.2 h/month down, fan-out slow 9.6%\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: six engineers, seven proposed services\nA RAG startup proposes separate services for ingestion, chunking, embedding, retrieval, chat, tenancy and\nevaluation.\n\n| Question | Answer | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Different hardware? | Only the self-hosted embedding model (GPU) | Own serving process |\n| Different failure domain? | Ingestion is bursty and can OOM on bad PDFs | `worker` deployable, same artifact |\n| Different team? | No: one team of six | No service boundary |\n| Already remote? | The hosted LLM | Already behind HTTP |\n\nOutcome: three runtime units instead of seven, one build, and written triggers (second team, different\ndata store) for any further split.","patterns":["**Boundary contracts in CI.** Use when a codebase has more than a couple of modules. Add one `import-linter` `forbidden` contract per module (as above) and run `lint-imports` as a required check. Monitor the number of contract exceptions; a growing ignore list means the boundary is wrong.","**One image, many entrypoints.** Use when roles need isolation but share code. Build one container image and start it with a different command per role (`api`, `worker`, `scheduler`), each with its own replica count and resource limits. Monitor per-role CPU, memory and restart counts.","**Strangler routing for extraction.** Use when a module has earned a service. Put the remote client behind the same `Retriever` interface and route a small, rising percentage of calls to it with a feature flag. Compare error rate and p99 latency between the two paths before moving the tables.","**Separate the model-serving tier from day one.** Use when you self-host models. Run the model server (for example vLLM) as its own deployment on GPU nodes and call it over HTTP from the API. Monitor GPU utilisation and queue depth separately from API concurrency.","**Track the distributed-monolith signal.** Count coordinated multi-service deploys and repositories touched per feature from your deploy and PR history. A rising count is the earliest sign a boundary provides no independence."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Module with facade and owned data\"] --> Q1{\"Different hardware or failure domain?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2{\"Different team, runtime or compliance?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Separate deployable, same artifact\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| K[\"Keep as module, CI-enforced\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Extract to service\"]\n    D -->|\"still not enough\"| S\n    S --> E1[\"Remote client behind same interface\"]\n    E1 --> E2[\"Strangler: route a rising percentage\"]\n    E2 --> E3[\"Move tables, delete in-process code\"]","caption":"The decision path for one boundary: stay a module by default, become a separate deployable from the same\nartifact for isolation, and become a service only for a named independence the monolith cannot give."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a modular monolith, and why is it not just \"a monolith with folders\"?","a":"- **Definition:** one deployable whose modules expose narrow public APIs, own their tables and talk via\n  events for side effects, with the boundaries enforced by CI tooling such as `import-linter`.\n- **Why it matters:** folders without enforcement erode into a big ball of mud; enforced facades and owned\n  data make a later extraction a transport change rather than a rewrite."},{"q":"Your services always ship together and one schema change broke two teams. What do you do?","a":"- **Diagnose:** this is a distributed monolith. Count coordinated deploys and repositories touched per\n  feature, and look for tables read by more than one service.\n- **Fix ownership first:** give each table one owner and route other access through its API or events.\n- **Then fix boundaries:** merge services that never change independently back into modules of one\n  deployable; keep only the splits whose independence you exercise."},{"q":"When would you not split a module into a service even if it is CPU-heavy?","a":"- **When a separate deployable is enough.** Run the same artifact with a `worker` entrypoint and its own\n  replica count; you get isolation and independent scaling without a versioned network contract.\n- **When the team is one team.** Without a different release cadence there is no ownership reason, and you\n  would pay idempotency, timeouts and contract versioning for nothing."},{"q":"A request path calls five services synchronously at 99.9% each. What availability should you expect?","a":"- **Roughly 0.999⁵ ≈ 99.5%**, so ~43 minutes of allowed monthly downtime per service becomes ~3.6 hours\n  for the path, assuming independent failures (real ones are correlated).\n- **Mitigate** by cutting synchronous links: async messaging, caches, fallbacks, and timeouts with partial\n  results for fan-out."},{"q":"How would you extract the retrieval module into its own service safely?","a":"- **Same interface, new transport:** write an HTTP client implementing the existing `Retriever` contract,\n  so `ChatService` does not change.\n- **Strangler routing:** send a small, rising percentage of calls to the new service behind a flag and\n  compare error rate and p99 with the in-process path.\n- **Then move data:** migrate the tables, delete the in-process implementation, and keep the contract tests\n  green throughout."}],"tradeoffs":["**One deploy, one transaction vs shared limits.** A modular monolith gives in-process latency and one transaction, but every module shares one process's resource limits and release train. It flips once a module needs different hardware or a team needs to ship without waiting for others.","**Independence vs per-boundary tax.** Microservices give independent scaling and failure isolation, but each boundary adds network failure handling, versioned contracts and eventual consistency. Worth it only for boundaries whose independence you will actually exercise.","**Enforced boundaries vs friction.** They cost up-front design and occasional friction when a quick cross-module query is forbidden. That is cheaper than untangling a shared database later, unless the product is a short-lived prototype that will be thrown away.","**Several deployables vs coupled versions.** \"One codebase, several deployables\" keeps contracts in one place while isolating processes, but couples release versions across roles. It becomes a problem when roles need genuinely different dependency sets (for example CUDA libraries only in the serving role)."],"pitfalls":["**Splitting by technical layer.** An \"LLM service\" and a \"database service\" instead of business capabilities. Symptom: every user-facing change touches every service. Fix: split by capability.","**A shared database behind \"separate\" services.** Symptom: a schema migration in one repository breaks another team's deploy with no compiler to warn anyone. Fix: one owner per table, access via API or events.","**\"Modular\" without CI enforcement.** Symptom: six months later the dependency graph is a single strongly connected component. Fix: boundary lint as a required check.","**Splitting early because components *feel* different.** Then most requests fan out across three services. Symptom: p99 latency dominated by hop count and retries, and incidents that need three on-call rotations to debug. Fix: require a named, measured independence (hardware, failure domain, team) before any split.","**Model server as \"just another module\" of the API process.** Symptom: a GPU box running web handlers at 5% GPU utilisation, or an API deploy that evicts a loaded model. Fix: separate serving tier."],"when":"- **Default:** a modular monolith for a new AI product. One Python codebase with enforced modules for\n  ingestion, retrieval, orchestration, evaluation and tenancy, run as separate processes (`api`, `worker`)\n  from the same artifact.\n- **Separate from the start:** the model-serving tier when you self-host, because its hardware and scaling\n  profile are provably different.\n- **Add a service boundary when:** you can name the specific independence needed (team, scaling, blast\n  radius, compliance) and have measured that the monolith cannot provide it.\n- **Measure before and after:** deploy frequency per module, change failure rate, p99 latency per request\n  path, synchronous hops on the critical path, and repositories a typical feature touches. A rising count of\n  coordinated multi-service deploys is the earliest sign of a distributed monolith.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer is not \"monolith\" or \"microservices\" but a sequence: which\n  boundaries, in what order, justified by which observed pressure, and how extraction stays reversible.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the module facades, keeps the import-linter contracts green, and extracts a module into a service by swapping an adapter rather than rewriting callers.","architect":"Owns the boundary map: which capabilities are modules, which are deployables, which are services, and the written criteria (scaling, team, blast radius) that promote one to the next.","fde":"Fits the product into a customer's deployment constraints, often collapsing several services into one deployable for an on-prem or VPC install where a platform team does not exist."},"check":{"q":"A six-engineer startup is building a RAG product: document ingestion, a chat API, a self-hosted\nembedding model and a hosted LLM. They propose seven microservices. How would you structure it and why?","a":"I would start with one modular monolith in Python with enforced modules (ingestion, retrieval,\nchat orchestration, tenancy, evaluation) and run it as two deployables from the same artifact: an `api`\nprocess and a `worker` process for ingestion, because ingestion is bursty and can crash on bad\ndocuments while chat must stay up. The embedding model gets its own serving process because it needs a\nGPU and scales on throughput, not request concurrency. The hosted LLM is already a service behind an\nHTTP boundary. That is three runtime units, not seven, with one build and one set of contracts. I would\nwrite down the triggers for further splits, for example a second team taking ownership of ingestion or\nretrieval needing a different data store, and keep module facades so extraction is an adapter swap."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most distinguishes a modular monolith from an ordinary monolith?","options":["It is written in several programming languages","Modules expose narrow public APIs, own their data, and the boundaries are enforced by tooling","It is deployed as several containers behind a load balancer","It uses an event broker for all internal communication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining property is enforced internal boundaries, including data ownership, inside one deployable. Multiple containers or a broker describe deployment and transport, which is exactly what the modular monolith avoids until a boundary needs it."},{"q":"Which observation is the strongest evidence that a service boundary was drawn in the wrong place?","options":["The service has its own database","The service is written in a different language from its callers","Most feature changes require coordinated deploys of this service and its callers","The service receives more traffic than any other"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lock-step deploys mean the boundary does not provide independence, which is the only thing a network boundary buys. A separate database and a different language are normal properties of a healthy service."},{"q":"A team self-hosts an embedding model and runs it inside the same process as their FastAPI chat API. What is the most likely consequence?","options":["Lower latency because no network hop exists, with no downside","Poor GPU utilisation and coupled scaling, because web concurrency and model throughput have different profiles","The model cannot be loaded because FastAPI is single-threaded","Requests must be made idempotent because of partial failure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPU serving scales on batched throughput while an API scales on connection concurrency; co-locating them wastes the expensive resource and makes every API deploy evict the model. Idempotency concerns arise from network boundaries, not from co-location."}]},{"key":"system-design/multi-tenancy","sources":["Official docs: AWS Well-Architected SaaS Lens","Official docs: PostgreSQL (row security policies)"],"why":"- **One deployment per customer does not scale.** Fine for three customers; for three hundred it means\n  three hundred databases to migrate, three hundred model-version rollouts and three hundred sets of\n  alerts. GPUs sit idle in most of them because no single customer's traffic fills a card.\n- **Cost should track usage, not customer count.** Per-customer stacks make cost scale with customers and\n  turn every upgrade into a campaign. Single-tenant deployments stay right for a few regulated or very\n  large customers, but cannot be the product's default shape.\n- **\"Add a `customer_id` column\" fails twice.** The filter is a convention, so one forgotten `WHERE`\n  clause returns another customer's records; in an LLM product that record lands in a prompt and is\n  paraphrased back to the wrong user.\n- **A column only separates data.** Tenants also need separate configuration (model, system prompt,\n  features), performance envelopes (one tenant's bulk ingestion must not starve another's chat), cost\n  accounting (who used which tokens) and sometimes jurisdictions (EU data stays in the EU).\n- **What the discipline buys.** All of those answered with one identity and a few explicit sharing\n  decisions, rather than rediscovered one by one in incidents.","how":"### Tenant identity, established once and carried everywhere\n1. **Credential in.** Every request carries a JWT from the identity provider, an API key or an SSO\n   assertion.\n2. **Resolve at the edge.** The tenant comes from that credential, never from a request body or query\n   parameter, *because* anything the caller can type is something the caller can change.\n3. **Carry it with the work:**\n   - in a request-scoped context variable inside the process;\n   - as a header or message attribute when work crosses into a queue;\n   - as a field on every log line, trace span and metric.\n4. **Never re-derive it.** No layer may need to work out the tenant from data, *because* re-derivation is\n   where mistakes hide.\n\n### Three sharing models, chosen per resource\nThe AWS SaaS Lens vocabulary names the options precisely:\n\n| Model | What it looks like | Gains | Costs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Pool** | Shared resource with a tenant key: one PostgreSQL schema with `tenant_id` on every table, one vector index with tenant metadata, one model-serving fleet | Cheapest, simplest to operate | Isolation rests entirely on correct filtering and quotas |\n| **Bridge** | Shared infrastructure, partitioned structure: a schema or namespace per tenant in a shared database, a collection per tenant in the vector store | Stronger separation; per-tenant backup, restore and migration timing | Every migration runs N times; catalog grows with N × tables; connection pools multiply if each tenant gets its own credentials |\n| **Silo** | Dedicated database, cluster or whole stack per tenant | Strongest isolation | Highest cost; operations drift back toward one deployment per customer |\n\n- **Mix them per resource and per tier.** Pooled compute and a pooled vector index for self-serve, bridge\n  databases for mid-market, silo for the enterprise tier that pays for it.\n- **The real decision** is not \"which model\" but \"which model for which resource, and what moves a\n  tenant between tiers\".\n\n### The tenant directory: placement as data\n- **A control-plane tenant directory** maps each tenant to its tier, region, cell, database or schema,\n  index namespace and encryption-key ID.\n- **The data plane looks it up (and caches it) on every request**, *because* that indirection lets you\n  move a tenant without touching callers.\n- **Moving a tenant from pool to silo has a fixed shape:**\n  1. Provision the new placement.\n  2. Copy the data.\n  3. Dual-write, or freeze writes for the cut-over window.\n  4. Verify row and vector counts.\n  5. Flip the directory entry.\n  6. Only then delete from the old placement.\n- **Cells extend this at scale.** Several identical, independently deployed pooled stacks each hold a\n  subset of tenants, so a bad deploy or runaway tenant hurts one cell, not everyone. The price is a\n  routing layer and N fleets to deploy.\n\n### Tenant is a boundary, not an authorisation model\n- **Outer boundary only.** Inside a tenant, users still have roles and documents still have ACLs.\n- **Necessary, not sufficient.** Scoping retrieval by tenant does not stop an intern and the CFO of the\n  same tenant retrieving the same board minutes.\n- **Pick the isolation level early** (reseller, organisation, workspace), *because* it is the key every\n  store is partitioned by, and changing it later means re-keying everything.\n\n### Lifecycle: onboarding and offboarding\n- **Onboarding is one idempotent workflow** that creates the directory entry, configuration, keys and any\n  dedicated resources, so a half-failed signup can simply be retried.\n- **Offboarding is where audits find gaps.** Deletion must reach the primary database, vector index,\n  object storage, caches, queues, logs and traces, evaluation samples, fine-tuned adapters and, on their\n  retention schedule, backups.\n- **Silo vs pool.** In a silo this is \"drop the stack\"; in a pool it is a delete keyed by tenant across\n  every store, which only works if every store carries the key.\n- **Crypto-shredding.** Per-tenant encryption keys turn the backup problem into deleting one key.\n\n### Tenant-aware configuration\n- **The tenant record carries:** allowed model (or model tier), system prompt and prompt version, enabled\n  features and tools, rate and token quotas, retention period, data-residency region, allowed connectors.\n- **Load once per request and pass explicitly**, rather than sprinkling `if tenant == \"acme\"` through the\n  code, *because* that is what keeps a hundred tenants from becoming a hundred code paths.\n\n### Fairness and noisy neighbours\nPooled resources need fair-share mechanisms *because* the model-serving queue and the database do not\nknow tenants exist:\n- **Per-tenant rate limits and concurrency caps** at the gateway.\n- **Per-tenant queues or weighted scheduling** for background work.\n- **Token budgets per billing period**, so one tenant's batch job does not become everyone's latency\n  incident.\n\n### Metering and cost allocation\n- **Record per LLM call:** input tokens, output tokens, model and tenant.\n- **Why:** token spend is the dominant variable cost of an AI product and is invisible without per-tenant\n  attribution.\n- **What it feeds:** billing, quota enforcement, and the margin analysis that decides which tier a\n  tenant belongs in.\n\n### What is specific to AI systems\nTenant identity must reach places a classic SaaS never had:\n\n| Place | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Retrieval filter | The vector query carries the tenant key, and the index enforces it rather than relying on a post-filter |\n| Prompt assembly | Tenant-specific instructions; no cross-tenant few-shot examples |\n| Semantic and prompt caches | Keys include the tenant, or a cache hit becomes a leak |\n| Agent memory and tool credentials | A tenant's connector token is used only for that tenant's runs |\n| Fine-tuned adapters | A LoRA trained on one tenant's data is that tenant's asset |\n| Evaluation sets | Per-tenant golden sets, because quality differs by corpus |","example":"### FastAPI slice: tenant from the token, carried to every layer\n- **What it shows:** the tenant is resolved from a verified token and stored in a context variable, so\n  deeper layers and `asyncio` tasks inherit it. Retrieval, configuration and token metering are scoped by\n  it; nothing downstream takes `tenant_id` from user input.\n- **The detail that matters:** the dependency is `async def`. FastAPI runs a plain `def` dependency in a\n  worker thread with a *copy* of the context, so a `ContextVar.set` made there is silently discarded\n  before the endpoint runs.\n\n```python\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\nimport jwt                                    # PyJWT\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header\n\ntenant_ctx: ContextVar[str] = ContextVar(\"tenant_id\")\napp = FastAPI()\n\nasync def current_tenant(authorization: str = Header(...)) -> str:\n    token = authorization.removeprefix(\"Bearer \")\n    claims = jwt.decode(token, PUBLIC_KEY, algorithms=[\"RS256\"], audience=\"assistant-api\")\n    tenant_id = claims[\"tenant_id\"]           # minted by the IdP, never read from the body\n    tenant_ctx.set(tenant_id)                 # async dep: same context as the endpoint and its tasks\n    return tenant_id                          # (a sync `def` dep would set it in a thread's copy)\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(req: ChatRequest, tenant_id: str = Depends(current_tenant)):\n    cfg = await tenant_config.load(tenant_id)              # model, prompt version, quotas, region\n    await quotas.check(tenant_id, cfg)                     # fail fast before spending tokens\n    passages = await vector_store.search(\n        req.message, top_k=8, filter={\"tenant_id\": tenant_id}   # enforced in the index, not after\n    )\n    result = await llm.complete(\n        model=cfg.model, system=cfg.system_prompt, context=passages, user=req.message,\n        cache_key=f\"{tenant_id}:{cfg.prompt_version}\",          # cache never crosses tenants\n    )\n    await meter.record(tenant_id, model=cfg.model,\n                       input_tokens=result.usage.input_tokens,\n                       output_tokens=result.usage.output_tokens)\n    return {\"answer\": result.text}\n\n# Work handed to a queue must carry the tenant explicitly: context does not cross processes.\nasync def enqueue_ingestion(doc_id: str):\n    await queue.send({\"tenant_id\": tenant_ctx.get(), \"doc_id\": doc_id})\n```\n\n### Runnable: a per-tenant fair queue for ingestion\nA FIFO queue puts a bulk upload in front of everyone. Round-robin across tenants means a small tenant\nwaits at most one turn per active tenant, not behind 1,000 queued chunks.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque, defaultdict\n\nclass FairQueue:\n    \"\"\"Round-robin across tenants so one tenant's backlog cannot starve the rest.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.queues = defaultdict(deque)   # tenant_id -> pending jobs\n        self.turns = deque()               # tenants with pending work, in turn order\n\n    def put(self, tenant_id: str, job: str) -> None:\n        if not self.queues[tenant_id]:\n            self.turns.append(tenant_id)   # tenant joins the rotation\n        self.queues[tenant_id].append(job)\n\n    def get(self) -> tuple[str, str]:\n        tenant_id = self.turns.popleft()\n        job = self.queues[tenant_id].popleft()\n        if self.queues[tenant_id]:\n            self.turns.append(tenant_id)   # back of the line, not the front\n        return tenant_id, job\n\nq = FairQueue()\nfor i in range(1000):                       # enterprise bulk upload arrives first\n    q.put(\"enterprise\", f\"chunk-{i}\")\nfor t in (\"small-a\", \"small-b\", \"small-c\"):\n    q.put(t, \"chunk-0\")\nprint([q.get()[0] for _ in range(6)])\n# ['enterprise', 'small-a', 'small-b', 'small-c', 'enterprise', 'enterprise']\n```\n\nWeighted fair scheduling generalises this by giving paying tiers more turns per round.\n\n### Worked scenario: promoting a tenant from pool to silo\nAn enterprise customer's contract now requires a dedicated database. The directory makes it a runbook,\nnot a code change.\n\n| Step | Action | Check before moving on |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Provision a dedicated database and index namespace | Directory still points at the pool |\n| 2 | Copy rows and vectors keyed by the tenant | Copy completes without errors |\n| 3 | Dual-write, or freeze writes for the cut-over window | Row and vector counts match; no writes lost |\n| 4 | Flip the directory entry | Cached directory lookups expire; traffic lands on the silo |\n| 5 | Delete the tenant's rows from the pool | Pool queries for that tenant return nothing |","patterns":["**Row-level security as a backstop.** Use when pooled tables share a schema. Enable PostgreSQL row security on each table with a policy comparing `tenant_id` to a per-transaction setting the app sets with `SET LOCAL` from the verified tenant. The table owner bypasses policies unless row security is forced, and superusers or `BYPASSRLS` roles always do, so the app must not connect as either. Monitor queries that run without the setting.","**Tenant on every signal.** Use from day one. Add the tenant ID as a structured log field and a trace span attribute on every request and job, and as a metric label while tenant count stays modest (thousands of label values strain most metrics backends). Monitor per-tenant p95 latency, errors and token spend.","**Gateway quotas per tenant.** Use for any pooled API. Keep a token-bucket or sliding-window counter keyed by tenant (for example in Redis) for requests and for LLM tokens, returning 429 with a retry hint. Monitor 429 rate per tenant and the share of tokens used by the top tenant.","**Two-tenant isolation tests in CI.** Use for every endpoint and background job. Seed two tenants with look-alike data, run each flow as tenant A, and assert nothing from tenant B appears in responses, caches or retrieved passages. Monitor coverage of endpoints by this suite.","**Per-tenant data keys for deletion.** Use when offboarding must reach backups. Encrypt each tenant's data with its own key held in a KMS, record the key ID in the tenant directory, and delete the key at offboarding. Monitor time from offboarding request to verified deletion across all stores."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with JWT or API key\"] --> E[\"Edge: verify credential\"]\n    E --> T[\"tenant_id from claims\"]\n    T --> D[(\"Tenant directory: tier, region, cell\")]\n    T --> C[\"Request context variable\"]\n    C --> CF[\"Tenant config and quota check\"]\n    CF --> V[(\"Vector search filtered by tenant\")]\n    V --> L[\"LLM call, cache key has tenant\"]\n    L --> M[\"Meter tokens per tenant\"]\n    C -->|\"explicit field\"| Q[\"Queue message for background work\"]","caption":"Follow the tenant ID from the verified credential: it is resolved once, then every store, cache, meter and\nqueue message receives it explicitly."},"interview":[{"q":"What are pool, bridge and silo, and why choose per resource rather than per system?","a":"- **Definition:** pool shares a resource with a tenant key, bridge partitions structure (schema or\n  collection per tenant) on shared infrastructure, silo dedicates the resource to one tenant.\n- **Why per resource:** compute, data and indexes have different isolation and cost needs, so a typical\n  system pools compute, bridges or pools data, and silos only the tier that pays for it."},{"q":"How does the tenant ID reach the vector store, the cache and a background job?","a":"- **Edge:** resolved from the verified token, then set in a request-scoped context variable.\n- **Stores and caches:** passed as an index-enforced filter on every vector query and included in every\n  semantic or prompt cache key.\n- **Jobs:** written as an explicit field on the queue message, because context variables do not cross\n  process boundaries."},{"q":"A customer reports an answer citing a document they never uploaded. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Treat it as a leak first:** find the request's trace, the retrieved passage IDs and their owning\n  tenant.\n- **Check the usual paths:** a vector search without the tenant filter, a cache key without the tenant,\n  a background job that ran with no tenant context, or a few-shot example drawn from another tenant.\n- **Then close the class of bug:** add a two-tenant test for that path and a database or index-level\n  backstop."},{"q":"When would you not use a pooled database for tenant data?","a":"- **When a contract or regulator demands physical separation**, or a tenant needs its own backup,\n  restore and migration timing.\n- **When one tenant is large enough to dominate** shared write paths or index compaction.\n- **The cost of leaving the pool:** migrations run N times, the catalog and connection pools grow, and a\n  silo tier drifts back toward per-customer operations."},{"q":"How would you allocate LLM cost to tenants on a pooled serving fleet?","a":"- **Meter every call:** input tokens, output tokens, model and tenant, recorded at the call site.\n- **Price by model:** multiply token counts by each model's per-token price to get spend per tenant per\n  period.\n- **Act on it:** feed billing and quotas, and compare spend with plan revenue to decide tier moves before\n  a flat-plan tenant erodes margin."}],"tradeoffs":["**Pooling: utilisation vs software-only isolation.** It maximises utilisation and minimises operations, but isolation rests on software correctness. It flips to bridge or silo when a tenant's contract or regulator demands physical separation.","**Siloing: strongest isolation vs linear cost.** It gives the strongest isolation and simplest per-tenant customisation, but cost grows with tenant count and upgrades become fleets. Sustainable only for a small, high-value tier.","**Per-tenant configuration: cheap customisation vs test matrix.** Every knob multiplies the test matrix. It pays off when tenants differ in policy (model, prompt, tools) rather than in code.","**Carrying tenant identity everywhere: plumbing vs forensics.** It costs discipline and a little plumbing, but is far cheaper than the forensic work of finding which code path lost it after a leak.","**Shared model-serving fleet: amortised GPUs vs KV-cache contention.** One tenant's long-context traffic can dominate the KV cache. Dedicated capacity becomes worth it when a tenant's load is both large and latency-sensitive."],"pitfalls":["**Accepting `tenant_id` from a request body or query string.** Symptom: a penetration test, or a curious customer, reads another tenant's conversations by editing one field. Fix: derive it from the verified credential at the edge.","**Forgetting the tenant key on the vector search or a semantic cache.** Symptom: an answer cites a document the user's organisation never uploaded. Fix: enforce the filter in the index and put the tenant in every cache key.","**Letting tenant context evaporate at a process or thread boundary.** Queues, cron jobs, thread pools, or a sync FastAPI dependency that sets a `ContextVar` in a worker thread. Symptom: background jobs or handlers run as \"no tenant\" and either fail or write into the wrong partition. Fix: put the tenant explicitly on every message and job, use `async def` for context-setting dependencies, and fail closed when no tenant is set.","**Hard-coding exceptions for a big customer.** Symptom: a release breaks only for them, discovered in production because the test suite has no such tenant. Fix: express the difference as tenant configuration.","**Offboarding by deleting database rows only.** Symptom: a departed customer's document resurfaces from the vector index or a cached answer, or a deletion audit finds it in traces and eval samples. Fix: one offboarding workflow that deletes by tenant key in every store, plus crypto-shredding for backups.","**Measuring cost per model but not per tenant.** Symptom: a tenant on a flat plan quietly consumes most of the token budget with no alarm until the invoice. Fix: meter tokens per tenant at the call site and alert on spend against plan."],"when":"- **Design for it from the first commit** of any product that will serve more than a handful of\n  customers, *because* retrofitting tenant identity into an existing schema, index and prompt pipeline is\n  one of the most expensive migrations there is.\n- **Choose the sharing model per resource:** start pooled for compute and bridge or pooled for data, and\n  write down what promotes a tenant to a dedicated tier.\n- **Keep single-tenant deployment as an explicit product option** for regulated buyers, not an accident.\n- **Measure:** per-tenant latency percentiles, error rates, token spend and quota hits. If you cannot\n  break a dashboard down by tenant, you cannot run the product.\n- **Interviews:** expect to be asked how the tenant ID reaches the vector store and the cache, and what\n  happens when one tenant uploads a million documents at once.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Threads the tenant through request context, queue messages, retrieval filters, cache keys and meter calls, and writes tests that run as two tenants and assert they never see each other.","architect":"Decides the pool/bridge/silo model per resource and per tier, the quota and fairness policy, and the promotion path from shared to dedicated capacity.","fde":"Maps a customer's requirements (residency, SSO, dedicated GPUs, retention) onto the tenant configuration and tier model, and knows when the honest answer is a single-tenant install."},"check":{"q":"Your pooled RAG product has 400 tenants. One enterprise tenant uploads 2 million documents\novernight and complains the next morning that chat is slow for them, while three small tenants also open\nlatency tickets. Diagnose the mechanism and propose a fix at each layer.","a":"The bulk ingestion flooded shared resources with no tenant-aware fairness. The embedding queue was\nFIFO, so the enterprise tenant's two million chunks sat in front of everyone else's small jobs; the\nembedding model server and the database write path were saturated; and the vector index was being\nrebuilt or compacted under load, slowing searches for all tenants. Fixes: per-tenant queues with weighted\nfair scheduling and a per-tenant ingestion concurrency cap; separate worker pools or at least priority\nfor interactive traffic over batch; an ingestion quota per hour in the tenant configuration with a\nback-pressure response rather than silent queuing; and, for a tenant of this size, a bridge or silo tier\nfor the index so its compaction cannot affect others. The dashboard should show queue depth and p95\nlatency per tenant so this is seen before the tickets."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where should a multi-tenant API derive the tenant identity for a request?","options":["From a `tenant_id` field in the JSON body, validated against a list of known tenants","From the verified credential (token claims, API key record) at the edge, then propagated","From the first row returned by the user's query","From the subdomain, with the body value taking precedence if both exist"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tenant must come from something the caller cannot forge. A body field is attacker-controlled even when validated against known tenants, because the attacker simply names another valid tenant."},{"q":"A team uses a shared semantic cache keyed only by the normalised question text. What is the risk?","options":["Cache hit rate will be too low to justify the cache","Embeddings will drift between tenants","One tenant can receive an answer generated from another tenant's documents","The cache will store duplicate entries per tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cached answer embeds the context it was generated from; without the tenant in the key, a similar question from a different tenant returns content grounded in someone else's data."},{"q":"Which statement about pool, bridge and silo is correct?","options":["Silo is always the right choice for data because it is most secure","Pool must be used for every resource or the system is not multi-tenant","The model is chosen per resource and per tier, and tenants may move between tiers","Bridge means one database per tenant on dedicated hardware"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The models describe degrees of sharing for a single resource. Real systems pool compute, bridge or pool data, and silo for the tier that pays for it; bridge is partitioned structure on shared infrastructure, not dedicated hardware."}]},{"key":"system-design/tenant-isolation","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL (row security policies)","Official docs: AWS Well-Architected SaaS Lens","OWASP, 2025 — Top 10 for LLM Applications"],"why":"- **Some layer, some day, will not respect the tenant.** Multi-tenancy tells every layer to; isolation is\n  what you build for when one does not. Typical misses: an admin report without the filter, an ORM\n  relationship lazy-loading across the tenant column, a background job with no tenant in context, a cache\n  key without the tenant, a retrieval filter passed as a soft re-ranking hint.\n- **In an LLM product a wrong row is read aloud.** In classic SaaS each mistake shows a wrong row on a\n  screen. Here the row becomes part of a prompt, and the model helpfully summarises another company's\n  contract to the user.\n- **\"We filter in the app and have tests\" is a single point of failure:** the discipline of every\n  engineer on every code path forever. Reviews catch most omissions; the leak comes from the one they\n  miss, and a customer discovers it.\n- **\"A database per tenant\" is isolation by cost.** Correct but unaffordable at scale, and compute,\n  caches, model servers, logs and agent credentials stay shared anyway.\n- **So: defence in depth you can prove.** At least two independent mechanisms between any tenant and any\n  other tenant's data, performance and secrets, so the app being wrong is not enough to leak. They must be\n  enforceable and testable, *because* you must show a security reviewer, not assert.","how":"### The dimensions you are isolating\nIsolation is not one property. A review walks each dimension and names its control:\n\n| # | Dimension | What must hold |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Data at rest and in query** | Who can read which rows, documents, embeddings and files |\n| 2 | **Performance** | One tenant's load must not become another's latency (noisy-neighbour control) |\n| 3 | **Secrets and credentials** | Connector tokens, API keys and encryption keys usable only in that tenant's work |\n| 4 | **Execution** | Agent tool calls and generated code run in a sandbox scoped to the tenant |\n| 5 | **Configuration and lifecycle** | Prompt versions, models and flags per tenant; a tenant can be migrated, suspended or deleted alone |\n| 6 | **Telemetry** | Logs, traces and eval samples carry the tenant tag and are access-controlled by it, because support staff reading traces is itself a cross-tenant read |\n| 7 | **Residency** | Data and inference stay in the tenant's required region or environment |\n\n### Layer 1: the database enforces the filter\n**Row-level security (RLS)** is the strongest cheap control for a pooled schema:\n1. The database attaches a predicate to every query on the table.\n2. The predicate reads the tenant from a per-transaction setting.\n3. The application sets it right after verifying the caller.\n\n*Why:* the filter moves from \"every query\" to \"one policy\", so a forgotten `WHERE` still returns only the\ncurrent tenant's rows.\n\n**Two details make or break it:**\n- **No bypass.** The application role must not be a superuser or hold `BYPASSRLS`, and the policy must\n  apply to the table owner (`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`); otherwise migrations and admin scripts silently\n  bypass it.\n- **Transaction-local setting.** Use `SET LOCAL`, *because* with transaction-mode connection pooling a\n  session-level setting leaks to whichever request gets the connection next.\n\n**Bridge and silo get the same property structurally:** a schema or database per tenant, with per-tenant\ndatabase credentials so a connection can only ever reach one tenant.\n\n**What RLS does and does not defend against:**\n- **Catches the forgotten filter**, because the tenant is set once after authentication and every later\n  query in the transaction inherits it.\n- **Does not stop a compromised application.** SQL injection, or any path that can issue `SET LOCAL`, can\n  name another tenant, because the setting is just a string the database trusts. Per-tenant credentials\n  (bridge or silo) address that threat, which is why RLS is one layer, not the whole answer.\n- **Index for it.** The policy adds `tenant_id = …` to every query, so composite indexes should lead with\n  `tenant_id`, or large tables degrade into scans that the policy then filters.\n\n### Layer 2: retrieval and caches are partitioned, not post-filtered\nVector search is where AI products most often leak. The tenant key must be a **hard pre-filter** or a\n**separate namespace**, never a re-ranking step after top-k, *because*:\n- a post-filter on a cross-tenant top-k can return fewer results than requested, tempting someone to widen\n  the search;\n- the unfiltered candidates have already crossed the boundary in memory and logs.\n\n| Store | How isolation holds | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| **pgvector** | The Layer 1 row policy runs inside the database, so no other tenant's row reaches the app | With an approximate index (HNSW, IVFFlat) the engine filters the index's candidate list: isolation holds but small tenants can get fewer than k rows. Iterative index scans (pgvector 0.8 and later) mitigate the recall loss; a partition or partial index per large tenant is the structural fix |\n| **Dedicated vector database** | Collection or namespace per tenant for larger tenants; mandatory metadata filtering for pooled ones | Make the filter non-optional in the retrieval client's signature |\n| **Semantic caches, prompt caches, agent memory** | The tenant is part of every key | A cache hit is a read |\n\n### Layer 3: credentials are scoped per tenant and per run\n- **Connector credentials.** Agent tools reaching a customer's CRM, ticketing or email use that tenant's\n  credentials: fetched from a secrets manager by tenant ID at run time, injected into the tool adapter for\n  that run only, never placed in the prompt.\n- **Per-tenant encryption keys** for stored documents: a storage-level leak yields ciphertext, and\n  deleting a tenant becomes deleting a key.\n- **Model-provider keys split per tenant or tier**, so a leaked key has a bounded blast radius and spend\n  can be attributed.\n\n### Layer 4: performance isolation\n- **Quotas and fair scheduling** at the gateway and in the job system cap requests per second, concurrent\n  generations, tokens per period and ingestion jobs in flight.\n- **Dedicated worker pools or model-serving capacity** for the top tier isolate the heaviest tenants\n  physically.\n- **The missing-layer indicator:** a latency incident whose root cause is one tenant's traffic.\n\n### Layer 5: verification\nIsolation that is not tested decays. Two tests earn their keep:\n1. **Cross-tenant canary.** Each tenant's corpus holds a unique marker document. An automated probe runs\n   representative questions as every other tenant and fails on any marker in retrieved passages or\n   generated answers.\n2. **Negative access tests in CI.** Every data-access function is called with tenant A's context against\n   tenant B's identifiers and must return nothing or raise.\n\nAdd log review for queries executed without a tenant setting, and isolation becomes something you can\nshow an auditor.","example":"### PostgreSQL row-level security on a pooled table with embeddings\n- **Transaction-local setting.** If the app forgets to set it, `current_setting(..., true)` returns NULL\n  on a fresh connection but an empty string on a connection that used the setting before (the\n  placeholder persists for the session). The policy wraps it in `NULLIF`, so either way the predicate is\n  NULL and the query returns no rows.\n- **Fails closed.** The failure mode is \"empty\", not \"leak\".\n- **Vector queries inherit it.** The policy predicate filters the scan, so a forgotten filter in the\n  vector query still cannot cross tenants. With HNSW or IVFFlat the filter may leave fewer than k rows:\n  a recall problem, not an isolation problem.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n    id         bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id  uuid NOT NULL,\n    body       text NOT NULL,\n    embedding  vector(1536)          -- pgvector\n);\n\nALTER TABLE documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;   -- owner is not exempt (migrations, admin scripts)\n\nCREATE POLICY tenant_rows ON documents\n    USING      (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid)   -- reads\n    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);  -- inserts/updates\n\n-- The application connects as a role with no BYPASSRLS and no ownership of the table.\nCREATE ROLE app_rw LOGIN NOBYPASSRLS;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON documents TO app_rw;\n\n-- Per request, inside one transaction (safe with transaction-mode pooling):\nBEGIN;\nSET LOCAL app.tenant_id = '2b6f1c1e-6c0a-4f7e-9d7e-1b8c3b2d9a10';\nSELECT id, body\nFROM   documents\nORDER  BY embedding <=> $1      -- cosine distance to the query embedding\nLIMIT  8;                        -- policy filters the scan, so the 8 rows are within-tenant\nCOMMIT;\n-- Same SELECT with no SET LOCAL: the predicate is NULL, so 0 rows come back (fails closed).\n-- INSERT naming another tenant_id: ERROR: new row violates row-level security policy for table \"documents\"\n```\n\n### Runnable: a cross-tenant canary probe\nPlant one unique marker per tenant, ask as every other tenant, and fail on any marker that appears. The\ntoy store shows the probe catching a search that drops the tenant pre-filter.\n\n```python\nimport uuid\n\nclass ToyStore:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.rows = []                                   # (tenant_id, text)\n    def add(self, tenant_id, text):\n        self.rows.append((tenant_id, text))\n    def search(self, tenant_id, query, k=8, enforce=True):\n        words = query.lower().split()\n        return [text for tid, text in self.rows\n                if (not enforce or tid == tenant_id)     # the hard pre-filter\n                and any(w in text.lower() for w in words)][:k]\n\ndef plant_canaries(store, tenants):\n    markers = {t: f\"CANARY-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]}\" for t in tenants}\n    for t, m in markers.items():\n        store.add(t, f\"internal codename is {m}\")\n    return markers\n\ndef find_leaks(search, tenants, markers, questions):\n    leaks = []\n    for asker in tenants:\n        for q in questions:\n            seen = \" \".join(search(asker, q))            # in production, also scan the answer\n            leaks += [(asker, owner) for owner, m in markers.items()\n                      if owner != asker and m in seen]\n    return leaks\n\ntenants = [\"acme\", \"globex\", \"initech\"]\nstore = ToyStore()\nmarkers = plant_canaries(store, tenants)\nqs = [\"what is our internal codename\"]\nprint(\"enforced:\", find_leaks(store.search, tenants, markers, qs))\nunfiltered = lambda t, q: store.search(t, q, enforce=False)\nprint(\"unfiltered:\", len(find_leaks(unfiltered, tenants, markers, qs)), \"leaks\")\n# enforced: []\n# unfiltered: 6 leaks   (3 askers x 2 other tenants)\n```\n\n### Worked incident: session-level SET behind a transaction pooler\n| Step | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| Change | A helper switched from `SET LOCAL` to a session-level `SET` \"to save a round trip\" |\n| Pooling | The pooler runs in transaction mode, so request 2 got request 1's connection |\n| Symptom | Intermittent, unreproducible reports of a user seeing another tenant's document titles |\n| Why tests missed it | Tests used one connection per test, so the setting never outlived a request |\n| Fix | Back to `SET LOCAL` inside the request transaction, plus a check that fails a query run with no tenant setting |","patterns":["**Mandatory tenant argument in the retrieval client.** Use for every vector store. Make the tenant a required keyword-only parameter of `search()` and have the client add the filter or pick the namespace itself, so no call site can omit it. Monitor the number of code paths that call the store's SDK directly instead of the client.","**Fail-closed connection checkout.** Use with RLS and a transaction pooler. Wrap transaction start so it always issues `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id` from the request context and raises if the context has no tenant. Monitor counts of raised \"no tenant\" errors per job type; they find background jobs that lost context.","**Continuous cross-tenant canary.** Use for every pooled corpus. Plant a marker document per tenant (or per tier for the long tail), run the probe on a schedule and after every retrieval or prompt change, and page on any hit. Monitor probe coverage and time since last successful run.","**Separate path for cross-tenant analytics.** Use when reporting needs all tenants. Give analytics a read replica and a dedicated role, and create reporting views with `security_invoker` (PostgreSQL 15+) so they check the caller's policies. Monitor which roles hold `BYPASSRLS`; the list should be tiny.","**Per-tenant secret paths.** Use for agent connectors. Store each tenant's tokens under a path keyed by tenant ID in the secrets manager, grant the runtime read access per run, and redact tokens from traces. Monitor secret reads whose tenant differs from the run's tenant."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Verified request for tenant A\"] --> B[\"App code, filter may be missing\"]\n    B --> C[\"Postgres RLS with SET LOCAL\"]\n    B --> D[\"Retrieval client: mandatory tenant filter\"]\n    B --> E[\"Cache and memory keys include tenant\"]\n    B --> F[\"Secrets fetched by tenant for this run\"]\n    C --> G[(\"Only tenant A rows\")]\n    D --> G\n    E --> G\n    H[\"Canary probe and negative CI tests\"] -.->|\"verify\"| C\n    H -.->|\"verify\"| D\n    H -.->|\"verify\"| E","caption":"A bug in application code still meets independent layers before any data crosses tenants; the canary and\nnegative tests continuously check that those layers hold."},"interview":[{"q":"What does row-level security protect against, and what does it not?","a":"- **Protects:** the forgotten filter. The tenant is set once per transaction after authentication, so any\n  query that omits `WHERE tenant_id = …` still sees only that tenant's rows.\n- **Does not protect:** a compromised application. SQL injection or any code that can run `SET LOCAL` can\n  name another tenant; per-tenant credentials in a bridge or silo model address that.\n- **Also bypassed by:** superusers, `BYPASSRLS` roles, unforced table owners, and unique or foreign-key\n  checks."},{"q":"Small tenants say search returns only two or three results. What is going on?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the tenant filter is applied to a shared approximate index (HNSW or IVFFlat), which\n  returns a candidate list that the filter then thins.\n- **Isolation is fine; recall is not.** Do not raise k across tenants as a workaround.\n- **Fixes:** iterative index scans in pgvector 0.8 and later, a partition or partial index per large\n  tenant, or a namespace per tenant in a dedicated vector database."},{"q":"When is a database per tenant worth it over RLS on a pooled schema?","a":"- **Worth it** when contracts or regulators require physical separation, when a compromised app must not\n  reach other tenants, or when a tenant needs its own backup and migration timing.\n- **Not worth it** for the long tail: migrations run per tenant and connections and catalogs multiply,\n  while RLS plus mandatory filters and canaries covers the forgotten-filter risk cheaply."},{"q":"How do you keep an agent's connector tokens from leaking across tenants?","a":"- **Fetch per run:** read the tenant's token from a secrets manager by tenant ID when the run starts.\n- **Inject into the adapter only:** the tool adapter holds it; the prompt, traces and eval samples never\n  do.\n- **Bound the blast radius:** scope tokens to one tenant, and split model-provider keys per tenant or tier\n  so a leak is contained and spend is attributable."},{"q":"After enabling RLS, queries on a large pooled table got much slower. Why, and what do you change?","a":"- **Cause:** the policy adds `tenant_id = …` to every query, so indexes that do not lead with `tenant_id`\n  force scans that the policy then filters row by row.\n- **Fix:** composite indexes that lead with `tenant_id` (for example `(tenant_id, created_at)`), and a\n  partition or partial vector index for the largest tenants.\n- **Verify:** compare `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` plans as the app role with the tenant set, because a superuser\n  bypasses the policy and shows a misleading plan."}],"tradeoffs":["**Database-enforced policies: catch app mistakes vs explicit privileged paths.** They add a per-query predicate and make \"run this across all tenants\" a privileged path. That is a feature until analytics needs cross-tenant reporting, which should then use a separate replica and role.","**Namespaces per tenant: clean separation vs overhead.** Per-tenant collections allow per-tenant re-indexing, but many small collections waste memory and connections; pooled indexes with mandatory filters win for the long tail.","**Per-tenant encryption keys: simple deletion vs key management on every read.** Deletion and breach containment become simple, but key management joins every read path. Justified when contracts require crypto-shredding or residency.","**Dedicated capacity: no noisy neighbour vs idle cost.** Worth it only when a tenant's load is both heavy and latency-sensitive, or when they pay for it.","**Canary and negative tests: a slower suite vs durable evidence.** They need per-tenant fixtures, but they are the only evidence of isolation that survives a refactor."],"pitfalls":["**Enabling RLS but connecting as the table owner or a superuser.** Symptom: tests pass, and a manual check with the production role shows every tenant's rows. Fix: a dedicated non-owner app role with `NOBYPASSRLS`, plus `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`.","**Using session-level `SET` behind a transaction-pooling proxy.** Symptom: intermittent, unreproducible cases where a request sees a previous request's tenant. Fix: `SET LOCAL` inside the request transaction.","**Applying the tenant filter after top-k in vector search.** Symptom: queries return fewer than k results for small tenants, followed by someone raising k to compensate. Fix: a hard pre-filter or a namespace per tenant.","**Putting customer connector tokens into the system prompt** so the agent can \"use them\". Symptom: a token appears in a trace, an eval sample or a model response. Fix: inject them into the tool adapter for that run only.","**Trusting the vector database's metadata filter without making it mandatory in the client.** Symptom: one new call site omits the filter and passes review because the code \"looks like the others\". Fix: a required tenant parameter in the retrieval client, and no direct SDK calls.","**Reading through views or `SECURITY DEFINER` functions owned by a superuser or a `BYPASSRLS` role.** Symptom: a reporting view returns every tenant's rows although the base table has a policy, because a view checks policies as its owner unless created with `security_invoker` (PostgreSQL 15+).","**Assuming RLS hides everything.** Unique and foreign-key checks bypass row security, so \"this email is already registered\" on a globally unique column tells tenant A a value exists in tenant B. Symptom: an enumeration finding in a pen test. Fix: scope unique constraints to `(tenant_id, …)`.","**Logging full prompts to a shared log store without tenant tags and access control.** Symptom: support engineers read tenant data they are not cleared for, which is itself a reportable breach. Fix: tag every log and trace with the tenant and gate access to them by it."],"when":"- **Baseline:** every multi-tenant AI system needs at least two independent isolation layers for data and\n  one for performance from the first production tenant; the question is how far up the ladder each tier\n  goes.\n- **Pooled tenants:** RLS or equivalent, plus mandatory retrieval filters, plus quotas.\n- **Mid tier:** add per-tenant namespaces and encryption keys.\n- **Enterprise or regulated:** bridge or silo data, dedicated capacity, and per-tenant credentials end to\n  end.\n- **Measure:** queries executed without a tenant setting (should be zero), the cross-tenant canary running\n  continuously, and per-tenant latency so noisy neighbours show up as a graph rather than a ticket.\n- **Interviews and security reviews:** name each layer, explain what failure it catches that the layer\n  above misses, and show the test that proves it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets the tenant in the transaction, makes the retrieval client refuse calls without a tenant filter, keys every cache by tenant, and writes the negative access tests.","architect":"Defines the isolation ladder per tier, the roles and policies in the database, the key-management model, and signs off that two independent layers exist for every data path.","fde":"Walks a customer's security team through the controls with evidence (policies, role grants, canary results), and translates their requirements into the tier that actually meets them."},"check":{"q":"A security reviewer says, \"Your API filters by tenant in every query, and you have unit tests. Why\nis that not enough for our data?\" Give the answer a strong architect would give, including what you\nwould add and how you would prove it works.","a":"Application filtering has one point of failure: a developer forgetting it on one path, and in an\nLLM product that missed row ends up in a prompt. I would add a second, independent layer that holds\neven when the code is wrong: row-level security in PostgreSQL with a transaction-local tenant setting,\nwith the application role denied `BYPASSRLS` and the policy forced on the owner, so a missing filter\nreturns nothing rather than everything. Retrieval gets a mandatory tenant filter in the client signature\nor a namespace per tenant, and every cache key includes the tenant. For evidence, I would show the\npolicy definitions and role grants, a CI suite that calls every data function as tenant A against\ntenant B's IDs, and a continuously running cross-tenant canary that plants marker documents and alerts\nif any marker is ever retrieved or generated for another tenant."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id` preferred over a session-level `SET` when using row-level security?","options":["It is faster because it avoids writing to the catalog","It is scoped to the transaction, so a pooled connection cannot carry one tenant's setting into the next request","It encrypts the setting in memory","Session-level settings cannot be read by `current_setting`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transaction-mode poolers hand the same connection to different requests; a session setting would persist across them and make a later request run as the earlier tenant. Transaction scope removes that failure mode."},{"q":"What is the main reason to make the tenant filter a hard pre-filter in vector search rather than filtering the top-k results afterwards?","options":["Pre-filtering produces better-calibrated similarity scores","Post-filtering is not supported by any vector database","Post-filtering lets cross-tenant candidates cross the boundary and can return fewer than k results, inviting a wider search","Pre-filtering is required for cosine distance to work"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross-tenant neighbours already fetched into memory and logs are a leak surface, and small tenants see depleted result sets after filtering, which pushes engineers to widen the query. A hard filter or a namespace prevents both."},{"q":"Which control best limits the blast radius if one tenant's connector token is leaked through an agent trace?","options":["Rotating the model-provider API key","Reducing the model's temperature","Per-tenant credentials fetched at run time and injected only into that tenant's tool adapter, never into prompts","Enabling row-level security on the conversations table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A leaked token is bounded to one tenant only if credentials are scoped per tenant and kept out of the prompt and traces. Row-level security protects rows, not secrets, and the provider key is unrelated."}]},{"key":"system-design/c4-diagrams","sources":["Brown — The C4 model for visualising software architecture (c4model.com)","Official docs: Structurizr DSL"],"why":"- **Ad-hoc diagrams mix abstraction levels.** One picture shows a Kubernetes cluster, a \"Users\" stick\n  figure, a PostgreSQL cylinder, an `AuthService` class and a team name, joined by unlabelled lines that\n  may mean \"calls\", \"deploys to\" or \"is owned by\". Every review spends its first twenty minutes working\n  out what the boxes are.\n- **UML was too heavy.** Its rigorous notation was learned by almost nobody, so teams fell back to\n  free-form whiteboards and the ambiguity returned.\n- **C4 borrows the idea of a map.** Country, city and street maps use different symbols and detail, and\n  you know which one you hold. C4 fixes four zoom levels and a tiny notation, so a Container box is a\n  deployable unit and a Component box is part of one. Tooling, layout and colours stay free.\n- **AI systems raise the stakes.** Security, legal and customer architects first ask \"where does our data\n  go, and which parts talk to the model provider?\" A diagram that cannot answer at a glance costs a review\n  cycle; C4's labelled technology and flow direction answer it.","how":"### The four levels\n| Level | What a box is | Audience | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. System Context** | Your system as one box, plus the people who use it and the external systems it depends on (identity provider, the CRM it reads tickets from, the hosted model provider). No technology inside the box | Executives, customers | Every audience first needs the system's place in the world |\n| **2. Container** | A separately runnable or deployable unit: web app, API, ingestion worker, database, vector store, queue, model server, LLM gateway, each with its technology (\"FastAPI\", \"PostgreSQL + pgvector\", \"vLLM\") | Engineers, operators | Where day-to-day design and operations live |\n| **3. Component** | Major structural parts of one container, e.g. inside the API: authentication middleware, tenancy resolver, orchestration, retrieval client, guardrail layer, usage meter | Those who depend on that container's internals | Maps to modules or packages, not classes |\n| **4. Code** | Class or entity diagrams for one component | Rarely anyone | Generate from code if needed at all; hand-maintained code diagrams rot first |\n\n- **\"Software system\" means what one team builds and owns.** Anything owned by someone else, including\n  another team's platform, is drawn as external.\n- **\"Container\" predates Docker and is wider than it.** A database is a container; a Docker image is not\n  necessarily one container.\n- **Draw Component diagrams only** for containers whose internals matter to someone other than their\n  authors.\n\n### Supplementary diagrams\nThese reuse the same elements:\n- **System Landscape:** several systems side by side.\n- **Dynamic:** numbered arrows telling the story of one use case. A RAG request: resolve tenant, embed\n  query, search, assemble prompt, call gateway, stream, meter.\n- **Deployment:** places containers onto infrastructure (region, VPC, cluster, GPU node pool, the\n  customer's network versus yours). Forward-deployed engineers draw it most, *because* it is where trust\n  boundaries and data residency become visible.\n\n### The notation rules that do the work\nEach rule removes one specific ambiguity:\n- **Every element has name, type, technology and a one-line description**, so a reader never guesses\n  whether \"Gateway\" is an API gateway, an LLM gateway or a network device.\n- **Every relationship is a single-direction arrow labelled with intent** and, where useful, protocol\n  (\"sends completion requests to, HTTPS/JSON\"), so data-flow direction is never inferred.\n- **Every diagram has a title stating level and scope**, and a **legend** if colours or shapes carry\n  meaning.\n- **Roughly twenty elements per diagram.** Past that, draw a new diagram one level down, not a smaller\n  font.\n- **Consistent colour and shape across diagrams:** people, your containers, external systems and data\n  stores each look the same everywhere.\n\n### Diagrams as code\n- **Structurizr separates model from views.** Elements and relationships are defined once; views choose\n  which subset to draw at which level.\n- **Why:** consistency is the hard part. With one source of truth, renaming a container updates every\n  diagram, and a relationship cannot appear on the Container view but be missing from the Context view.\n- **Lighter options:** C4-PlantUML and Mermaid's C4 syntax give the same notation in text, with less model\n  discipline.\n- **Text in the repository is the cure for diagram rot.** It changes in pull requests next to the code it\n  describes and can be regenerated in CI.\n\n### What to make visible in an AI system\n- **Model provider as an external system on the Context diagram**, *because* that arrow is a data egress a\n  reviewer must see.\n- **Gateway, vector store and evaluation pipeline as containers**, *because* each enforces a decision:\n  routing, isolation, release gating.\n- **On the Deployment view**, draw the boundary between the customer's environment and yours and mark\n  which flows carry customer documents or prompts.\n- **Prefer a Dynamic diagram of one request** over a Component diagram of everything; it is usually worth\n  more.\n\n### Trust boundaries and threat models\n- **C4 has no first-class trust-boundary element** and does not pretend to be a threat model.\n- **Draw boundaries with deployment nodes** (the customer's VPC, your cloud account, the provider's\n  region) or named groups, and state in the legend what crossing one means.\n- **A STRIDE-style review still wants a data-flow diagram**; a good Deployment view gives most of it.","example":"### Structurizr DSL: one model, three views\n- **Three views, one model:** the Context diagram for stakeholders, the Container diagram for engineers and\n  the Dynamic view of one request cannot disagree.\n- **Labelling:** the model provider is external, the vector-capable database is tagged to render as a\n  cylinder, and every relationship states intent and protocol.\n- **Self-checking story:** the dynamic view numbers the steps of one reply, and Structurizr checks each\n  step against the model's relationships. Delete the `api -> gateway` arrow and the workspace fails to\n  build, instead of the request story silently going stale.\n- **What the rendered Context view reveals:** two arrows leave the system, to the CRM and to the model\n  provider. The second is the data egress a privacy review asks about first.\n\n```\nworkspace \"Support Copilot\" \"Drafts ticket replies grounded in the knowledge base\" {\n    model {\n        agent   = person \"Support Agent\" \"Answers customer tickets\"\n        crm     = softwareSystem \"CRM\" \"System of record for tickets and articles\" \"External\"\n        llm     = softwareSystem \"Model Provider\" \"Hosted LLM API\" \"External\"\n        copilot = softwareSystem \"Support Copilot\" \"Suggests grounded replies\" {\n            web     = container \"Web App\" \"Agent-facing UI\" \"React\"\n            api     = container \"API\" \"Auth, tenancy, orchestration, guardrails\" \"FastAPI\"\n            worker  = container \"Ingestion Worker\" \"Chunks and embeds articles\" \"Python\"\n            gateway = container \"LLM Gateway\" \"Routing, quotas, prompt cache\" \"Python\"\n            db      = container \"Database\" \"Tenants, chunks, embeddings, feedback\" \"PostgreSQL + pgvector\" \"Database\"\n        }\n        agent   -> web     \"Uses\"\n        web     -> api     \"Sends chat requests to\" \"HTTPS/JSON\"\n        api     -> db      \"Reads and writes\" \"SQL\"\n        api     -> gateway \"Requests completions from\" \"HTTPS\"\n        gateway -> llm     \"Sends prompts and receives completions\" \"HTTPS\"\n        worker  -> crm     \"Pulls articles from\" \"REST\"\n        worker  -> db      \"Writes chunks and embeddings to\" \"SQL\"\n    }\n    views {\n        systemContext copilot \"Context\"    { include * autolayout lr }\n        container     copilot \"Containers\" { include * autolayout lr }\n        dynamic copilot \"DraftReply\" \"One grounded reply\" {\n            agent -> web \"Opens a ticket and asks for a draft\"\n            web -> api \"Sends ticket text\"\n            api -> db \"Fetches top-8 chunks for this tenant\"\n            api -> gateway \"Requests a draft grounded in the chunks\"\n            gateway -> llm \"Forwards prompt; streams completion\"\n            autolayout lr\n        }\n        styles {\n            element \"External\" { background #999999 color #ffffff }\n            element \"Database\" { shape Cylinder }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### C4-PlantUML: a deployment view with the trust boundary drawn\nFor a pilot in a customer's VPC, deployment nodes act as the trust boundary, and the one arrow that leaves\nthe customer's environment is labelled with what it carries.\n\n```\n@startuml\n!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/plantuml-stdlib/C4-PlantUML/master/C4_Deployment.puml\ntitle Deployment: Support Copilot pilot in customer VPC\n\nDeployment_Node(cust, \"Customer VPC\", \"Customer cloud account\") {\n    Deployment_Node(k8s, \"Kubernetes cluster\", \"Customer-managed\") {\n        Container(api, \"API\", \"FastAPI\", \"Auth, tenancy, orchestration\")\n        Container(worker, \"Ingestion Worker\", \"Python\", \"Chunks and embeds articles\")\n        ContainerDb(db, \"Database\", \"PostgreSQL + pgvector\", \"Chunks, embeddings\")\n    }\n}\nDeployment_Node(vendor, \"Vendor cloud\", \"Vendor account\") {\n    Container(gateway, \"LLM Gateway\", \"Python\", \"Routing, quotas\")\n}\nSystem_Ext(llm, \"Model Provider\", \"Hosted LLM API\")\n\nRel(api, db, \"Reads and writes\", \"SQL\")\nRel(worker, db, \"Writes chunks and embeddings to\", \"SQL\")\nRel(api, gateway, \"Sends prompts containing ticket text\", \"HTTPS\")\nRel(gateway, llm, \"Forwards prompts\", \"HTTPS\")\nSHOW_LEGEND()\n@enduml\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: which view answers which reviewer\nA customer's security review for that pilot, mapped to the view that answers each first question.\n\n| Reviewer | First question | View to send | Must make explicit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Privacy and legal | Which third parties touch our data? | System Context | Model provider and CRM as external systems |\n| Customer architects | What runs, on what technology? | Container | Every deployable with technology and labelled arrows |\n| Security | Where does data leave our VPC? | Deployment | Trust boundary and flows carrying prompts or documents |\n| New engineer | What happens on one request? | Dynamic | Numbered steps of a chat request and the ingestion flow |","patterns":["**Diagrams as code, rendered in CI.** Use for every system more than one team touches. Keep the Structurizr DSL or C4-PlantUML files next to the code, and have a CI job render them (for example with the Structurizr CLI's export command or PlantUML) so a broken model fails the build. Monitor time since each diagram file last changed relative to the services it describes.","**Same-PR diagram updates.** Use when adding or removing a queue, model server or external dependency. Add a pull-request template checkbox and a code-owner rule on the architecture folder so the architect reviews diagram changes. Monitor containers in the deployment manifests that are missing from the model.","**Per-customer deployment views.** Use for every forward-deployed or VPC install. Keep one deployment environment per customer topology in the same workspace, reusing the shared model, so the Container view stays common while placement differs. Monitor open review questions per customer.","**Tag flows that carry customer data.** Use when privacy reviews are routine. Tag relationships that carry prompts or documents and give that tag a distinct style and a legend entry, so egress is visible without reading every label. Monitor untagged arrows that cross a deployment boundary.","**Element budget per view.** Use in design reviews. Treat roughly twenty elements as the ceiling and split into a level-down view or a Dynamic view instead. Monitor views that exceed the budget."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[(\"One model: elements and relationships\")] --> L1[\"System Context: system as one box\"]\n    L1 -->|\"zoom into system\"| L2[\"Container: deployables and tech\"]\n    L2 -->|\"zoom into one container\"| L3[\"Component: modules inside it\"]\n    L3 -->|\"rarely, generated\"| L4[\"Code: classes\"]\n    M --> DY[\"Dynamic: numbered steps of one request\"]\n    M --> DP[\"Deployment: containers on infrastructure\"]\n    M --> LS[\"System Landscape: many systems\"]\n    DP -.->|\"shows\"| TB[\"Trust boundaries and data egress\"]","caption":"Each level zooms into one box of the level above; the supplementary views reuse the same elements to\nanswer behaviour and placement questions."},"interview":[{"q":"What are the four C4 levels, and why separate them?","a":"- **Levels:** System Context (the system as one box among people and external systems), Container\n  (deployables with technology), Component (modules inside one container), Code (classes, usually\n  generated).\n- **Why:** each level has one audience and one density of detail, so a reader always knows what a box\n  means; mixing levels is what makes ad-hoc diagrams unreadable."},{"q":"Sketch the architecture of a RAG assistant using C4. Where do you start?","a":"- **Start with Context:** users, the system as one box, the identity provider, the document source and\n  the hosted model provider as external systems.\n- **Then Container:** web app, API, ingestion worker, LLM gateway, database with a vector extension, each\n  with technology and labelled arrows.\n- **Then one Dynamic view** of a chat request: resolve tenant, embed, search, assemble prompt, call\n  gateway, stream, meter."},{"q":"Your diagrams are always out of date. How do you fix that?","a":"- **Move them to text in the repository** (Structurizr DSL or C4-PlantUML) so they change in the same\n  pull requests as the code.\n- **Use one model with several views** so a rename or deletion propagates and inconsistent views fail to\n  build.\n- **Render in CI and require diagram updates** in PRs that add or remove containers."},{"q":"When would C4 not be enough?","a":"- **Threat modelling:** C4 has no trust-boundary element; a STRIDE-style review still wants a data-flow\n  diagram, though a good Deployment view covers most of it.\n- **Behaviour and protocols:** beyond numbered arrows, use sequence diagrams.\n- **Enterprise capability mapping:** heavier notations such as ArchiMate earn their cost there."},{"q":"A diagram has forty boxes. What do you do?","a":"- **Split it:** about twenty elements is the practical ceiling, so move detail one level down into a\n  Component view of the container that matters.\n- **Replace breadth with stories:** a Container view plus two Dynamic views of the important requests\n  usually answers more questions than one dense picture."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fixed levels: instant readability vs constraint.** Anyone who knows C4 reads the diagrams at once, but a system that does not decompose neatly feels constrained. The fix is usually a Dynamic or Deployment view, not a bespoke notation.","**Diagrams as code: in sync vs uglier layout.** They stay in sync with the repository and survive reviews, but auto-layout rarely matches a hand-arranged picture. Hand layout wins only for a diagram shown far more often than it changes.","**One model, many views: consistency vs an owner.** Consistency is guaranteed only if someone owns the model; without that owner, C4-PlantUML files per diagram are easier to keep alive.","**Lighter than UML or ArchiMate: learnable vs limited.** C4 is learnable in an afternoon, but says nothing about behaviour beyond numbered arrows or about formal semantics. For enterprise-wide capability mapping or protocol-level design, heavier notations (or sequence diagrams) earn their cost.","**Component diagrams: explain internals vs rot.** They rot fastest and are read least; draw them for the containers whose structure others depend on and skip the rest."],"pitfalls":["**Mixing levels in one diagram.** Symptom: a picture shows \"Kubernetes\" and \"the retry helper class\" at once, and the review is spent asking what the boxes are. Fix: one level per diagram, titled with its level and scope.","**Unlabelled or double-headed arrows.** Symptom: a security reviewer asks \"which direction does customer data flow here?\" and nobody is sure. Fix: one direction per arrow, labelled with intent and protocol.","**Reading *container* as Docker container.** Symptom: a managed database and a hosted model provider are missing because \"they are not containers\". Fix: draw every runnable unit and data store, and every dependency you do not own as an external system.","**Leaving the model provider off the Context diagram** because it is \"just an API\". Symptom: a procurement or privacy review discovers the data egress late. Fix: show it as an external system with a labelled arrow.","**Diagrams in a slide deck disconnected from the code.** Symptom: a Container view still shows a service deleted two quarters ago. Fix: diagrams as code in the repository, rendered in CI.","**Drawing everything.** Symptom: a forty-box Container diagram nobody can read, instead of a Container view plus two Dynamic views of the requests that matter. Fix: keep to about twenty elements per view."],"when":"- **Always:** a Context and a Container diagram for every system that more than one team or organisation\n  needs to understand, kept in the repository as text.\n- **Add a Deployment view** the moment the system crosses a trust boundary, which for an AI product is\n  usually immediately (a hosted model provider, a customer VPC).\n- **Add Dynamic views** for the two or three request paths reviewers and new engineers ask about most,\n  typically a chat request and the ingestion flow.\n- **Skip Component and Code diagrams** unless a specific audience needs them.\n- **Test:** can a new engineer, a security reviewer and a customer architect each find the diagram meant\n  for them and answer their first question without you in the room?\n- **Interviews:** sketching a Context then a Container diagram structures any system-design answer;\n  naming technologies and flow directions as you go signals the discipline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the Container and Dynamic diagrams in the repo as code, updates them in the same pull request that adds a queue or a model server, and uses them to onboard teammates.","architect":"Owns the model behind the views, decides which containers and boundaries exist, and uses the Context and Deployment diagrams as the shared picture in design and security reviews.","fde":"Produces the Deployment and Context views for each customer environment, marking where customer data crosses into the vendor's systems, which is often the document that unblocks procurement."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks for \"an architecture diagram\" of your RAG product before approving\na pilot in their VPC. Which C4 views do you send, what must each make explicit, and why not just one\npicture?","a":"Three views, each for a different question. A System Context diagram showing their users, your\nsystem as one box, and every external system including the hosted model provider, because their first\nquestion is which third parties touch their data. A Container diagram naming each deployable with its\ntechnology, API, worker, gateway, database with vector extension, model server if self-hosted, with\narrows labelled by intent and protocol, so they can see where prompts and documents are stored and\nprocessed. And a Deployment diagram placing those containers inside their VPC versus your environment,\nwith the trust boundary drawn and the flows that cross it marked. One picture would have to mix the\nthree levels, which is exactly what makes reviewers unsure what each box is and which way data flows."},"quiz":[{"q":"In the C4 model, what does \"container\" mean?","options":["A Docker or OCI container image","A separately runnable or deployable unit such as an application, database, queue or model server","A bounded context from domain-driven design","A cloud account or Kubernetes namespace"],"answer":1,"explanation":"C4's container predates Docker and is broader: anything that runs or is deployed on its own, including managed databases and queues. Bounded contexts and namespaces are different concepts that may or may not align with containers."},{"q":"Why does the C4 model insist on labelling each relationship with its intent and direction?","options":["So that diagram tools can auto-layout more efficiently","Because UML requires it for sequence diagrams","So readers never have to infer which way data flows or what one element does to another","To make diagrams compatible with Structurizr's licensing model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An unlabelled line can mean calls, deploys to, reads from or owns, and a two-headed arrow hides who initiates. In security and data-flow reviews that ambiguity is precisely the question being asked."},{"q":"Which view best shows a customer where its data crosses from its environment into the vendor's?","options":["A Component diagram of the API","A Code diagram generated from the data model","A Deployment diagram placing containers on infrastructure with the trust boundary drawn","A System Landscape diagram of all the vendor's products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the deployment view maps containers onto environments, so it is the one where \"inside your VPC\" versus \"in our cloud\" and the flows between them become visible. Component and code views are below that level of concern."}]},{"key":"system-design/adrs","sources":["Nygard, 2011 — Documenting Architecture Decisions","Official docs: MADR (Markdown Any Decision Records)"],"why":"- **Decisions are rarely written down.** Six months on, a new engineer finds the vector store is\n  PostgreSQL with pgvector rather than a dedicated database, the context window is capped at 32k tokens,\n  and every LLM call goes through a gateway. Nobody knows why.\n- **Two bad outcomes follow.**\n  - **Blind acceptance:** nobody dares change the choice because nobody knows what it protects, so the\n    system calcifies around constraints that may no longer exist.\n  - **Blind reversal:** someone \"fixes\" it and the original incident recurs, because the reason lived in\n    the head of someone who left.\n- **Existing artefacts do not record decisions.** Wiki pages describe current state and are edited in\n  place, so reasoning and alternatives vanish. Design documents are long proposals written before the\n  decision. Commit messages record *what* changed, not what was weighed.\n- **AI systems move faster than the system.** \"We chose model X because it scored best on our eval\" is\n  true on the day and unknowable a year later unless eval version, scores, prices and assumptions were\n  captured. Without them the team cannot tell whether a decision is still right or merely old.\n- **What the ADR buys.** The cheapest artefact that makes decisions inspectable, reversible on purpose,\n  and reviewable like code.","how":"### What deserves one\n- **The test is architectural significance:** would reversing it be expensive, or would someone later\n  need to know why it was made?\n- **Yes:** the vector store, tenancy model, gateway, retrieval strategy, model tier for a workload,\n  evaluation gate for releases, data-residency approach.\n- **No:** a logging library's formatter.\n- **Calibration:** an ADR a week is probably recording tasks; three ADRs after a year is probably not\n  recording decisions.\n\n### Anatomy: Nygard's five parts\n| Part | What it holds | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Title and number** | `ADR-0007: Serve v1 through a hosted model API behind our gateway` | Numbers give a stable reference; the title states the decision, not the topic |\n| **Status** | Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, or Superseded by ADR-NNNN | The only field that changes after acceptance, *because* the record must stay an honest snapshot |\n| **Context** | Forces stated neutrally: NFRs, constraints, team size, deadlines, costs, risks, uncertainties | Lets a future reader judge whether the decision still applies, so it describes conditions, not advocacy |\n| **Decision** | \"We will …\" in full sentences, including what is explicitly *not* being done | Removes ambiguity about scope |\n| **Consequences** | What gets easier, what gets harder, new risks, follow-up work | Every real decision has downsides; a record without them was written to justify, not to decide |\n\n### MADR extensions\n- **Decision drivers**, **considered options** with pros and cons, a **decision outcome** with its\n  justification, and a **confirmation** section describing how compliance will be checked.\n- **Worth it when there were real alternatives**, which for architecture is nearly always.\n- **A one-line Y-statement summary** at the top: \"In the context of …, facing …, we decided for … to\n  achieve …, accepting …\".\n\n### Lifecycle and mechanics\n1. **Lives in the repository**, typically `docs/adr/NNNN-title.md`, next to the code it governs,\n   *because* that is where engineers look and where pull-request review already happens.\n2. **Opened as *Proposed* in a pull request**, argued in review, and *Accepted* when merged.\n3. **Never edited after that except for status.** Changing a decision means a new ADR that supersedes the\n   old one, linked both ways. Immutability is the feature: the chain of records is the history of why the\n   system looks the way it does.\n4. **One or two pages, written at decision time.** An ADR reconstructed months later is a story, not a\n   record.\n5. **Linked from the C4 diagrams and README**, so the \"why\" is one click from the \"what\".\n\n- **Design doc vs ADR.** A longer design doc or RFC often comes first: it explores the space and may run\n  to ten pages. The ADR is the one-page outcome that links back to it.\n- **Tooling is optional.** A numbered Markdown file is enough; adr-tools or log4brains only script the\n  numbering, linking and index.\n\n### Who decides, and how a decision stays true\n- **Decision rights are recorded.** The Deciders line says who could accept it.\n- **Two workable models.** Route every significant decision through an architect, or use an advice\n  process: anyone may decide after consulting those affected and those with expertise, and the ADR is the\n  evidence that advice was sought. What fails is an unstated rule that surfaces as a veto after merge.\n- **Unchecked decisions decay quietly.** Six months later someone imports the provider SDK directly \"just\n  for this one feature\", and the gateway decision is void without anyone deciding so.\n- **MADR's confirmation section exists for this.** Prefer an automated check, what *Building Evolutionary\n  Architectures* calls a fitness function: an import-lint rule, a CI test, a policy-as-code rule or a\n  dashboard alert. With automated confirmation a decision is enforced; without, it is a suggestion.\n\n### What AI systems add to the template\n- **Evidence snapshot.** Eval set version, scores, latency percentiles and cost per request for each option\n  at decision time, so the decision can be re-run, not just re-argued.\n- **Assumptions with expiry.** Provider pricing, rate limits, context limits, licence terms and expected\n  traffic, each labelled with the date it was checked.\n- **Revisit triggers.** Concrete conditions that force re-evaluation: a volume threshold, an open-weight\n  model reaching eval parity, a residency requirement, a price change. Without one, an ADR becomes a\n  reason never to look again.\n- **Reversibility.** A two-way door (swap a model behind the gateway) or a one-way door (train on customer\n  data, choose an embedding model that fixes the index).\n- **Compliance and data flow implications.** Where customer data goes under this decision, and which\n  approvals it needed.\n\n### How it fits with the rest of the design toolkit\n| Artefact | Role |\n|---|---|\n| Non-functional requirements | Define what matters |\n| Trade-off analysis | Scores the options against them |\n| ADR | Records the outcome and the reasoning |\n| C4 diagrams | Show the resulting structure |\n\nWhen one is missing the others degrade: an ADR with no NFRs behind it is an opinion, and a diagram with\nno ADRs behind it is a snapshot nobody can question.","example":"### A MADR-style record for an early AI decision\nThe figures are illustrative scenario values, not benchmarks. The point is the structure: neutral\ncontext, real options, named drivers, honest negative consequences, an evidence snapshot, an automated\nconfirmation and explicit revisit triggers.\n\n```markdown\n# ADR-0007: Serve v1 through a hosted model API behind our own gateway\n\n**Status:** Accepted (2026-03-12)  **Supersedes:** none  **Deciders:** platform lead, security, CTO\n\n**Context.** Six engineers, launch in ten weeks. Expected launch volume ~20M tokens/month, highly\nuncertain (illustrative). NFRs: p95 time-to-first-token <= 1.5 s, cost <= $0.04 per conversation,\nEU data residency, provider must be swappable without client changes.\n\n**Decision drivers.** Time to launch; engineering capacity; residency; cost at uncertain volume;\nability to switch models as quality and prices change.\n\n**Considered options.**\n1. Hosted model API (EU region) behind an internal gateway.\n2. Self-hosted open-weight model on two GPUs with vLLM.\n3. Hybrid: hosted for chat, self-hosted for embeddings.\n\n**Decision.** Option 1. All model calls go through the gateway, which owns routing, quotas, prompt\ncaching and per-tenant metering. We will not self-host any generation model in v1.\n\n**Consequences.**\n- Easier: launch on time; model swaps are a gateway config change; no GPU operations.\n- Harder: per-token cost grows linearly with usage; dependence on provider rate limits and uptime;\n  residency relies on the provider's EU endpoint and DPA.\n- Follow-up: contract tests against the gateway interface; monthly cost and quality review.\n\n**Confirmation.** CI import rule: only `gateway/` may import a provider SDK; provider keys exist only in\nthe gateway's secret scope.\n\n**Evidence at decision time.** Golden set v3 (312 cases): hosted model faithfulness 0.91 vs best\nopen-weight candidate 0.84; p95 TTFT 0.9 s vs 1.3 s on a single GPU under load test. Illustrative.\n\n**Revisit when:** sustained volume > 2B tokens/month for three months; an open-weight model reaches\n>= 0.90 on golden set v3 or later; the provider cannot meet a customer's residency requirement; or\nprovider price changes by more than 30%.\n```\n\n### Runnable: a CI check that supersede links go both ways\nImmutability only works if the chain is navigable. This check fails the build when a new record\nsupersedes an old one that was never marked *Superseded*.\n\n```python\nimport re, tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nSTATUS = re.compile(r\"\\*\\*Status:\\*\\*\\s*(Proposed|Accepted|Deprecated|Superseded by ADR-(\\d{4}))\")\nSUPERSEDES = re.compile(r\"\\*\\*Supersedes:\\*\\*\\s*(none|ADR-(\\d{4}))\")\n\ndef lint(adr_dir):\n    recs, errors = {}, []\n    for p in sorted(Path(adr_dir).glob(\"[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-*.md\")):\n        text = p.read_text()\n        st, sup = STATUS.search(text), SUPERSEDES.search(text)\n        if not st:\n            errors.append(f\"{p.name}: missing or unknown Status\")\n        recs[p.name[:4]] = (st.group(2) if st else None, sup.group(2) if sup else None)\n    for num, (superseded_by, supersedes) in recs.items():\n        if supersedes and recs.get(supersedes, (None,))[0] != num:\n            errors.append(f\"ADR-{num} supersedes ADR-{supersedes}, which is not marked Superseded by ADR-{num}\")\n        if superseded_by and recs.get(superseded_by, (None, None))[1] != num:\n            errors.append(f\"ADR-{num} says Superseded by ADR-{superseded_by}, which does not link back\")\n    return errors\n\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n    Path(d, \"0007-hosted-model-api.md\").write_text(\n        \"# ADR-0007\\n**Status:** Accepted (2026-03-12)  **Supersedes:** none\\n\")\n    Path(d, \"0019-self-host-chat-model.md\").write_text(\n        \"# ADR-0019\\n**Status:** Accepted (2026-09-30)  **Supersedes:** ADR-0007\\n\")\n    print(lint(d))\n# ['ADR-0019 supersedes ADR-0007, which is not marked Superseded by ADR-0019']\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a quarterly revisit of ADR-0007\nThe triggers make the review a checklist instead of a debate. Observed values are illustrative.\n\n| Trigger in ADR-0007 | Observed this quarter | Fired? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Volume > 2B tokens/month for three months | 2.4B, 2.6B, 2.9B | Yes |\n| Open-weight model >= 0.90 on golden set v3 or later | Best candidate 0.88 on v4 | No |\n| Provider cannot meet a residency requirement | All customers served from the EU endpoint | No |\n| Provider price change > 30% | List price down 12% | No |\n\n- **Outcome:** one trigger fired, so the team re-runs the cost comparison for option 3 (hybrid) at the new\n  volume, with fresh evidence.\n- **If it wins:** write a new ADR that supersedes ADR-0007 and records the new evidence; ADR-0007's status\n  becomes *Superseded*, its text untouched.","patterns":["**ADR in the same pull request as the change.** Use for every architecturally significant change. Add a pull-request template question (\"Does this need an ADR?\") and a code-owner rule on `docs/adr/` so the architect or deciders review it. Monitor significant PRs (new datastore, new provider, new deployable) merged without a linked ADR.","**Confirmation as a fitness function.** Use whenever a decision can be checked by a machine. For the gateway decision, an `import-linter` `forbidden` contract stops any module outside `gateway/` from importing a provider SDK, run as a required CI check. Monitor contract exceptions; each one is an undocumented reversal.","**Generated index with current status.** Use once the log passes a dozen records. Generate an index page (number, title, status, superseded-by) from the files in CI, as adr-tools or log4brains can, and publish it where non-engineers can read it. Monitor records stuck in *Proposed* for weeks.","**Revisit triggers wired to dashboards.** Use for AI decisions with expiring assumptions. Turn each numeric trigger (monthly token volume, eval score of the best alternative, price) into a dashboard alert whose description names the ADR number. Monitor fired triggers that have no follow-up record.","**Quarterly review of AI decisions.** Use for model, vendor and retrieval ADRs. Walk each record's dated assumptions and triggers, re-run the golden set where needed, and either note \"still holds\" in the review log or open a superseding ADR."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Proposed: opened in a pull request\n    Proposed --> Proposed: argued in review\n    Proposed --> Accepted: merged\n    Accepted --> Superseded: new ADR replaces it, linked both ways\n    Accepted --> Deprecated: decision no longer relevant\n    Superseded --> [*]\n    Deprecated --> [*]","caption":"An ADR's content is frozen at acceptance; only its status moves, and a changed decision is a new record\nthat supersedes the old one."},"interview":[{"q":"What is an ADR, and how is it different from a design doc?","a":"- **Definition:** a one- or two-page, numbered, immutable record of one significant decision: context,\n  options, decision, consequences, status.\n- **Versus a design doc:** a design doc or RFC explores the space before the decision and can run to ten\n  pages; the ADR records what was finally chosen and why the alternatives lost, and links back to the doc."},{"q":"Which decisions in an AI product would you write ADRs for, and which not?","a":"- **Yes:** anything expensive to reverse or likely to be questioned: vector store, tenancy model, gateway,\n  retrieval strategy, model tier per workload, eval release gate, residency approach.\n- **No:** cheap, reversible, local choices such as a log formatter.\n- **Calibrate:** an ADR a week means you are logging tasks; three a year means you are missing decisions."},{"q":"Code in production contradicts an accepted ADR. What happened and what do you change?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the decision had no confirmation mechanism, so a \"just this once\" shortcut, such as a\n  direct provider SDK import, voided it silently.\n- **Immediate:** decide whether the code or the decision is wrong; if the decision, write a superseding\n  ADR rather than editing the old one.\n- **Lasting:** add an automated fitness function (import rule, CI test, policy-as-code) so the next\n  violation fails the build."},{"q":"What does an ADR for choosing an LLM need that a database ADR usually does not?","a":"- **An evidence snapshot:** eval set version, scores, latency percentiles and cost per request per option.\n- **Dated assumptions:** pricing, rate limits, context limits, licence terms, expected traffic.\n- **Revisit triggers with numbers:** for example volume over 2B tokens a month for three months, or an\n  open-weight model reaching parity on the golden set, because the model landscape moves monthly."},{"q":"Why supersede rather than edit, and what does that cost?","a":"- **Gain:** the original context and reasoning survive, so a future reader can see what changed and why.\n- **Cost:** chains of records to follow. Keep a generated index with current status and links both ways so\n  the latest decision is always one click away."}],"tradeoffs":["**An hour or two of writing vs repeated re-litigation.** The ADR saves debating the same choice again; not worth it for decisions that are cheap to reverse and nobody will ask about.","**Immutability: honest history vs chains to follow.** Superseding records form chains a reader must follow; an index page with current status keeps this navigable.","**Rich template (MADR): real alternatives vs writing friction.** It forces alternatives and evidence into the open, but discourages writing anything for medium-sized decisions. Many teams use the full template for one-way doors and the short Nygard form otherwise.","**In the repository: under review vs invisible to non-engineers.** ADRs sit where engineers look, but other stakeholders never see them unless they are also published or summarised."],"pitfalls":["**Writing the ADR after the decision to justify it.** Symptom: one option, no negative consequences, and a context section that reads like a sales pitch.","**Editing an accepted ADR when the decision changes.** Symptom: `git blame` shows the decision text rewritten with no superseding record and no explanation of what changed.","**Recording decisions about trivia.** Symptom: eighty ADRs in a year and no one able to find the five that matter.","**Omitting evidence and assumptions in AI decisions.** Symptom: a team still on a model two generations old because \"there is an ADR\", with no way to tell whether its reasons still hold.","**No revisit trigger.** Same symptom, discovered only when a competitor's cost per conversation is a third of yours.","**Accepting a decision with no confirmation mechanism.** Symptom: code contradicting an accepted ADR (a direct provider call, a second vector store) discovered months later during an incident.","**Storing ADRs in a wiki away from the code.** Symptom: nobody updates status, and engineers learn about a decision from the code rather than the record."],"when":"- **Write one whenever a choice shapes structure, cost, risk or data flow** and would be expensive to\n  reverse: tenancy model, vector store, gateway, retrieval strategy, model tier per workload, evaluation\n  gates, residency approach, build versus buy.\n- **Write it as the decision is made**, in the pull request where it is argued, and link it from the\n  diagram and the README.\n- **Measure the practice:** do new engineers cite ADR numbers in design discussions, and do superseding\n  records exist for the decisions that changed?\n- **Interviews:** describing a past decision in ADR shape (context, options, choice, consequences, and\n  what would have made you choose differently) is one of the clearest signals of architectural maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Opens an ADR in the same pull request as a significant change, keeps the evidence section honest with real eval and latency numbers, and reads the existing chain before proposing a reversal.","architect":"Owns the ADR log, decides what is architecturally significant, enforces supersede-not-edit, and makes sure every ADR traces to the NFRs and trade-off analysis behind it.","fde":"Writes customer-specific ADRs for deviations from the reference architecture (residency, SSO, on-prem model serving) so the next engineer on the account inherits the reasoning, not just the config."},"check":{"q":"An ADR from fourteen months ago says \"use provider X's model Y because it scored best on our\nevaluation set\". A new engineer wants to switch to a cheaper model this sprint. How should the team\nhandle it, and what does the answer reveal about how the original ADR should have been written?","a":"Do not edit the old record and do not switch on a hunch. Read its context and evidence: which\neval set version, what scores, what cost and latency targets, which assumptions. Re-run the current\ngolden set against both models, measure cost per conversation and p95 latency under the same NFRs, and\ncheck whether any assumption (price, rate limits, residency) has changed. If the cheaper model meets the\ndrivers, write a new ADR that supersedes ADR-N, records the new evidence, and marks the old one\n*Superseded*. The exercise shows what the original should have contained: an evidence snapshot with the\neval version, dated assumptions, and a revisit trigger such as \"re-evaluate when a model within 10% of\nthe cost achieves parity\", which would have made this review routine rather than a debate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are accepted ADRs treated as immutable, with changes made by superseding records?","options":["Because version control cannot track edits to Markdown files reliably","So the record stays an honest snapshot of what was decided and why, and the history of changes is preserved as a chain","Because only architects are permitted to edit them","To keep the number of files in the repository stable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Editing in place destroys the context and reasoning of the original decision, which is exactly what a future reader needs to judge whether it still applies. Superseding keeps both the old and the new reasoning."},{"q":"Which element of an ADR is most often missing when the record was written to justify a decision after the fact?","options":["A number and title","The names of the deciders","Genuine alternative options and negative consequences of the chosen one","A link to the related C4 diagram"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A real decision weighs options and accepts costs. A record with a single option and \"no downsides\" is advocacy, and it gives a future team nothing to re-evaluate when conditions change."},{"q":"What should an ADR about choosing an LLM provider include that a classic database-choice ADR usually would not need?","options":["A sequence diagram of the request path","An evidence snapshot (eval set version, scores, cost, latency), dated assumptions and explicit revisit triggers","The names of all competing vendors in the market","A rollback script"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model quality, pricing and limits change far faster than database characteristics, so the decision must carry the data to be re-run and the conditions under which it should be. Without them the ADR becomes a reason never to re-evaluate."}]},{"key":"system-design/nfrs","sources":["ISO/IEC 25010 — Systems and software quality models","Bass, Clements, Kazman, 2012 — Software Architecture in Practice (3rd ed.)"],"why":"- **Features do not determine architecture; quality attributes do.** Two systems with the same feature\n  list can be completely different: one serves ten internal users from a single process, the other ten\n  thousand tenants across regions at 99.9% availability. That is why experienced architects say the NFRs\n  *are* the architecture.\n- **Adjectives cannot be designed or tested.** \"Fast, accurate, secure and scalable\": fast for whom, at\n  what load, at which percentile? Accurate on which inputs, measured how, against what threshold?\n- **Vagueness is paid late, in production.** A pilot that works for five users streams its first token\n  in eight seconds at five hundred; a security review surfaces a residency requirement the model\n  provider cannot meet; cost per conversation exceeds the price per seat.\n- **AI adds statistical requirements.** Answer quality and safety (harmful output rate, prompt-injection\n  resistance) can only be stated over a defined evaluation set, with a threshold and a tolerance.\n- **The big three conflict.** Quality, latency and cost pull against each other, so an unprioritised list\n  cannot resolve the first real design conflict. Writing NFRs properly makes trade-offs explicit before\n  the code does.","how":"### From adjective to scenario\nThe quality attribute scenario format (Bass, Clements and Kazman) has six parts, each closing a loophole:\n\n| Part | Example | Loophole it closes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Source** | A logged-in support agent | Whose experience counts |\n| **Stimulus** | Submits a chat message | What triggers the behaviour |\n| **Environment** | Normal operation, 50 concurrent sessions | \"It was fast in the demo\" |\n| **Artifact** | The chat API | Arguments about where latency is counted |\n| **Response** | First token streamed back | What \"done\" means |\n| **Response measure** | Within 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile over any five-minute window | Averages hiding tail pain |\n\n### The categories, and what AI adds\n- **ISO/IEC 25010 as a discovery checklist:** functional suitability, performance efficiency,\n  compatibility, usability, reliability, security, maintainability and portability (a later revision adds\n  safety and renames some). It is a checklist, not a template to fill in full.\n- **AI-specific attributes classic software never specified:**\n\n| Attribute | What to specify |\n|---|---|\n| **Answer quality** | Faithfulness or groundedness, task success rate, citation correctness, on a versioned golden set with a threshold and minimum sample size |\n| **Safety** | Harmful or policy-violating output rate, refusal correctness, resistance to a prompt-injection test suite |\n| **Freshness** | Maximum lag between a source document changing and the index reflecting it |\n| **Consistency** | How much output may vary for the same input; whether a run can be reproduced |\n| **Cost** | Cost per successful task or conversation, including retries and tool calls |\n| **Data handling** | Residency, retention, no-training clauses, what may appear in logs and traces |\n| **Operability** | Time to roll back a prompt or model version, and to re-index after an embedding change |\n\n### Making each one measurable and owned\nEvery NFR gets five fields: **metric, target, measurement method, condition** (load, data, time window)\nand **enforcement point**. The last is most often missing, and it exists *because* an NFR with no\nenforcement point is a wish.\n\n| Attribute type | Becomes |\n|---|---|\n| Runtime (latency, availability, freshness) | SLOs with alerts |\n| Quality and safety | Evaluation gates in CI and release pipelines |\n| Security and residency | Review checklist items and automated tests |\n| Cost | A per-tenant, per-feature dashboard with budgets |\n\n- **Percentiles, never averages**, for anything latency-shaped, and state the window over which the\n  percentile is computed.\n\n### Statistical targets need their uncertainty\n- **Standard error of a proportion** measured on *n* cases is about √(p(1−p)/n).\n- **Worked:** at p = 0.90 and n = 300 that is ≈ 0.017, so the 95% interval is roughly ±3.4 points. A run\n  scoring 0.91 cannot be told apart from one scoring 0.88.\n- **Write the gate accordingly**, either:\n  - \"the lower bound of the interval is at least 0.90\", or\n  - \"no statistically significant regression against the current release on the same cases\".\n- **The paired form needs far fewer cases** to detect a small drop, *because* each case is compared with\n  itself.\n\n### Decomposing a target into budgets\nAn end-to-end NFR is not actionable for one team until it is split along the request path.\n\n**Latency budgets add.** A 1.5 s p95 time-to-first-token might be allotted (illustrative):\n\n| Stage | Budget |\n|---|---|\n| Auth and tenant resolution | 50 ms |\n| Query embedding | 100 ms |\n| Vector search | 150 ms |\n| Guardrails and prompt assembly | 200 ms |\n| Model's own first token | about 1 s left |\n\n- **Summed component p95s usually overestimate** the end-to-end p95, *because* components rarely hit their\n  tails on the same request. Budgets set this way are conservative; the end-to-end measurement still\n  counts.\n\n**Availability multiplies.**\n- A chat path depending serially on your stack at 99.95% and a provider at 99.9% delivers about\n  0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 99.85%, below a 99.9% target before your own bugs.\n- That one multiplication is why the availability row in the example below forces a provider fallback\n  rather than a better runbook.\n- A 99.9% monthly target is about 43 minutes of allowed unavailability in a 30-day month; stating the\n  error budget in minutes makes each dependency's cost concrete.\n\n### Prioritising, because they conflict\n- **Architecturally significant requirements** are the handful whose satisfaction forces a design choice:\n  - a 1.5-second time-to-first-token forces streaming and a model-tier decision;\n  - EU residency forces region pinning and provider selection;\n  - a cost ceiling forces routing and caching.\n- **ATAM's utility tree** ranks scenarios by business importance and architectural difficulty, so the team\n  argues about the top five rather than the full list.\n- **Order the conflicts explicitly.** If quality and latency collide, which gives? That written priority\n  is trade-off analysis's tie-breaker; without it every design review reopens the question.\n\n### Traceability\n- **Forward** to the decision it drove (an ADR) and to the test, SLO or eval gate that verifies it.\n- **Backward** to the stakeholder and business reason it came from.\n- **Why:** requirements change. When the cost ceiling moves, you need to know which decisions and tests\n  depended on the old number.\n\n### Where they come from\n| Source | Supplies |\n|---|---|\n| Finance and product | Cost and availability targets |\n| Security and legal | Residency, retention and access requirements |\n| Users | Latency tolerance, which differs: an agent drafting replies tolerates less than a batch summariser |\n| Operations | Observability and rollback needs |\n| Regulation | Audit and explainability demands |\n\n- **A baseline of the current process** (how long a human takes, how often they are wrong) is often the\n  most defensible source for a quality target.","example":"### Worked NFR set for a multi-tenant support assistant\nThe assistant drafts replies for human agents. Figures are illustrative targets for the scenario, not\nbenchmarks. Each row is measurable, has an enforcement point and visibly drives an architectural decision;\nthe last column is where trade-off analysis and ADRs begin.\n\n| Attribute | Scenario and measure (illustrative) | Enforced by | Architectural consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Latency | First token within 1.5 s at p95, 50 concurrent sessions, 5-min window | SLO + load test in staging | Streaming responses; model tier chosen for TTFT; gateway timeout budget |\n| Answer quality | Faithfulness ≥ 0.90 and citation correctness ≥ 0.95 on golden set v3 (≥ 300 cases) | Eval gate in CI; blocks release | RAG with mandatory citations; reranking; no release without eval run |\n| Safety | Harmful-output rate ≤ 0.5% on red-team suite; ≥ 99% refusal of injection probes | Eval gate + quarterly red team | Input/output guardrails as a separate component; tool allow-lists |\n| Availability | 99.9% monthly for the chat API; degraded mode allowed | SLO, error budget policy | Provider fallback via gateway; graceful degradation to retrieval-only |\n| Cost | ≤ $0.04 per conversation averaged weekly, per tenant | Meter + budget alert | Model routing by difficulty; prompt caching; context trimming |\n| Residency | EU tenants' prompts, documents and logs never leave EU regions | Architecture review + automated egress test | Region-pinned deployment; EU provider endpoint; log pipeline per region |\n| Freshness | Knowledge-base edits searchable within 15 minutes at p95 | SLO on index lag | Incremental ingestion with change feed, not nightly rebuild |\n| Operability | Prompt or model rollback in under 10 minutes without a deploy | Runbook drill | Versioned prompt registry; gateway config as the switch |\n\n- **Read it against the conflicts.** The quality row wants a large model and long context; latency and\n  cost want a small one and short prompts.\n- **The written priority decides.** It was quality, then latency, then cost, so the consequence column\n  resolves to routing (large model only for hard cases) rather than a single small model. Change the\n  priority and the architecture changes with it.\n\n### Runnable: the arithmetic behind the gates and budgets\nCheck an eval gate's uncertainty, a latency budget and a serial availability chain before committing to\nthe numbers.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef interval(p: float, n: int, z: float = 1.96) -> tuple[float, float, float]:\n    se = sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)                    # standard error of a proportion\n    return se, p - z * se, p + z * se\n\nse, lo, hi = interval(0.90, 300)\nprint(f\"SE {se:.3f}, 95% interval {lo:.3f} to {hi:.3f}\")\n\ndef gate(score: float, n: int, target: float = 0.90) -> bool:\n    return interval(score, n)[1] >= target        # lower bound must clear the target\n\nprint(\"0.91 on 300 passes:\", gate(0.91, 300), \"| 0.93 on 300 passes:\", gate(0.93, 300))\n\nbudget_ms = {\"auth + tenant\": 50, \"query embedding\": 100, \"vector search\": 150,\n             \"guardrails + prompt\": 200}\nprint(\"left for model first token:\", 1500 - sum(budget_ms.values()), \"ms\")\n\nserial = 0.9995 * 0.999                           # your stack x provider, in series\nprint(f\"serial availability {serial:.2%} vs target 99.90%\")\nprint(\"99.9% error budget:\", round((1 - 0.999) * 30 * 24 * 60, 1), \"min per 30-day month\")\n# SE 0.017, 95% interval 0.866 to 0.934\n# 0.91 on 300 passes: False | 0.93 on 300 passes: True\n# left for model first token: 1000 ms\n# serial availability 99.85% vs target 99.90%\n# 99.9% error budget: 43.2 min per 30-day month\n```\n\nThe lower-bound gate is strict: to pass at 0.90 on 300 cases the run must score about 0.93. That is why\nteams often prefer the paired \"no significant regression\" form for release gates.","patterns":["**NFR register in the repository.** Use from the first design review. Keep one file listing each NFR with metric, target, method, condition, enforcement point and owner, reviewed in pull requests like code. Monitor NFRs with no enforcement point or owner; the count should be zero.","**SLOs with error-budget alerts.** Use for latency, availability and freshness. Define the SLO on the same percentile and window as the NFR and alert on error-budget burn rate over a short and a long window (the multi-window approach in the Google SRE workbook), not on single spikes. Monitor budget remaining per month.","**Paired eval gate in CI.** Use for quality and safety NFRs. Run the versioned golden set on the candidate and the current release, compare per case, and block the release on a statistically significant drop. Monitor gate flakiness; a gate that flips on reruns of one build is under-powered.","**Load test at the stated condition.** Use before every launch and capacity change. Drive the staging stack at the NFR's concurrency (for example with Locust or k6) and record time-to-first-token p95 from the client side, not server logs. Monitor the gap between staging and production p95.","**Per-tenant cost budgets.** Use when a cost ceiling exists. Meter tokens and tool calls per conversation per tenant, compute weekly cost per conversation, and alert at a fraction of the ceiling. Monitor the top tenants' share of spend."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Stakeholder says fast, accurate, cheap\"] --> Q[\"Quality attribute scenario\"]\n    Q --> F[\"Metric, target, method, condition, enforcement\"]\n    F --> P{\"Forces a structural choice?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes\"| U[\"Rank in utility tree\"]\n    U --> T[\"Trade-off analysis\"]\n    T --> A[\"ADR records the decision\"]\n    P -->|\"no\"| C[\"Keep as checklist item\"]\n    F --> E1[\"SLO with alert\"]\n    F --> E2[\"Eval gate in CI\"]\n    F --> E3[\"Security or egress test\"]\n    F --> E4[\"Cost budget alert\"]","caption":"An adjective becomes a scenario with five fields; the significant few go through prioritisation into\ntrade-offs and ADRs, and every NFR lands on an enforcement point."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes an NFR testable, and why do architects say NFRs are the architecture?","a":"- **Testable means a scenario:** source, stimulus, environment, artifact, response and a numeric response\n  measure with percentile and window, plus an enforcement point.\n- **Why they are the architecture:** the same features at 10 users or 10,000 tenants across regions need\n  completely different structures; quality attributes, not features, force those choices."},{"q":"Your eval gate passes and fails on reruns of the same build. What is wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** the gate compares a point estimate from a small set against a fixed threshold. At 300\n  cases and p = 0.90 the 95% interval is about ±3.4 points, wider than the margin.\n- **Fixes:** gate on the interval's lower bound, or better, on a paired comparison against the current\n  release over the same cases; add cases if the paired test is still under-powered."},{"q":"The target is 99.9% availability and you depend on a provider at 99.9%. Is that achievable?","a":"- **Not by runbooks:** your stack at 99.95% in series with the provider gives about 99.85%, already below\n  target.\n- **Architectural fix:** a provider fallback through the gateway and a degraded retrieval-only mode, so\n  the provider is no longer a serial dependency for every request.\n- **Make it concrete:** 99.9% is about 43 minutes a month of error budget."},{"q":"How do you split a 1.5 s p95 time-to-first-token across teams?","a":"- **Allocate budgets along the path:** for example auth and tenant 50 ms, embedding 100 ms, search 150 ms,\n  guardrails and prompt 200 ms, leaving about 1 s for the model's first token (illustrative).\n- **Know the bias:** summed component p95s overestimate end-to-end p95, so the budgets are conservative,\n  but the end-to-end client-side measurement is still the one that counts."},{"q":"When would you deliberately not tighten an NFR the customer asks for?","a":"- **When its cost outweighs its value:** \"99.99%\" for an internal tool used in office hours buys a\n  multi-region design and on-call rotation nobody needs.\n- **How to handle it:** attach the cost of each tightening (capacity, model size, regions) and let the\n  business choose; keep internal SLOs tighter than any external SLA."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tight NFRs: unambiguous design vs real money.** Each tightened target costs capacity, bigger models or more regions. Negotiate the number with its cost attached rather than accept a round figure.","**Percentile targets: protect the tail vs noise at low volume.** p99 on a low-traffic endpoint is noisy; use p95 or longer windows until volume justifies p99.","**Long NFR list: thorough vs unread.** Only the architecturally significant few deserve scenario-level rigour; spreading effort evenly produces a document nobody reads.","**Hard contracts (SLAs): certainty vs room to trade.** SLAs give customers certainty but remove room to trade; keep internal SLOs tighter than external SLAs so the trade-off space stays yours.","**Early quality thresholds: release gates vs arbitrary bars.** A threshold set before a baseline exists is arbitrary; measure the human baseline first, then set the bar relative to it."],"pitfalls":["**Specifying latency as an average.** Symptom: a green dashboard while a fifth of users wait ten seconds for a first token.","**A quality NFR with no named evaluation set or sample size.** Symptom: \"accuracy 92%\" that cannot be reproduced and silently changes when someone edits the test cases.","**Gating releases on a point estimate from a small eval set.** Symptom: a gate that flips between pass and fail on reruns of the same build, and a team that learns to rerun until green.","**A latency target with no load condition.** Symptom: a staging pass followed by a launch-day collapse at ten times the concurrency.","**Copying \"99.99% availability\" from a template.** Symptom: a multi-region design and on-call rotation for an internal tool whose users go home at six.","**Forgetting cost as an NFR.** Symptom: a successful pilot whose unit economics make the product unprofitable at scale, discovered from the invoice.","**NFRs without an owner or enforcement point.** Symptom: a requirements document nobody has opened since the kickoff."],"when":"- **Write them at the start** of any system with real users, and revisit at every scope change, *because*\n  they are the inputs to every architecture decision that follows.\n- **Focus rigour** on the five to eight that are architecturally significant; keep the rest as a\n  checklist.\n- **Attach an enforcement mechanism to each:** an SLO, an eval gate, a security test, a budget alert.\n- **In an engagement**, get the customer to state and prioritise NFRs early, so residency or cost\n  constraints do not surface in week ten.\n- **Interviews:** before drawing anything, state the NFRs you are assuming, with numbers and percentiles,\n  and name which one drives each structural choice. That is the difference between a feature sketch and\n  an architecture.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns each NFR into a concrete check, a load test, an eval gate, an SLO alert, and knows the numeric target behind the component being built.","architect":"Owns the elicitation, prioritisation and traceability of NFRs, and uses them as the scoring dimensions for every trade-off and the justification for every ADR.","fde":"Extracts the customer's real constraints (residency, latency tolerance, budget, audit) early, writes them as measurable NFRs, and uses them to set acceptance criteria for the pilot."},"check":{"q":"A product manager says the new assistant must be \"fast, accurate and cheap\". Turn that into\nNFRs, explain why they conflict, and say what you would ask the business to decide.","a":"I would write each as a measurable scenario with an enforcement point. Fast: first token within\n1.5 seconds at p95 under the expected peak concurrency, enforced as an SLO and a staging load test.\nAccurate: faithfulness and task success above a threshold on a versioned golden set of at least a few\nhundred representative cases, enforced as a release gate, with the threshold set relative to the measured\nhuman baseline. Cheap: a cost per successful conversation ceiling, metered per tenant with alerts. They\nconflict because quality pushes toward larger models and longer context, which raise latency and cost,\nwhile the cost ceiling pushes toward smaller models and shorter prompts, which lower quality. I would ask\nthe business to rank the three explicitly, so the design can resolve the conflict, typically by routing\nonly hard cases to the expensive model, and to attach a cost to any tightening of the targets."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a well-formed non-functional requirement?","options":["The system must be highly available","The chat API returns its first token within 1.5 s at p95 under 50 concurrent sessions, measured over any 5-minute window","The model should be as accurate as possible on customer questions","Latency must average under one second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A testable NFR names the artifact, the condition, the percentile and the window. An average hides tail latency, and \"highly available\" or \"as accurate as possible\" cannot be verified or designed to."},{"q":"Why should an AI quality requirement name a versioned evaluation set and a minimum sample size?","options":["Because model providers require it for support contracts","To make the requirement shorter and easier to read","Because a statistical threshold is meaningless without the population it is measured on, and the gate must be reproducible as cases change","Because regulators mandate exactly 300 test cases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality is a probabilistic property; \"92% accurate\" means nothing without the set, its version and enough cases for the estimate to be stable. Versioning lets a release gate be re-run and compared over time."},{"q":"What makes a requirement \"architecturally significant\"?","options":["It appears in the customer contract","It concerns security rather than performance","Satisfying it forces a structural choice, such as streaming, region pinning or model routing","It has the largest numeric target"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Architectural significance is about consequences for structure, not about the source or the domain of the requirement. Many contractual or security requirements are satisfied by configuration and do not shape the architecture."}]},{"key":"system-design/trade-off-analysis","sources":["Richards, Ford, 2020 — Fundamentals of Software Architecture","Ford, Richards, Sadalage, Dehghani, 2021 — Software Architecture: The Hard Parts","Kazman, Klein, Clements, 2000 — ATAM: Method for Architecture Evaluation"],"why":"- **Everything is a trade-off.** Richards and Ford's first law; their corollary is that if you think a\n  choice has none, you have not found it yet. The practice exists because teams keep deciding in ways that\n  hide the cost until it is paid.\n- **The common methods fail predictably.**\n  - **Pros-and-cons lists** have no weights, evidence or conditions, so they can justify anything.\n  - **Weighted scoring matrices** look rigorous but are usually built backwards: favourite first, weights\n    tuned until it wins, single-digit precision pretending to be measurement.\n  - **Best-practice and vendor-benchmark decisions** import someone else's context, the one thing a\n    trade-off is sensitive to.\n  - **Analysis paralysis:** with no method, every decision is a debate won by the loudest voice.\n- **AI makes it worse.** Options change monthly and published benchmarks do not transfer to your data.\n  Hosted or self-hosted, large or small model, long context or retrieval, fine-tune or prompt: the honest\n  answer is \"it depends on volume, quality threshold and how often you will need to switch\".\n- **What a good analysis outputs.** Not \"we picked X\", but what the choice depends on, at what value the\n  answer changes, and how expensive being wrong is. That output survives contact with next quarter.","how":"### 1. Frame the decision\n- **One sentence:** the decision, its scope, who decides, by when.\n- **Separate hard constraints from dimensions.** Constraints (EU residency, a licence forbidding\n  commercial use, a budget ceiling, a launch date) are filters: an option violating one is removed before\n  scoring.\n- **Why filter first:** treating a constraint as a weighted dimension is how a disqualified option \"wins\n  on points\".\n- **Constraints come from the NFRs**, which is why trade-off analysis without written NFRs degrades into\n  opinion.\n\n### 2. Enumerate real options\n- **At least three**, including \"do nothing or defer\", and usually a hybrid.\n- **Why:** one option is a ratification; two is often a false dichotomy.\n- **The hybrid is frequently the answer** (hosted model for generation, self-hosted for embeddings; small\n  model escalating to a large one), but only found if it is on the list.\n\n### 3. Choose the dimensions from the NFR priorities\n- **Four to seven dimensions**, drawn from the architecturally significant requirements, not a generic\n  template: quality on your golden set, p95 latency, cost per successful task, operational burden, vendor\n  or lock-in risk, time to deliver, reversibility.\n- **Fewer dimensions with real evidence** beat a twelve-column matrix of guesses.\n- **The priority order is the one the business already agreed in the NFRs**, so the tie-breaker is not\n  invented during the analysis.\n\n### 4. Fill each cell with evidence, and label its grade\n| Grade | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Measured** | Your workload, your eval set |\n| **Prototyped** | A spike on representative data |\n| **Estimated** | Back-of-envelope from known prices and throughput |\n| **Asserted** | Someone's belief |\n\n- **Write the grade next to the number.**\n- **Spend a prototyping day on the two cells with the highest weight and lowest grade**, *because* they\n  decide the outcome and are the most likely to be wrong.\n- **Carry ranges, not points:** \"cost per 1k conversations $30–55 depending on cache hit rate\" beats\n  \"$42\".\n\n### 5. Find the sensitivity points and the flip conditions\nThis step distinguishes analysis from scoring.\n- **For each important input, ask at what value the ranking changes:** monthly volume, GPU utilisation,\n  provider price, the quality threshold, team size.\n- **ATAM vocabulary:** these are sensitivity points; a parameter that moves two quality attributes in\n  opposite directions is a trade-off point.\n- **A flip condition close to your expected operating point means the decision is fragile.** The right\n  output is then not a winner but a design that makes switching cheap (an abstraction such as a gateway)\n  plus a trigger to re-run the analysis.\n\n### 6. Weigh reversibility and the cost of being wrong\n- **Two-way doors** (swap the model behind a gateway, change a chunking strategy) can be decided quickly on\n  thin evidence, because the cost of error is small.\n- **One-way doors** (train on customer data, commit to an embedding model that fixes a billion-vector\n  index, sign a multi-year commitment) deserve prototypes, ranges and a pre-mortem.\n- **Under uncertainty, reversibility has option value** that a static matrix does not show, so give it\n  explicit credit.\n\n### 7. Decide, record, schedule the revisit\n- **Write the outcome as an ADR:** constraints, options, the matrix with evidence grades, flip conditions\n  and revisit triggers.\n- **Record the inputs, or the analysis is not reusable.** \"We chose hosted\" is unreproducible; \"we chose\n  hosted because break-even was 18B tokens a month at 50% utilisation and we expect 2B\" can be re-run in\n  five minutes next year.\n\n### Techniques that fit inside the steps\n| Technique | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| Break-even arithmetic | Cost cells |\n| Scenario walkthroughs (top NFR scenarios through each option on a whiteboard) | Availability and operability |\n| Utility tree | Agreeing which dimensions matter |\n| Pre-mortem (\"a year later this choice failed, why?\") | Risks the matrix missed |\n| Halving test (\"still choose this if volume halved or doubled?\") | A cheap sensitivity check |\n\n### Scoring under forecast uncertainty: regret\nWhen the forecast itself is the uncertainty, score per scenario rather than at one point:\n1. Compute each option's cost at the low, expected and high ends of the range.\n2. Its **regret** in each scenario is its cost minus the best option's cost in that scenario.\n3. Choose the option whose worst-case regret is smallest.\n\nThis is a defensible rule when nobody can honestly narrow the range, and the table makes visible which\nscenario each option is betting on.","example":"### Break-even: the cost cell of hosted vs self-hosted\n- **Illustrative figures only:** replace them with your own quotes and a load-tested throughput number.\n  The script is the shape of the reasoning, not a benchmark.\n- **The finding:** at these inputs self-hosting only wins above roughly 18 billion tokens a month, and a\n  drop in utilisation from 50% to 40% makes it never win, *because* idle GPUs still bill.\n- **That utilisation sensitivity belongs in the ADR**, alongside the quality and risk dimensions this cell\n  says nothing about.\n\n```python\n# Illustrative figures only. Replace with your provider quote and your measured throughput.\nHOSTED_PER_M  = 2.00         # $ per 1M tokens, blended input/output\nGPU_HOUR      = 2.50         # $ per GPU-hour, on-demand\nTOK_PER_GPU_H = 3_000_000    # tokens one GPU serves per hour on YOUR workload (load-tested)\nOPS_PER_MONTH = 6_000        # $ people + tooling to run serving, paid regardless of volume\n\ndef monthly(tokens: float, util: float) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    hosted = tokens / 1e6 * HOSTED_PER_M\n    self_hosted = tokens / (TOK_PER_GPU_H * util) * GPU_HOUR + OPS_PER_MONTH\n    return hosted, self_hosted\n\ndef break_even_tokens(util: float) -> float:\n    # hosted == self_hosted  =>  tokens * (price_per_token - gpu_cost_per_token) == fixed ops cost\n    gap = HOSTED_PER_M / 1e6 - GPU_HOUR / (TOK_PER_GPU_H * util)\n    return OPS_PER_MONTH / gap if gap > 0 else float(\"inf\")\n\nfor util in (0.5, 0.4):\n    be = break_even_tokens(util)\n    verdict = f\"{be / 1e9:.1f}B tokens/month\" if be != float(\"inf\") else \"never (GPU cost/token exceeds hosted)\"\n    print(f\"utilisation {util:.0%}: self-hosting pays off above {verdict}\")\n    for t in (5e8, 5e9, 5e10):\n        h, s = monthly(t, util)\n        print(f\"   {t / 1e9:>5.1f}B tokens   hosted ${h:>9,.0f}   self-hosted ${s:>9,.0f}\")\n\n# utilisation 50%: self-hosting pays off above 18.0B tokens/month\n#      0.5B tokens   hosted $    1,000   self-hosted $    6,833\n#      5.0B tokens   hosted $   10,000   self-hosted $   14,333\n#     50.0B tokens   hosted $  100,000   self-hosted $   89,333\n# utilisation 40%: self-hosting pays off above never (GPU cost/token exceeds hosted)\n#      0.5B tokens   hosted $    1,000   self-hosted $    7,042\n#      5.0B tokens   hosted $   10,000   self-hosted $   16,417\n#     50.0B tokens   hosted $  100,000   self-hosted $  110,167\n```\n\n**Utilisation is not a free parameter.** It is served tokens divided by the capacity you pay for, and two\nthings cap it:\n- **Peak sizing.** A fleet sized for peak idles off-peak, so with a peak-to-average ratio of 2 and no\n  autoscaling, utilisation cannot exceed 50%.\n- **A minimum fleet.** Say two GPUs for redundancy running 24/7 (~1,460 GPU-hours, ~$3,650 a month here),\n  billed at any volume. At 0.5B tokens it would run about 11% utilised, so the real self-hosted cost is\n  ~$9,650, not $6,833.\n- **What it changes.** The floor does not move the 18B break-even, where the fleet is already ~16 GPUs,\n  but it shows why the utilisation cell should be derived from a traffic profile, not typed in.\n\n### Runnable: minimax regret when the forecast is a range\nSame illustrative cost model at 50% utilisation, scored across a low, expected and high volume. The choice\nflips with the high end of the forecast, which is exactly what the ADR must record.\n\n```python\n# Illustrative: same cost model as the break-even script, at 50% utilisation.\ndef hosted(t):      return t / 1e6 * 2.00\ndef self_hosted(t): return t / (3_000_000 * 0.5) * 2.50 + 6_000\n\nOPTIONS = {\"hosted\": hosted, \"self-hosted\": self_hosted}\n\ndef minimax_regret(scenarios: dict[str, float]) -> str:\n    cost = {o: {s: f(t) for s, t in scenarios.items()} for o, f in OPTIONS.items()}\n    best = {s: min(cost[o][s] for o in OPTIONS) for s in scenarios}\n    worst_regret = {o: max(cost[o][s] - best[s] for s in scenarios) for o in OPTIONS}\n    for o in OPTIONS:\n        print(f\"  {o:<12}\" + \"\".join(f\"{s}: ${cost[o][s]:>7,.0f}  \" for s in scenarios)\n              + f\"worst regret ${worst_regret[o]:,.0f}\")\n    return min(worst_regret, key=worst_regret.get)\n\nfor high in (30e9, 50e9):\n    print(f\"forecast 1B / 5B / {high / 1e9:.0f}B tokens per month\")\n    pick = minimax_regret({\"low\": 1e9, \"expected\": 5e9, \"high\": high})\n    print(\"  minimax-regret choice:\", pick)\n```\n\n| Forecast (tokens/month) | Hosted worst regret | Self-hosted worst regret | Choice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1B / 5B / 30B | $4,000 | $5,667 | Hosted |\n| 1B / 5B / 50B | $10,667 | $5,667 | Self-hosted |\n\n- **Read it as a sensitivity finding**, not a verdict: the answer depends on how plausible the high\n  scenario is.\n- **Add the minimum-fleet floor** from the note above and self-hosting's low-volume regret grows, which\n  pushes back toward hosted behind a gateway plus a volume trigger.","patterns":["**Evidence-graded matrix in the ADR.** Use for every one-way door. Each cell holds a range and a grade (measured, prototyped, estimated, asserted), and the ADR template has a column for both. Monitor the share of high-weight cells still graded \"asserted\" at decision time.","**Time-boxed spike on the riskiest cells.** Use when the two highest-weight, lowest-grade cells could flip the result. Run the spike on your golden set and your traffic shape, not a vendor demo. Monitor whether spike results changed the ranking; if they never do, the matrix is being built backwards.","**Pin priorities before scoring.** Use in every review. Commit the NFR priority order and the dimension list before any option is scored, so the history shows weights did not move afterwards. Monitor weight changes after the first scoring pass.","**Gateway plus flip-input dashboard.** Use when the flip condition is near the operating point. Put the choice behind an abstraction (an LLM gateway, a retriever interface) and chart the flip input (monthly tokens, utilisation, price) against its threshold. Alert when it crosses and link the ADR.","**Pre-mortem and halving test in the review agenda.** Use for one-way doors. Ask \"a year later this failed, why?\" and \"would we still choose this at half or double the volume?\" before deciding. Monitor risks raised there that later appear in incidents."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Frame decision and owner\"] --> C[\"Filter by hard constraints\"]\n    C --> O[\"Three or more options incl. hybrid\"]\n    O --> D[\"Dimensions from NFR priorities\"]\n    D --> E[\"Cells with evidence grades and ranges\"]\n    E --> S{\"Flip condition near operating point?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| W[\"Pick the winner\"]\n    S -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Design for cheap switching\"]\n    W --> R[\"Weigh reversibility\"]\n    G --> R\n    R --> A[\"Record ADR with triggers\"]\n    A -.->|\"trigger fires\"| F","caption":"The seven steps as a pipeline; the sensitivity check decides whether you pick a winner or design for cheap\nswitching, and the revisit trigger loops back to the start."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is a weighted scoring matrix often worse than it looks?","a":"- **It is usually built backwards:** the favourite is chosen first and weights are tuned until it wins.\n- **It hides uncertainty:** single-digit totals such as 7.4 vs 7.1 pretend to be measurements when most\n  cells are asserted.\n- **Fix:** fix the priority order from the NFRs before scoring, grade every cell's evidence, carry ranges,\n  and run a sensitivity analysis instead of trusting the total."},{"q":"Walk me through deciding between a large hosted model and a small self-hosted one.","a":"- **Filter:** residency, licence, security review, and whether anyone can operate GPUs.\n- **Options:** hosted large, self-hosted small, and a hybrid where the small model escalates hard cases.\n- **Evidence:** quality on your golden set, p95 latency under load, cost per successful task, operational\n  burden, each graded.\n- **Decide on the flip:** compute break-even volume and utilisation, compare with the forecast range, and\n  keep the choice behind a gateway."},{"q":"Self-hosting looked cheaper on paper but the bill is higher. What went wrong?","a":"- **Utilisation was typed in, not derived:** a fleet sized for peak with a peak-to-average ratio of 2\n  cannot exceed 50% utilisation without autoscaling.\n- **Fixed costs were missed:** the operations team and a minimum redundant fleet bill at any volume; in the\n  example, two GPUs 24/7 add ~$3,650 a month.\n- **At 40% utilisation** the illustrative model never breaks even, because cost per served token exceeds\n  the hosted price."},{"q":"When would you not run a full trade-off analysis?","a":"- **For two-way doors**, such as swapping a model behind a gateway or changing a chunking strategy: the cost\n  of being wrong is small, so a paragraph with one flip condition is enough.\n- **When inputs do not exist yet:** say so, choose the most reversible option, and set a short revisit date\n  rather than inventing numbers."},{"q":"What is a sensitivity point, and what do you do when one is near your operating point?","a":"- **Definition (ATAM):** an input whose change shifts the ranking; one that moves two quality attributes in\n  opposite directions is a trade-off point.\n- **Near the operating point the decision is fragile:** design for cheap switching, monitor the input on a\n  dashboard, and record a trigger to re-run the analysis when it crosses."}],"tradeoffs":["**Full matrix: decisions that survive review vs days of work.** For two-way doors, a one-paragraph analysis with a named flip condition is the right amount of rigour.","**Prototyping uncertain cells: measurements vs unrepresentative spikes.** A spike on unrepresentative data is worse than an honest estimate; the prototype must use your golden set and your traffic shape.","**Explicit weights: arguable priorities vs tuning toward a favourite.** The defence is fixing the priority order in the NFRs before any option is scored.","**Optimising for reversibility: open options vs abstraction cost.** Every switching abstraction costs something and can hide capabilities of what sits behind it. It pays off when the flip condition is near, not when one option clearly dominates.","**Sensitivity analysis: knowing what matters vs needing real inputs.** It needs volume forecasts, utilisation and prices; with none, say so and set a short revisit date instead of inventing them."],"pitfalls":["**Weights adjusted after the first scoring pass.** Symptom: the matrix history shows the favourite losing, then the weights changing, then the favourite winning.","**Dimensions copied from a generic template, not the NFRs.** Symptom: a column such as \"community size\" outweighs the quality threshold the product will be judged on.","**Point estimates with false precision.** Symptom: a decision resting on a 7.4 versus 7.1 total that no one can reproduce.","**A provider's or paper's benchmark as the quality cell.** Symptom: a model wins on the leaderboard and loses on your golden set after the contract is signed.","**Forgetting the fixed operational cost of running things yourself.** Symptom: a self-hosting plan whose per-token cost looked lower and whose monthly bill is higher.","**Typing in a utilisation figure instead of deriving it from the traffic profile.** Symptom: a GPU bill well above plan, because the fleet is sized for peak and idles overnight and at weekends.","**Treating a hard constraint as a weighted dimension.** Symptom: a vendor that \"scored best\" cannot satisfy the customer's residency requirement.","**Never recording the inputs.** Symptom: the same debate, from scratch, every time a new model or price list appears."],"when":"- **Full method:** one-way doors and any choice whose flip condition is plausibly near your operating\n  point: hosted versus self-hosted, embedding model and vector store, tenancy model, fine-tune versus\n  retrieval, build versus buy on a core capability.\n- **Short form for everything else:** constraints, three options, one flip condition, a reversibility\n  note.\n- **Skip it** for decisions that are cheap to undo.\n- **Measure:** your own quality on your own golden set, your own throughput under your own load, and your\n  own volume forecast with a range.\n- **Interviews:** the strongest answers to \"would you use X or Y\" name the dimensions, state the deciding\n  assumption, give the value at which the answer flips, and say how they keep the choice reversible until\n  the data arrives.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the measured cells, latency under load, eval scores, token counts, cache hit rates, and builds the spike that turns the riskiest estimate into a measurement.","architect":"Owns the framing, the constraint-versus-dimension split, the priority order inherited from the NFRs, the sensitivity analysis and the written ADR with revisit triggers.","fde":"Runs the analysis with the customer's real volumes, prices and constraints, and shows them the flip condition so the recommendation is understood as conditional rather than as vendor preference."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks whether they should self-host an open-weight model or keep using a hosted API,\nand wants a yes or no. How do you structure the answer so it is both decisive and honest?","a":"I would first apply the hard constraints: residency, licence, security review, and whether they\nhave anyone to operate GPUs. If self-hosting survives those, I would score the live options, hosted,\nself-hosted and a hybrid, on the dimensions their NFRs prioritise: quality on their golden set, p95\nlatency, cost per successful task, operational burden and reversibility, labelling each cell as measured,\nprototyped or estimated. The decisive part is the flip condition: with their quotes and a load-tested\nthroughput, self-hosting breaks even above some monthly token volume at a given utilisation, and I would\ncompare that to their forecast range. If the forecast is well below it, the answer is hosted, behind a\ngateway so the switch stays cheap, with a revisit trigger at a stated volume or when an open-weight model\nreaches eval parity. If the forecast straddles the flip point, the recommendation is the hybrid and a\nmeasured pilot rather than a bet."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should hard constraints be applied as filters before scoring, rather than as heavily weighted dimensions?","options":["Because scoring matrices cannot represent more than five dimensions","Because an option that violates a constraint can still win on total points, producing a choice that cannot actually be deployed","Because constraints always come from legal rather than engineering","Because filtering is faster to compute"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A residency or licence violation is disqualifying regardless of how well the option scores elsewhere; giving it a weight lets strong scores on other columns outvote a requirement that has no compensation."},{"q":"What is the most valuable output of a sensitivity analysis in an architecture decision?","options":["A more precise total score for the winning option","A list of all the stakeholders consulted","The input values at which the ranking of options changes, compared with the expected operating point","A ranking of dimensions by how hard they are to measure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Knowing where the answer flips tells you whether the decision is robust or fragile and what to monitor as a revisit trigger. A more precise score does not reveal how much the result depends on uncertain inputs."},{"q":"In the break-even example, why can lowering GPU utilisation from 50% to 40% make self-hosting never pay off?","options":["Because the hosted provider lowers its price at lower volumes","Because lower utilisation increases the fixed operational cost","Because idle GPU time still bills, so the effective cost per served token rises above the hosted per-token price","Because utilisation below 50% triggers autoscaling penalties"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-hosted cost per token is GPU cost per hour divided by tokens actually served per hour; serving fewer tokens on the same billed hardware raises it. Once it exceeds the hosted price per token, no volume recovers the fixed costs."}]}]});
